ForgeData.register("econ",{"ECON-1.1":{"label":"Supply & Demand","color":"#1e3a5f","questions":[{"id":"SD-01","stem":"A student says: 'When the price of coffee rises, demand falls.' What is the more precise economic statement?","options":{"A":"When the price of coffee rises, the quantity demanded falls.","B":"The statement is correct as written.","C":"Demand rises whenever price rises, because the Veblen effect explains every good a household chooses to buy.","D":"Price has no effect on demand."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-MOVEMENT","scaffold":"'Demand' = the whole relationship between price and quantity (the curve). 'Quantity demanded' = a specific amount at a specific price (a point on the curve). A price change causes a movement ALONG the curve (change in Qd). A shift of the whole demand curve is caused by non-price factors: income, tastes, prices of related goods, expectations, population. This distinction is tested constantly.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of the following would shift the demand curve for electric cars to the RIGHT?","options":{"A":"A fall in electric-car production costs, which shifts supply rather than demand.","B":"A fall in the price of electric cars, causing movement along the existing demand curve.","C":"Higher incomes for a normal good or a higher petrol price, with no change in the car's own price.","D":"A fall in petrol prices that makes petrol cars more attractive, with no change in electric-car prices."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"ECON-1.1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"SD-02","stem":"A rise in wages for baristas increases the cost of producing coffee. On a supply and demand diagram, this causes:","options":{"A":"A guaranteed surplus of homes, since landlords respond to a lower legal rent by building and offering more properties","B":"The supply curve shifts left — higher production costs mean less supplied at every price.","C":"A movement along the existing supply curve, not a shift.","D":"Both supply and demand curves shift left simultaneously."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SD-02","scaffold":"Supply shifts: LEFTWARD when costs rise (wages, raw materials, taxes, energy) or regulations increase. RIGHTWARD when costs fall (technology improvements, subsidies, cheaper inputs). Demand curve is unaffected by production costs — it reflects consumer willingness to pay, not firm costs. A movement along supply = price changes; a shift = non-price factors change.","reforge":{"stem":"A government subsidy is given to UK solar panel manufacturers. What happens to the supply curve for solar panels?","options":{"A":"Shifts left, because a government subsidy is treated by firms as if it were an extra cost that reduces profit margins on every unit sold, so output falls at each price.","B":"No change — subsidies only affect demand.","C":"The demand curve shifts right.","D":"Shifts right — subsidies lower production costs, so firms are willing to supply more at every price."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"ECON-1.1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"SD-03","stem":"At the market equilibrium price for wheat, which of the following is true?","options":{"A":"Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied.","B":"Quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded.","C":"Quantity demanded equals quantity supplied","D":"Price is at its maximum possible level."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-EQUILIBRIUM","scaffold":"Equilibrium = where demand and supply curves intersect. At this price: Qd = Qs, no surplus (excess supply) and no shortage (excess demand). Above equilibrium price: surplus → price falls back to equilibrium. Below equilibrium price: shortage → price rises back to equilibrium. The price mechanism automatically moves markets toward equilibrium.","reforge":{"stem":"A government sets a maximum price (price ceiling) for rent BELOW the equilibrium market rent. What results?","options":{"A":"A shortage of housing: at the lower price, quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied.","B":"No effect — the market adjusts automatically.","C":"Equilibrium is re-established immediately.","D":"The highest price firms can charge before every single consumer refuses to buy any of the good at all"},"correct":"A"},"spec":"ECON-1.1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"SD-04","stem":"The price elasticity of demand (PED) for insulin is estimated at −0.1. This means:","options":{"A":"Demand is price elastic — a 1% rise in price causes a 10% fall in demand.","B":"There is no relationship between price and demand for insulin.","C":"Demand rises when price rises.","D":"Demand is price inelastic"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-INELASTIC","scaffold":"PED = % change in Qd / % change in price. Inelastic: |PED| < 1 (quantity barely responds to price). Elastic: |PED| > 1 (quantity responds strongly). Factors affecting PED: necessity vs luxury, availability of substitutes, proportion of income spent, time period. Insulin = necessity + no close substitutes = very inelastic. Revenue implication: price rise → higher revenue for inelastic goods.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm raises the price of its product by 10% and total revenue rises. This suggests:","options":{"A":"Demand is horizontal, so even the smallest possible change in price causes quantity demanded to change enormously","B":"Demand is price inelastic: quantity falls proportionally less than price rises.","C":"Supply is perfectly inelastic.","D":"The good is an inferior good."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"ECON-1.1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"SD-05","stem":"Butter and margarine are substitute goods. If the price of butter rises significantly, what happens in the market for margarine?","options":{"A":"Demand for margarine rises","B":"Demand for margarine falls — consumers have less money after buying butter.","C":"Supply of margarine falls — butter manufacturers cut back.","D":"The margarine market is unaffected."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-XED","scaffold":"Substitutes: goods that can be used in place of each other (butter/margarine, Coke/Pepsi, bus/train). A rise in the price of Good A → consumers switch to Good B → demand for B increases (rightward shift). Complements work oppositely: goods consumed together (cars and petrol) — rise in price of cars → demand for petrol falls. Cross-price elasticity of demand (XED) distinguishes: positive XED = substitutes, negative XED = complements.","reforge":{"stem":"Petrol and cars are complement goods. If the government imposes a large tax on petrol, raising its price significantly, what happens to the demand for cars?","options":{"A":"Demand for cars is unaffected — they are different markets.","B":"Demand for cars rises, because drivers switch to buying more fuel-efficient models to offset the higher cost of running them.","C":"Demand for cars falls because higher petrol costs raise the cost of car ownership.","D":"Supply of cars falls."},"correct":"C"},"spec":"ECON-1.1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"SD-06","stem":"A student correctly draws a supply and demand diagram but labels the vertical axis 'price' and the horizontal axis 'quantity'. Why might this be penalised in an Edexcel Economics exam?","options":{"A":"The labels are already correct — no change needed.","B":"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal.","C":"The axes should be reversed: quantity on vertical, price on horizontal.","D":"Economics diagrams do not require labelled axes in exam answers, since examiners mark only the position and slope of the curves drawn."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-TECH-04","scaffold":"Edexcel marks scheme requires: vertical axis = Price (P or £), horizontal axis = Quantity (Q) per period (per week/month/year). Also: both curves must be labelled (D and S or D1/D2 for shifts), equilibrium must be marked (P* and Q* or Pe and Qe), arrows showing direction of shift. Missing any of these is flagged in examiner reports as a common error worth losing easy marks.","reforge":{"stem":"A student draws a supply curve shifting right but forgets to label the new curve. What does the examiner report say about this?","options":{"A":"A limit on the quantity supplied, set by government to prevent producers from selling beyond a fixed amount","B":"Only demand curves need labels.","C":"Arrows replace the need for labels.","D":"Unlabelled curves are a common error; the new curve and equilibrium changes must be identified."},"correct":"D"},"spec":"ECON-1.1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"SD-07","stem":"In a free market, a sudden frost destroys 30% of the UK's strawberry crop. Trace the effect through to the new equilibrium.","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"The frost reduces the supply of strawberries — the supply curve shifts left (fewer strawberries available at every price) (K). At the original price, quantity supplied now falls short of quantity demanded — a shortage exists. This excess demand puts upward pressure on price (An). Price rises until a new equilibrium is reached where quantity demanded has fallen (movement along demand curve) and quantity supplied has recovered to meet it — at a higher price and lower quantity than before (An).","tag":"MC-SD-02","scaffold":"Chain: supply shock (frost) → supply shifts left → shortage at old price → price rises → Qd falls + Qs rises → new equilibrium at higher P, lower Q. Draw: original D and S, S shifts left to S2, new equilibrium P2 (higher) and Q2 (lower). Mark with dotted lines.","correct":"C","spec":"ECON-1.1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"SD-08","stem":"Price elasticity of supply (PES) for new housing is very low in the short run. Which factor MOST explains this?","options":{"A":"Low demand for housing.","B":"Builders choose not to respond to higher prices.","C":"The government controls all house building, issuing every permit and directly constructing every new home itself.","D":"Supply of housing is constrained by planning permission and construction time."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-PES-SHORTRUN","scaffold":"PES factors: time period (supply more elastic in long run), spare capacity (elastic if factories idle), ease of storage (storable goods more elastic), factor mobility (can resources switch to this industry?). Housing: long production time, planning constraints, land scarcity → very low PES (inelastic). Implication: a rise in house prices leads to little new supply in the short run, mainly just higher prices — relevant to UK housing crisis.","reforge":{"stem":"PES for oil extracted from the North Sea is low because:","options":{"A":"There is too much oil — supply is very elastic, since producers can drill as many new wells as they like within days whenever the price rises, with no capacity constraints at all.","B":"Extraction requires large fixed capital investment and infrastructure that cannot be rapidly scaled up in response to price rises — making supply inelastic in the short run.","C":"North Sea oil is of poor quality.","D":"Oil prices are controlled by the government."},"correct":"B"},"spec":"ECON-1.1","specGranularity":"bank"},{"id":"TH1-SC-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What does scarcity mean in economics?","options":{"A":"Resources are limited relative to wants","B":"Every want can be satisfied eventually","C":"Prices always rise when demand falls","D":"Money is the only resource that is scarce"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-SCARCITY","scaffold":"Scarcity exists because land, labour, capital and enterprise are finite while wants are unlimited. Every economic choice therefore has an opportunity cost.","reforge":{"stem":"Why must households make economic choices?","options":{"A":"All goods are produced by governments","B":"They cannot compare alternative uses, because households are assumed to have no way of ranking their own preferences","C":"Their resources cannot satisfy every want","D":"Choices have no opportunity cost"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-SC-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the opportunity cost of attending a revision session?","options":{"A":"Every activity missed during the session","B":"The best alternative use of that time","C":"The money already spent on textbooks","D":"The total number of hours in the school day"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-SCARCITY","scaffold":"Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative forgone, not every alternative and not necessarily a money cost.","reforge":{"stem":"A student chooses paid work over revision. What is the opportunity cost?","options":{"A":"The wage earned from the chosen work","B":"The student's whole future income","C":"All school subjects studied that year","D":"The value of the revision given up"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-SC-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"Which factor of production is rewarded by wages?","options":{"A":"Capital","B":"Land","C":"Labour","D":"Enterprise"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-SCARCITY","scaffold":"Labour is human effort, physical or mental, and its factor reward is wages. Land earns rent, capital earns interest and enterprise earns profit.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor of production is rewarded by rent?","options":{"A":"Land, rewarded through rent","B":"Labour, rewarded through wages","C":"Capital, rewarded through interest","D":"Enterprise, rewarded through profit"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-PPC-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"A point inside a production possibility frontier indicates:","options":{"A":"Productive capacity has reached its maximum","B":"Output is impossible with current resources","C":"The economy has no opportunity cost","D":"Resources are not being used fully"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-PPF","scaffold":"A point inside the PPF shows productive inefficiency or spare capacity. A point on the frontier is attainable and efficient; a point outside is unattainable with current resources.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a point outside a current PPF show?","options":{"A":"It is inefficient but attainable now","B":"It requires more or better resources","C":"It shows zero opportunity cost","D":"It must be the current equilibrium"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-PPC-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"What would shift a PPF outward?","options":{"A":"An improvement in production technology","B":"A fall in consumer confidence","C":"A movement from unemployment to work, which shifts the whole economy from inside the frontier out onto a new, higher curve","D":"A rise in the price of one good"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-PPF","scaffold":"Better technology raises productive capacity and shifts the frontier outward. Demand changes move the economy towards or along the frontier but do not change capacity.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change could expand an economy's PPF?","options":{"A":"A fall in export demand","B":"Lower household spending","C":"A larger skilled labour force","D":"A change in the price level, which is treated as shifting the frontier whenever inflation alters the value of output"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-PPC-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"Why is a bowed-out PPF common?","options":{"A":"All resources are equally suited to both goods, so every unit transferred between them is given up at exactly the same rate","B":"Opportunity cost rises as resources are transferred","C":"The economy has unlimited resources","D":"Prices must fall as output expands"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-PPF","scaffold":"Resources are specialised, so shifting more of them towards one good sacrifices increasingly large amounts of the other good. This creates increasing opportunity cost.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a straight-line PPF imply?","options":{"A":"Demand determines productive capacity, so the frontier itself shifts in and out purely in response to changes in consumer spending","B":"No resources are scarce","C":"Production is always inefficient","D":"Constant opportunity cost between the goods"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-SPL-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is absolute advantage?","options":{"A":"Exporting more than every rival","B":"Having the lower opportunity cost of production compared with every other producer in the market","C":"Producing more output with the same resources","D":"Producing without using scarce resources"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-ABSOLUTE","scaffold":"Absolute advantage compares productivity: a producer can make more of a good from the same inputs. Comparative advantage instead compares opportunity cost.","reforge":{"stem":"A country makes 12 cars or 24 tonnes of wheat with its resources. What is being measured?","options":{"A":"Its possible productivity trade-off","B":"Its inflation rate, calculated from the change in the cost of producing cars and wheat over the period","C":"Its current account balance","D":"Its money supply"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-SPL-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"Why can specialisation raise total output?","options":{"A":"Workers no longer need different skills","B":"Scarcity is eliminated by trade, because specialising according to comparative advantage removes the need to make further choices","C":"Every country becomes best at every product","D":"Resources are concentrated where opportunity cost is lower"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-SPECIALISATION","scaffold":"Specialisation based on comparative advantage allows resources to be used more efficiently, increasing combined output before trade distributes the gains.","reforge":{"stem":"Comparative advantage is based on differences in:","options":{"A":"Population size only","B":"Opportunity cost","C":"Currency denomination","D":"Government spending"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-SPL-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is one risk of excessive specialisation?","options":{"A":"The economy becomes exposed to a sector-specific shock","B":"Opportunity cost disappears permanently once an economy specialises fully in the industry it produces most efficiently","C":"Trade becomes impossible","D":"Productivity must fall in every industry"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-SPECIALISATION","scaffold":"Specialisation can raise efficiency but creates dependence on particular goods, markets or supply chains. A price fall or demand shock can therefore cause serious losses.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a specialised region have unstable employment?","options":{"A":"Its PPF cannot be drawn","B":"All workers become perfectly mobile, so a fall in demand for the main industry is absorbed instantly by other sectors","C":"Its main industry may face a sudden demand fall","D":"Trade guarantees stable prices"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-DEM-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the law of demand?","options":{"A":"Demand always rises with price, because consumers treat a higher price as a signal of higher quality and buy more of it","B":"Quantity demanded usually falls as price rises","C":"Supply is fixed at every price","D":"Income has no effect on purchases"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-MOVEMENT","scaffold":"Ceteris paribus, a higher price reduces quantity demanded and a lower price increases it. A change in price causes movement along the demand curve.","reforge":{"stem":"A fall in a product's price usually causes:","options":{"A":"A movement along supply only","B":"A leftward demand shift, because a lower price is treated as a change in one of the non-price determinants of demand","C":"A fall in consumer income","D":"An extension of quantity demanded"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-DEM-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"Which would shift demand for a normal good right?","options":{"A":"A fall in the population of buyers","B":"A rise in the good's own price","C":"A rise in household incomes","D":"A rise in the price of a complement"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-SHIFT","scaffold":"Demand for a normal good rises when incomes increase. Other demand shifters include tastes, population, expectations and prices of substitutes or complements.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would increase demand for a normal good?","options":{"A":"More consumers enter the market","B":"Its own price rises","C":"A complement becomes more expensive","D":"Household incomes fall"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-DEM-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"Tea and coffee are substitutes. If coffee becomes dearer, demand for tea is likely to:","options":{"A":"Supply of margarine falls — butter manufacturers cut back.","B":"Decrease to zero","C":"Remain fixed by supply","D":"Increase as consumers switch away from dearer coffee"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-XED","scaffold":"Substitutes satisfy similar wants. A rise in coffee's price makes tea relatively more attractive, shifting tea demand right.","reforge":{"stem":"If printers and ink cartridges are complements, cheaper printers may:","options":{"A":"Reduce demand for cartridges","B":"Increase demand for cartridges","C":"Leave all related demand unchanged","D":"Shift cartridge supply left automatically"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-SUP-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the law of supply?","options":{"A":"Quantity supplied usually rises as price rises","B":"Supply always falls when price rises, because firms interpret a higher price as a signal to withdraw output from the market","C":"Demand is fixed by firms","D":"Costs never affect output decisions"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-SUPPLY-DETERMINANTS","scaffold":"A higher price can make production more profitable, so firms generally offer more for sale, ceteris paribus. A change in price causes movement along the supply curve.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in a product's price usually causes:","options":{"A":"A fall in production incentives","B":"A leftward supply shift, because a higher price is treated as a change in one of the non-price determinants of supply","C":"An extension of quantity supplied","D":"A shift in consumer preferences"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-SUP-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"Which change would shift supply right?","options":{"A":"A rise in the product's own price","B":"A fall in firms' production costs","C":"A fall in the number of sellers","D":"A new tax on each unit"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-SUPPLY-DETERMINANTS","scaffold":"Lower costs increase profitability at each price and shift supply right. Technology, input prices, taxes, subsidies and the number of firms can shift supply.","reforge":{"stem":"A production subsidy is most likely to:","options":{"A":"Reduce the number of suppliers","B":"Shift demand to the left","C":"Raise firms' unit costs","D":"Shift supply to the right"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-SUP-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"How can bad weather affect the supply of farm produce?","options":{"A":"It shifts demand right without changing output","B":"It always increases demand for the crop","C":"It can shift supply left by reducing yields","D":"It removes the crop's opportunity cost"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-SUPPLY-DETERMINANTS","scaffold":"Weather can damage crops and reduce the quantity producers can supply at each price. The result is a leftward supply shift and potential price increase.","reforge":{"stem":"A disease destroys part of a potato harvest. The likely immediate effect is:","options":{"A":"Lower supply and a higher market price","B":"Higher supply and a lower price, because farmers respond to crop losses by planting and harvesting extra potatoes immediately","C":"Lower demand with unchanged supply","D":"No effect on equilibrium"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-EQ-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What determines a competitive market equilibrium?","options":{"A":"The government's preferred output","B":"The highest price firms can charge before every single consumer refuses to buy any of the good at all","C":"The quantity consumers would like for free","D":"The price where quantity demanded equals supplied"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-EQUILIBRIUM","scaffold":"Equilibrium occurs where demand and supply intersect. At that price there is no pressure from a shortage or surplus to change the market outcome.","reforge":{"stem":"At an equilibrium price, a market has:","options":{"A":"A permanent shortage, because the price mechanism is unable to adjust quantity demanded and quantity supplied together","B":"No excess demand or excess supply","C":"A guaranteed monopoly","D":"No consumer choice"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-EQ-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"What happens when price is below equilibrium?","options":{"A":"A shortage is likely","B":"A surplus must occur","C":"Supply shifts right immediately","D":"Demand becomes perfectly inelastic"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-EQUILIBRIUM","scaffold":"At a price below equilibrium, quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied. Competition between buyers may push the price upwards.","reforge":{"stem":"A shortage means that:","options":{"A":"Price is necessarily above equilibrium, since any imbalance between buyers and sellers only ever occurs when price is too high","B":"Firms have unsold stock","C":"Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied","D":"Demand has shifted left"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-EQ-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"What happens when price is above equilibrium?","options":{"A":"Consumers compete for too few units","B":"A surplus is likely","C":"Demand must shift right","D":"Firms cannot reduce prices"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-EQUILIBRIUM","scaffold":"At a price above equilibrium, quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded, leaving unsold stock and pressure for price reductions.","reforge":{"stem":"Unsold stock at the current price indicates:","options":{"A":"A fall in productive capacity","B":"Excess demand","C":"A shortage of producers","D":"Excess supply"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-PED-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What does price elasticity of demand measure?","options":{"A":"The level of profit at each price","B":"Responsiveness of price to income, showing how far a firm's price must move whenever household income changes","C":"Responsiveness of quantity demanded to price","D":"The change in supply after a tax"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-ELASTIC","scaffold":"PED measures the percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in price. It is usually negative, though answers often use its absolute value.","reforge":{"stem":"A PED of 0.4 indicates demand is:","options":{"A":"Price inelastic","B":"Unit elastic","C":"Perfectly elastic","D":"Income elastic"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-PED-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"If demand is price elastic, a price cut will usually:","options":{"A":"Reduce quantity demanded","B":"Reduce sales revenue in every case","C":"Leave revenue unchanged","D":"Increase total revenue"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-ELASTIC","scaffold":"With elastic demand, the percentage rise in quantity demanded exceeds the percentage fall in price, so total revenue rises. The reverse applies to a price rise.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm with elastic demand raises price. Total revenue is likely to:","options":{"A":"Rise by the same percentage","B":"Fall","C":"Stay fixed","D":"Rise because quantity falls"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-PED-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"Which factor tends to make demand more price elastic?","options":{"A":"Many close substitutes are available","B":"The good is a basic necessity","C":"The time period is very short","D":"The good takes a tiny share of income"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-ELASTIC","scaffold":"Consumers can switch easily when substitutes exist, so quantity demanded responds strongly to price. Necessities, short time periods and low budget shares tend to make demand inelastic.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is demand for a branded soft drink often more elastic than demand for water?","options":{"A":"Branded drinks have no substitutes","B":"Water is always a luxury, purchased only when households have enough spare income left over after buying branded drinks","C":"Close alternatives are easier to choose","D":"Water demand changes only with income"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-PES-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What does price elasticity of supply measure?","options":{"A":"Responsiveness of demand to income, showing how far households alter their spending when their income changes","B":"Responsiveness of quantity supplied to price","C":"The effect of advertising on tastes","D":"The size of a firm's fixed costs"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-PES-SHORTRUN","scaffold":"PES is the percentage change in quantity supplied divided by the percentage change in price. It depends on how easily producers can change output.","reforge":{"stem":"Supply is likely to be inelastic in the short run when firms:","options":{"A":"Have no fixed inputs","B":"Can store unlimited stock","C":"Can enter the market instantly","D":"Have limited spare capacity"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-PES-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"Why can agricultural supply be inelastic in the short run?","options":{"A":"Demand fixes harvest size","B":"Farmers can create land instantly whenever a higher price makes it profitable to plant a larger harvest","C":"Growing conditions limit rapid output changes","D":"Crops have no production costs"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-PES-SHORTRUN","scaffold":"Land, seasons and biological growth cycles limit how quickly farms can respond to price changes, making short-run agricultural supply relatively inelastic.","reforge":{"stem":"Which market is likely to have the most elastic short-run supply?","options":{"A":"A service with unused staff capacity","B":"A harvest already planted, where farmers can nonetheless expand this season's output the moment price rises","C":"A fixed-seat concert tonight","D":"A rare painting"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-YED-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What does income elasticity of demand measure?","options":{"A":"How price responds to tax","B":"How income responds to price","C":"How supply responds to wages, measuring the change in output firms choose whenever wage costs rise or fall","D":"How demand responds to a change in income"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-YED","scaffold":"YED is the percentage change in quantity demanded divided by the percentage change in income. Its sign helps classify normal and inferior goods.","reforge":{"stem":"A positive YED usually indicates a:","options":{"A":"Inferior good","B":"Normal good","C":"Public good","D":"Good with no demand"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-YED-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"An inferior good has a negative income elasticity because demand:","options":{"A":"Falls as consumer income rises","B":"Rises faster than income","C":"Never changes with income","D":"Falls only when its price rises"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-YED","scaffold":"Consumers may switch away from lower-quality or cheaper alternatives as incomes rise, giving inferior goods a negative YED.","reforge":{"stem":"Which purchase could be an inferior good for some households?","options":{"A":"A normal staple with positive YED","B":"A luxury holiday, since spending on any luxury good automatically falls whenever household income increases","C":"A cheaper substitute replaced as income rises","D":"A good bought only after a tax"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-XED-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What does cross elasticity of demand measure?","options":{"A":"How supply responds to a firm's revenue, measuring the change in output triggered by a rise in sales income","B":"How demand for one good responds to another's price","C":"How income changes after a tax","D":"How price responds to advertising"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-XED","scaffold":"XED compares the percentage change in demand for one product with the percentage change in the price of another. It identifies substitutes and complements.","reforge":{"stem":"A positive XED usually suggests two goods are:","options":{"A":"Unrelated by definition","B":"Complements","C":"Public goods","D":"Substitutes"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-XED-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"A negative cross elasticity usually indicates that goods are:","options":{"A":"Inferior only","B":"Substitutes","C":"Complements","D":"Perfectly competitive"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-XED","scaffold":"If the price of one good rises and demand for the other falls, the goods are complements used together, such as cars and fuel.","reforge":{"stem":"If the price of games consoles rises and game demand falls, the goods are:","options":{"A":"Complements","B":"Substitutes","C":"Both inferior goods","D":"Unrelated goods"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-MKT-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is a monopoly?","options":{"A":"A price set by consumers alone","B":"A market with many identical firms","C":"A tax on imported goods","D":"A market dominated by one supplier"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-COMPETITION","scaffold":"A monopoly is a market structure in which one firm has substantial control over supply. Legal definitions may use a market-share threshold, but dominance and barriers to entry are central.","reforge":{"stem":"Which feature can help a monopoly persist?","options":{"A":"Free entry by new firms","B":"Barriers to entry","C":"Perfect information for all buyers","D":"Many equally large suppliers"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-MKT-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is one possible benefit of competition?","options":{"A":"Pressure to improve efficiency and choice","B":"Guaranteed profits for every firm, since competition forces rivals to match whatever price and output level is most profitable","C":"A ban on innovation","D":"Higher costs for all consumers"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-COMPETITION","scaffold":"Competition can lower costs, encourage innovation and improve quality or choice. Its effects depend on the number and strength of rivals.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can competition benefit consumers?","options":{"A":"Prices must always rise, because rival firms respond to competition by coordinating to raise their prices together","B":"Firms can ignore demand","C":"Firms have incentives to offer better value","D":"Output is legally restricted"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-MKT-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is a barrier to entry?","options":{"A":"A fall in a firm's average cost that happens automatically whenever a new competitor enters the market","B":"A cost or restriction facing new firms","C":"A guarantee of free market access","D":"A rise in consumer income"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-COMPETITION","scaffold":"Barriers such as patents, large capital requirements, brand loyalty or legal restrictions can protect incumbent firms from new competition.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a natural barrier to entry?","options":{"A":"A lower sunk cost","B":"A temporary fall in demand","C":"A rise in consumer choice","D":"Large economies of scale"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-EXT-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is a negative externality of production?","options":{"A":"A private cost paid by the buyer","B":"A benefit received only by the producer","C":"A third-party cost caused by production","D":"A tax collected on every sale"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-EXT-NEGATIVE","scaffold":"A negative production externality imposes an external cost on people not involved in the transaction, such as pollution from a factory.","reforge":{"stem":"Factory smoke affecting nearby residents is an example of:","options":{"A":"A negative production externality","B":"A private benefit","C":"A positive consumption externality","D":"A normal market price"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-EXT-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is a positive externality of consumption?","options":{"A":"A loss suffered by the consumer only, arising every time they consume a good that also affects other people","B":"A cost paid by the producer","C":"A tax on a harmful good","D":"A wider benefit from someone's consumption"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-EXT-POSITIVE","scaffold":"Education and vaccination can benefit others as well as the consumer. Because some benefits are external, consumption may be below the socially efficient level.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may education be under-consumed in a free market?","options":{"A":"It creates only private costs, since the benefits of studying are received solely by the student who pays for it","B":"Some benefits spill over to wider society","C":"It is always an inferior good","D":"The government bans education"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-EXT-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the social cost of an activity?","options":{"A":"Private cost plus external cost","B":"Private benefit minus tax revenue","C":"The firm's fixed cost only","D":"The market price paid by consumers"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-EXT-NEGATIVE","scaffold":"Social cost includes costs borne by the decision-maker and costs imposed on third parties. This distinction is central to market-failure analysis.","reforge":{"stem":"If a firm's private cost is £8 and external cost is £3, social cost is:","options":{"A":"£8","B":"£5","C":"£11","D":"£24"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-PUB-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"Which feature makes a good non-rival?","options":{"A":"Users can be excluded easily, since access to the good can always be restricted to those who have paid for it","B":"One person's use does not reduce others' use","C":"The good has no opportunity cost","D":"Only firms can consume it"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-PUBLIC-GOODS","scaffold":"Non-rivalry means consumption by one person does not reduce availability to others. National defence is a standard example.","reforge":{"stem":"What does non-excludability mean?","options":{"A":"Firms must supply it at a profit, since no good can ever be provided unless a private firm can charge for using it","B":"Use by one person stops another","C":"The good has no social benefit","D":"Non-payers cannot easily be prevented from benefiting"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-PUB-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"Why can public goods create a free-rider problem?","options":{"A":"The good is always rival","B":"Every consumer must pay twice","C":"People can benefit without paying","D":"Firms can exclude non-payers easily"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-PUBLIC-GOODS","scaffold":"Non-excludability allows people to enjoy benefits without contributing to provision, weakening the private incentive to supply the good.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a likely result of free riding?","options":{"A":"Private supply may be below the socially desired level","B":"All consumers pay voluntarily, since free riding is prevented once a good is available to everyone at no charge","C":"The good becomes rival","D":"The government cannot provide it"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-ASY-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is asymmetric information?","options":{"A":"Firms and consumers have equal knowledge, since every buyer and seller can observe exactly the same information before trading","B":"All buyers have identical preferences","C":"Prices are fixed by law","D":"One party knows more relevant information than another"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-INFO-FAILURE","scaffold":"Asymmetric information can cause market failure when one side of a transaction knows more about quality, risk or actions than the other.","reforge":{"stem":"A used-car seller knows a defect that the buyer cannot observe. This is:","options":{"A":"Perfect information, because both the buyer and the seller can observe the car's full condition before agreeing a price","B":"Asymmetric information","C":"A public good","D":"A production subsidy"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-ASY-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"Why can insurance create moral hazard?","options":{"A":"Protection may encourage riskier behaviour","B":"Insurance removes all uncertainty for society","C":"Insurers cannot charge premiums","D":"Consumers stop buying risky goods"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-INFO-FAILURE","scaffold":"Moral hazard arises when protection changes behaviour because the insured person no longer bears the full consequence of risk.","reforge":{"stem":"Which action could reduce moral hazard in insurance?","options":{"A":"Removing all risk checks","B":"Unlimited cover with no conditions","C":"An excess paid by the policyholder","D":"Paying claims before any loss"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-GOV-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the main aim of a corrective tax?","options":{"A":"To increase harmful consumption, by making the taxed good cheaper relative to substitutes once revenue is collected","B":"To make decision-makers face an external cost","C":"To remove all private costs","D":"To guarantee a monopoly profit"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-CORRECTIVE-TAX","scaffold":"A tax on a negative externality can internalise the external cost, raising the private cost towards the social cost and reducing over-consumption or over-production.","reforge":{"stem":"A tax on carbon emissions is intended to:","options":{"A":"Remove all energy demand, by pricing every unit of carbon so high that households stop using energy altogether","B":"Increase pollution deliberately","C":"Make fossil fuels costless","D":"Reflect environmental costs in market decisions"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1-GOV-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is one possible drawback of an indirect tax?","options":{"A":"It is paid only by producers abroad, since a UK indirect tax cannot legally be passed on to a domestic consumer","B":"It can never change behaviour","C":"It may be regressive relative to income","D":"It always reduces government revenue"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-CORRECTIVE-TAX","scaffold":"Indirect taxes can affect lower-income households proportionately more because they spend a larger share of income on taxed goods. The effect depends on the good and tax design.","reforge":{"stem":"A tax on a basic household fuel may be regressive because:","options":{"A":"Lower-income households spend a larger income share on it","B":"Only rich households buy fuel, since low-income households are assumed to spend nothing at all on household energy","C":"The tax is paid by no one","D":"Demand is always perfectly elastic"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1-GOV-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is a maximum price?","options":{"A":"A subsidy for every consumer, paid directly by government to lower the price that shoppers face in the market","B":"A guaranteed minimum wage","C":"A tax on firms' profits","D":"A legal upper limit on the price charged"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-CEILING-BINDING","scaffold":"A maximum price prevents sellers charging above a legal ceiling. If set below equilibrium, it may create shortages and non-price rationing.","reforge":{"stem":"A binding rent ceiling is most likely to create:","options":{"A":"A guaranteed surplus of homes, since landlords respond to a lower legal rent by building and offering more properties","B":"Excess demand for rented homes","C":"Higher supply at every rent","D":"No change in access"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1-MIN-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is a minimum price?","options":{"A":"A legal lower limit on the price charged","B":"A limit on the quantity supplied, set by government to prevent producers from selling beyond a fixed amount","C":"A subsidy paid to buyers","D":"A maximum price set below equilibrium"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-FLOOR","scaffold":"A minimum price prevents transactions below a legal floor. If above equilibrium, it can create excess supply, as with some agricultural support schemes.","reforge":{"stem":"A binding agricultural price floor can create:","options":{"A":"No producer response","B":"A shortage caused by low prices","C":"Surplus output","D":"A fall in supply by definition"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1-MIN-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"Why might a government buy surplus under a price floor?","options":{"A":"To reduce producer revenue","B":"To support the guaranteed price","C":"To create a shortage deliberately","D":"To make the floor non-binding"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-FLOOR","scaffold":"A government purchasing excess supply can maintain a price floor, but it creates storage, disposal and fiscal costs and may encourage over-production.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one cost of maintaining a farm price floor?","options":{"A":"The policy needs no public funds, since the higher price paid to farmers is covered entirely by consumers at the till","B":"Demand becomes perfectly elastic","C":"Farms stop producing","D":"The government may pay to store or dispose of surplus"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-03","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should a movement along demand be understood here?","options":{"A":"A change in population","B":"A change in tastes, which shifts the whole demand curve rather than moving along the existing one","C":"A response to the good's own price","D":"A change in income"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-MOVEMENT","scaffold":"A movement along a demand curve is caused by the good's own price and nothing else: price changes, quantity demanded changes, and the curve stays where it is. Anything else that alters what people want to buy — income, tastes, the price of a substitute, population — shifts the whole curve instead. Ask first whether the good's own price changed; that single question settles which one a scenario is describing.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in the price of coffee causes quantity demanded to fall, with the demand curve unchanged. What does this show?","options":{"A":"A shift of the demand curve caused by changing tastes","B":"A movement along the demand curve, caused by the price change itself","C":"A shift of the demand curve caused by a change in income","D":"A shift of the demand curve caused by a change in the size or age structure of the population over time"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-04","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a movement along demand. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A change in income","B":"A change in tastes, which shifts the whole demand curve rather than moving along the existing one","C":"A change in population","D":"A response to the good's own price"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-MOVEMENT","scaffold":"A movement along a demand curve is caused by the good's own price and nothing else: price changes, quantity demanded changes, and the curve stays where it is. Anything else that alters what people want to buy — income, tastes, the price of a substitute, population — shifts the whole curve instead. Ask first whether the good's own price changed; that single question settles which one a scenario is describing.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in the price of coffee causes quantity demanded to fall, with the demand curve unchanged. What does this show?","options":{"A":"A shift of the demand curve caused by changing tastes","B":"A shift of the demand curve caused by a change in income","C":"A movement along the demand curve, caused by the price change itself","D":"A shift of the demand curve caused by a change in the size or age structure of the population over time"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-05","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of a demand shift?","options":{"A":"A change in a non-price determinant","B":"A movement caused only by price, which shifts the whole curve to a new position on the diagram","C":"A change in fixed cost","D":"A supply response"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-SHIFT","scaffold":"A demand shift is caused by a non-price determinant: income, tastes, the price of a related good, population or expectations. The entire curve moves left or right. A change in the good's own price never shifts demand — it moves you along the curve you already have. Drawing a shift because price changed is the most common diagram error in Theme 1.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in average income causes demand for a normal good to increase at every price. What does this represent?","options":{"A":"A movement along the demand curve caused by the good's own price","B":"A shift of the supply curve caused by a change in cost","C":"A movement along the supply curve caused by price","D":"A shift of the demand curve caused by a change in income"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-06","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a demand shift. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A movement caused only by price, which shifts the whole curve to a new position on the diagram","B":"A change in a non-price determinant","C":"A change in fixed cost","D":"A supply response"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-SHIFT","scaffold":"A demand shift is caused by a non-price determinant: income, tastes, the price of a related good, population or expectations. The entire curve moves left or right. A change in the good's own price never shifts demand — it moves you along the curve you already have. Drawing a shift because price changed is the most common diagram error in Theme 1.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in average income causes demand for a normal good to increase at every price. What does this represent?","options":{"A":"A shift of the demand curve caused by a change in income","B":"A movement along the demand curve caused by the good's own price","C":"A shift of the supply curve caused by a change in cost","D":"A movement along the supply curve caused by price"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-07","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should consumer surplus be understood here?","options":{"A":"Producer cost below price","B":"Revenue above total cost, which is earned by a firm rather than enjoyed directly by a consumer","C":"Benefit above the price paid","D":"Tax revenue from sales"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-CONSUMER-SURPLUS","scaffold":"Consumer surplus is the gap between what a buyer was willing to pay and what they actually paid: the area under the demand curve and above the price. It is a benefit enjoyed by consumers — not revenue earned by a firm, and not tax collected by government. Producer surplus is its mirror image, above the supply curve and below the price.","reforge":{"stem":"A consumer would have paid £15 for a ticket but only pays £10. What does the £5 difference represent?","options":{"A":"Producer surplus, the benefit gained above the minimum acceptable price","B":"Consumer surplus, the benefit gained above the price paid","C":"Tax revenue collected on the sale","D":"The firm's profit margin on the ticket"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-08","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising consumer surplus. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Tax revenue from sales","B":"Revenue above total cost, which is earned by a firm rather than enjoyed directly by a consumer","C":"Producer cost below price","D":"Benefit above the price paid"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-CONSUMER-SURPLUS","scaffold":"Consumer surplus is the gap between what a buyer was willing to pay and what they actually paid: the area under the demand curve and above the price. It is a benefit enjoyed by consumers — not revenue earned by a firm, and not tax collected by government. Producer surplus is its mirror image, above the supply curve and below the price.","reforge":{"stem":"A consumer would have paid £15 for a ticket but only pays £10. What does the £5 difference represent?","options":{"A":"Producer surplus, the benefit gained above the minimum acceptable price","B":"Tax revenue collected on the sale","C":"Consumer surplus, the benefit gained above the price paid","D":"The firm's profit margin on the ticket"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-09","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of producer surplus?","options":{"A":"Price received above minimum supply price","B":"Consumer benefit above price, which is enjoyed by the buyer rather than the seller of a good","C":"Total fixed cost per unit","D":"The value of imports"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-PRODUCER-SURPLUS","scaffold":"Producer surplus is the gap between the price a seller receives and the minimum they would have accepted: the area above the supply curve and below the price. It belongs to the seller, where consumer surplus above the price belongs to the buyer. It is not profit, because fixed costs have not been deducted.","reforge":{"stem":"A farmer would have accepted £8 for a crate of apples but sells it for £12. What does the £4 difference represent?","options":{"A":"Producer surplus, the price received above the minimum acceptable price","B":"Consumer surplus, the extra benefit enjoyed by the buyer over and above the price they actually paid for the apples","C":"A subsidy paid to the farmer by government","D":"Deadweight loss arising from the transaction"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-10","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising producer surplus. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Consumer benefit above price, which is enjoyed by the buyer rather than the seller of a good","B":"Price received above minimum supply price","C":"Total fixed cost per unit","D":"The value of imports"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-PRODUCER-SURPLUS","scaffold":"Producer surplus is the gap between the price a seller receives and the minimum they would have accepted: the area above the supply curve and below the price. It belongs to the seller, where consumer surplus above the price belongs to the buyer. It is not profit, because fixed costs have not been deducted.","reforge":{"stem":"A farmer would have accepted £8 for a crate of apples but sells it for £12. What does the £4 difference represent?","options":{"A":"Consumer surplus, the extra benefit enjoyed by the buyer over and above the price they actually paid for the apples","B":"Producer surplus, the price received above the minimum acceptable price","C":"A subsidy paid to the farmer by government","D":"Deadweight loss arising from the transaction"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-11","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should a binding price ceiling be understood here?","options":{"A":"A tax on producers","B":"A legal minimum above equilibrium","C":"A legal maximum below equilibrium","D":"A subsidy to sellers"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-CEILING-BINDING","scaffold":"A price ceiling is a legal maximum, and it only binds when set below the equilibrium price. At that lower price quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, so a shortage appears — the rationing, queues and black markets that ceiling questions are really about. A maximum above equilibrium changes nothing.","reforge":{"stem":"A government sets a maximum rent below the market equilibrium rent. What is the likely effect?","options":{"A":"A surplus, because the ceiling is set above equilibrium","B":"A shortage, because the ceiling is binding below equilibrium","C":"No effect, because the ceiling does not bind","D":"A rise in supply caused by higher prices, since landlords respond to the rent ceiling by building more rental homes"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-12","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a binding price ceiling. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A subsidy to sellers","B":"A legal minimum above equilibrium","C":"A tax on producers","D":"A legal maximum below equilibrium"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-CEILING-BINDING","scaffold":"A price ceiling is a legal maximum, and it only binds when set below the equilibrium price. At that lower price quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, so a shortage appears — the rationing, queues and black markets that ceiling questions are really about. A maximum above equilibrium changes nothing.","reforge":{"stem":"A government sets a maximum rent below the market equilibrium rent. What is the likely effect?","options":{"A":"A surplus, because the ceiling is set above equilibrium","B":"No effect, because the ceiling does not bind","C":"A shortage, because the ceiling is binding below equilibrium","D":"A rise in supply caused by higher prices, since landlords respond to the rent ceiling by building more rental homes"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-13","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of a non-binding price ceiling?","options":{"A":"A maximum above equilibrium","B":"A shortage created by law","C":"A minimum below equilibrium","D":"A quota on output"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-CEILING-NONBINDING","scaffold":"A ceiling set above the equilibrium price is non-binding: the market already clears below it, so the legal maximum never bites and price, quantity and welfare are all unchanged. Only a maximum below equilibrium produces the shortage ceilings are famous for. Check where the line sits relative to equilibrium before predicting any effect at all.","reforge":{"stem":"A government sets a maximum price above the market equilibrium price. What is the likely effect?","options":{"A":"A shortage, because the ceiling is set below equilibrium","B":"A surplus, because the ceiling forces price down","C":"A rise in quantity demanded caused by the law, since any price ceiling automatically pulls more buyers into the market","D":"No effect, because the ceiling sits above equilibrium and does not bind"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-14","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a non-binding price ceiling. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A shortage created by law","B":"A maximum above equilibrium","C":"A minimum below equilibrium","D":"A quota on output"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-CEILING-NONBINDING","scaffold":"A ceiling set above the equilibrium price is non-binding: the market already clears below it, so the legal maximum never bites and price, quantity and welfare are all unchanged. Only a maximum below equilibrium produces the shortage ceilings are famous for. Check where the line sits relative to equilibrium before predicting any effect at all.","reforge":{"stem":"A government sets a maximum price above the market equilibrium price. What is the likely effect?","options":{"A":"No effect, because the ceiling sits above equilibrium and does not bind","B":"A shortage, because the ceiling is set below equilibrium","C":"A surplus, because the ceiling forces price down","D":"A rise in quantity demanded caused by the law, since any price ceiling automatically pulls more buyers into the market"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-15","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should a binding price floor be understood here?","options":{"A":"A voluntary discount","B":"A maximum below equilibrium, imposed by government to stop a price rising any further above the market price","C":"A legal minimum above equilibrium","D":"A fall in supply"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-FLOOR","scaffold":"A price floor is a legal minimum and only binds when set above equilibrium. Quantity supplied then exceeds quantity demanded and a surplus accumulates — the mechanism behind agricultural surpluses and the unemployment argument against minimum wages. A minimum below equilibrium is irrelevant, since the market already trades above it.","reforge":{"stem":"A government sets a minimum wage above the market equilibrium wage. What is the likely effect?","options":{"A":"A shortage of labour, because the floor is set below equilibrium","B":"A surplus of labour, because the floor is binding above equilibrium","C":"No effect, because the floor does not bind","D":"A fall in the wage paid by firms, since a minimum wage is treated as removing the legal requirement for firms to pay it"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-16","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a binding price floor. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A fall in supply","B":"A maximum below equilibrium, imposed by government to stop a price rising any further above the market price","C":"A voluntary discount","D":"A legal minimum above equilibrium"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-FLOOR","scaffold":"A price floor is a legal minimum and only binds when set above equilibrium. Quantity supplied then exceeds quantity demanded and a surplus accumulates — the mechanism behind agricultural surpluses and the unemployment argument against minimum wages. A minimum below equilibrium is irrelevant, since the market already trades above it.","reforge":{"stem":"A government sets a minimum wage above the market equilibrium wage. What is the likely effect?","options":{"A":"A shortage of labour, because the floor is set below equilibrium","B":"No effect, because the floor does not bind","C":"A surplus of labour, because the floor is binding above equilibrium","D":"A fall in the wage paid by firms, since a minimum wage is treated as removing the legal requirement for firms to pay it"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-17","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of a subsidy to consumers?","options":{"A":"A payment that lowers effective purchase cost","B":"A tax on output, charged to producers to discourage them from making the subsidised good at all","C":"A limit on quantity","D":"A compulsory saving scheme"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-SUBSIDY","scaffold":"A subsidy is a government payment that lowers the effective price the consumer pays, shifting the relevant curve and raising the quantity traded. It is the mirror of a tax, which raises the effective price and contracts the market. Who gains most from it depends on the relative elasticities of demand and supply, which is where the evaluation marks sit.","reforge":{"stem":"A government pays first-time buyers part of their deposit. How does this affect the buyer's costs?","options":{"A":"It raises the tax the buyer pays on the purchase","B":"It limits the quantity the buyer may purchase","C":"It forces the buyer into compulsory saving","D":"It lowers the effective cost the buyer pays"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-18","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a subsidy to consumers. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A tax on output, charged to producers to discourage them from making the subsidised good at all","B":"A payment that lowers effective purchase cost","C":"A limit on quantity","D":"A compulsory saving scheme"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-SUBSIDY","scaffold":"A subsidy is a government payment that lowers the effective price the consumer pays, shifting the relevant curve and raising the quantity traded. It is the mirror of a tax, which raises the effective price and contracts the market. Who gains most from it depends on the relative elasticities of demand and supply, which is where the evaluation marks sit.","reforge":{"stem":"A government pays first-time buyers part of their deposit. How does this affect the buyer's costs?","options":{"A":"It lowers the effective cost the buyer pays","B":"It raises the tax the buyer pays on the purchase","C":"It limits the quantity the buyer may purchase","D":"It forces the buyer into compulsory saving"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-19","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should specific taxation be understood here?","options":{"A":"A payment to exporters","B":"A fixed percentage of income","C":"A fixed amount per unit","D":"A price ceiling"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-TAX-SPECIFIC","scaffold":"A specific, or unit, tax is a fixed sum per unit — the duty on a litre of fuel — so it shifts supply upward by the same vertical distance at every price and the curve stays parallel. An ad valorem tax is a percentage, so the gap widens as price rises and the curve pivots instead. Getting the shape of that shift right is what earns the diagram mark.","reforge":{"stem":"A government adds 50p to every litre of fuel sold, regardless of its price. What type of tax is this?","options":{"A":"An ad valorem tax, a percentage of the sale price","B":"A specific tax, a fixed amount charged per unit","C":"A subsidy paid to fuel producers","D":"A price ceiling imposed on fuel"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-20","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising specific taxation. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A price ceiling","B":"A fixed percentage of income","C":"A payment to exporters","D":"A fixed amount per unit"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-TAX-SPECIFIC","scaffold":"A specific, or unit, tax is a fixed sum per unit — the duty on a litre of fuel — so it shifts supply upward by the same vertical distance at every price and the curve stays parallel. An ad valorem tax is a percentage, so the gap widens as price rises and the curve pivots instead. Getting the shape of that shift right is what earns the diagram mark.","reforge":{"stem":"A government adds 50p to every litre of fuel sold, regardless of its price. What type of tax is this?","options":{"A":"An ad valorem tax, a percentage of the sale price","B":"A subsidy paid to fuel producers","C":"A specific tax, a fixed amount charged per unit","D":"A price ceiling imposed on fuel"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-21","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of ad valorem taxation?","options":{"A":"A percentage of the transaction value","B":"A fixed charge per unit, set at the same fixed amount of money regardless of how much the good is sold for","C":"A subsidy per worker","D":"A legal minimum price"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-TAX-ADVALOREM","scaffold":"An ad valorem tax is charged as a percentage of the transaction value, VAT at 20% being the standard case, so the cash amount grows with price and the supply curve pivots rather than shifting in parallel. A specific tax is a flat sum per unit and shifts supply by the same distance everywhere. The two produce visibly different diagrams.","reforge":{"stem":"A government adds 20% VAT to the sale price of a laptop. What type of tax is this?","options":{"A":"A specific tax, a fixed charge per unit that stays exactly the same no matter how expensive the laptop being sold is","B":"A subsidy paid to laptop producers","C":"A legal minimum price on laptops","D":"An ad valorem tax, a percentage of the transaction value"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-22","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising ad valorem taxation. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A fixed charge per unit, set at the same fixed amount of money regardless of how much the good is sold for","B":"A percentage of the transaction value","C":"A subsidy per worker","D":"A legal minimum price"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-TAX-ADVALOREM","scaffold":"An ad valorem tax is charged as a percentage of the transaction value, VAT at 20% being the standard case, so the cash amount grows with price and the supply curve pivots rather than shifting in parallel. A specific tax is a flat sum per unit and shifts supply by the same distance everywhere. The two produce visibly different diagrams.","reforge":{"stem":"A government adds 20% VAT to the sale price of a laptop. What type of tax is this?","options":{"A":"An ad valorem tax, a percentage of the transaction value","B":"A specific tax, a fixed charge per unit that stays exactly the same no matter how expensive the laptop being sold is","C":"A subsidy paid to laptop producers","D":"A legal minimum price on laptops"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-23","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should unitary price elasticity be understood here?","options":{"A":"Revenue always falls","B":"Quantity does not change at all, no matter how large a percentage change occurs in the good's price","C":"Quantity changes by the same percentage as price","D":"Supply changes by income"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-UNITARY","scaffold":"Unitary elasticity means quantity changes by exactly the same percentage as price, so PED = 1 and total revenue is unchanged by a price change. Above 1, demand is elastic and a price cut raises revenue; below 1 it is inelastic and a price cut lowers it. Checking what happens to revenue is the fastest way to identify which case you are in.","reforge":{"stem":"If a 10% rise in price leads to exactly a 10% fall in quantity demanded, what is true of total revenue?","options":{"A":"Total revenue always falls after any price rise","B":"Total revenue stays exactly the same, since the changes offset","C":"Total revenue always rises after any price rise","D":"Quantity demanded does not change at all, regardless of the size of the percentage change in price"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-24","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising unitary price elasticity. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Supply changes by income","B":"Quantity does not change at all, no matter how large a percentage change occurs in the good's price","C":"Revenue always falls","D":"Quantity changes by the same percentage as price"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-UNITARY","scaffold":"Unitary elasticity means quantity changes by exactly the same percentage as price, so PED = 1 and total revenue is unchanged by a price change. Above 1, demand is elastic and a price cut raises revenue; below 1 it is inelastic and a price cut lowers it. Checking what happens to revenue is the fastest way to identify which case you are in.","reforge":{"stem":"What would provide evidence of unitary price elasticity?","options":{"A":"Quantity stays completely fixed regardless of how large a percentage change in price takes place","B":"Quantity changes by the same percentage as price","C":"Revenue always falls","D":"Supply changes by income"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-25","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of perfectly inelastic demand?","options":{"A":"Quantity demanded does not respond to price","B":"Demand is horizontal, so even the smallest possible change in price causes quantity demanded to change enormously","C":"Consumers buy nothing","D":"Price never changes"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-INELASTIC","scaffold":"Perfectly inelastic demand means quantity demanded does not respond to price at all: PED = 0 and the curve is vertical. Consumers still buy — they simply buy the same amount whatever the price, which is why the burden of a tax falls almost entirely on them. Perfectly elastic demand is the horizontal opposite.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in the price of a life-saving medicine leads to no change at all in the quantity patients buy. What does this describe?","options":{"A":"Perfectly elastic demand, where quantity falls to zero","B":"Unitary elastic demand, where revenue stays constant","C":"Perfectly inelastic demand, where quantity does not respond to price","D":"A shift of the demand curve caused by income, moving the whole curve rather than changing quantity along it"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-26","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising perfectly inelastic demand. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Demand is horizontal, so even the smallest possible change in price causes quantity demanded to change enormously","B":"Quantity demanded does not respond to price","C":"Consumers buy nothing","D":"Price never changes"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-INELASTIC","scaffold":"Perfectly inelastic demand means quantity demanded does not respond to price at all: PED = 0 and the curve is vertical. Consumers still buy — they simply buy the same amount whatever the price, which is why the burden of a tax falls almost entirely on them. Perfectly elastic demand is the horizontal opposite.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in the price of a life-saving medicine leads to no change at all in the quantity patients buy. What does this describe?","options":{"A":"Perfectly elastic demand, where quantity falls to zero","B":"Unitary elastic demand, where revenue stays constant","C":"A shift of the demand curve caused by income, moving the whole curve rather than changing quantity along it","D":"Perfectly inelastic demand, where quantity does not respond to price"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-27","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should perfectly elastic demand be understood here?","options":{"A":"Price is fixed by government","B":"Quantity is fixed no matter how far the price is raised or lowered by sellers in the market","C":"A tiny price rise reduces demand to zero","D":"Supply is vertical"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-ELASTIC","scaffold":"Perfectly elastic demand is horizontal: at the going price consumers will buy any quantity, but the smallest price rise cuts quantity demanded to zero. It is the theoretical demand curve facing a single firm in perfect competition. Do not confuse it with a vertical, perfectly inelastic curve, where quantity never responds at all.","reforge":{"stem":"In a highly competitive market, a firm that raises its price even slightly above the going rate loses all its customers. What does this describe?","options":{"A":"Perfectly elastic demand, where a tiny price rise cuts demand to zero","B":"Perfectly inelastic demand, where quantity never changes","C":"Unitary elastic demand, where revenue is unaffected","D":"A price floor set above equilibrium, which is imposed by government rather than arising from how consumers behave"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-28","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising perfectly elastic demand. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Supply is vertical","B":"Quantity is fixed no matter how far the price is raised or lowered by sellers in the market","C":"Price is fixed by government","D":"A tiny price rise reduces demand to zero"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-PED-ELASTIC","scaffold":"Perfectly elastic demand is horizontal: at the going price consumers will buy any quantity, but the smallest price rise cuts quantity demanded to zero. It is the theoretical demand curve facing a single firm in perfect competition. Do not confuse it with a vertical, perfectly inelastic curve, where quantity never responds at all.","reforge":{"stem":"In a highly competitive market, a firm that raises its price even slightly above the going rate loses all its customers. What does this describe?","options":{"A":"Perfectly inelastic demand, where quantity never changes","B":"Perfectly elastic demand, where a tiny price rise cuts demand to zero","C":"Unitary elastic demand, where revenue is unaffected","D":"A price floor set above equilibrium, which is imposed by government rather than arising from how consumers behave"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-29","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of short-run supply?","options":{"A":"Supply with at least one fixed factor","B":"Supply after all firms exit the industry, once every firm has stopped producing the good altogether","C":"Supply with no production costs","D":"Demand over a decade"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-PES-SHORTRUN","scaffold":"The short run is defined by having at least one fixed factor, usually capital, so output can only be changed by varying inputs such as labour. That constraint is exactly why short-run supply is less elastic than long-run supply. It is a statement about what can be varied, not about a particular number of weeks or months.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory can hire more workers this month but cannot build a new production line in time. What period of supply does this describe?","options":{"A":"Long-run supply, where every factor can be varied","B":"Supply after every firm has exited the market, once none of the original producers remain in the industry","C":"Short-run supply, where at least one factor of production is fixed","D":"Demand measured over a full decade"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-30","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising short-run supply. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Supply after all firms exit the industry, once every firm has stopped producing the good altogether","B":"Supply with at least one fixed factor","C":"Supply with no production costs","D":"Demand over a decade"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-PES-SHORTRUN","scaffold":"The short run is defined by having at least one fixed factor, usually capital, so output can only be changed by varying inputs such as labour. That constraint is exactly why short-run supply is less elastic than long-run supply. It is a statement about what can be varied, not about a particular number of weeks or months.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory can hire more workers this month but cannot build a new production line in time. What period of supply does this describe?","options":{"A":"Long-run supply, where every factor can be varied","B":"Supply after every firm has exited the market, once none of the original producers remain in the industry","C":"Demand measured over a full decade","D":"Short-run supply, where at least one factor of production is fixed"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-31","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should long-run supply be understood here?","options":{"A":"A demand curve after tax","B":"Supply with fixed capital only, describing a firm that can never expand its factories or its total input of capital","C":"Supply after all factors can adjust","D":"Output at zero profit"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-PES-LONGRUN","scaffold":"In the long run every factor of production is variable: firms can build capacity and enter or leave the industry, so supply responds much more elastically to price. The short run is defined by at least one fixed factor. Both are about what can adjust, not about a fixed length of calendar time.","reforge":{"stem":"Over several years, a firm can build new factories and change every input it uses. What period of supply does this describe?","options":{"A":"Long-run supply, where every factor of production can adjust","B":"Short-run supply, where capital is fixed","C":"A demand curve measured after a tax change, showing how much consumers buy once the tax has been added to price","D":"Output produced at exactly zero profit"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-32","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising long-run supply. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Output at zero profit","B":"Supply with fixed capital only, describing a firm that can never expand its factories or its total input of capital","C":"A demand curve after tax","D":"Supply after all factors can adjust"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-PES-LONGRUN","scaffold":"In the long run every factor of production is variable: firms can build capacity and enter or leave the industry, so supply responds much more elastically to price. The short run is defined by at least one fixed factor. Both are about what can adjust, not about a fixed length of calendar time.","reforge":{"stem":"Over several years, a firm can build new factories and change every input it uses. What period of supply does this describe?","options":{"A":"Short-run supply, where capital is fixed","B":"Long-run supply, where every factor of production can adjust","C":"A demand curve measured after a tax change, showing how much consumers buy once the tax has been added to price","D":"Output produced at exactly zero profit"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-33","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of a positive externality of production?","options":{"A":"A spillover benefit from producing","B":"A private cost paid by a firm alone, with no effect at all on anyone outside the transaction","C":"A consumer tax","D":"A shortage of labour"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-EXT-POSITIVE","scaffold":"A positive production externality is a benefit spilling onto third parties from the act of producing — a firm's research raising the productivity of others is the standard case. Because that benefit is external to the firm's decision, the market under-produces relative to the social optimum. Consumption externalities come from using a good, not from making it.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm training its own staff also raises the skill level of workers who later move to other employers. What does this external benefit represent?","options":{"A":"A private cost paid directly by the training firm, with the benefit captured entirely within that one firm","B":"A tax imposed on the firm's output","C":"A positive externality of production, a spillover benefit to third parties","D":"A shortage of skilled labour in the market"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-34","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a positive externality of production. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A private cost paid by a firm alone, with no effect at all on anyone outside the transaction","B":"A spillover benefit from producing","C":"A consumer tax","D":"A shortage of labour"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-EXT-POSITIVE","scaffold":"A positive production externality is a benefit spilling onto third parties from the act of producing — a firm's research raising the productivity of others is the standard case. Because that benefit is external to the firm's decision, the market under-produces relative to the social optimum. Consumption externalities come from using a good, not from making it.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm training its own staff also raises the skill level of workers who later move to other employers. What does this external benefit represent?","options":{"A":"A private cost paid directly by the training firm, with the benefit captured entirely within that one firm","B":"A tax imposed on the firm's output","C":"A shortage of skilled labour in the market","D":"A positive externality of production, a spillover benefit to third parties"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-35","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should over-production be understood here?","options":{"A":"A fall in private cost","B":"Output below all demand, produced at a level lower than every consumer in the market wishes to buy","C":"Output above the socially efficient level","D":"A rise in consumer surplus only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-OVERPRODUCTION","scaffold":"Over-production means output exceeds the socially efficient level, where marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost. It is the standard result of a negative production externality: the firm counts only its private cost and so produces past the point society would choose. The welfare loss is the triangle between the private and social optima.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory's pollution imposes costs on nearby residents that are not reflected in its market price, so it produces beyond the socially optimal quantity. What does this represent?","options":{"A":"Over-production, where output exceeds the socially efficient level","B":"Under-consumption, where demand falls short of the social optimum","C":"A fall in the firm's private production costs, brought about entirely by the pollution the factory produces","D":"A rise in consumer surplus alone"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-36","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising over-production. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A rise in consumer surplus only","B":"Output below all demand, produced at a level lower than every consumer in the market wishes to buy","C":"A fall in private cost","D":"Output above the socially efficient level"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-OVERPRODUCTION","scaffold":"Over-production means output exceeds the socially efficient level, where marginal social benefit equals marginal social cost. It is the standard result of a negative production externality: the firm counts only its private cost and so produces past the point society would choose. The welfare loss is the triangle between the private and social optima.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory's pollution imposes costs on nearby residents that are not reflected in its market price, so it produces beyond the socially optimal quantity. What does this represent?","options":{"A":"Under-consumption, where demand falls short of the social optimum","B":"Over-production, where output exceeds the socially efficient level","C":"A fall in the firm's private production costs, brought about entirely by the pollution the factory produces","D":"A rise in consumer surplus alone"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-37","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of under-consumption?","options":{"A":"Consumption below the socially efficient level","B":"Consumption above equilibrium by law, forced on consumers through a government mandate to buy more of the good","C":"A fall in every external benefit","D":"A rise in supply"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-UNDERCONSUMPTION","scaffold":"Under-consumption means the quantity consumed sits below the socially efficient level — the usual outcome for merit goods and positive consumption externalities, because individuals weigh only their private benefit and ignore the spillover to everyone else. The policy response is subsidy, provision or information, all aimed at raising consumption towards the social optimum.","reforge":{"stem":"Because people underestimate the benefits of vaccination, take-up stays below the level that would maximise social welfare. What does this represent?","options":{"A":"Over-production, where output exceeds the social optimum","B":"A fall in every external benefit associated with the good","C":"Under-consumption, where consumption falls below the socially efficient level","D":"A rise in market supply, caused entirely by people underestimating the benefits of getting vaccinated"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-38","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising under-consumption. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Consumption above equilibrium by law, forced on consumers through a government mandate to buy more of the good","B":"Consumption below the socially efficient level","C":"A fall in every external benefit","D":"A rise in supply"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-UNDERCONSUMPTION","scaffold":"Under-consumption means the quantity consumed sits below the socially efficient level — the usual outcome for merit goods and positive consumption externalities, because individuals weigh only their private benefit and ignore the spillover to everyone else. The policy response is subsidy, provision or information, all aimed at raising consumption towards the social optimum.","reforge":{"stem":"Because people underestimate the benefits of vaccination, take-up stays below the level that would maximise social welfare. What does this represent?","options":{"A":"Over-production, where output exceeds the social optimum","B":"A fall in every external benefit associated with the good","C":"A rise in market supply, caused entirely by people underestimating the benefits of getting vaccinated","D":"Under-consumption, where consumption falls below the socially efficient level"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-39","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should information failure be understood here?","options":{"A":"A fixed exchange rate","B":"Perfectly informed trade, in which both the buyer and the seller know everything relevant before they agree a price","C":"Decisions made with incomplete or misleading knowledge","D":"A fall in scarcity"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-INFO-FAILURE","scaffold":"Information failure means decisions are taken on incomplete, asymmetric or misleading information, so consumers misjudge the true costs or benefits and demand settles away from the socially efficient level. It is the reason merit and demerit goods are mis-consumed. Perfect information is the assumption it violates, not a description of it.","reforge":{"stem":"A buyer purchases a used car without knowing about a serious hidden fault the seller failed to disclose. What market failure does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Information failure, a decision made without complete or accurate knowledge","B":"A fixed exchange rate imposed by government, set independently of anything either party to the sale knows","C":"Perfectly informed trade between buyer and seller","D":"A fall in the overall scarcity of the good"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-40","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising information failure. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A fall in scarcity","B":"Perfectly informed trade, in which both the buyer and the seller know everything relevant before they agree a price","C":"A fixed exchange rate","D":"Decisions made with incomplete or misleading knowledge"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-INFO-FAILURE","scaffold":"Information failure means decisions are taken on incomplete, asymmetric or misleading information, so consumers misjudge the true costs or benefits and demand settles away from the socially efficient level. It is the reason merit and demerit goods are mis-consumed. Perfect information is the assumption it violates, not a description of it.","reforge":{"stem":"A buyer purchases a used car without knowing about a serious hidden fault the seller failed to disclose. What market failure does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"A fixed exchange rate imposed by government, set independently of anything either party to the sale knows","B":"Information failure, a decision made without complete or accurate knowledge","C":"Perfectly informed trade between buyer and seller","D":"A fall in the overall scarcity of the good"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-41","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of a merit good?","options":{"A":"A good with benefits consumers may underestimate","B":"A good with no social benefit at all, consumed purely for the private enjoyment of the person who buys it","C":"A good supplied only abroad","D":"A good with perfectly elastic supply"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-MERIT","scaffold":"A merit good is one whose benefits consumers systematically underestimate — education and healthcare are the exam standards — so it is under-consumed relative to the social optimum. Positive externalities usually accompany it, but the defining feature is the consumer's own misjudgement, which is why information campaigns sit alongside subsidy as a remedy.","reforge":{"stem":"Education raises a person's future earnings and skills, benefits that young people often undervalue when deciding whether to stay in school. What type of good does this describe?","options":{"A":"A demerit good, whose costs consumers may underestimate","B":"A good supplied only through international trade, and never produced or consumed within the domestic economy","C":"A merit good, whose benefits consumers may underestimate","D":"A good with perfectly elastic supply"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-42","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a merit good. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A good with no social benefit at all, consumed purely for the private enjoyment of the person who buys it","B":"A good with benefits consumers may underestimate","C":"A good supplied only abroad","D":"A good with perfectly elastic supply"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-MERIT","scaffold":"A merit good is one whose benefits consumers systematically underestimate — education and healthcare are the exam standards — so it is under-consumed relative to the social optimum. Positive externalities usually accompany it, but the defining feature is the consumer's own misjudgement, which is why information campaigns sit alongside subsidy as a remedy.","reforge":{"stem":"Education raises a person's future earnings and skills, benefits that young people often undervalue when deciding whether to stay in school. What type of good does this describe?","options":{"A":"A demerit good, whose costs consumers may underestimate","B":"A good supplied only through international trade, and never produced or consumed within the domestic economy","C":"A good with perfectly elastic supply","D":"A merit good, whose benefits consumers may underestimate"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-43","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should a demerit good be understood here?","options":{"A":"A good that cannot be taxed","B":"A good with no private demand at all, since nobody would ever choose to consume it without being forced to","C":"A good whose costs consumers may underestimate","D":"A good with no externality"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-DEMERIT","scaffold":"A demerit good is one whose costs consumers underestimate, tobacco and alcohol being the standard cases, so it is over-consumed relative to the social optimum. A negative externality normally sits alongside, but the defining feature is the misjudgement of harm to themselves — which is why both taxation and information are used against it.","reforge":{"stem":"A smoker often underestimates the long-term health costs of cigarettes when deciding to buy them. What type of good does this describe?","options":{"A":"A demerit good, whose costs consumers may underestimate","B":"A merit good, whose benefits consumers may underestimate","C":"A good that cannot legally be taxed","D":"A good with no associated externality"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-44","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a demerit good. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A good with no externality","B":"A good with no private demand at all, since nobody would ever choose to consume it without being forced to","C":"A good that cannot be taxed","D":"A good whose costs consumers may underestimate"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-DEMERIT","scaffold":"A demerit good is one whose costs consumers underestimate, tobacco and alcohol being the standard cases, so it is over-consumed relative to the social optimum. A negative externality normally sits alongside, but the defining feature is the misjudgement of harm to themselves — which is why both taxation and information are used against it.","reforge":{"stem":"A smoker often underestimates the long-term health costs of cigarettes when deciding to buy them. What type of good does this describe?","options":{"A":"A merit good, whose benefits consumers may underestimate","B":"A demerit good, whose costs consumers may underestimate","C":"A good that cannot legally be taxed","D":"A good with no associated externality"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-45","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of a natural monopoly?","options":{"A":"A market where one firm can supply at lower average cost","B":"A market with no fixed cost, in which every potential competitor can enter and produce at the same low cost","C":"A market with unlimited entrants","D":"A market with identical small firms"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-NAT-MONOPOLY","scaffold":"A natural monopoly exists where fixed costs are so large relative to the size of the market that one firm can supply the whole market at a lower average cost than two or more could — water networks and rail track are the standard examples. Splitting the market would duplicate the infrastructure and raise unit costs, which is why regulation rather than competition is the usual policy.","reforge":{"stem":"A national water pipe network can be supplied more cheaply by a single provider than by several firms duplicating pipework. What market structure does this describe?","options":{"A":"A market with no fixed costs at all","B":"A market open to unlimited new entrants under standard assumptions in a typical market","C":"A natural monopoly, where one firm achieves the lowest average cost","D":"A market of many identical small firms"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-46","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a natural monopoly. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A market with no fixed cost, in which every potential competitor can enter and produce at the same low cost","B":"A market where one firm can supply at lower average cost","C":"A market with unlimited entrants","D":"A market with identical small firms"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-NAT-MONOPOLY","scaffold":"A natural monopoly exists where fixed costs are so large relative to the size of the market that one firm can supply the whole market at a lower average cost than two or more could — water networks and rail track are the standard examples. Splitting the market would duplicate the infrastructure and raise unit costs, which is why regulation rather than competition is the usual policy.","reforge":{"stem":"A national water pipe network can be supplied more cheaply by a single provider than by several firms duplicating pipework. What market structure does this describe?","options":{"A":"A market with no fixed costs at all","B":"A market open to unlimited new entrants under standard assumptions in a typical market","C":"A market of many identical small firms","D":"A natural monopoly, where one firm achieves the lowest average cost"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-47","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should allocative inefficiency be understood here?","options":{"A":"Maximum productive capacity, reached whenever an economy operates exactly on its production possibility frontier","B":"Minimum average cost","C":"Price differs from the marginal social cost of output","D":"A balanced budget"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-ALLOCATIVE","scaffold":"Allocation is efficient when price equals marginal social cost, so what society pays for the last unit matches what it cost society to produce. Allocative inefficiency is any departure from that. Productive efficiency is a different idea — producing at minimum average cost — and the two are routinely confused in exams.","reforge":{"stem":"A polluting factory charges a price that ignores the harm caused to nearby residents. What does this represent?","options":{"A":"Allocative inefficiency, where price diverges from the marginal social cost","B":"Productive efficiency, where average cost is minimised","C":"Maximum use of the economy's productive capacity","D":"A balanced government budget, achieved when tax revenue collected exactly equals total public spending"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH1C-48","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising allocative inefficiency. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A balanced budget","B":"Minimum average cost","C":"Maximum productive capacity, reached whenever an economy operates exactly on its production possibility frontier","D":"Price differs from the marginal social cost of output"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-ALLOCATIVE","scaffold":"Allocation is efficient when price equals marginal social cost, so what society pays for the last unit matches what it cost society to produce. Allocative inefficiency is any departure from that. Productive efficiency is a different idea — producing at minimum average cost — and the two are routinely confused in exams.","reforge":{"stem":"A polluting factory charges a price that ignores the harm caused to nearby residents. What does this represent?","options":{"A":"Productive efficiency, where average cost is minimised","B":"Allocative inefficiency, where price diverges from the marginal social cost","C":"Maximum use of the economy's productive capacity","D":"A balanced government budget, achieved when tax revenue collected exactly equals total public spending"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH1C-49","spec":"1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of government failure?","options":{"A":"Intervention creates a worse allocation than the market","B":"Every policy achieves its target exactly, without ever producing an unintended or costly side effect","C":"Markets have perfect information","D":"Taxes always improve welfare"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-GOV-FAILURE","scaffold":"Government failure occurs when intervention leaves a worse allocation of resources than the market outcome it was meant to correct, through unintended consequences, distorted incentives, administrative cost or poor information. The test is not that a policy missed its target: it is that the intervention itself reduced welfare.","reforge":{"stem":"Which application is most consistent with government failure?","options":{"A":"A fall in scarcity","B":"Every policy achieves its target exactly, without ever producing an unintended or costly side effect","C":"Intervention creates a worse allocation than the market","D":"Taxes always improve welfare"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-50","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising government failure. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Every policy achieves its target exactly, without ever producing an unintended or costly side effect","B":"Intervention creates a worse allocation than the market","C":"Markets have perfect information","D":"Taxes always improve welfare"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MICRO-GOV-FAILURE","scaffold":"Government failure occurs when intervention leaves a worse allocation of resources than the market outcome it was meant to correct, through unintended consequences, distorted incentives, administrative cost or poor information. The test is not that a policy missed its target: it is that the intervention itself reduced welfare.","reforge":{"stem":"A government subsidy intended to help farmers instead leads to persistent overproduction and costly surplus stockpiles. What does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"A policy that perfectly achieves its intended target","B":"A market that already has perfect information, so government support could add nothing of further value","C":"Government failure, where intervention creates a worse outcome than the market","D":"A tax that automatically improves welfare"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH1C-51","spec":"1.1","stem":"How should a deadweight welfare loss be understood here?","options":{"A":"Government revenue only","B":"All producer surplus, captured entirely by firms whenever a market fails to reach an efficient outcome","C":"Total surplus lost through inefficient allocation","D":"A rise in real income"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MICRO-DEADWEIGHT","scaffold":"Deadweight loss is the total surplus destroyed when output sits away from the allocatively efficient level — value that neither consumers, producers nor government receive. On a diagram it is the triangle between the demand and supply curves over the units no longer traded. Tax revenue is a transfer between parties, not a deadweight loss.","reforge":{"stem":"After a tax is imposed, some mutually beneficial trades no longer take place, and the resulting loss is not captured by government revenue. What does this loss represent?","options":{"A":"Government revenue collected from the tax under standard assumptions in a typical market","B":"The entire producer surplus in the market","C":"A rise in real incomes across the economy","D":"A deadweight welfare loss, total surplus lost through inefficient allocation"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH1C-52","spec":"1.1","stem":"A student is revising a deadweight welfare loss. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A rise in real income","B":"All producer surplus, captured entirely by firms whenever a market fails to reach an efficient outcome","C":"Government revenue only","D":"Total surplus lost through inefficient allocation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-DEADWEIGHT","scaffold":"Deadweight loss is the total surplus destroyed when output sits away from the allocatively efficient level — value that neither consumers, producers nor government receive. On a diagram it is the triangle between the demand and supply curves over the units no longer traded. Tax revenue is a transfer between parties, not a deadweight loss.","reforge":{"stem":"After a tax is imposed, some mutually beneficial trades no longer take place, and the resulting loss is not captured by government revenue. What does this loss represent?","options":{"A":"A deadweight welfare loss, total surplus lost through inefficient allocation","B":"Government revenue collected from the tax under standard assumptions in a typical market","C":"The entire producer surplus in the market","D":"A rise in real incomes across the economy"},"correct":"A"}}]},"2.1.1":{"label":"Economic Growth","color":"#2563eb","questions":[{"id":"GRO-01","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"UK real GDP was £2.1tn in 2022 and £2.15tn in 2023. A student says: 'The UK has high economic growth because GDP is over £2 trillion.' What is wrong with this statement?","options":{"A":"The student is describing the level of GDP, not the rate of growth","B":"The student should have used nominal GDP, not real GDP.","C":"They convert all figures into nominal rather than real terms for accuracy","D":"Growth can only be measured using GDP per capita."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GRO-01","scaffold":"Growth is a rate of change, not an absolute level — this is one of the most common Year 12 errors. The formula is: growth rate = ((GDP₁ − GDP₀) / GDP₀) × 100. Here: ((2.15 − 2.1) / 2.1) × 100 = 0.05 / 2.1 × 100 ≈ 2.4%. The UK has a large GDP because it is a large economy — that tells us nothing about the rate at which it is growing. China grew at ~5% in 2023 with a larger GDP than the UK; that doesn't make China's growth rate bigger than every smaller economy. Examiner tip: whenever a question gives you two GDP figures, calculate the growth rate before drawing any conclusion. 'High GDP' ≠ 'high growth'.","reforge":{"stem":"Country X has GDP of $500bn growing at 6% p.a. Country Y has GDP of $15tn growing at 1.2% p.a. Which has higher economic growth?","options":{"A":"Country Y, because its GDP is larger.","B":"They are equal because both are growing.","C":"Country X, because 6% > 1.2% — growth is about the rate, not the level.","D":"They remove the effect of population change from the comparison entirely"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"GRO-02","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"A government cuts income tax to boost consumer spending, raising short-run real GDP. Which best describes what has occurred?","options":{"A":"Potential growth — productive capacity has increased.","B":"Actual growth — real GDP rose due to higher AD, but productive capacity is unchanged.","C":"Neither, because tax cuts don't affect growth.","D":"Demand rises whenever price rises, because the Veblen effect explains every good a household chooses to buy."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GRO-02","scaffold":"Edexcel examiners consistently penalise confusion between actual and potential growth — they are fundamentally different concepts requiring different diagrams. Actual growth: AD shifts right → the economy moves along the SRAS curve toward (or beyond) full capacity. Real GDP increases but no new productive capacity is created. Potential growth: LRAS shifts right → the economy can produce more at every price level. This requires investment in factors of production (capital, labour quality, technology). A tax cut works through C → AD → actual growth. It cannot shift LRAS because it does not improve productive capacity. Draw both diagrams and label clearly: actual growth = movement, potential growth = shift of the production frontier.","reforge":{"stem":"A government invests in broadband and STEM education over 10 years. Which type of growth does this primarily represent?","options":{"A":"Actual growth — AD increases immediately.","B":"Neither — it's fiscal policy, not growth.","C":"The growth rate that was recorded in the single most recent quarter of the year","D":"Potential growth — productive capacity shifts outward over time."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"GRO-03","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"The economy is producing above its trend level of output. What type of output gap does this represent?","options":{"A":"A negative output gap — spare capacity exists.","B":"A positive output gap — spare capacity for further growth.","C":"A positive output gap — actual GDP exceeds potential GDP.","D":"No output gap exists when the economy is growing."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GRO-03","scaffold":"Output gap direction confuses most students because 'positive' sounds good. Positive output gap = actual GDP > potential GDP → the economy is overheating. Resources are overstretched, wages bid up, inflationary pressure builds. SRAS cannot sustain this — costs rise, SRAS shifts left, output falls back to potential. Negative output gap = actual GDP < potential → spare capacity, higher unemployment, deflationary pressure. Examiner language: 'inflationary pressure' (positive gap), 'deflationary pressure / deflationary gap' (negative). On a diagram: positive gap = point to the right of where LRAS meets SRAS; negative = point to the left. Never say a positive output gap is 'good for the economy' — it signals overheating.","reforge":{"stem":"Unemployment is rising and factories run below capacity. Which is correct?","options":{"A":"Negative output gap — actual output is below potential.","B":"Positive output gap because production is positive.","C":"Positive output gap — spare capacity means room to grow.","D":"Output gaps only exist during recessions."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"GRO-04","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"On a trade cycle diagram, which phase shows real GDP rising back toward the trend line after a trough?","options":{"A":"Slowdown","B":"Boom","C":"Recession","D":"Recovery"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GRO-04","scaffold":"Trade cycle sequence: boom (above trend) → downturn → recession (two consecutive quarters of negative growth) → trough (lowest point) → recovery (GDP rising back toward trend) → boom again. Recovery is the upswing — GDP is increasing, but it has not yet returned to trend. Students often confuse recovery with boom: in recovery the economy is growing but still below trend potential. On a diagram: the trough is the lowest point of the wave; recovery is the positive slope that follows. Examiner tip: if asked to identify a phase, check two things: (1) direction of GDP change, and (2) position relative to trend line.","reforge":{"stem":"During recovery, which is most likely?","options":{"A":"Real GDP falling toward the trough.","B":"Real GDP rising; the negative output gap narrowing.","C":"Real GDP flat at trend.","D":"A boom, because such a large change indicates strong economic activity"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"GRO-05","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"Between 2010 and 2023, UK GDP per capita grew more slowly than total GDP. Which factor best explains this?","options":{"A":"Population growth meant output was shared among more people.","B":"GDP per capita only measures services.","C":"The UK experienced deflation.","D":"Prices are 128% higher than they were in the chosen base year"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GRO-01","scaffold":"GDP per capita = total real GDP ÷ population. If population grows at the same rate as GDP, per-capita GDP is unchanged even though total GDP rose. Between 2010–2023, UK population grew due to net migration and natural increase — so GDP had to grow faster than population for per-capita growth to be positive. This distinction matters for welfare analysis: a country could double its GDP purely through population growth with no improvement in average living standards. Examiner application: always distinguish between aggregate GDP growth and per-capita GDP growth when assessing living standards. The question 'has growth improved living standards?' requires per-capita analysis.","reforge":{"stem":"Country Z's GDP grows at 4% but population also grows at 3.5%. What can we say about living standards?","options":{"A":"Rising rapidly at 4%.","B":"Falling because population growth is unsustainable.","C":"Broadly stagnant — per-capita growth is only ~0.5%.","D":"We cannot comment using GDP data."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"GRO-06","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"LRAS shifts right through investment in education and technology. What happens to long-run price level and real output?","options":{"A":"The highest rate of growth the economy has ever achieved historically","B":"Price level falls (or rises more slowly), output rises.","C":"Price level falls, output falls.","D":"Both unchanged."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GRO-02","scaffold":"When LRAS shifts right, productive capacity increases. With the same position of AD, the new equilibrium has: (1) higher real output — the economy can produce more goods and services, and (2) lower price level — more supply at the same demand pushes prices down. This is called non-inflationary or supply-side growth — it avoids the inflationary trade-off of demand-side stimulus. Contrast: if AD shifts right with unchanged LRAS, output rises AND the price level rises (inflationary growth). Draw the AD/AS diagram: LRAS shifts right → new intersection of AD with new LRAS at higher Y and lower P. Label both changes clearly — examiners expect both consequences.","reforge":{"stem":"Which correctly matches growth type to its AD/AS effect?","options":{"A":"The rate of growth required to eliminate unemployment altogether","B":"Both shift AD right.","C":"Actual growth has no diagram.","D":"Actual = AD right (along SRAS); Potential = LRAS right."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"GRO-08","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"The UK's PPF shifts outward. Which could have caused this?","options":{"A":"A rise in consumer confidence.","B":"A cut in interest rates by the BoE.","C":"Net migration of skilled workers and new capital investment.","D":"The level of consumer confidence reported in the monthly survey data"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GRO-02","scaffold":"The PPF shows the maximum possible output of two goods given fixed resources. It shifts outward only when productive capacity increases — more or better resources. Causes of outward shift: more capital (investment), more labour (population growth, immigration), better technology (raises productivity of existing resources), improved human capital (education, training). Demand-side factors (rising AD, consumer confidence, lower interest rates) do NOT shift the PPF — they move the economy from inside the PPF toward its boundary but cannot push it outward. Exam trap: 'the economy grew because of rising consumer spending' → this explains actual growth (movement toward PPF), not potential growth (outward PPF shift).","reforge":{"stem":"Which represents a movement FROM inside the PPF TO the boundary, not a shift?","options":{"A":"Reducing structural unemployment, putting idle workers back into production.","B":"Discovery of new oil reserves.","C":"The growth rate that was recorded in the single most recent quarter of the year","D":"Large-scale apprenticeship programme."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"GRO-09","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"UK real GDP grew by 0.1% in 2023. A student writes: 'The economy shrank because growth was almost zero.' Is this correct?","options":{"A":"A fall in electric-car production costs, which shifts supply rather than demand.","B":"Yes — 0.1% is effectively a contraction.","C":"Yes — growth below 2% counts as recession.","D":"No — the economy still grew, just slowly. Contraction requires negative growth."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GRO-01","scaffold":"Recession has a precise definition: two or more consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth. 0.1% growth means real GDP increased — the economy expanded. It is weak growth, but it is growth. Examiners penalise students who use 'recession' loosely. On the mark scheme: 'very slow growth' / 'near-stagnation' is acceptable; 'recession' when growth is positive is not. Related error: confusing disinflation (falling rate of inflation) with deflation (falling price level). Both involve 'things getting smaller' but mean very different things. When reading data questions, identify: (1) is the number positive or negative? (2) is it a level or a rate of change?","reforge":{"stem":"GDP growth: −0.3% (Q1), −0.1% (Q2), +0.4% (Q3). Which is accurate?","options":{"A":"Recession for all three quarters.","B":"Recession in Q1–Q2 (two consecutive negative quarters), then growth returned in Q3.","C":"A fall in the price of electric cars, causing movement along the existing demand curve.","D":"Recession only applies below −1%."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"GRO-10","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"Why are purchasing power parity (PPP) exchange rates used when comparing GDP per capita across countries?","options":{"A":"They adjust for differences in the cost of living between countries","B":"They convert all figures into nominal rather than real terms for accuracy","C":"They remove the effect of population change from the comparison entirely","D":"They are set by the IMF and are more stable than market exchange rates"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GRO-05","scaffold":"Market exchange rates are driven by traded goods and by speculative capital flows, so they say little about what money actually buys domestically. A haircut, a bus fare or a bowl of rice costs far less in a low-income country, so converting its GDP at market rates understates real living standards. PPP rates equalise the cost of a common basket, giving a much fairer welfare comparison — India's GDP roughly triples when measured at PPP rather than at market rates. Examiner tip: if a question asks you to compare living standards internationally, write 'GDP per capita at PPP' and you have earned the evaluation mark for measurement.","reforge":{"stem":"Which comparison would be most misleading if market exchange rates were used?","options":{"A":"Comparing two neighbouring economies that already share a single currency","B":"Comparing one country's own GDP in two consecutive years","C":"Comparing a high-income and a low-income country's living standards","D":"Comparing nominal and real GDP within one single economy"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"GRO-11","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"A large informal (shadow) economy causes official GDP figures to be:","options":{"A":"Overstated, because informal activity ends up being counted twice over","B":"Understated, because unrecorded transactions are excluded from the data","C":"Unaffected, since the informal economy is captured by the income method","D":"Overstated, because black-market prices are higher than official prices"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GRO-05","scaffold":"GDP counts only recorded transactions. Cash-in-hand work, subsistence farming, unpaid household labour and illegal trade all generate real output and real welfare but never reach the statistics, so measured GDP understates true economic activity. This matters most in international comparison: the informal sector is estimated at under 10% of GDP in the UK but over 30% in many developing economies, so the measured income gap overstates the real gap. Note the related paradox — if a person marries their cleaner, measured GDP falls even though nothing real has changed.","reforge":{"stem":"Which activity contributes to welfare but is excluded from measured GDP?","options":{"A":"A government department paying the salaries of all of its staff","B":"A restaurant meal that is purchased and recorded through the till","C":"A cleaner employed through an agency and paid by formal invoice","D":"A parent providing unpaid childcare for their own children"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"GRO-12","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"A country's GDP rises because of increased output from heavily polluting factories. What does this reveal about GDP?","options":{"A":"GDP automatically deducts the cost of the environmental damage caused","B":"GDP rises only when production is genuinely sustainable over the long run","C":"GDP measures output but not the negative externalities that accompany it","D":"GDP would fall in this case because pollution reduces living standards"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GRO-05","scaffold":"GDP is a gross measure of output, blind to how that output is produced or to what it destroys. Pollution, resource depletion and congestion all impose real welfare costs that never appear as a deduction — worse, the clean-up spending afterwards is added to GDP, so an oil spill can raise measured output twice over. This is why alternative measures exist: the Index of Sustainable Economic Welfare and Green GDP attempt to net off environmental cost, and the ONS publishes wellbeing statistics alongside GDP. Use this whenever you evaluate whether growth genuinely improves living standards.","reforge":{"stem":"Which measure attempts to correct GDP for environmental degradation?","options":{"A":"Green GDP, which nets off the environmental costs of output","B":"Gross National Income measured at current market prices","C":"Nominal GDP adjusted to a chosen base year for easier comparison","D":"GDP per capita measured at purchasing power parity rates"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"GRO-13","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"The Easterlin paradox observes that beyond a certain income level:","options":{"A":"Happiness falls sharply as national income continues to rise over time","B":"National income and happiness become perfectly correlated with each other","C":"Only absolute income, and never relative income, affects reported wellbeing","D":"Further rises in national income bring little gain in reported happiness"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GRO-06","scaffold":"Easterlin found that within a country richer people report greater happiness, but as a whole country grows richer over decades average reported happiness barely moves. Two explanations are usually offered: relative income matters more than absolute income, so what counts is your position against others rather than your purchasing power; and hedonic adaptation means people quickly normalise a higher standard of living. The policy implication is significant and worth deploying in evaluation — it questions whether maximising GDP growth should be the overriding objective, and it underpins the case for wellbeing measures alongside output.","reforge":{"stem":"Which explanation is most consistent with the Easterlin paradox?","options":{"A":"Absolute income is the single determinant of reported happiness levels","B":"Relative income position matters more than absolute income does","C":"Reported happiness cannot be measured in any meaningful way at all","D":"Happiness rises in exact proportion to real GDP per capita over time"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"GRO-14","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"What is meant by an economy's trend rate of growth?","options":{"A":"The average sustainable growth rate over time, set by productive capacity","B":"The growth rate that was recorded in the single most recent quarter of the year","C":"The highest rate of growth the economy has ever achieved historically","D":"The rate of growth required to eliminate unemployment altogether"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GRO-02","scaffold":"The trend rate is the long-run average rate at which an economy can grow without generating inflationary pressure — it is determined by growth in the labour force and in productivity, so it is effectively the growth rate of LRAS. The UK's trend rate is usually estimated at around 2 to 2.5%, though productivity stagnation since 2008 has led many economists to revise it down. Its analytical use is that it defines the output gap: actual growth above trend opens a positive gap and inflationary pressure, growth below trend opens a negative gap and spare capacity. Supply-side policy aims to raise the trend rate itself.","reforge":{"stem":"An economy grows at 4% when its trend rate is 2%. What is the likely consequence?","options":{"A":"A negative output gap and a rise in spare capacity","B":"No change at all to the price level in the economy","C":"A positive output gap and inflationary pressure","D":"An immediate rightward shift of the LRAS curve"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"GRO-15","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"Which best describes sustainable economic growth?","options":{"A":"Growth that continues at exactly the same rate in every quarter of a year","B":"Growth that can be maintained without compromising future generations","C":"Growth achieved entirely through increases in aggregate demand alone","D":"Growth that is the fastest among all of a country's competitors"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GRO-06","scaffold":"Sustainable growth has two dimensions and examiners reward both. Environmentally, it means growth that does not deplete natural capital or generate pollution at a rate that damages future productive capacity. Economically, it means growth that does not create imbalances forcing a later correction — a consumer boom financed by borrowing, or an unsustainable housing bubble, produces growth that reverses. This is why supply-side driven growth is preferred to demand-driven growth: shifting LRAS raises output without the inflationary pressure and subsequent contraction that an AD-only boom generates.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is growth driven by an unsustainable credit boom problematic?","options":{"A":"It raises productive capacity far too quickly for firms to manage","B":"It shifts the LRAS curve permanently to the left over a long period","C":"It has no effect at all on the rate of measured inflation","D":"It creates imbalances that force a later contraction"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"GRO-16","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"Which factor is the most important long-run determinant of a country's growth rate?","options":{"A":"The level of consumer confidence reported in the monthly survey data","B":"The size of the government's budget deficit measured as a share of GDP","C":"Growth in labour productivity, meaning output produced per worker hour","D":"Short-term movements in the country's nominal exchange rate index"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GRO-02","scaffold":"Long-run growth comes from expanding productive capacity, and with a broadly fixed labour force that means productivity. Output equals workers multiplied by output per worker, so once population growth slows, productivity growth is essentially the whole story. Productivity itself is driven by investment in physical capital, in human capital through education and training, and in technology and infrastructure. The UK's productivity puzzle since 2008 — growth of well under 1% a year against a pre-crisis 2% — is the standard application, and it explains stagnant real wages. The other options affect AD and short-run output, not capacity.","reforge":{"stem":"Which would most directly raise labour productivity in the long run?","options":{"A":"Sustained investment in workforce training and technology","B":"A depreciation of the currency against the US dollar","C":"A rise in the level of unemployment benefit payments","D":"A temporary cut in the rate of VAT to boost consumer spending"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"GRO-17","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"What characterises export-led growth?","options":{"A":"Growth achieved by restricting imports through the use of tariff barriers","B":"Growth financed entirely by borrowing from overseas capital markets","C":"Growth measured by the size of the country's current account deficit","D":"Growth driven by rising demand for a country's goods from abroad"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GRO-02","scaffold":"Export-led growth uses net exports as the driver of AD rather than domestic consumption, and it is the model followed by China, South Korea and post-war Germany and Japan. Its attractions are real: it avoids the current account deficit that consumption-led growth tends to produce, it exposes domestic firms to international competition which raises productivity, and it can be supported by a competitive exchange rate. The risks are equally examinable — dependence on world demand leaves the economy vulnerable to a global downturn, and the strategy can invite accusations of currency manipulation or provoke retaliatory protectionism.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main risk of relying on export-led growth?","options":{"A":"It always produces a very large current account deficit","B":"It leaves the economy exposed to a global downturn","C":"It permanently reduces domestic productivity levels over time","D":"It prevents any investment at all in domestic infrastructure"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"GRO-18","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"Which is a genuine economic cost of rapid economic growth?","options":{"A":"Increased inequality if the gains accrue mainly to owners of capital","B":"A fall in the general level of unemployment across the whole economy","C":"An increase in the tax revenues available to fund public services","D":"Rising real incomes for the majority of households in the country"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GRO-06","scaffold":"A balanced answer on growth needs costs as well as benefits. The costs: environmental damage and resource depletion; inequality, if returns flow disproportionately to owners of capital and to high-skilled workers; inflationary pressure if growth exceeds the trend rate; a current account deficit as rising incomes suck in imports; and structural unemployment where growth is driven by technological change that displaces workers. The benefits are the other three options here — lower unemployment, higher tax revenues funding public services, and rising real incomes. Examiners want the trade-off, not a one-sided list.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might rapid growth worsen a country's current account position?","options":{"A":"Growth always causes the domestic currency to depreciate","B":"Exports automatically fall whenever national output rises","C":"Rising incomes increase spending on imported goods","D":"Faster growth reduces the country's productive capacity"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"GRO-19","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"A price index has a base year value of 100. In the current year it stands at 128. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Prices are 128% higher than they were in the chosen base year","B":"Prices have risen by 28% since the base year was established","C":"The rate of inflation in the current year is exactly 128 per cent","D":"Real output has increased by 28% since the base year measured"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GRO-01","scaffold":"Index numbers express a value relative to a base year set at 100, so the index minus 100 gives the percentage change since that base year — 128 means prices are 28% higher, not 128% higher. Two errors recur. First, confusing the level with the rate: an index rising from 120 to 126 means prices rose 5% that year, calculated as the change divided by the starting value, not 6%. Second, forgetting that a falling inflation rate still means prices are rising, just more slowly — that is disinflation, not deflation. Index numbers are also used for real GDP, wages and share prices, and the same reading rules apply throughout.","reforge":{"stem":"A price index rises from 150 to 156 over one year. What is the inflation rate?","options":{"A":"6%","B":"56%","C":"3.8%","D":"4%"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"GRO-20","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"Why is the output gap difficult for policymakers to measure accurately?","options":{"A":"Because real GDP data are never published by national statistics agencies","B":"Because actual output cannot be estimated once a recession has begun","C":"Because potential output is unobservable and must itself be estimated","D":"Because inflation and unemployment data are always released with no delay"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GRO-03","scaffold":"The output gap compares actual output with potential output, but potential output is not a statistic anyone collects — it is an estimate of what the economy could produce at full capacity, derived from modelling productivity and labour supply. Different institutions produce materially different estimates for the same year, and figures are revised substantially after the event. Actual GDP data are themselves revised for years afterwards. The policy consequence matters: if the Bank of England believes there is spare capacity when there is not, it holds rates too low and inflation follows. This uncertainty is a strong evaluation point against fine-tuning demand management.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the risk if policymakers overestimate the size of the negative output gap?","options":{"A":"Policy will be loosened too much, generating inflation","B":"Policy will be tightened too aggressively, causing a recession","C":"The economy's trend rate of growth will fall immediately","D":"The output gap will automatically close on its own over time"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"GRO-21","spec":"2.1.1","stem":"An economy records four consecutive quarters of negative growth totalling a 12% fall in GDP. How is this best described?","options":{"A":"A boom, because such a large change indicates strong economic activity","B":"A recovery, as the economy has now passed through its lowest point","C":"Disinflation, because output is growing at a progressively slower rate","D":"A depression, since the contraction is unusually deep and prolonged"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GRO-04","scaffold":"A recession is the technical minimum: two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth. A depression has no single agreed definition but is conventionally applied to a contraction that is both prolonged — often two years or more — and deep, commonly taken as a fall in GDP exceeding 10%. The 1930s and Greece after 2009 are the standard examples. Be precise with this vocabulary, because examiners penalise loose use: 'depression' for an ordinary recession, 'recession' for merely slow positive growth, and 'disinflation' confused with 'deflation' are the three errors that recur most often.","reforge":{"stem":"Which correctly describes disinflation?","options":{"A":"The general price level is falling in absolute terms","B":"The rate of inflation is falling but remains positive","C":"Inflation has risen well above the central bank's target","D":"Real GDP has fallen for two consecutive quarters"},"correct":"B"}}]},"2.1.2":{"label":"Inflation","color":"#dc2626","questions":[{"id":"INF-01","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"UK CPI rose from 120 to 126 over a year. A student says: 'Prices are high.' What is the correct interpretation?","options":{"A":"The student is confusing the price level with inflation","B":"The CPI doesn't measure prices.","C":"Yes — hyperinflation is defined as any inflation rate above 10%.","D":"126 means prices fell."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-INF-RATE-VS-LEVEL","scaffold":"Inflation = the rate of change of the price level, not the level itself. A CPI of 126 tells you the level; the inflation rate is ((126−120)/120)×100 = 5%. High prices ≠ high inflation — prices could be high but stable (0% inflation).","reforge":{"stem":"A country's CPI is 200 but hasn't changed in 3 years. What is the inflation rate?","options":{"A":"200% — the CPI is very high.","B":"Yes — any double-digit inflation qualifies as hyperinflation.","C":"0% — the price level is unchanged, so inflation is zero.","D":"It cannot be calculated from CPI data."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"INF-02","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"A student says the CPI basket 'always contains the same goods.' Why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"The basket is fixed permanently by law and never updated.","B":"The basket is updated annually to reflect changing consumer spending patterns.","C":"The basket changes daily to reflect real-time price movements.","D":"The basket contains only food and fuel — the two largest household expenditures."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-INF-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"The ONS updates the CPI basket every year. Items are added (e.g. oat milk, smartwatches) and removed (e.g. CD players) to reflect how households actually spend. A fixed basket would quickly become unrepresentative.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a valid limitation of CPI despite annual basket updates?","options":{"A":"It never changes so it's always outdated.","B":"It only measures food prices.","C":"UK inflation reaches 11% a year and prices keep rising, but the rate remains far below the extreme monthly increases associated with hyperinflation.","D":"It reflects average spending, so it may not match any individual household’s inflation experience."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"INF-03","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"A student writes: 'Demand-pull inflation is too much money chasing too few goods' but cannot explain it using a diagram. Which diagram explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"UK inflation reaches 11% a year and prices keep rising, but the rate remains far below the extreme monthly increases associated with hyperinflation.","B":"LRAS shifts right, reducing the price level.","C":"AD shifts right, increasing real output and the price level — demand-pull inflation.","D":"AD shifts left, creating deflation."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-INF-03","scaffold":"Demand-pull inflation: AD shifts right → economy moves along the SRAS → higher price level AND higher real output (up to full capacity). Causes include: rising consumer confidence, fiscal stimulus, lower interest rates, weaker exchange rate boosting net exports.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these would cause demand-pull inflation?","options":{"A":"A large cut in income tax boosting disposable income and shifting AD right.","B":"A rise in oil prices increasing firms' costs.","C":"Real output grew quickly enough to absorb the entire monetary expansion completely","D":"A fall in consumer confidence."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"INF-04","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"A student explains cost-push inflation using only wage increases. Which additional cost factors should they include?","options":{"A":"Interest rates and money supply.","B":"None — wages are the only cost-push factor.","C":"Consumer confidence and government spending.","D":"Raw material prices (e.g. oil), energy costs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-INF-COSTPUSH","scaffold":"Cost-push inflation = SRAS shifts left due to higher production costs. Wages are ONE factor, but also: commodity/energy prices (oil shocks), exchange rate depreciation (imports cost more in domestic currency), higher indirect taxes (VAT), supply chain disruptions.","reforge":{"stem":"Sterling depreciates by 15%. How might this cause cost-push inflation?","options":{"A":"It can't — exchange rates only affect the current account.","B":"Imported inputs cost more in pounds, raising firms’ costs and shifting SRAS left.","C":"It makes exports cheaper, reducing costs.","D":"Real output grew quickly enough to absorb the entire monetary expansion completely"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"INF-05","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"A student claims: 'All deflation is bad for the economy.' How should this be evaluated?","options":{"A":"Deflation from falling demand (malign) can cause debt spirals and recession","B":"Deflation is always good because prices are falling.","C":"The basket contains only food and fuel — the two largest household expenditures.","D":"Deflation and disinflation are the same thing."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-INF-05","scaffold":"Malign deflation: falling AD → lower prices, but also falling output, rising unemployment, rising real debt burden → deflationary spiral (Japan 1990s–2000s). Benign deflation: technological progress / productivity gains → costs fall → prices fall while output rises (e.g. consumer electronics). Context matters.","reforge":{"stem":"The price of electronics falls by 8% over a year due to improved manufacturing technology, while the overall economy grows at 3%. Is this harmful deflation?","options":{"A":"Yes — any falling prices are dangerous.","B":"Yes — it will cause a deflationary spiral.","C":"No — productivity-led deflation can occur while output is rising.","D":"Borrowers to lenders, since debts become harder to repay in real terms"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"INF-06","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"A student uses RPI and CPI interchangeably. Which key difference should they know?","options":{"A":"UK inflation reaches 11% a year and prices keep rising, but the rate remains far below the extreme monthly increases associated with hyperinflation.","B":"CPI uses a geometric mean and excludes some housing costs; RPI uses an arithmetic mean and includes more.","C":"CPI measures wholesale prices; RPI measures retail consumer prices.","D":"RPI is updated annually but CPI uses the same formula since it was introduced."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-INF-06","scaffold":"CPI: geometric mean, excludes mortgage interest payments and council tax. RPI: arithmetic mean (tends to give higher figure), includes mortgage interest payments. RPI is no longer an official National Statistic due to formula flaws, but is still used for some contracts (e.g. rail fares, student loans).","reforge":{"stem":"Interest rates rise sharply. What happens to RPI relative to CPI?","options":{"A":"RPI falls below CPI.","B":"Both move identically.","C":"A fall in petrol prices that makes petrol cars more attractive, with no change in electric-car prices.","D":"RPI rises relative to CPI because it includes mortgage interest payments, which have increased."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"INF-07","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"What is the Bank of England's current inflation target?","options":{"A":"UK inflation reaches 11% a year and prices keep rising, but the rate remains far below the extreme monthly increases associated with hyperinflation.","B":"5% — a moderate level encourages spending.","C":"2% CPI — set by the government and targeted by the Bank of England via monetary policy.","D":"2% RPI — the RPI measure is used."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-INF-MEASUREMENT","scaffold":"The target is 2% CPI (symmetric — undershooting is as much a concern as overshooting). Set by the Chancellor, pursued by the MPC. The letter-writing threshold is ±1pp (i.e. below 1% or above 3%). The target is NOT 0% — moderate inflation greases the wheels of the economy.","reforge":{"stem":"UK CPI inflation reaches 0.8%. What is the BoE required to do?","options":{"A":"The Governor must explain in an open letter why inflation is below target and the MPC’s response.","B":"Shifts left, because a government subsidy is treated by firms as if it were an extra cost that reduces profit margins on every unit sold, so output falls at each price.","C":"Immediately raise interest rates.","D":"Change the target to 1%."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"INF-08","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"In 2015, the price of a basket of goods was £80. In 2023, it was £104. Using 2015 as the base year (= 100), what is the index number for 2023?","options":{"A":"80","B":"104","C":"24","D":"130"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-INF-RATE-VS-LEVEL","scaffold":"Index number = (value in current year / value in base year) × 100 = (104/80) × 100 = 130. This means prices are 30% higher than the base year. Common error: using the raw price (104) as the index.","reforge":{"stem":"Base year index = 100 (price: £200). Current year price: £230. What is the index number?","options":{"A":"230","B":"115","C":"30","D":"130"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"INF-09","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"A country's inflation rate reaches 12%. A student labels this 'hyperinflation.' Is this correct?","options":{"A":"No — the rate is high but does not meet the threshold for hyperinflation.","B":"Yes — hyperinflation is defined as any inflation rate above 10%.","C":"Yes — any double-digit inflation qualifies as hyperinflation.","D":"CPI and RPI are identical — they measure the same basket with the same formula."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-INF-RATE-VS-LEVEL","scaffold":"Hyperinflation is extreme: prices doubling every few weeks/months. Cagan's threshold: 50% per month. 12% annually is high but manageable with normal monetary policy. Zimbabwe peaked at ~79.6 billion % per month in 2008. Don't conflate uncomfortable inflation with hyperinflation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which scenario best illustrates hyperinflation?","options":{"A":"UK inflation reaches 11% a year and prices keep rising, but the rate remains far below the extreme monthly increases associated with hyperinflation.","B":"Japan experiencing 3% inflation after decades near 0%.","C":"Venezuela's currency becomes worthless within weeks as prices rise extremely rapidly, so people switch to foreign currency or barter.","D":"The US Federal Reserve raising rates to 5%."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"INF-10","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"Explain how a wage-price spiral develops. (2 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Workers demand higher wages to keep up with rising prices (knowledge). Firms pass on higher wage costs through higher prices, which triggers further wage demands, creating a self-reinforcing cycle of rising wages and prices (analysis).","tag":"MC-INF-COSTPUSH","scaffold":"The spiral: prices rise → workers demand higher wages to maintain real income → firms face higher costs → firms raise prices to protect margins → prices rise further → workers demand even higher wages → repeat. It requires a mechanism that sustains the loop (e.g. strong trade unions, accommodative monetary policy).","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor would most likely BREAK a wage-price spiral?","options":{"A":"The basket contains only food and fuel — the two largest household expenditures.","B":"Government increasing the minimum wage.","C":"A further depreciation of the exchange rate.","D":"The central bank raises rates, reducing AD and firms’ ability to pass on costs."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"INF-11","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"According to the quantity theory of money (MV = PQ), sustained inflation results from:","options":{"A":"Trade unions successfully negotiating higher wages for their members","B":"A one-off rise in the price of imported energy and raw materials","C":"The money supply growing faster than real output over the long run","D":"An increase in the rate of value added tax charged on most goods"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-INF-03","scaffold":"Fisher's equation of exchange states MV = PQ, where M is the money supply, V the velocity of circulation, P the price level and Q real output. Monetarists assume V and Q are broadly stable in the short run, so a rise in M must feed through into P — hence Friedman's claim that inflation is always and everywhere a monetary phenomenon. The evaluation is important: V is not actually stable, and quantitative easing after 2009 expanded the money supply enormously without producing the inflation monetarists predicted, because banks held reserves rather than lending and velocity collapsed. The other options are genuine causes of inflation, but one-off cost shocks raise the price level once rather than sustaining inflation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did large-scale quantitative easing after 2009 not produce the inflation monetarists expected?","options":{"A":"The velocity of circulation fell as banks held reserves rather than lending","B":"The money supply was not actually increased by the asset purchases","C":"Real output grew quickly enough to absorb the entire monetary expansion completely","D":"The quantity theory of money applies only to developing economies"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"INF-12","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"Unanticipated inflation tends to redistribute real income from:","options":{"A":"Borrowers to lenders, since debts become harder to repay in real terms","B":"Importers to exporters, since import prices fall relative to export prices","C":"Employers to workers, because nominal wages adjust immediately upward","D":"Savers and lenders to borrowers, as the real value of debt is eroded"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-INF-05","scaffold":"Debt is fixed in nominal terms, so inflation erodes what it is really worth. A £200,000 mortgage after a decade of high inflation represents far less purchasing power, which benefits the borrower at the lender's expense. The same logic makes government debt easier to service, one reason governments are sometimes accused of tolerating inflation. Losers are savers holding cash or fixed-rate bonds, and anyone on a fixed nominal income such as a non-indexed pension. The word 'unanticipated' matters: if inflation is expected, lenders build it into nominal interest rates in advance, so no redistribution occurs — that is the Fisher effect.","reforge":{"stem":"Which group is most protected against unanticipated inflation?","options":{"A":"Pensioners receiving a fixed nominal annuity payment","B":"Workers whose pay is index-linked to the CPI each year","C":"Holders of long-term fixed-rate government bonds","D":"Savers holding money in a non-interest current account"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"INF-13","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"If UK inflation is persistently higher than that of its trading partners, the likely effect is:","options":{"A":"A loss of price competitiveness, worsening the current account","B":"An automatic fall in the UK's rate of structural unemployment","C":"An improvement in the UK's international price competitiveness","D":"A rise in the real value of the UK's outstanding national debt"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-INF-05","scaffold":"If UK prices rise faster than competitors' prices, UK exports become relatively expensive and imports relatively cheap, so export volumes fall and import volumes rise, worsening the current account. This is a standard chain to write out in full for the marks: higher relative inflation, then reduced price competitiveness, then falling net exports, then a fall in AD. The important evaluation is the exchange rate: under a floating system, a depreciation can offset the inflation differential and restore competitiveness, which is why the effect is far more damaging inside a fixed exchange rate system or a currency union, where that adjustment is unavailable.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is persistently high relative inflation more damaging inside a currency union?","options":{"A":"Currency unions require members to run a budget surplus each year","B":"Inflation is always higher inside a currency union than outside it","C":"The exchange rate cannot depreciate to restore lost competitiveness","D":"Members cannot use tariffs to protect their own domestic industries"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"INF-14","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"'Shoe-leather costs' of inflation refer to:","options":{"A":"The cost to firms of frequently reprinting their price lists and menus","B":"Resources used seeking the best prices and minimising cash holdings","C":"The tendency for workers to demand compensating pay increases","D":"The loss of real income suffered by savers holding cash deposits"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-INF-05","scaffold":"Two classic microeconomic costs of inflation are examined together and easily confused. Shoe-leather costs are the time and resources spent avoiding holding cash whose value is falling — historically, wearing out your shoes walking to the bank, and today the search costs of shopping around and moving money between accounts. Menu costs, which is option A, are the administrative costs of changing prices: reprinting, relabelling and updating systems. Both are modest at low inflation but become severe under hyperinflation. Add the broader costs for a full answer: uncertainty deterring investment, fiscal drag, and loss of international competitiveness.","reforge":{"stem":"Which cost of inflation is most likely to deter long-term business investment?","options":{"A":"The need to update labels on supermarket shelves","B":"The administrative cost of reprinting existing price lists","C":"The time spent by households comparing shop prices","D":"Uncertainty about future costs and returns on projects"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"INF-15","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"Fiscal drag occurs when:","options":{"A":"Tax revenues fall automatically during an economic recession period","B":"Government spending rises more slowly than the general rate of inflation","C":"Inflation pushes earnings into higher tax bands that are not uprated","D":"Public sector borrowing increases the national debt over several years"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-INF-05","scaffold":"Income tax thresholds are set in nominal terms. If they are frozen while nominal wages rise with inflation, workers are dragged into higher tax bands — or into paying tax at all — without any increase in real income. The result is a rise in the average tax rate and a fall in real disposable income, achieved without any announced tax rise, which is why it is sometimes called a stealth tax. This is highly topical: the UK froze personal allowance and higher-rate thresholds from 2021, and with high inflation this raised very substantial revenue. It is a strong applied example in questions about the effects of inflation or about fiscal policy.","reforge":{"stem":"How can a government prevent fiscal drag from occurring?","options":{"A":"By uprating tax thresholds in line with the rate of inflation","B":"By reducing the overall level of public spending each year","C":"By raising the headline rate of income tax across all of the bands","D":"By increasing the amount of government borrowing undertaken"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"INF-16","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"Nominal interest rates are 5% and inflation is 7%. What is the real interest rate?","options":{"A":"Positive at 12%, since the two rates are added together","B":"Zero, because inflation and nominal interest rates always cancel out","C":"Positive at 2%, meaning savers gain in real terms this year","D":"Negative at −2%, so savers lose purchasing power over the year"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-INF-05","scaffold":"The real interest rate is approximately the nominal rate minus the inflation rate, so 5% minus 7% gives −2%. A negative real rate means money left in a savings account buys less at the end of the year than at the start: savers are penalised and borrowing is effectively subsidised. This is exactly the situation across much of 2022 and 2023 in the UK, and it explains why high inflation discourages saving and can inflate asset prices as people seek returns elsewhere. Always check which rate a question gives you — the Bank of England sets a nominal rate, but investment and saving decisions depend on the real rate.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the likely effect of a persistently negative real interest rate?","options":{"A":"Saving becomes more attractive relative to spending","B":"It discourages saving and encourages borrowing","C":"The exchange rate appreciates against other currencies","D":"Inflation automatically returns to the target level"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"INF-17","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"Why does the Bank of England place such emphasis on 'anchoring' inflation expectations?","options":{"A":"Because expected inflation feeds into wage and price setting behaviour","B":"Because anchored expectations remove the need for any interest rate changes","C":"Because expectations are used directly to calculate the CPI each month","D":"Because the inflation target is set by expectations rather than by government"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-INF-03","scaffold":"Expectations are self-fulfilling. If workers expect 8% inflation they will demand roughly 8% pay rises, and firms expecting higher costs will raise prices pre-emptively — so expected inflation becomes actual inflation through the wage-price spiral. Anchored expectations mean the public believes inflation will return to 2% whatever happens in the short run, so a temporary energy shock does not become embedded. This is why central bank credibility and independence matter so much, and why the Bank raises rates partly to signal resolve rather than purely to reduce demand. It is also the strongest argument for central bank independence in evaluation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy most helps keep inflation expectations anchored?","options":{"A":"Allowing the government to set the level of interest rates directly","B":"Abolishing the published inflation target altogether","C":"A credible, independent central bank with a clear target","D":"Increasing the money supply whenever inflation rises"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"INF-18","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"What is core inflation?","options":{"A":"The rate of inflation recorded in the core industrial regions of a country","B":"Inflation excluding volatile items such as energy, food, alcohol and tobacco","C":"The long-run average rate of inflation over the whole of an economic cycle","D":"The inflation rate calculated using the retail prices index instead of the CPI"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-INF-06","scaffold":"Headline CPI includes everything in the basket, but energy and food prices swing sharply for reasons unconnected to domestic demand — a global oil shock or a poor harvest. Core inflation strips these out to reveal the underlying trend, which is what monetary policy can actually influence. This is why the MPC watches core alongside headline: if headline inflation is high but core is at target, the shock is external and may pass without a policy response, whereas rising core inflation signals genuine domestic overheating that requires higher rates. Know CPIH too — CPI including owner-occupiers' housing costs, now the ONS's preferred measure.","reforge":{"stem":"Headline inflation is 9% but core inflation is 2.5%. What does this suggest?","options":{"A":"Domestic demand is overheating and needs tightening","B":"The CPI basket must have been calculated incorrectly","C":"The economy is currently experiencing deflation across most sectors","D":"Inflation is driven by external shocks such as energy prices"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"INF-19","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"Which measure of inflation includes owner-occupiers' housing costs?","options":{"A":"The GDP deflator, which measures only the prices of imported goods","B":"CPI, which has always included mortgage interest payments in full","C":"CPIH, which the ONS now treats as its preferred headline measure","D":"Core inflation, which is calculated after removing all housing costs"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-INF-06","scaffold":"The measures differ in what they cover, and examiners test the distinctions. CPI excludes most housing costs — a serious weakness in a country where housing is the largest household outlay. CPIH adds owner-occupiers' housing costs using a rental equivalence approach, and is now the ONS's preferred measure. RPI includes mortgage interest payments and uses a different averaging formula, so it typically runs about one percentage point above CPI; it is no longer a national statistic but is still used for some index-linked debt and rail fares. The GDP deflator covers all domestically produced output rather than a fixed consumer basket.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does RPI typically show a higher inflation rate than CPI?","options":{"A":"It includes mortgage interest and uses a different formula","B":"It is calculated using a much smaller sample of households","C":"It excludes food and energy from the basket of goods","D":"It measures only the prices of imported manufactured goods"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"INF-20","spec":"2.1.2","stem":"A government wishes to reduce demand-pull inflation. Which policy is most directly appropriate?","options":{"A":"Reducing income tax rates in order to raise household disposable income","B":"Increasing public sector pay in line with the current rate of inflation","C":"Introducing tariffs on imported goods to protect domestic producers","D":"Raising interest rates to reduce consumption and investment spending"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-INF-03","scaffold":"Demand-pull inflation arises when AD exceeds productive capacity, so the remedy is to reduce AD — contractionary monetary policy through higher interest rates, or contractionary fiscal policy through higher taxes or lower government spending. Higher rates raise borrowing costs, increase the reward for saving, reduce disposable income for mortgage holders and tend to appreciate the currency, all of which reduce AD. Note the evaluation points: monetary policy operates with a lag of up to two years, it is a blunt instrument affecting the whole economy, and it is far less effective against cost-push inflation, where reducing demand causes unemployment without addressing the underlying cost shock.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is raising interest rates a poor remedy for cost-push inflation?","options":{"A":"Higher rates always increase the costs faced by firms directly","B":"It reduces demand and output without addressing the cost shock","C":"Interest rates have no effect on the exchange rate at all","D":"Cost-push inflation always corrects itself within one quarter"},"correct":"B"}}]},"2.1.3":{"label":"Employment & Unemployment","color":"#7c3aed","questions":[{"id":"EMP-01","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"A 45-year-old leaves work to care for an elderly parent. Under the ILO definition, this person is:","options":{"A":"Economically inactive — not seeking or available for paid work.","B":"Employed — caring is work.","C":"A worker made redundant last month who is applying for new jobs weekly","D":"Unemployed — they don't have a job."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-EMP-01","scaffold":"The ILO/LFS definition has three strict criteria, all of which must be met simultaneously: (1) without paid work — not even one hour; (2) available to start work within two weeks; (3) actively sought work in the past four weeks. Failing any one criterion means the person is NOT counted as unemployed. Economic inactivity is a distinct category: people who are neither employed nor unemployed — they are not seeking or available for work. Includes: full-time students, carers, long-term sick, discouraged workers (gave up seeking), and early retirees. Exam trap: a redundant worker who stops looking ('discouraged') moves from unemployed to economically inactive — the unemployment rate falls even though nothing improved for that person. Examiners test whether you can distinguish employed / ILO-unemployed / economically inactive.","reforge":{"stem":"Which person counts as unemployed (ILO)?","options":{"A":"A retired teacher, 63, not looking for work.","B":"A part-time employee who would prefer to be working full-time hours","C":"A graduate applying for jobs for 3 months, available immediately.","D":"A full-time university student."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"EMP-02","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"A region's coal mines close permanently due to the shift to renewables. Workers can't find jobs because their skills don't match tech-sector vacancies. This is:","options":{"A":"Cutting interest rates to boost aggregate demand and create jobs.","B":"Funding retraining programmes and improving geographical mobility through housing reform.","C":"Demand for cars rises, because drivers switch to buying more fuel-efficient models to offset the higher cost of running them.","D":"Raising unemployment benefits to increase worker bargaining power."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-EMP-02","scaffold":"The three types of unemployment require distinct policy responses — confusing them is a standard examiner-flagged error. Frictional: between jobs, short-term, normal in a dynamic labour market → information provision (job centres, Universal Jobmatch). Cyclical/demand-deficient: caused by recession → demand-side policies (fiscal stimulus, lower interest rates). Structural: long-term mismatch between skills demanded and supplied, often from deindustrialisation → supply-side policies only (retraining, occupational mobility, housing reform for geographical mobility). The coal miners example is structural: their specific skills (mining) have no demand, and they cannot easily retrain or relocate. A government cutting interest rates (cyclical fix) would not help them find work.","reforge":{"stem":"A newly qualified teacher takes 6 weeks to find a post, turning down two unsuitable offers. This is best classified as:","options":{"A":"Cyclical unemployment, resulting from a fall in aggregate demand in a recession","B":"Structural — skills mismatch.","C":"Cyclical — low aggregate demand.","D":"Frictional — short-term search between entering the market and finding a role."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"EMP-03","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"A student draws a Phillips curve showing a permanent trade-off. What critique should they consider?","options":{"A":"It only applies to developing economies.","B":"The Phillips curve is always vertical — there's never a trade-off.","C":"Friedman/Phelps: the trade-off is only short-run","D":"Inflation and unemployment always move together."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-EMP-03","scaffold":"The expectations-augmented Phillips curve (Friedman/Phelps, 1968) is the key critique of the original Phillips relationship. Short run: a trade-off appears — lower unemployment comes with higher inflation (as AD rises, firms hire more but also raise prices). Long run: workers realise real wages have fallen (money wages rose less than prices) → they demand higher nominal wages → costs rise → SRAS shifts left → unemployment returns to the natural rate (NAIRU) at a higher price level. The SRPC shifts UP. Repeat stimulus: each attempt to push unemployment below NAIRU results in a higher inflation rate with no permanent employment gain. LRPC is vertical at the NAIRU. Exam diagram: draw multiple SRPCs shifting up; LRPC vertical; label the short-run move and the long-run adjustment.","reforge":{"stem":"Government pushes unemployment below the natural rate via expansionary fiscal policy. What does the expectations-augmented Phillips curve predict over time?","options":{"A":"Workers revise expectations up; SRPC shifts up and unemployment returns to the natural rate.","B":"Economics diagrams do not require labelled axes in exam answers, since examiners mark only the position and slope of the curves drawn.","C":"Inflation falls as the economy adjusts.","D":"The natural rate itself falls."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"EMP-04","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"UK claimant count: 1.6m. ILO unemployment: 1.4m. Which best explains the difference?","options":{"A":"The government controls all house building, issuing every permit and directly constructing every new home itself.","B":"The two measures always produce exactly the same figures by design.","C":"ILO surveys are restricted to London and do not cover the regions.","D":"Claimant count includes some benefit recipients not actively seeking work under ILO criteria."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-EMP-04","scaffold":"Two measures, two different methodologies — examiners test whether you understand the precise difference. Claimant count: administrative data — those claiming Universal Credit (or JSA) for unemployment reasons. Quick to produce, but politically influenced (changing eligibility rules changes the count without affecting real unemployment). ILO/LFS: survey-based — must be (1) without paid work, (2) available to start within 2 weeks, (3) actively seeking work in the past 4 weeks. More internationally comparable, wider measure. Common reason claimant > ILO: some claimants don't actively seek work. Common reason ILO > claimant: some actively seeking work don't claim benefits (e.g. recent graduates, those ineligible). Never use the two interchangeably in an essay.","reforge":{"stem":"Government tightens benefit eligibility, removing 200k from UC. What happens?","options":{"A":"Both measures fall by 200k.","B":"Claimant count falls, but ILO is largely unaffected — those people may still be jobless and seeking.","C":"Both unaffected by benefit policy.","D":"They cannot compare alternative uses, because households are assumed to have no way of ranking their own preferences"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"EMP-05","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"On a labour market diagram, the real wage is set above equilibrium by a minimum wage. The gap between Qs and Qd represents:","options":{"A":"Real-wage unemployment — Qs exceeds Qd at that wage.","B":"An immediate fall in the level of poverty with no other economic effects","C":"Full employment.","D":"A labour shortage."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-EMP-05","scaffold":"Labour market diagram with a minimum wage above equilibrium: at wage W_min, quantity of labour supplied (Qs) > quantity demanded (Qd). The gap (Qs − Qd) = real-wage (classical) unemployment. More workers want to work at this higher wage but fewer firms are willing to hire — the price floor creates a surplus of labour. Key examiner distinction: this is not cyclical (caused by demand deficiency) or structural (caused by skills mismatch) — it is caused by the wage being artificially held above market-clearing level. Policy: lower the minimum wage (market solution) or increase labour productivity so labour demand shifts right (making the minimum wage no longer binding).","reforge":{"stem":"What would reduce real-wage unemployment caused by a minimum wage?","options":{"A":"A movement from unemployment to work, which shifts the whole economy from inside the frontier out onto a new, higher curve","B":"Increasing labour supply.","C":"Increasing labour productivity (shifting demand right) so firms hire more at the prevailing wage.","D":"Reducing the retirement age."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"EMP-07","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"Which policy would most directly reduce structural unemployment?","options":{"A":"Cutting interest rates to boost aggregate demand and create jobs.","B":"Funding retraining programmes and improving geographical mobility through housing reform.","C":"A change in the price level, which is treated as shifting the frontier whenever inflation alters the value of output","D":"Raising unemployment benefits to increase worker bargaining power."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-EMP-02","scaffold":"Structural unemployment requires supply-side solutions because its cause is on the supply side — skills mismatch or immobility. Occupational immobility: workers' skills don't match available vacancies → retraining programmes, apprenticeships, vocational education. Geographical immobility: workers can't/won't move to where jobs are → affordable housing, transport investment, childcare provision. Demand-side policies (lower interest rates, fiscal stimulus) increase total demand and can cure cyclical unemployment — but if the jobs require skills that unemployed workers don't have, stimulus merely bids up wages for skilled workers without helping the structurally unemployed. Examiner tip: always identify the type of unemployment before evaluating the policy — the match between cause and remedy is what earns evaluation marks.","reforge":{"stem":"Cyclical unemployment rising in a recession. Which policy directly addresses it?","options":{"A":"Reducing trade union power.","B":"Increasing government infrastructure spending to stimulate the economy.","C":"Deregulating the labour market.","D":"Expansionary fiscal or monetary policy to increase AD."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"EMP-08","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"The natural rate of unemployment best refers to:","options":{"A":"Inflation and unemployment are always positively related over the long run period","B":"Zero unemployment.","C":"Unemployment at full capacity: frictional + structural, but no cyclical.","D":"The rate at which inflation reaches 0%."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-EMP-03","scaffold":"The natural rate of unemployment (NAIRU — Non-Accelerating Inflation Rate of Unemployment) is the level of unemployment consistent with stable inflation. At the NAIRU: frictional unemployment exists (people between jobs) and structural unemployment exists (skills mismatches) — but there is no cyclical (demand-deficient) unemployment. Full employment is NOT zero unemployment — it means the economy is at potential output with no cyclical component. Reducing unemployment below NAIRU through demand stimulus causes accelerating inflation (as per the Friedman/Phelps expectations-augmented model). The NAIRU itself can be lowered through supply-side reform — better matching, retraining, reducing geographical immobility.","reforge":{"stem":"If a government reduces occupational immobility through retraining, what happens to the natural rate?","options":{"A":"Falls — structural unemployment decreases.","B":"Unchanged — only demand-side policy affects it.","C":"Rises — more enter the labour force.","D":"Falls to zero."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"EMP-10","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"An increase in immigration of skilled workers would shift:","options":{"A":"Labour demand right — firms demand more.","B":"Shift labour demand right, raising both employment and real wage rates","C":"Neither — immigration has no labour market effect.","D":"Labour supply right — more workers available at each wage."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-EMP-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"Immigration shifts the labour supply curve to the right: at any given wage, more workers are available. On a standard labour market diagram: Ls shifts right → new equilibrium has lower wage (W₁ < W₀) and higher employment (Q₁ > Q₀). However, immigrants also increase aggregate demand (they spend on goods and services), potentially shifting labour demand right too — partially offsetting the wage effect. Exam evaluation: the net wage effect depends on the skill level of migrants (high-skill migrants complement native workers; low-skill migrants may substitute), the elasticity of labour demand, and the time horizon (short run: supply shock; long run: demand adjusts). Never state categorically that immigration 'always lowers wages' — this ignores the demand-side effect.","reforge":{"stem":"An ageing population with rising retirements and no offsetting immigration would shift:","options":{"A":"Labour supply left — immigrants compete for fewer jobs.","B":"Labour supply left — fewer workers at each wage.","C":"Both curves right.","D":"Labour demand left."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"EMP-11","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"A graduate spends three months searching for a job that matches their skills. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Frictional unemployment, arising from the time taken to match workers to jobs","B":"Structural unemployment, caused by a permanent decline in a whole industry","C":"Cyclical unemployment, resulting from a fall in aggregate demand in a recession","D":"Seasonal unemployment, reflecting predictable changes in demand across a year"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-EMP-02","scaffold":"Frictional unemployment is transitional: workers between jobs, or entering the labour market, who will find work reasonably quickly. It exists even at full employment because job search takes time and information is imperfect, which is why a zero unemployment rate is neither achievable nor desirable. Policies to reduce it target information and incentives — better job-matching services, and reducing benefits or the replacement ratio to shorten search time. Distinguish it sharply from structural unemployment, where the worker's skills no longer match available jobs and retraining is needed, and from cyclical unemployment, which requires demand-side stimulus.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy would most directly reduce frictional unemployment?","options":{"A":"A large increase in government infrastructure spending","B":"Retraining programmes provided for redundant manufacturing workers","C":"Improved job-matching services and better vacancy information","D":"A cut in interest rates to stimulate aggregate demand"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"EMP-12","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"During a recession, unemployment rises across almost all sectors of the economy. This is best described as:","options":{"A":"Frictional unemployment, since many workers are between jobs at any time","B":"Cyclical unemployment, caused by deficient aggregate demand in the economy","C":"Structural unemployment, caused by a mismatch between skills and vacancies","D":"Real wage unemployment, caused by wages being held above the market rate"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-EMP-02","scaffold":"Cyclical or demand-deficient unemployment moves with the trade cycle: as AD falls, firms produce less and need fewer workers, so unemployment rises across the whole economy rather than in one industry. That economy-wide pattern is the diagnostic clue. Because the cause is deficient demand, the remedy is demand-side — expansionary fiscal or monetary policy to shift AD right. This contrasts with structural unemployment, which persists even in a boom and requires supply-side remedies such as retraining and geographical mobility support. Getting the diagnosis right determines which policy earns the marks.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is most appropriate for reducing cyclical unemployment?","options":{"A":"Reducing trade union power in wage negotiations","B":"Expanding vocational retraining for redundant workers","C":"Subsidising relocation costs for unemployed workers","D":"Expansionary fiscal policy to raise aggregate demand"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"EMP-13","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"A worker holds a part-time job but wants and is available for full-time work. In official statistics they are counted as:","options":{"A":"Unemployed, since they are actively seeking additional paid work","B":"Economically inactive, because they are not currently seeking any employment","C":"Employed, though they represent hidden underemployment in the economy","D":"Outside the labour force entirely, as part-time work is excluded"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-EMP-04","scaffold":"The ILO measure counts anyone who did at least one hour of paid work in the reference week as employed, so involuntary part-time workers, those on zero-hours contracts with few shifts, and graduates in low-skilled jobs all appear as employed. This means the headline unemployment rate understates labour market slack — a serious limitation, and a strong evaluation point. The UK after 2010 is the standard example: employment reached record highs while underemployment and weak real wage growth persisted. When assessing labour market health, look at underemployment, hours worked and real wage growth alongside the unemployment rate.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a record high employment rate still conceal labour market weakness?","options":{"A":"It may hide underemployment, low hours and weak real wages","B":"Employment figures are only published once every five years","C":"Employment counts only workers in the public sector","D":"The employment rate excludes anyone working part-time hours"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"EMP-14","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"Which group is classified as economically inactive rather than unemployed?","options":{"A":"A worker made redundant last month who is applying for new jobs weekly","B":"A part-time employee who would prefer to be working full-time hours","C":"A recent graduate registered with several recruitment agencies for work","D":"A full-time student who is not seeking and not available for work"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-EMP-01","scaffold":"The ILO definition requires three conditions to be counted as unemployed: without work, actively seeking work in the last four weeks, and available to start within two weeks. Anyone of working age failing the seeking or availability tests is economically inactive — students, the long-term sick, carers, early retirees and discouraged workers who have given up looking. The distinction matters because inactivity does not appear in the unemployment rate at all, so a falling unemployment rate can conceal people leaving the labour force entirely. UK inactivity rose sharply after 2020 through long-term sickness and early retirement, which tightened the labour market and contributed to wage pressure.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can the unemployment rate fall while the labour market weakens?","options":{"A":"The claimant count always rises faster than the ILO measure","B":"Discouraged workers stop searching and become inactive","C":"Employment and unemployment are measured in the same survey","D":"Inactive workers are automatically counted as unemployed"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"EMP-15","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"An economy has 30 million employed, 1.5 million unemployed and 12 million inactive. What is the unemployment rate?","options":{"A":"Approximately 4.8%, calculated as unemployed divided by the labour force","B":"Approximately 3.5%, calculated as unemployed divided by the total population","C":"Approximately 5.0%, calculated as unemployed divided by those employed","D":"Approximately 12.5%, calculated as unemployed divided by inactive people"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-EMP-04","scaffold":"The unemployment rate is unemployed divided by the labour force, where the labour force is the employed plus the unemployed — the economically inactive are excluded entirely. Here the labour force is 30m plus 1.5m, giving 31.5m, so the rate is 1.5 ÷ 31.5, which is about 4.8%. The most common error is dividing by the total population, which produces a misleadingly low figure. Know the related measures too: the employment rate is those employed as a share of the working-age population, and the participation or activity rate is the labour force as a share of the working-age population.","reforge":{"stem":"Using the same figures, what is the participation (activity) rate?","options":{"A":"About 68.8%, being the employed as a share of the population","B":"About 4.8%, being the unemployed divided by the labour force","C":"About 72.4%, being the labour force divided by working-age population","D":"About 27.6%, being the inactive divided by all of those in employment today"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"EMP-16","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"What is hysteresis in the labour market?","options":{"A":"The tendency for wages to adjust instantly whenever labour demand changes","B":"Long-term unemployment causing a permanent rise in the natural rate","C":"The seasonal pattern of unemployment in tourism and agriculture","D":"The delay between a policy change and its effect on employment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-EMP-08","scaffold":"Hysteresis means a temporary shock leaves permanent damage. Workers unemployed for a long period lose skills, lose contact with workplace routines and become stigmatised by employers, so they become effectively unemployable even once demand recovers — cyclical unemployment converts into structural unemployment and the natural rate ratchets upward. The policy implication is significant and worth stating in evaluation: it strengthens the case for acting fast and decisively in a recession, because allowing unemployment to persist imposes a permanent cost on productive capacity, shifting LRAS left. The 1980s UK experience is the standard example.","reforge":{"stem":"What does hysteresis imply about the appropriate response to a recession?","options":{"A":"Governments should allow unemployment to correct naturally","B":"Supply-side policy alone will always restore full employment","C":"Cyclical unemployment can never become structural over time","D":"Rapid intervention is needed to prevent permanent damage"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"EMP-17","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"Which is an economic rather than a social cost of high unemployment?","options":{"A":"Increased rates of crime and antisocial behaviour in affected areas","B":"A rise in mental and physical health problems among the unemployed","C":"Lost output and lower tax revenue combined with higher benefit spending","D":"The breakdown of family relationships and of community cohesion locally"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-EMP-08","scaffold":"Structure your answer by grouping the costs. Economic costs to the government: lost income tax and National Insurance receipts, plus higher welfare spending, which together worsen the budget deficit. Economic costs to the economy: lost output, since the economy operates inside its PPF with a negative output gap, and the loss of human capital through hysteresis. Economic costs to the individual: lost earnings and lower lifetime income. Social costs, which are the other three options, cover crime, health and family breakdown. Examiners reward clear categorisation, and the distinction between a cost to the government and a cost to the economy is one that weaker answers blur.","reforge":{"stem":"How does high unemployment worsen the government's budget position?","options":{"A":"Lower tax receipts combine with higher welfare spending","B":"Interest rates automatically rise when unemployment increases","C":"Government spending on infrastructure is legally required to rise","D":"The national debt is revalued upward during any recession"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"EMP-18","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"An increase in net inward migration of working-age people will, other things equal:","options":{"A":"Shift labour demand right, raising both employment and real wage rates","B":"Reduce the size of the labour force and increase the rate of inactivity","C":"Have no effect on the labour market because migrants do not seek work","D":"Shift labour supply right, tending to lower wages in affected sectors"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-EMP-LABOUR-SUPPLY","scaffold":"Migration increases labour supply, which on a standard labour market diagram shifts the supply curve right, lowering the equilibrium real wage and raising employment. But the evaluation is where the marks are. Migrants also consume goods and services, raising AD and therefore labour demand, so the demand curve shifts right too and the wage effect is ambiguous. Empirically the effect on average UK wages has been found to be small, concentrated at the lower-skilled end. Migration also raises LRAS by expanding the workforce, improves the dependency ratio, and can relieve specific shortages — so the net effect depends on migrants' skills and on which sectors they enter.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the wage effect of inward migration theoretically ambiguous?","options":{"A":"Migrants never participate in the domestic labour market","B":"Migrants raise labour supply but also raise AD and labour demand","C":"Migration reduces both labour supply and labour demand by equal amounts","D":"Wages are fixed by the government and cannot respond to supply"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"EMP-19","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"The NAIRU is best defined as:","options":{"A":"The unemployment rate at which the rate of inflation remains stable","B":"The lowest unemployment rate ever achieved in a country's history","C":"The rate of unemployment that is recorded during the trough of a recession","D":"The total number of people claiming unemployment-related benefits"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-EMP-08","scaffold":"The NAIRU is the non-accelerating inflation rate of unemployment — the rate consistent with stable inflation. Below it, the labour market is tight, workers bid up wages and inflation accelerates; above it, inflation falls. It is essentially the natural rate expressed in inflation terms, made up of frictional and structural unemployment, so it cannot be reduced by demand-side policy without generating inflation — only supply-side policies that improve labour market flexibility, skills and mobility can lower it. Like potential output, the NAIRU is unobservable and estimates vary widely, which is a genuine limitation for policymakers relying on it.","reforge":{"stem":"How can a government reduce the NAIRU?","options":{"A":"Increasing government borrowing in order to fund public spending","B":"Expansionary monetary policy to raise aggregate demand","C":"Supply-side reforms improving skills and labour mobility","D":"Raising the level of unemployment benefit payments"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"EMP-20","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"A national minimum wage set above the market equilibrium wage is most likely to cause:","options":{"A":"An immediate fall in the level of poverty with no other economic effects","B":"Excess supply of labour in low-paid sectors, creating some unemployment","C":"An increase in labour demand as firms respond by hiring more workers","D":"A reduction in labour supply as fewer workers seek low-paid employment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-EMP-05","scaffold":"On a competitive labour market diagram, a wage floor above equilibrium means quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded, and that excess supply is classical or real wage unemployment. But the evaluation genuinely cuts both ways and examiners expect it. In a monopsony — a single dominant employer, common in care work and retail in some towns — a minimum wage can raise both wages and employment, because the monopsonist was previously restricting hiring to hold wages down. Empirical UK evidence since 1999 has found limited employment effects, and firms may absorb the cost through lower profits, higher prices or productivity gains.","reforge":{"stem":"Under what market condition can a minimum wage raise employment?","options":{"A":"When the labour market is perfectly competitive","B":"When labour demand is perfectly elastic at every possible wage rate","C":"When the minimum wage is set below the equilibrium wage","D":"When a monopsony employer dominates the local labour market"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"EMP-21","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"Which factor best explains persistent regional differences in unemployment within the UK?","options":{"A":"The national minimum wage being set at a different rate in each region","B":"Identical industrial structures existing across all of the UK's regions","C":"Geographical and occupational immobility of labour between regions","D":"Uniform house prices across the country making relocation costless"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-EMP-02","scaffold":"Regional unemployment persists because labour does not move freely. Geographical immobility is driven by housing cost differences — moving from the North East to London means a very large increase in housing costs — plus family ties and the cost of moving. Occupational immobility means a redundant steelworker cannot simply take a job in financial services without retraining. Together these prevent the labour market from clearing regionally, so structural unemployment concentrates in areas where a dominant industry has declined. Policy responses follow directly: relocation assistance, housing supply, retraining programmes and regional investment such as devolution and levelling-up funding.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy most directly addresses geographical immobility of labour?","options":{"A":"Increasing housing supply and offering relocation assistance","B":"Raising the national minimum wage across the country","C":"Increasing unemployment benefit in high-unemployment regions","D":"Cutting corporation tax for firms in all regions equally"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"EMP-22","spec":"2.1.3","stem":"The long-run Phillips curve is vertical at the natural rate because:","options":{"A":"Inflation and unemployment are always positively related over the long run period","B":"Governments can permanently reduce unemployment by accepting inflation","C":"Unemployment is entirely unaffected by monetary policy in the short run","D":"Workers eventually adjust expectations, so only inflation rises permanently"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-EMP-03","scaffold":"The short-run Phillips curve shows a trade-off: expansionary policy raises inflation and lowers unemployment because workers are fooled by higher nominal wages into supplying more labour. Once they realise real wages have not risen, they demand compensating pay increases, employment falls back to the natural rate and the short-run curve shifts up — the economy ends with the same unemployment but higher inflation. Repeating the trick produces accelerating inflation, which is Friedman's expectations-augmented argument and describes 1970s stagflation. The policy conclusion is that only supply-side reform can permanently reduce unemployment, since demand-side policy moves you along a curve that then shifts.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the vertical long-run Phillips curve imply for policy?","options":{"A":"Demand-side policy can permanently lower unemployment","B":"Only supply-side policy can lower unemployment permanently","C":"Inflation and unemployment cannot both fall at the same time","D":"The natural rate of unemployment is always exactly zero"},"correct":"B"}}]},"2.1.4":{"label":"Balance of Payments","color":"#059669","questions":[{"id":"BOP-01","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"UK current account deficit: 3.1% of GDP (2023). A student writes: 'This is very bad for the UK economy.' Which best evaluates this?","options":{"A":"Partly right: a persistent deficit can signal uncompetitiveness","B":"Wrong — deficits are always beneficial.","C":"Raise interest rates to strengthen the currency and make exports more competitive.","D":"Correct — any deficit is harmful."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BOP-01","scaffold":"A current account deficit is not inherently bad — context determines significance. Arguments that it matters: (1) if financed by 'hot money' (short-term capital flows), a sudden reversal (sudden stop) can cause a currency crisis; (2) persistent deficit implies the country is living beyond its means — importing more than it earns; (3) may signal weak export competitiveness. Arguments that it doesn't matter: (1) the US has run persistent deficits for decades while remaining the world's largest economy; (2) a deficit could reflect strong domestic demand and investment (productive imports); (3) it is automatically financed — the financial account must surplus by the same amount. Examiner evaluation: size, persistence, financing method, and cause all determine significance.","reforge":{"stem":"A developing country's deficit is 2% of GDP, driven by importing capital machinery for new infrastructure. Is this necessarily a problem?","options":{"A":"Yes — all deficits reduce GDP.","B":"All resources are equally suited to both goods, so every unit transferred between them is given up at exactly the same rate","C":"Not necessarily — the imports are productive investments that may boost future export capacity.","D":"No — deficits never matter."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"BOP-02","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"A student writes: 'The UK's budget deficit widened to 5% of GDP, worsening the current account deficit.' The error is:","options":{"A":"Demand determines productive capacity, so the frontier itself shifts in and out purely in response to changes in consumer spending","B":"The fiscal deficit (G − T) and current account deficit (M − X) measure different things.","C":"The fiscal deficit cannot be expressed as a percentage of GDP.","D":"The current account only measures trade in goods, not services."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-BOP-TWIN-DEFICITS","scaffold":"Twin deficits hypothesis suggests fiscal and current account deficits may be linked: G > T → government borrows → higher interest rates → capital inflows → exchange rate appreciates → exports fall, imports rise → current account worsens. But they are conceptually distinct and do not always move together. Fiscal deficit = government's own budget: G − T. Current account = the whole economy's trade position: X − M (plus income and transfers). A government can have a fiscal surplus while running a current account deficit (China historically), or run a fiscal deficit with a current account surplus (Japan). Examiners penalise treating them as the same thing — always define each separately before exploring any link.","reforge":{"stem":"Which pair correctly identifies two separate deficits?","options":{"A":"Having the lower opportunity cost of production compared with every other producer in the market","B":"Fiscal = exports − imports; Current account = G − T.","C":"Both the same concept.","D":"Fiscal = G − T; Current account = trade balance + primary + secondary income (when negative)."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"BOP-03","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"After sterling depreciates, a student draws the J-curve starting with an immediate improvement. What's wrong?","options":{"A":"It gets worse and never recovers.","B":"No error — the fiscal deficit and current account deficit are the same concept.","C":"The current account worsens first, then improves — the J-curve effect.","D":"J-curve only applies to fixed exchange rates."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-JCURVE","scaffold":"The J-curve traces the time path of the current account after a depreciation. Immediately after depreciation: import prices rise in domestic currency (same volume × higher price = larger import bill); export contracts are pre-set in foreign currency so revenue doesn't change immediately → current account WORSENS (downward dip of the J). Medium/long run (Marshall-Lerner conditions permitting): export volumes rise (foreigners find UK goods cheaper) and import volumes fall (UK consumers substitute domestic goods) → the volume effects outweigh the price effects → current account IMPROVES (the upward stroke). Exam diagram: draw the J shape clearly — label the initial deterioration, the turning point, and the eventual improvement. State the time lags involved.","reforge":{"stem":"Six months after a 15% peso depreciation, the current account deficit has widened. Which is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Expected — the economy is in the initial worsening phase; improvement may follow as volumes adjust.","B":"Its inflation rate, calculated from the change in the cost of producing cars and wheat over the period","C":"The depreciation has failed.","D":"The current account always worsens permanently after depreciation."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"BOP-04","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"A student argues: 'If the pound depreciates, the UK current account will definitely improve.' What critical condition are they ignoring?","options":{"A":"The inflation target must be met first.","B":"The government must run a budget surplus.","C":"The country already runs a large current account surplus before the depreciation","D":"The Marshall-Lerner condition — improvement only if PEDx + PEDm > 1."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-BOP-04","scaffold":"Marshall-Lerner condition: a depreciation improves the current account ONLY IF the sum of price elasticity of demand for exports (PEDx) plus price elasticity of demand for imports (PEDm) exceeds 1. If PEDx + PEDm > 1: volume effects dominate → current account improves. If PEDx + PEDm < 1: demand is too inelastic (people keep buying the same volumes regardless of price changes) → import bill rises more than export revenue → current account worsens. The condition is less likely to hold in the short run (contracts, habits, search costs delay adjustment) but more likely in the long run (time to find substitutes). UK exports include financial services and pharmaceuticals — relatively price-inelastic. Always question whether ML holds before assuming depreciation helps.","reforge":{"stem":"A country importing mostly essential energy and food sees its currency depreciate 20%. Why might the current account NOT improve?","options":{"A":"Scarcity is eliminated by trade, because specialising according to comparative advantage removes the need to make further choices","B":"Energy and food are price-inelastic, so demand barely falls and the import bill rises.","C":"Exports are too cheap.","D":"Energy isn't counted in the current account."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"BOP-05","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"The current account of the balance of payments is made up of:","options":{"A":"Trade in goods, trade in services, primary income, and secondary income.","B":"UK service exports are recorded in the financial account, inflating the surplus","C":"Government borrowing and lending to other nations only.","D":"Trade in goods (visible trade) only, excluding all services."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BOP-05","scaffold":"Current account has four components — examiners regularly test whether students know all four and can classify correctly. (1) Trade in goods (visible balance): exports minus imports of physical goods. (2) Trade in services (invisible balance): exports minus imports of services — financial services, tourism, education, insurance. (3) Primary income: investment income (dividends, interest from overseas assets) and compensation of employees working abroad. (4) Secondary income (current transfers): government transfers (EU contributions when member), remittances, overseas aid. FDI and portfolio investment flows belong in the financial account — a very common misclassification error. In the exam: always check whether a flow is a transaction in current output (current account) or a change in financial assets/liabilities (financial account).","reforge":{"stem":"A UK pension fund receives £2bn in dividends from US shares. This appears in:","options":{"A":"Trade in services.","B":"Services are excluded from the calculation of the current account balance","C":"Primary income on the current account — investment income from abroad.","D":"Secondary income."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"BOP-06","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"Which policy could reduce a persistent current account deficit?","options":{"A":"Raise interest rates to strengthen the currency and make exports more competitive.","B":"Supply-side policies to improve productivity and reduce unit labour costs.","C":"Increase government spending to boost AD.","D":"Abolish all tariffs to encourage more imports."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-BOP-01","scaffold":"Three broad approaches to reducing a current account deficit, with different mechanisms and trade-offs. (1) Expenditure-reducing: deflationary fiscal/monetary policy → lower national income → fewer imports (but also lower growth, higher unemployment). (2) Expenditure-switching: depreciation, tariffs, subsidies → consumers and foreign buyers switch between domestic and imported goods (but depreciation requires ML to hold; tariffs risk retaliation). (3) Supply-side: productivity improvements reduce unit costs → UK goods more price-competitive → exports rise without needing depreciation (no inflationary side-effect, but slow to take effect). Examiner evaluation: which policy is most appropriate depends on the cause of the deficit (demand-pull vs competitiveness), the time horizon, and any constraint (e.g. exchange rate regime).","reforge":{"stem":"A government uses contractionary fiscal policy to reduce a current account deficit. This is:","options":{"A":"Protectionism.","B":"When it is caused by a permanent loss of all international competitiveness","C":"Supply-side policy.","D":"Expenditure-reducing — lower national income reduces import demand."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"BOP-08","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"The balance of payments must always balance overall. This means:","options":{"A":"Government borrowing and lending to other nations only.","B":"Trade in goods (visible trade) only, excluding all services.","C":"Foreign direct investment and portfolio investment flows only.","D":"Trade in goods, trade in services, primary income, and secondary income."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-BOP-05","scaffold":"The balance of payments MUST balance as an accounting identity: Current Account + Capital Account + Financial Account + Net Errors and Omissions = 0. This doesn't mean trade is balanced — it means every deficit on one account is automatically matched by a surplus elsewhere. A current account deficit (more money leaving for imports than entering from exports) must be financed: foreign investors buy UK assets (financial account surplus — capital inflows) or foreign governments accumulate sterling reserves. The UK's large current account deficit is financed by net inflows on the financial account (foreign purchases of UK property, equities, gilts). 'The BoP balances' does NOT mean the current account is zero — it means the overall sum of all accounts is zero.","reforge":{"stem":"UK current account deficit: £80bn. What must be true?","options":{"A":"Corresponding surplus on financial/capital accounts of ~£80bn — capital flowing in.","B":"Government must borrow £80bn.","C":"Opportunity cost disappears permanently once an economy specialises fully in the industry it produces most efficiently","D":"BoP doesn't need to balance."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"BOP-10","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"Which would be a credit (positive entry) on the UK's current account?","options":{"A":"Trade in goods (visible trade) only, excluding all services.","B":"Foreign direct investment and portfolio investment flows only.","C":"Trade in goods, trade in services, primary income, and secondary income.","D":"Government borrowing and lending to other nations only."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-BOP-05","scaffold":"Credits = money flowing INTO the UK economy; debits = money flowing OUT. Trade in services credit: UK earns money from abroad by exporting services — overseas students paying UK university fees is an export of educational services → credit on the services account. Tourism: overseas visitors spending in the UK = credit; UK residents spending abroad = debit. Financial services: UK banks earning fees from foreign clients = credit. Common misclassification: a UK resident buying foreign shares = debit on financial account, not current account. A UK firm receiving dividends from an overseas subsidiary = credit on primary income (current account). Always ask: which direction is the money flowing, and what type of transaction is it?","reforge":{"stem":"A French tourist spends £3,000 in London. This is recorded as:","options":{"A":"Debit on trade in goods.","B":"Credit on trade in services — tourism is a UK service export.","C":"Debit on secondary income.","D":"A UK company exporting machinery to a manufacturer based in Germany"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"BOP-11","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"The Marshall-Lerner condition states that a depreciation will improve the current account only if:","options":{"A":"The combined price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceed one","B":"The country already runs a large current account surplus before the depreciation","C":"Domestic inflation is lower than the rate in all major trading partners","D":"The depreciation is at least ten per cent against every major currency"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BOP-04","scaffold":"A depreciation makes exports cheaper abroad and imports dearer at home, but whether that improves the current account depends on how much quantities respond. The Marshall-Lerner condition requires PEDx + PEDm > 1 in absolute terms. If demand is inelastic on both sides, the higher price paid for imports outweighs any gain in export volume and the current account worsens. This is precisely why the J-curve exists: in the short run elasticities are low because contracts are fixed and buyers take time to switch, so the balance deteriorates first, then improves as demand becomes more elastic over time.","reforge":{"stem":"An economy's PEDx is 0.3 and PEDm is 0.4. What happens after a depreciation?","options":{"A":"The current account improves immediately and substantially","B":"The exchange rate automatically returns to its original starting level","C":"The current account worsens, as Marshall-Lerner is not satisfied","D":"There is no effect at all on the current account balance"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"BOP-12","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"Which transaction is recorded in the financial account rather than the current account?","options":{"A":"A UK company exporting machinery to a manufacturer based in Germany","B":"A German investor purchasing shares in a company listed in London","C":"A UK resident receiving dividend income from shares held overseas","D":"A British household paying for a holiday hotel stay in Spain"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-BOP-05","scaffold":"Keep the accounts separate. The current account records trade in goods and services, primary income such as interest, profit and dividends, and secondary income such as remittances and aid. The financial account records transactions in assets and liabilities — foreign direct investment, portfolio investment in shares and bonds, and reserve movements. The capital account is small, covering things like debt forgiveness and migrants' asset transfers. Note the trap in option C: receiving dividends from overseas shares is primary income and sits in the current account, even though the underlying asset purchase was a financial account transaction.","reforge":{"stem":"In which part of the balance of payments are remittances sent home by migrant workers recorded?","options":{"A":"The financial account, as a portfolio investment flow","B":"The capital account, as a transfer of migrants' assets","C":"The current account, under trade in services provided","D":"The current account, as secondary income transfers"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"BOP-13","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"A country running a large current account deficit must, by definition, be:","options":{"A":"Running an equally large government budget deficit in the same period","B":"Experiencing a rapidly depreciating currency against all its trading partners","C":"Financing it with a surplus on the combined capital and financial accounts","D":"Suffering from higher unemployment than each of its main trading partners"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-BOP-08","scaffold":"The balance of payments balances by construction: a current account deficit means the country is buying more from abroad than it sells, so it must be financed by selling assets to foreigners or borrowing from them, which appears as a surplus on the capital and financial accounts. The UK is the standard example, financing a persistent deficit through inward investment and foreign purchases of gilts and London property. This framing matters for evaluation — a deficit financed by long-term FDI that raises productive capacity is far more sustainable than one financed by short-term 'hot money' that can leave rapidly, triggering a currency crisis.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a deficit financed by short-term 'hot money' flows more risky?","options":{"A":"Such flows can reverse rapidly, causing a currency crisis","B":"Hot money is not recorded in the balance of payments","C":"It automatically raises the country's rate of inflation","D":"Short-term flows always reduce the country's productive capacity"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"BOP-14","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"Which is an example of an expenditure-switching policy to reduce a current account deficit?","options":{"A":"Raising income tax to reduce total household disposable income","B":"Cutting government spending to lower overall aggregate demand","C":"Raising interest rates in order to reduce consumption and investment","D":"Devaluing the currency to make imports dearer and exports cheaper"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-BOP-06","scaffold":"Two policy families are examined here. Expenditure-switching policies redirect spending from foreign to domestic goods without necessarily reducing total spending — devaluation, tariffs and quotas. Expenditure-reducing policies cut total demand, so imports fall as a by-product: higher taxes, lower government spending, higher interest rates. The evaluation differs sharply between them. Expenditure-reducing works but at the cost of lower growth and higher unemployment, since you are deliberately contracting the economy. Devaluation risks imported inflation and may fail the Marshall-Lerner condition. Protectionism invites retaliation. The long-run answer is supply-side: raising productivity and non-price competitiveness.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main drawback of using expenditure-reducing policies?","options":{"A":"They always cause the exchange rate to appreciate sharply","B":"They reduce growth and raise unemployment in the economy","C":"They breach World Trade Organisation rules on free trade","D":"They have no effect on the volume of imported goods"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"BOP-15","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"A country's terms of trade improve. This means:","options":{"A":"Export prices have risen relative to import prices for that country","B":"The volume of exports has risen relative to the volume of imports","C":"The current account has automatically moved into a larger surplus","D":"The country's currency has depreciated against its trading partners"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BOP-04","scaffold":"The terms of trade is an index of export prices divided by import prices, multiplied by 100. An improvement means each unit of exports buys more imports, which sounds unambiguously good — but the effect on the current account is ambiguous, and that is the examinable subtlety. If export prices rise and demand is price elastic, export volumes fall sharply and export revenue may drop, worsening the current account. So improved terms of trade raise living standards per unit traded while potentially damaging competitiveness. Commodity-dependent developing economies are the classic application, since volatile commodity prices swing their terms of trade violently.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might improved terms of trade worsen the current account?","options":{"A":"Import prices rise faster than export prices in response","B":"Improved terms of trade always cause a currency depreciation","C":"Higher export prices reduce export volumes if demand is elastic","D":"The terms of trade have no relationship with the current account"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"BOP-16","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"Which best explains the persistent UK deficit in trade in goods alongside a surplus in services?","options":{"A":"The UK imposes tariffs on all imported services but not on goods","B":"Comparative advantage in financial and professional services, not manufacturing","C":"UK service exports are recorded in the financial account, inflating the surplus","D":"Services are excluded from the calculation of the current account balance"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-BOP-05","scaffold":"The UK's trade pattern reflects long-run structural change: manufacturing has declined as a share of output since the 1970s while financial, legal, consultancy, education and creative services have grown, so the UK specialises where it holds comparative advantage. Hence a large goods deficit and a substantial services surplus, with the goods deficit historically the larger of the two. The evaluation to reach for is vulnerability: dependence on financial services concentrates risk, as 2008 demonstrated, and services trade faces more non-tariff barriers than goods, which is why post-Brexit arrangements matter more for services than the headline tariff position suggests.","reforge":{"stem":"Which UK sector contributes most to the surplus in trade in services?","options":{"A":"Agriculture and the production of primary commodities","B":"Motor vehicle and heavy machinery manufacturing","C":"Extraction of North Sea oil and natural gas reserves","D":"Financial, legal and professional business services"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"BOP-17","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"Which factor most improves a country's non-price international competitiveness?","options":{"A":"A sustained depreciation of the country's nominal exchange rate","B":"A reduction in the rate of corporation tax paid by exporting firms","C":"Improvements in product quality, design, reliability and after-sales service","D":"A sustained fall in the domestic rate of inflation relative to all trading partners"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-BOP-06","scaffold":"Competitiveness has two dimensions and questions frequently hinge on the distinction. Price competitiveness depends on relative inflation, the exchange rate, unit labour costs and productivity — options A and D both work through this channel. Non-price competitiveness depends on quality, design, branding, reliability, delivery times and after-sales service, and is built through investment in R&D, skills and innovation. Germany's export success rests heavily on non-price factors, which is why it sustains a surplus despite a strong currency and high wages. Non-price competitiveness is more durable because it cannot be eroded overnight by a currency movement, making it the better long-run strategy.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is non-price competitiveness considered more sustainable than price competitiveness?","options":{"A":"It cannot be eroded quickly by exchange rate movements","B":"It requires no investment in research or workforce skills","C":"It is unaffected by the actions of competing foreign firms","D":"It guarantees a permanent surplus on the current account"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"BOP-18","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"Global imbalances refer to:","options":{"A":"Differences in the rate of inflation recorded between the developed economies","B":"The uneven distribution of income within a single national economy","C":"Variation in unemployment rates across the regions of one country","D":"Large, persistent current account surpluses and deficits between countries"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-BOP-08","scaffold":"Global imbalances describe the pattern of large, sustained surpluses in economies such as China, Germany and the oil exporters set against persistent deficits in the US and UK. Because every deficit must be matched by a surplus elsewhere, the world current account nets to zero — one country's deficit is another's surplus, so imbalances are a systemic issue rather than a national failing. The concern is that they are destabilising: surplus countries accumulate claims on deficit countries, capital flows into deficit economies can inflate asset bubbles, and unwinding the imbalance can force sharp exchange rate and demand adjustments. This fed directly into the 2008 crisis narrative.","reforge":{"stem":"Why must global current account balances sum to approximately zero?","options":{"A":"Because the IMF sets a quota limiting each country's deficit","B":"Because every country's exports are another country's imports","C":"Because exchange rates always adjust to eliminate imbalances","D":"Because surpluses are excluded from official global statistics"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"BOP-19","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"Under what circumstances is a current account deficit least likely to be a cause for concern?","options":{"A":"When it results from importing capital goods that raise future capacity","B":"When it is large, persistent and financed by short-term borrowing","C":"When it is accompanied by a rapidly depreciating national currency","D":"When it is caused by a permanent loss of all international competitiveness"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BOP-01","scaffold":"Deficits are not automatically bad, and the strongest answers say why. A deficit driven by importing machinery, technology and capital goods raises future productive capacity, so it should generate the export earnings to repay it — this is exactly how many fast-growing economies develop. A deficit is also benign if it is small relative to GDP, temporary, or financed by long-term inward FDI rather than volatile hot money. It becomes a genuine problem when it reflects structural loss of competitiveness, when it is large and persistent, when it is financed by short-term borrowing, or when it signals excessive consumption rather than investment.","reforge":{"stem":"Which feature makes a current account deficit most concerning?","options":{"A":"It is small relative to the size of the country's GDP","B":"It arises from importing machinery that is used in domestic production","C":"It reflects a structural loss of competitiveness and is persistent","D":"It is financed by long-term foreign direct investment inflows"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"BOP-20","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"A government imposes tariffs to reduce a current account deficit. What is the most significant risk?","options":{"A":"Tariffs are prohibited under domestic competition law in most countries","B":"Retaliation by trading partners, reducing exports and overall welfare","C":"Tariff revenue must legally be returned to the exporting country","D":"Tariffs cause the domestic currency to appreciate uncontrollably"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-BOP-06","scaffold":"Tariffs raise import prices and switch expenditure toward domestic goods, so they can reduce a deficit in the short run. The risks are substantial. Trading partners retaliate, so exports fall and any gain is offset — the 1930s Smoot-Hawley experience and the US-China tariff exchanges from 2018 both illustrate this. Domestic consumers face higher prices, which is regressive and inflationary, and protected firms lose the competitive pressure to raise productivity, so the underlying competitiveness problem worsens. Tariffs may also breach WTO commitments. This is why supply-side improvement is the preferred long-run remedy in evaluation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can protectionism worsen competitiveness in the long run?","options":{"A":"Tariffs directly raise the productivity of domestic firms","B":"Tariffs reduce the price of goods for domestic consumers","C":"Protectionism always causes the exchange rate to depreciate sharply","D":"Protected firms face less pressure to raise their productivity"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"BOP-21","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"A rise in domestic interest rates relative to those abroad is likely to:","options":{"A":"Cause a depreciation as investors move their capital funds overseas","B":"Have no effect at all on either the exchange rate or on the current account balance","C":"Attract capital inflows, appreciating the currency and worsening the trade balance","D":"Reduce the financial account surplus while improving the current account"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-BOP-04","scaffold":"Higher relative interest rates make domestic assets more attractive, so hot money flows in, demand for the currency rises and it appreciates. The consequences run in both directions and a full answer traces both: a stronger currency makes exports dearer and imports cheaper, worsening the current account — the SPICED mnemonic, Strong Pound Imports Cheap Exports Dear — while the capital inflow itself improves the financial account. This is a key transmission channel for monetary policy and explains a real policy conflict: raising rates to control inflation simultaneously damages export competitiveness, so the objectives of price stability and a satisfactory current account can pull against each other.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy conflict does this transmission channel illustrate?","options":{"A":"Reducing inflation may worsen the current account position","B":"Raising employment always reduces the rate of inflation","C":"Cutting the budget deficit always improves competitiveness","D":"Currency appreciation always improves the trade balance"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"BOP-22","spec":"2.1.4","stem":"Which measure would most effectively improve the UK current account in the long run?","options":{"A":"A one-off devaluation of sterling against all other major world currencies","B":"Temporary import quotas applied to manufactured consumer goods from abroad","C":"An immediate increase in the interest rate set by the Monetary Policy Committee","D":"Sustained investment in productivity, skills and innovation across the economy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-BOP-06","scaffold":"Demand-side and exchange rate measures shift the deficit around without curing it. A devaluation may be offset by imported inflation that erodes the competitiveness gain, quotas invite retaliation and dull the incentive to improve, and higher interest rates appreciate the currency and worsen the trade balance. The durable remedy is supply-side: raising productivity lowers unit labour costs, which improves price competitiveness permanently, while investment in skills, R&D and infrastructure improves non-price competitiveness. This is slow and expensive, and the standard evaluation is that supply-side policies have long time lags and uncertain returns — but they address the cause rather than the symptom.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main limitation of relying on supply-side policy to correct a deficit?","options":{"A":"It provokes immediate retaliation from trading partners","B":"It works only with long time lags and uncertain returns","C":"It permanently reduces the economy's productive capacity","D":"It requires the exchange rate to be permanently fixed"},"correct":"B"}}]},"2.2.1":{"label":"Aggregate Demand","color":"#0891b2","questions":[{"id":"AD-01","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"Which of the following is the correct formula for aggregate demand?","options":{"A":"AD = C + I + G + (X − M)","B":"AD = C + S + G + X","C":"AD = C + I + T + (X − M)","D":"AD = GDP − inflation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-AD-01","scaffold":"AD = Consumer spending (C) + Investment (I) + Government spending (G) + Net exports (X−M). Savings (S) and taxation (T) are withdrawals from the circular flow, not components of AD.","reforge":{"stem":"A government increases G by £10bn while imports rise by £10bn. What is the net effect on AD?","options":{"A":"AD rises by £20bn.","B":"AD falls by £10bn.","C":"AD is unchanged — the rise in G is offset by the rise in M (which reduces net exports).","D":"All workers become perfectly mobile, so a fall in demand for the main industry is absorbed instantly by other sectors"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"AD-02","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"Interest rates fall from 5% to 3%. Explain the likely effect on TWO components of AD. (4 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Consumption (C) is likely to rise: lower interest rates reduce the cost of borrowing and the reward for saving, encouraging households to spend more, especially on credit-financed purchases like cars (K+An). Investment (I) is likely to rise: firms face a lower cost of borrowing, making more investment projects profitable (expected rate of return now exceeds the interest rate), so capital spending increases (K+An).","tag":"MC-MON-TRANSMISSION","scaffold":"Interest rate transmission: lower rates → cheaper borrowing + lower saving reward → C rises. Also: lower cost of capital for firms → more projects profitable → I rises. Could also discuss: mortgage costs fall → disposable income rises → C rises; exchange rate may depreciate → X rises / M falls."},{"id":"AD-03","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"A fall in consumer confidence would shift:","options":{"A":"SRAS left — confidence affects costs.","B":"AD right — worried consumers spend more.","C":"AD left — lower confidence reduces consumption, a major component of AD.","D":"An unexpected rise in interest rates set by the Monetary Policy Committee"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-AD-03","scaffold":"Consumer confidence affects willingness to spend. Lower confidence → households save more (precautionary saving) → C falls → AD shifts left. Confidence is a demand-side factor — it doesn't change firms' costs (SRAS) or the economy's productive capacity (LRAS).","reforge":{"stem":"House prices rise significantly. Which component of AD is most directly affected and in which direction?","options":{"A":"C rises — the wealth effect means homeowners feel wealthier and spend more.","B":"AD shifts have no effect on the price level — only potential output matters.","C":"I falls — houses are too expensive for firms.","D":"X rises — foreigners buy UK houses."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AD-04","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"A student draws AD shifting right and labels the result 'economic growth and lower inflation.' On a standard AD/AS diagram with an upward-sloping SRAS, why is this wrong?","options":{"A":"AD shifts always lower the price level by boosting productive efficiency.","B":"Demand always rises with price, because consumers treat a higher price as a signal of higher quality and buy more of it","C":"AD shifts only affect inflation, not real output or employment.","D":"An AD shift right along an upward SRAS raises both real output AND the price level."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-AD-SHIFT-INFLATION","scaffold":"On a standard diagram: AD shifts right → moves along upward-sloping SRAS → higher real GDP (growth) BUT also higher price level (inflationary pressure). The only way to get growth WITHOUT inflation is an LRAS/SRAS shift right (supply-side improvement).","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination correctly shows the effect of an AD shift RIGHT on a standard AD/SRAS diagram?","options":{"A":"Output falls, price level falls.","B":"Output rises, price level rises.","C":"Output rises, price level unchanged.","D":"Output unchanged, price level rises."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"AD-05","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"The UK's exchange rate depreciates. Trace the effect on AD through net exports. (2 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"A weaker pound makes UK exports cheaper in foreign currency, increasing demand for exports (X rises) (knowledge). It also makes imports more expensive in pounds, reducing demand for imports (M falls). Net exports (X−M) improve, shifting AD to the right (analysis).","tag":"MC-AD-05","scaffold":"Depreciation → exports cheaper abroad (more competitive) → X rises. Imports dearer at home → consumers switch to domestic goods → M falls. Both effects improve net exports (X−M), a component of AD. Assumes Marshall-Lerner holds."},{"id":"AD-06","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"Which factor would cause a movement ALONG the AD curve rather than a SHIFT of it?","options":{"A":"A change in consumer confidence.","B":"A change in the price level.","C":"A change in the exchange rate.","D":"A change in government spending."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-AD-06","scaffold":"A change in the price level causes a movement along AD (the real balance effect, interest rate effect, and trade effect explain why AD slopes down). Changes in C, I, G, or (X−M) caused by anything OTHER than the price level shift the whole AD curve.","reforge":{"stem":"Government announces a £20bn infrastructure programme. On an AD/AS diagram, this causes:","options":{"A":"A movement along AD to the right.","B":"A shift of SRAS to the right.","C":"Because the marginal propensity to consume is always far greater than one whole","D":"A shift of AD to the right — G has increased independently of the price level."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"AD-07","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"A student lists 'higher wages' as a factor that shifts AD right. Is this always correct?","options":{"A":"A leftward demand shift, because a lower price is treated as a change in one of the non-price determinants of demand","B":"No — wages have no direct effect on aggregate demand.","C":"Not necessarily — higher wages raise household income and spending but also increase firms' costs.","D":"Yes, but only if wage rises exceed the rate of inflation."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-AD-WAGES","scaffold":"Higher wages → more disposable income → C rises → AD shifts right. BUT higher wages → higher costs for firms → may reduce profits/investment (I falls) and shift SRAS left. The net AD effect is ambiguous. This is why examiners reward nuanced analysis over blanket statements.","reforge":{"stem":"Oil prices fall globally. What is the most likely combined effect on AD and SRAS?","options":{"A":"AD and SRAS both shift right: lower fuel costs support consumption and reduce firms’ costs.","B":"Supply always falls when price rises, because firms interpret a higher price as a signal to withdraw output from the market","C":"Only AD shifts right.","D":"Only SRAS shifts left."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AD-08","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"The multiplier effect means that an initial injection of spending leads to:","options":{"A":"No change in GDP if the economy is already operating at full capacity.","B":"A leftward supply shift, because a higher price is treated as a change in one of the non-price determinants of supply","C":"A smaller increase in GDP due to the dampening effect of inflation.","D":"A larger final increase in GDP as each spending round generates income for further spending."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-TECH-05","scaffold":"Multiplier: initial injection (e.g. £1bn G) → becomes income → recipients spend a fraction (MPC) → that spending becomes someone else's income → repeat. Final ΔY = injection × multiplier. Multiplier = 1/(1−MPC) or 1/MPW. The effect is larger than the initial injection because of successive rounds of re-spending.","reforge":{"stem":"MPC = 0.8. The government injects £5bn. What is the final increase in national income?","options":{"A":"£5bn","B":"£25bn (multiplier = 1/(1−0.8) = 5; 5 × £5bn = £25bn)","C":"£4bn","D":"An equal and proportional increase in GDP matching the initial spending."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"AD-09","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"Which component typically accounts for the largest share of UK aggregate demand?","options":{"A":"Consumption, at roughly 60 to 65 per cent of aggregate demand","B":"Investment by firms, at roughly 30 per cent of aggregate demand","C":"Government spending, at roughly 40 per cent of aggregate demand","D":"Net exports, which contribute around 25 per cent of the total"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-AD-01","scaffold":"AD = C + I + G + (X−M). In the UK, consumption is around 60-65%, government spending roughly 20-25%, investment about 17%, and net exports are usually slightly negative because the UK runs a trade deficit. Knowing the rough proportions matters for evaluation: because consumption dominates, policies affecting household disposable income and confidence have the largest effect on AD, which is why interest rate changes work substantially through mortgage holders. Investment is the smallest of the domestic components but by far the most volatile, so it drives much of the trade cycle even though its share is modest.","reforge":{"stem":"Which component of AD is typically the most volatile over the trade cycle?","options":{"A":"Consumption, because household incomes change constantly","B":"Government spending, because departmental budgets are revised each year","C":"Investment, because it depends heavily on business confidence","D":"Imports, because exchange rates move on a daily basis"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"AD-10","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"What does the marginal propensity to consume (MPC) measure?","options":{"A":"The total proportion of all household income that is spent on goods","B":"The proportion of any additional income that households choose to spend","C":"The proportion of national income taken by the government in taxation","D":"The rate at which household savings accumulate over a given time period"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-AD-08","scaffold":"The MPC is the fraction of each extra pound of income that is spent rather than saved, and it must be distinguished from the average propensity to consume, which is total spending divided by total income. The MPC drives the multiplier, since the multiplier equals 1 ÷ (1 − MPC), or equivalently 1 ÷ MPW where the marginal propensity to withdraw is MPS + MPT + MPM. Lower-income households have a higher MPC because they must spend most of what they receive, which is why targeting fiscal stimulus at poorer households produces a larger multiplier effect — a genuinely useful evaluation point on the design of fiscal policy.","reforge":{"stem":"MPS is 0.15, MPT is 0.2 and MPM is 0.15. What is the multiplier?","options":{"A":"3.3","B":"5.0","C":"1.5","D":"2.0"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"AD-11","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"A rise in house prices is most likely to increase consumption through:","options":{"A":"The accelerator effect, as firms respond by raising planned investment","B":"A fall in the general price level increasing real disposable incomes","C":"The wealth effect, as households feel richer and save a smaller share","D":"An automatic reduction in the rate of interest set by the central bank"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-AD-03","scaffold":"The wealth effect links asset values to spending: when house prices or share prices rise, households feel wealthier, save a smaller proportion of income, and some remortgage to release equity. Because housing is the dominant asset for most UK households, house prices have an unusually strong influence on UK consumption. Note the evaluation — the effect is asymmetric and unequal. Falling house prices produce negative equity and a sharp retrenchment in spending, and the gains flow to existing owners rather than to renters or first-time buyers, so the wealth effect can widen inequality while boosting AD.","reforge":{"stem":"Which other factor most directly influences consumption alongside income?","options":{"A":"Consumer confidence about future income and job security","B":"The size of the government's outstanding national debt","C":"The level of corporation tax that is charged on all company profits","D":"The volume of goods currently being exported overseas"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AD-12","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"The accelerator effect describes the relationship between:","options":{"A":"Government spending and the resulting change in total tax revenue","B":"Interest rates and the level of household saving in the economy","C":"The exchange rate and the volume of exports sold overseas","D":"The rate of change of national income and the level of investment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-AD-08","scaffold":"The accelerator holds that investment depends not on the level of output but on its rate of change. If demand is growing, firms must add capital to expand capacity; if growth merely slows — even while output is still rising — firms need no new capacity and investment can fall sharply. This is why investment is so much more volatile than GDP, and why it amplifies the trade cycle. The accelerator and the multiplier interact: an initial rise in AD raises income through the multiplier, which raises investment through the accelerator, which raises AD again. Together they explain the momentum of booms and the severity of slumps.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the accelerator make investment more volatile than output?","options":{"A":"Investment is always a fixed proportion of the total national output","B":"Investment responds to the rate of change of demand, not its level","C":"Firms invest only when interest rates are at historic lows","D":"Investment decisions are made entirely by the government"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"AD-13","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"Which factor would be most likely to increase business investment?","options":{"A":"Improved business confidence about expected future demand conditions","B":"An increase in the level of spare capacity across most industries","C":"A rise in the rate of corporation tax charged on all company profits","D":"An unexpected rise in interest rates set by the Monetary Policy Committee"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-AD-03","scaffold":"Investment determinants worth listing: interest rates, since borrowing cost and the opportunity cost of retained profit both matter; business confidence or 'animal spirits', which Keynes stressed and which is often decisive; expected demand, via the accelerator; corporation tax and investment allowances, which affect post-tax returns; the level of spare capacity, since firms with idle machinery have no reason to buy more; and technological change creating new opportunities. Confidence deserves emphasis in evaluation because it explains why very low interest rates after 2009 still failed to generate strong investment — cheap borrowing is no use if firms expect weak demand.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did very low interest rates after 2009 fail to generate strong investment?","options":{"A":"Corporation tax rates were raised sharply at the same time","B":"Firms were legally prevented from borrowing at low rates","C":"Weak expected demand and low confidence deterred firms","D":"Investment does not respond to interest rates in any way"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"AD-14","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"A depreciation of sterling affects aggregate demand primarily by:","options":{"A":"Reducing consumption because imported goods have become cheaper to buy","B":"Raising net exports as exports become cheaper and imports become dearer","C":"Lowering government spending on all overseas aid and defence commitments","D":"Cutting investment because the cost of borrowing has automatically risen"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-AD-05","scaffold":"A depreciation lowers the foreign-currency price of exports and raises the domestic price of imports, so export volumes rise and import volumes fall — net exports increase and AD shifts right. Use SPICED to keep the direction straight: Strong Pound, Imports Cheap, Exports Dear, and the reverse for a weak pound. Two evaluations matter. The size of the effect depends on elasticities, so the Marshall-Lerner condition must hold and the J-curve means the improvement is delayed. And a depreciation raises the cost of imported raw materials and energy, shifting SRAS left and generating cost-push inflation, so the net effect on output is ambiguous.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a negative consequence of a sterling depreciation?","options":{"A":"Export volumes fall as UK goods become more expensive abroad","B":"The current account deficit widens immediately and permanently","C":"Interest rates must automatically be reduced by the central bank","D":"Imported raw materials cost more, causing cost-push inflation"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"AD-15","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"Which of these represents a movement along the AD curve rather than a shift?","options":{"A":"A rise in consumer confidence leading households to spend a much greater share","B":"An increase in government spending on infrastructure across the country","C":"A change in the general price level altering the quantity of output demanded","D":"A cut in income tax rates increasing household disposable income levels"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-AD-06","scaffold":"The AD curve plots the price level against real output, so only a change in the price level itself causes a movement along it. Everything else shifts the curve. The AD curve slopes downward for three reasons worth naming: the real balance effect, where a lower price level raises the real value of money holdings; the interest rate effect, where a lower price level reduces money demand and interest rates, encouraging investment; and the international trade effect, where a lower domestic price level makes exports more competitive. The other three options here all change a component of C, I, G or net exports, so all shift AD.","reforge":{"stem":"Which explains why the AD curve slopes downwards?","options":{"A":"A lower price level raises the real value of money balances","B":"Firms produce less output when the price level falls","C":"Government spending rises whenever prices are falling","D":"Higher domestic prices always increase a country's export volumes"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AD-16","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"An economy has an MPC of 0.8. The government increases spending by £5bn. Assuming no other withdrawals, the eventual rise in national income is:","options":{"A":"£4bn, because some of the initial spending leaks abroad as imports","B":"£5bn, because government spending has no multiplied effect at all","C":"£40bn, calculated by multiplying the injection by the MPC of 0.8","D":"£25bn, since the multiplier is 1 divided by 0.2, which equals five"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-AD-08","scaffold":"The multiplier is 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5, so £5bn × 5 = £25bn. The mechanism is that one person's spending is another's income, which is partly spent again, and so on in a converging series. In reality the multiplier is smaller than this simple version suggests because income also leaks into saving, taxation and imports — so use 1 ÷ MPW where MPW = MPS + MPT + MPM. UK estimates are typically between 0.5 and 1.5, well below textbook values. The multiplier is larger when there is spare capacity, since extra demand raises output rather than prices.","reforge":{"stem":"When is the multiplier effect likely to be largest?","options":{"A":"When the economy is operating at full productive capacity","B":"When there is substantial spare capacity in the economy","C":"When the marginal propensity to import is unusually high","D":"When income tax rates have just been increased sharply"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"AD-17","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"Which would shift the AD curve to the left?","options":{"A":"A rise in interest rates reducing both consumption and investment","B":"A large increase in public sector investment in road and rail networks","C":"An increase in the general level of consumer and business confidence","D":"A fall in the rate of income tax paid by most working households"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-AD-03","scaffold":"AD shifts left when any component falls. Higher interest rates hit two components at once: consumption falls as borrowing costs rise, saving becomes more attractive and mortgage holders have less disposable income; investment falls as projects become less viable. There is a third channel — higher rates attract capital inflows, appreciating the currency and reducing net exports — so monetary tightening works through C, I and net exports together. The other three options here all shift AD right. When answering, always identify which component of C + I + G + (X−M) is affected and in which direction before drawing the diagram.","reforge":{"stem":"Through which three components does a rise in interest rates reduce AD?","options":{"A":"Consumption, government spending and imports","B":"Government spending, exports and imports","C":"Consumption, investment and net exports","D":"Investment, government spending and exports"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"AD-18","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"Why might a large fiscal stimulus produce a smaller rise in national income than the simple multiplier predicts?","options":{"A":"Because government spending is never counted within aggregate demand","B":"Because leakages into saving, taxation and imports reduce the multiplier's size","C":"Because the marginal propensity to consume is always far greater than one whole","D":"Because fiscal policy affects only the supply side of the economy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-AD-08","scaffold":"The simple multiplier assumes the only leakage is saving. In an open economy with a government, income also leaks into taxation and imports, so the realistic multiplier is 1 ÷ (MPS + MPT + MPM), which is considerably smaller. Add three further reasons the effect disappoints: crowding out, if government borrowing raises interest rates and displaces private investment; the economy operating near full capacity, so extra demand raises prices rather than output; and time lags in recognising, implementing and transmitting the policy. Together these explain why real-world UK multiplier estimates cluster around 0.5 to 1.5 rather than 4 or 5.","reforge":{"stem":"Which additional factor reduces the effectiveness of a fiscal stimulus?","options":{"A":"An unusually large amount of spare capacity in the economy","B":"A reduction in the rate of income tax at the same time","C":"A very low marginal propensity to import in the economy","D":"Crowding out of private investment by government borrowing"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"AD-19","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"Government spending that forms part of aggregate demand excludes:","options":{"A":"Public sector salaries paid to teachers, nurses and civil servants","B":"Capital investment in new hospitals, schools and transport links","C":"Transfer payments such as pensions and unemployment benefits","D":"Purchases of equipment and supplies by government departments"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-AD-01","scaffold":"The G in AD covers government consumption and investment — actual purchases of goods and services, and the salaries of public sector workers who provide them. Transfer payments are excluded because no good or service is produced in exchange; the government is simply moving purchasing power from one household to another. That said, transfers do affect AD indirectly through the C component, and they do so powerfully, because recipients typically have a high marginal propensity to consume. This is a subtle distinction examiners test, and it explains why welfare spending can be an effective stimulus even though it is not itself part of G.","reforge":{"stem":"How do transfer payments still affect aggregate demand?","options":{"A":"They raise consumption because recipients have a high MPC","B":"They have no effect on aggregate demand under any circumstances","C":"They reduce aggregate demand by increasing the tax burden","D":"They are added directly to the G component of the formula"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AD-20","spec":"2.2.1","stem":"A fall in the savings ratio, other things being equal, will:","options":{"A":"Shift the long-run aggregate supply curve to the left immediately","B":"Reduce investment because banks will have fewer deposits available to lend","C":"Have no effect on aggregate demand since saving is not a component","D":"Increase consumption and shift the aggregate demand curve to the right"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-AD-03","scaffold":"The savings ratio is the proportion of disposable income saved rather than spent, so a fall means households are consuming a greater share and AD shifts right. The ratio moves with confidence: during the 2020 lockdowns it spiked as spending opportunities vanished and precaution rose, then fell back as restrictions lifted, which explains much of the subsequent demand surge. Evaluate with the paradox of thrift — an individual saving more is prudent, but if everyone does so simultaneously, AD falls, incomes fall and total saving may end up lower. Option B has some long-run force but is not the main short-run effect.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the paradox of thrift describe?","options":{"A":"Saving always increases investment and long-run economic growth","B":"Higher saving by all households reduces income and total saving","C":"Households save more when interest rates are reduced to zero","D":"The savings ratio has no relationship with consumer confidence"},"correct":"B"}}]},"2.2.2":{"label":"Aggregate Supply","color":"#ca8a04","questions":[{"id":"AS-01","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"What is the key difference between SRAS and LRAS?","options":{"A":"SRAS reflects current costs of production (and slopes upward)","B":"Because prices and wages are assumed to be completely rigid downwards","C":"SRAS is always vertical; LRAS always slopes upward.","D":"There is no difference — they are the same curve."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-AS-01","scaffold":"SRAS slopes upward: as the price level rises, firms supply more because revenues rise relative to (sticky) costs. LRAS is vertical at potential output (classical view): in the long run, output is determined by factors of production and technology, not the price level.","reforge":{"stem":"In the Keynesian model, what shape is the AS curve?","options":{"A":"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal.","B":"Always vertical.","C":"SRAS is flat with spare capacity, then steepens and becomes vertical at full capacity.","D":"A straight 45° line."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"AS-02","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"Which of the following would shift SRAS to the LEFT?","options":{"A":"A fall in oil prices.","B":"A rise in the national minimum wage increasing firms' costs.","C":"An increase in labour productivity.","D":"AD shifts right — firms pass costs to consumers who spend more."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-AS-02","scaffold":"SRAS shifts left when production costs rise: higher wages, higher raw material/energy costs, higher indirect taxes, exchange rate depreciation raising import costs. SRAS shifts right when costs fall. Think: anything that makes it more expensive for firms to produce at every price level.","reforge":{"stem":"The government cuts employers' national insurance contributions. What happens to SRAS?","options":{"A":"Higher supply and a lower price, because farmers respond to crop losses by planting and harvesting extra potatoes immediately","B":"No effect — NI is paid by workers.","C":"LRAS shifts right instead.","D":"Shifts right — lower labour costs for firms mean they can supply more at every price level."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"AS-03","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"A student labels the vertical axis on an AD/AS diagram as 'price.' Why does this lose marks?","options":{"A":"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal.","B":"The axes should be reversed: quantity on vertical, price on horizontal.","C":"The labels are already correct — no change needed.","D":"Economics diagrams do not require labelled axes in exam answers."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-TECH-04","scaffold":"AD/AS diagrams show the GENERAL price level (an index of all prices, like CPI) on the y-axis and real GDP/output on the x-axis. 'Price' = one good. 'Price level' = economy-wide average. Examiners consistently flag this as a diagram error.","reforge":{"stem":"Which pair of axis labels is correct for an AD/AS diagram?","options":{"A":"Price level (y-axis), Real GDP (x-axis).","B":"Inflation rate (y-axis), Nominal GDP (x-axis).","C":"CPI (y-axis), Output (x-axis).","D":"Price (y-axis), Quantity (x-axis)."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AS-04","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"Which of the following would shift LRAS to the RIGHT?","options":{"A":"A cut in interest rates.","B":"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal.","C":"A rise in consumer confidence.","D":"Investment in education and training improving the quality of the labour force."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-AS-04","scaffold":"LRAS shifts right when productive capacity increases: better education/skills (human capital), new technology, more capital stock (investment), discovery of resources, institutional improvements (property rights, deregulation). Demand-side changes (interest rates, confidence, G) shift AD, not LRAS.","reforge":{"stem":"Which combination would shift BOTH SRAS right AND LRAS right?","options":{"A":"A tax cut for consumers.","B":"A technological breakthrough that reduces production costs AND increases potential output.","C":"The highest price firms can charge before every single consumer refuses to buy any of the good at all","D":"A rise in the exchange rate."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"AS-05","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"Explain the difference between a movement along SRAS and a shift of SRAS. (2 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"A movement along SRAS is caused by a change in the price level (driven by an AD shift), as firms adjust output in response to changing revenues relative to sticky costs (knowledge). A shift of SRAS is caused by a change in production costs (e.g. oil prices, wages, indirect taxes) that alters how much firms are willing to supply at EVERY price level (analysis).","tag":"MC-AS-MOVEMENT-SHIFT","scaffold":"Movement along = price level changes (AD shifts, economy moves along existing SRAS). Shift of SRAS = costs change independently of the price level (e.g. oil shock, wage rise, tax change)."},{"id":"AS-06","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"An economy experiences rising unit labour costs due to wages growing faster than productivity. What is the effect on SRAS?","options":{"A":"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal.","B":"SRAS shifts left — higher unit costs mean firms supply less at every price level.","C":"LRAS shifts right — wages drive long-run growth.","D":"No effect on AS — wages only affect AD."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-AS-06","scaffold":"Unit labour costs = wage costs per unit of output. If wages rise faster than productivity, each unit of output costs more to produce → firms' costs increase → SRAS shifts left → inflationary pressure (cost-push). This was flagged in the 2019 examiner report on Estonia's competitiveness.","reforge":{"stem":"A country's productivity grows at 4% while wages grow at 2%. What happens to unit labour costs and SRAS?","options":{"A":"LRAS shifts left.","B":"A permanent shortage, because the price mechanism is unable to adjust quantity demanded and quantity supplied together","C":"No change — they cancel out.","D":"Unit costs fall (productivity outpaces wages), SRAS shifts right — firms can produce more cheaply."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"AS-07","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"A student draws SRAS as a vertical line. When is this appropriate?","options":{"A":"Always — SRAS is vertical like LRAS.","B":"Because governments will always intervene to maintain full employment output","C":"Only in the classical/monetarist model where all markets clear instantly","D":"Only during a recession."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-AS-01","scaffold":"Classical model: both SRAS and LRAS are vertical — prices and wages adjust instantly, so output is always at potential. Keynesian/standard model: SRAS slopes upward (sticky wages/prices in the short run). For Edexcel A-level, draw SRAS as upward-sloping unless specifically discussing the classical model.","reforge":{"stem":"In the Keynesian model, why does SRAS slope upward rather than being vertical?","options":{"A":"Because wages and other input costs are 'sticky' in the short run — they don't adjust immediately to price changes, so rising prices increase profit margins and incentivise higher output.","B":"Because the government controls output.","C":"Because firms always produce more when prices rise. The economic explanation must account for incentives, trade-offs, market conditions, distribution and the time period; the relevant relationship is reversed, so the proposed answer cannot explain the outcome.","D":"Because LRAS determines the slope of SRAS."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AS-08","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"The government introduces a carbon tax on all manufacturing. Which curves are affected and in which direction?","options":{"A":"Price is necessarily above equilibrium, since any imbalance between buyers and sellers only ever occurs when price is too high","B":"LRAS shifts left — productive capacity falls immediately.","C":"AD and SRAS both shift right.","D":"SRAS shifts left — the tax raises production costs; LRAS is unaffected in the short run."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-AS-02","scaffold":"A carbon tax raises firms' costs → SRAS shifts left (cost-push). LRAS isn't immediately affected unless the tax causes permanent capacity reduction. AD isn't directly shifted (though the resulting price rise may reduce real incomes and feed back into lower C). The primary, first-round effect is SRAS left.","reforge":{"stem":"The government removes VAT on energy bills. First-round effect?","options":{"A":"AD shifts right.","B":"SRAS shifts right — lower indirect tax reduces firms'/households' energy costs.","C":"SRAS shifts left.","D":"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"AS-09","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"On a Keynesian AS curve, the horizontal section represents an economy where:","options":{"A":"There is substantial spare capacity, so output rises without raising prices","B":"All resources are fully employed and output cannot rise any further","C":"The price level falls continuously as real national output increases","D":"Supply-side policy has permanently removed all unemployment from the economy"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-AS-07","scaffold":"The Keynesian AS curve has three sections and each carries a different policy implication. The horizontal section applies in deep recession with mass unemployment: firms can hire idle workers and machines, so raising AD increases output with no inflationary pressure — this is the strongest case for fiscal stimulus. The upward-sloping middle section shows bottlenecks emerging as capacity tightens, so demand raises both output and prices. The vertical section is full capacity, where extra demand is purely inflationary. Contrast this with the classical view, where LRAS is vertical at all times, so demand management can never raise output in the long run.","reforge":{"stem":"On the vertical section of the Keynesian AS curve, an increase in AD causes:","options":{"A":"An increase in real output with no change in the price level","B":"A fall in the price level and a rise in employment levels","C":"A rise in the price level with no increase in real output","D":"A leftward shift of the long-run aggregate supply curve"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"AS-10","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"Which best explains why the classical LRAS curve is drawn as vertical?","options":{"A":"Because prices and wages are assumed to be completely rigid downwards","B":"Because output at full employment is set by factor supply and productivity","C":"Because aggregate demand has no influence on the general price level","D":"Because governments will always intervene to maintain full employment output"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-AS-01","scaffold":"The classical position holds that in the long run the economy always returns to full employment output, determined entirely by the quantity and quality of factors of production — land, labour, capital and enterprise — together with productivity and technology. Because output is supply-determined, shifting AD changes only the price level, so demand management is inflationary and futile in the long run. This assumes wages and prices are flexible, so any unemployment is temporary. The policy conclusion is that only supply-side policies, which shift LRAS right, can raise output permanently. Keynesians dispute the flexibility assumption, arguing wages are sticky downwards.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the policy implication of a vertical LRAS curve?","options":{"A":"Demand-side policy can permanently raise the level of real output","B":"Inflation can never occur when aggregate demand rises","C":"Governments should always run a large budget deficit","D":"Only supply-side policy can permanently increase real output"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"AS-11","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"A sharp rise in world oil prices will most directly:","options":{"A":"Shift AD right, increasing both real output and the general price level","B":"Shift SRAS right, lowering the price level and raising real output","C":"Shift SRAS left, raising the price level and reducing real output","D":"Shift LRAS right by encouraging investment in energy efficiency measures"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-AS-02","scaffold":"Oil is an input to almost all production and distribution, so a price spike raises firms' costs across the economy, shifting SRAS left. The result is stagflation: a higher price level combined with lower real output and rising unemployment, which is the uncomfortable outcome because standard demand management cannot fix both at once. Tightening policy to control inflation deepens the output loss; loosening to protect output entrenches the inflation. The 1973 and 1979 oil shocks and the 2022 energy crisis are the standard applications. In the long run, high energy prices may induce efficiency investment that shifts LRAS right, which is option D's grain of truth.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a leftward shift of SRAS particularly difficult for policymakers?","options":{"A":"It causes inflation and lower output at the same time","B":"It has no effect on the general level of prices","C":"It raises both real output and employment levels simultaneously","D":"It can only be corrected by raising interest rates"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AS-12","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"Which would shift the long-run aggregate supply curve to the right?","options":{"A":"A temporary reduction in the standard rate of value added tax","B":"An expansion of the money supply through quantitative easing measures","C":"A rise in consumer confidence encouraging households to spend more","D":"An increase in net inward migration of skilled working-age people"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-AS-04","scaffold":"LRAS shifts right only when productive capacity expands, which requires more or better factors of production. The drivers are: more labour, through migration, higher participation or population growth; more capital, through net investment; better human capital, through education and training; technological advance; improved infrastructure; and greater efficiency from competition or deregulation. The other three options here all affect AD rather than capacity. This distinction is the single most common source of lost marks in Theme 2 — a policy that raises spending is demand-side even if it feels productive, unless it demonstrably increases what the economy can produce.","reforge":{"stem":"Which government policy would most clearly shift LRAS to the right?","options":{"A":"A one-off cash payment made to every household","B":"Sustained investment in vocational education and training","C":"A temporary increase in the level of unemployment benefit rates","D":"A reduction in interest rates by the central bank"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"AS-13","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"Rising labour productivity will, other things being equal:","options":{"A":"Reduce unit labour costs, shifting SRAS right and easing inflationary pressure","B":"Shift AD left because higher productivity reduces total employment levels","C":"Shift SRAS left because firms must pay their workers higher wage rates","D":"Have no effect on aggregate supply because productivity is a demand-side factor"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-AS-06","scaffold":"Unit labour cost is the wage cost per unit of output, so it depends on wages relative to productivity. If wages rise 4% while productivity rises 4%, unit costs are unchanged and there is no inflationary pressure — this is why the Bank of England watches the gap between wage growth and productivity growth so closely. Rising productivity lowers unit costs, shifting SRAS right, which raises output and lowers the price level simultaneously. Over the long run productivity growth also shifts LRAS right, so it is the one variable that delivers non-inflationary growth. The UK's post-2008 productivity stagnation is therefore central to explaining both weak growth and stagnant real wages.","reforge":{"stem":"Wages rise 6% while productivity rises 2%. What is the likely consequence?","options":{"A":"Unit labour costs fall, shifting SRAS to the right","B":"There is no change at all to the firms' overall costs of production","C":"Unit labour costs rise, creating cost-push inflationary pressure","D":"The LRAS curve shifts immediately to the right"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"AS-14","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"An increase in the national minimum wage would most likely:","options":{"A":"Shift LRAS right by immediately increasing the total supply of labour","B":"Shift SRAS left by raising labour costs for firms employing low-paid staff","C":"Shift AD left because households will have less disposable income to spend","D":"Have no macroeconomic effect because wages are a transfer between groups"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-AS-02","scaffold":"A higher minimum wage raises costs for firms with many low-paid workers, shifting SRAS left and putting upward pressure on prices. But a strong answer notes the offsetting effects. On the demand side, low-paid workers have a high MPC, so higher wages raise consumption and shift AD right. Firms may absorb the cost through lower profits or respond by raising productivity through better training and capital investment, which would limit or reverse the SRAS effect. Empirically the UK's minimum wage since 1999 has had modest measured effects on employment and prices, so the theoretical prediction should be stated with appropriate caution.","reforge":{"stem":"How might firms respond to higher wage costs in a way that offsets the SRAS effect?","options":{"A":"By reducing the prices that they charge to all of their own customers","B":"By reducing the amount of output they sell overseas","C":"By increasing the number of low-paid staff they employ","D":"By raising productivity through training and capital investment"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"AS-15","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"On an AD/AS diagram, a rightward shift of LRAS with AD unchanged results in:","options":{"A":"Higher real output and a higher price level in the new long-run equilibrium","B":"Lower real output combined with a higher general price level than before","C":"Higher real output and a lower price level in the new long-run equilibrium","D":"Unchanged real output with a lower price level in the new equilibrium"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-AS-04","scaffold":"When capacity expands and demand is unchanged, the economy can produce more and competitive pressure pushes prices down — output rises and the price level falls. This is non-inflationary or supply-side growth, and it is why supply-side policy is presented as superior to demand management: it delivers growth without the inflation trade-off. Contrast the alternative: AD shifting right along an upward-sloping SRAS raises output but also raises prices, and if the economy is at full capacity it raises prices alone. Always label both the output and price level changes on your diagram, because examiners award marks for identifying both consequences.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is supply-side growth described as non-inflationary?","options":{"A":"Output rises while the price level falls or rises more slowly","B":"It has no effect on the level of real national output","C":"It requires the money supply to be sharply reduced at the same time","D":"It shifts aggregate demand leftward at the same time"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AS-16","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"Which factor affects short-run aggregate supply but not long-run aggregate supply?","options":{"A":"The overall quality of a country's education and training system","B":"The level of technology used across the whole of the economy","C":"The total quantity of capital equipment available to firms","D":"A temporary change in the world price of imported raw materials"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-AS-01","scaffold":"SRAS shifts with anything that changes firms' costs of production in the short run: wage rates, raw material and energy prices, exchange rate movements affecting import costs, indirect taxes and subsidies. These change what it costs to produce but not what the economy is capable of producing. LRAS shifts only with the quantity or quality of factors of production — capital stock, labour force size and skills, technology and infrastructure — which are the other three options here. A temporary commodity price spike raises costs now but leaves productive capacity untouched, so it moves SRAS alone.","reforge":{"stem":"Which would shift SRAS right without affecting LRAS?","options":{"A":"A major breakthrough in manufacturing technology","B":"A fall in world energy prices reducing firms' costs","C":"A large increase in the size of the labour force","D":"A sustained national programme of infrastructure investment"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"AS-17","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"Stagflation is best described as:","options":{"A":"Rising inflation combined with stagnant output and rising unemployment","B":"Falling real output alongside a falling general price level","C":"Stable real output with prices rising at exactly the official target rate","D":"Rising real output combined with a falling general price level"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-AS-02","scaffold":"Stagflation combines stagnation with inflation, and it is caused by a leftward shift of SRAS — typically a supply shock such as an energy price spike. It is difficult precisely because the two problems demand opposite remedies: contractionary policy tames inflation but deepens the recession, while expansionary policy supports output but worsens inflation. This is why the 1970s discredited simple demand management and shifted attention toward supply-side economics and monetary targets. The 2022 episode, with energy costs driving inflation above 10% while growth stalled, is the modern application and is exactly the dilemma the MPC faced.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does stagflation create a policy dilemma?","options":{"A":"Both problems require identical policy responses to solve","B":"Neither inflation nor unemployment responds to any policy","C":"Curing inflation worsens output, and supporting output worsens inflation","D":"Stagflation can only ever be solved by rapidly increasing the money supply"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"AS-18","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"A depreciation of the exchange rate affects aggregate supply by:","options":{"A":"Shifting SRAS right because exports become cheaper for overseas buyers","B":"Shifting SRAS left because imported inputs become more expensive for firms","C":"Shifting LRAS left because the country's productive capacity has fallen","D":"Leaving aggregate supply unchanged since exchange rates affect only demand"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-AS-02","scaffold":"A depreciation works on both sides of the diagram, and recognising that earns strong evaluation marks. On the demand side it raises net exports, shifting AD right. On the supply side it raises the domestic price of imported raw materials, components and energy, increasing firms' costs and shifting SRAS left. The net effect on output is therefore ambiguous while the effect on the price level is unambiguously upward — imported inflation. How the balance falls depends on how import-dependent the economy's production is: an economy assembling imported components gains far less from depreciation than one using mostly domestic inputs.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the effect of a depreciation on real output ambiguous?","options":{"A":"The price level always falls following a depreciation","B":"Both AD and SRAS shift leftward simultaneously","C":"Exchange rates have no effect on firms' production costs","D":"AD shifts right while SRAS shifts left at the same time"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"AS-19","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"An increase in corporation tax is most likely to:","options":{"A":"Shift LRAS right by raising government revenue used for public investment","B":"Shift SRAS right because firms will reduce their prices to compete","C":"Shift SRAS left and reduce investment, potentially shifting LRAS left too","D":"Shift AD right as government spending rises with the extra revenue"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-AS-04","scaffold":"Higher corporation tax reduces post-tax profits, which raises effective costs and shifts SRAS left in the short run. More importantly it reduces retained earnings and lowers the expected return on investment, so capital accumulation slows and LRAS growth is dampened over time — this is the standard supply-side argument for lower corporate taxes. The counter-argument matters for balance: the revenue funds infrastructure, education and healthcare that themselves raise productive capacity, so the net effect depends on how productively the government spends it. Evidence on the responsiveness of investment to corporation tax rates is genuinely mixed.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the counter-argument to cutting corporation tax to raise LRAS?","options":{"A":"Lost revenue may reduce public investment that also raises capacity","B":"Corporation tax has no effect on firms' retained profits","C":"Lower taxes always reduce the level of business investment","D":"Firms are legally required to reinvest all of their retained profits"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"AS-20","spec":"2.2.2","stem":"Why might an economy operate to the left of its LRAS curve for a prolonged period?","options":{"A":"Because productive capacity has permanently been destroyed by the shortfall","B":"Because supply-side policies have successfully raised productive potential","C":"Because the long-run aggregate supply curve is always upward sloping","D":"Because deficient aggregate demand leaves resources persistently underused"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-AS-07","scaffold":"Operating inside capacity means a negative output gap: unemployment above the natural rate and idle capital, caused by AD being too low. The Keynesian claim is that this can persist because wages are sticky downwards — workers and unions resist nominal pay cuts — so the labour market does not clear automatically and the economy does not self-correct quickly. That is the case for active demand management. Classical economists disagree, arguing flexible wages and prices restore full employment without intervention. Note the link to hysteresis, which is option A's grain of truth: a prolonged shortfall can eventually erode skills and capital, shifting LRAS left.","reforge":{"stem":"Which assumption underpins the Keynesian case for demand management?","options":{"A":"Wages and prices adjust instantly to clear all markets","B":"Wages are sticky downwards, so markets may not clear","C":"The economy always operates at full employment output","D":"Aggregate demand has no effect on real output at all"},"correct":"B"}}]},"2.2.3":{"label":"National Income","color":"#6366f1","questions":[{"id":"NI-01","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"In the circular flow model, which of the following is an injection?","options":{"A":"Investment","B":"Increase national income — savings fund investment.","C":"Savings","D":"Imports"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-NI-01","scaffold":"Injections ADD spending to the circular flow: Investment (I), Government spending (G), Exports (X). Withdrawals REMOVE spending: Savings (S), Taxation (T), Imports (M). The economy is in equilibrium when total injections = total withdrawals (I + G + X = S + T + M).","reforge":{"stem":"For national income to be in equilibrium:","options":{"A":"AD must exceed AS.","B":"Increase the multiplier, because more spending circulates within the economy","C":"Total injections (I + G + X) must equal total withdrawals (S + T + M).","D":"Savings must be zero."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"NI-02","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"The multiplier is 4. The government increases spending by £3bn. What is the final increase in national income?","options":{"A":"An equal and proportional increase in GDP matching the initial spending.","B":"No change in GDP if the economy is already operating at full capacity.","C":"A larger final increase in GDP as each spending round generates income for further spending.","D":"A smaller increase in GDP due to the dampening effect of inflation."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-TECH-05","scaffold":"Final ΔY = multiplier × initial injection = 4 × £3bn = £12bn. The multiplier amplifies the initial injection through successive rounds of re-spending.","reforge":{"stem":"MPT = 0.1, MPS = 0.15, MPM = 0.25. What is the multiplier?","options":{"A":"10","B":"4","C":"A leftward supply shift, because a higher price is treated as a change in one of the non-price determinants of supply","D":"2 (multiplier = 1/MPW = 1/(0.1+0.15+0.25) = 1/0.5 = 2)"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"NI-03","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"A student says: 'GDP and GNI are the same thing.' What is the key difference?","options":{"A":"There is no difference.","B":"Lower than its GDP, because overseas earnings are excluded from the total","C":"GDP measures output produced within a country's borders","D":"GNI only measures government income."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-NI-03","scaffold":"GDP = output produced within borders (regardless of who owns the factors). GNI = GDP + net primary income from abroad (income UK residents earn overseas minus income foreign residents earn in the UK). For countries with large overseas investments (e.g. UK), GNI can differ significantly from GDP.","reforge":{"stem":"A country has many citizens working abroad who send remittances home, but few foreign workers within its borders. Which is likely larger?","options":{"A":"GNI is higher because net income from abroad is positive.","B":"Identical to its GDP, since the two measures always give the same figure","C":"They must be equal.","D":"Neither — remittances aren't counted."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"NI-04","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"What is the difference between nominal GDP and real GDP?","options":{"A":"They are calculated using different formulas for AD.","B":"Nominal GDP is per person; real GDP is total.","C":"Real GDP includes imports; nominal does not.","D":"Nominal GDP is measured in current prices"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-NI-04","scaffold":"Nominal GDP can rise simply because prices rose (inflation), even if actual output is unchanged. Real GDP strips out inflation by using a base-year price level, isolating genuine changes in output. Always use real GDP when comparing across time periods.","reforge":{"stem":"Nominal GDP rises from £2tn to £2.1tn (5%) while inflation is 4%. What is approximate real GDP growth?","options":{"A":"Risen by approximately 10%, since the two rates are added together","B":"Approximately 1% — real growth = nominal growth minus inflation.","C":"9%","D":"4%"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"NI-05","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Which of the following would increase the size of the multiplier?","options":{"A":"No change in GDP if the economy is already operating at full capacity.","B":"A smaller increase in GDP due to the dampening effect of inflation.","C":"An equal and proportional increase in GDP matching the initial spending.","D":"A larger final increase in GDP as each spending round generates income for further spending."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-TECH-05","scaffold":"Multiplier = 1/MPW where MPW = MPS + MPT + MPM. A higher MPC means a lower MPS (since MPC + MPS = 1 in a simple model), so MPW falls and the multiplier rises. Anything that increases withdrawals (higher tax, saving, or imports) reduces the multiplier.","reforge":{"stem":"A government raises income tax rates. What happens to the multiplier and why?","options":{"A":"A leftward supply shift, because a higher price is treated as a change in one of the non-price determinants of supply","B":"No change — tax doesn't affect the multiplier.","C":"The multiplier falls because higher MPT means more income is withdrawn rather than respent.","D":"Multiplier rises — higher taxes reduce inflation."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"NI-06","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Explain one limitation of using GDP to compare living standards between countries. (2 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"GDP does not account for differences in the cost of living between countries (knowledge). A country with a high GDP per capita may have very high prices, meaning residents' purchasing power is lower than the headline figure suggests — this is why economists use PPP (Purchasing Power Parity) adjusted figures for international comparisons (analysis).","tag":"MC-NI-06","scaffold":"Limitations: doesn't account for PPP/cost of living differences, ignores income distribution, excludes the informal/shadow economy, ignores environmental degradation, doesn't capture non-market activity (household work), and says nothing about quality of life or leisure."},{"id":"NI-07","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"In the circular flow, a rise in the savings ratio (MPS) would, other things equal:","options":{"A":"Increase national income — savings fund investment.","B":"Have no effect — savings always equal investment.","C":"Decrease national income in the short run","D":"Increase exports."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-NI-01","scaffold":"Higher MPS → more income is withdrawn as savings → less re-spent each round → smaller multiplier → national income falls (paradox of thrift in the short run). Note: in the long run, higher savings COULD fund investment, but the immediate circular-flow effect is contractionary.","reforge":{"stem":"The 'paradox of thrift' suggests that:","options":{"A":"If everyone tries to save more simultaneously, total spending falls, reducing national income — potentially leaving everyone worse off and unable to save as much as intended.","B":"Thrift only matters in developing countries.","C":"Higher savings always lead to higher investment immediately, since firms automatically borrow every pound households save regardless of business confidence, interest rates or the state of demand.","D":"Saving is always good for the economy."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"NI-08","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Which of the three methods of measuring GDP counts the value added at each stage of production?","options":{"A":"The income method.","B":"The employment method.","C":"The expenditure method, which totals all spending on final goods and services","D":"The output (production) method."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-NI-08","scaffold":"Three methods, all yielding the same GDP figure: (1) Output/production — sum of value added by each industry (avoids double counting). (2) Income — sum of all factor incomes (wages, profits, rent, interest). (3) Expenditure — sum of all final spending (C + I + G + X − M). Value added = output minus intermediate inputs.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the output method use 'value added' rather than total sales revenue at each stage?","options":{"A":"Sales revenue is confidential.","B":"Total revenue would double-count intermediate goods at different production stages.","C":"Value added is always larger than revenue.","D":"A larger final increase in GDP as each spending round generates income for further spending."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"NI-09","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"In the circular flow of income, which of the following is a withdrawal?","options":{"A":"Saving by households placed into bank and building society accounts","B":"Government spending on public services such as health and education","C":"Investment spending by firms on new machinery and buildings","D":"Export earnings received from customers based in overseas markets"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-NI-01","scaffold":"The circular flow has three injections — investment, government spending and exports — and three withdrawals: saving, taxation and imports. Injections add spending to the domestic economy; withdrawals remove it. The economy is in equilibrium when total injections equal total withdrawals, so J = W. If injections exceed withdrawals, national income expands through the multiplier; if withdrawals exceed injections, it contracts. Remember the pairing for the exam: S, T and M leak out, while I, G and X flow in. Confusing saving with investment is the classic error, since saving is a withdrawal while investment is an injection even though banks channel one into the other.","reforge":{"stem":"An economy has injections of £80bn and withdrawals of £95bn. What follows?","options":{"A":"The economy is already in a state of equilibrium","B":"National income will expand through the multiplier effect","C":"National income will contract until the two are equal","D":"The price level will rise but output will stay constant"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"NI-10","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Which method of calculating GDP sums wages, rent, interest and profit?","options":{"A":"The expenditure method, which totals all spending on final goods and services","B":"The income method, which totals the payments made to factors of production","C":"The output method, which totals the value added at each stage of production","D":"The deflator method, which adjusts nominal figures for the rate of inflation"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-NI-08","scaffold":"There are three routes to the same total, and in principle they give identical answers because every pound of output generates an equal pound of income and is bought by an equal pound of spending. The income method sums factor rewards: wages for labour, rent for land, interest for capital and profit for enterprise — transfer payments are excluded because no output corresponds to them. The expenditure method sums C + I + G + (X−M). The output method sums value added at each stage, which avoids double counting. In practice measurement error means the three differ slightly, so the ONS publishes a balanced estimate.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the output method sum value added rather than total sales?","options":{"A":"Because sales figures are never available to statisticians","B":"Because intermediate goods are not produced within the economy","C":"To exclude the profits earned by firms from the calculation","D":"To avoid double counting intermediate goods in the total"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"NI-11","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Nominal GDP rises by 6% while inflation is 4%. Real GDP has therefore:","options":{"A":"Risen by approximately 10%, since the two rates are added together","B":"Fallen by approximately 2%, because inflation exceeded real growth","C":"Risen by approximately 2%, once the effect of inflation is removed","D":"Remained unchanged, as nominal and real measures always move together"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-NI-04","scaffold":"Real GDP strips out price changes to show the genuine change in the volume of output, so real growth is approximately nominal growth minus inflation: 6% − 4% = 2%. This distinction is fundamental because nominal figures can rise purely through inflation while the economy produces no more than before — during high inflation, nominal GDP can grow strongly while real GDP shrinks. The conversion uses the GDP deflator, a price index covering all domestically produced output rather than the fixed consumer basket used for CPI. Always check whether data in a question are nominal or real before drawing any conclusion about growth.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the GDP deflator measure?","options":{"A":"Prices across all domestically produced output","B":"The change in the volume of goods that are exported","C":"The prices of a fixed basket of consumer goods only","D":"The difference between GDP and gross national income"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"NI-12","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"A country has a large number of citizens working abroad and sending money home. Its GNI will be:","options":{"A":"Lower than its GDP, because overseas earnings are excluded from the total","B":"Identical to its GDP, since the two measures always give the same figure","C":"Impossible to calculate without knowing the country's rate of inflation","D":"Higher than its GDP, because net income from abroad is added to output"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-NI-03","scaffold":"GDP measures output produced within a country's borders regardless of who owns the factors; GNI measures income earned by a country's residents regardless of where it is produced. GNI equals GDP plus net income from abroad — remittances, profits and interest flowing in, minus those flowing out. Countries with many workers overseas, such as the Philippines, have GNI above GDP. The reverse holds where foreign multinationals dominate production and repatriate profits: Ireland's GDP is far above its GNI for exactly this reason, which is why Ireland's own statisticians prefer modified GNI for judging living standards.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is Ireland's GDP substantially higher than its GNI?","options":{"A":"Irish workers abroad send very large remittances home","B":"Foreign multinationals produce there and repatriate profits","C":"Ireland's inflation rate is higher than that of its neighbours","D":"Ireland imports far more goods than it manages to export"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"NI-13","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Which limitation most undermines GDP per capita as a measure of living standards?","options":{"A":"It is an average that conceals how income is distributed within the population","B":"It is published quarterly rather than on a monthly basis by statisticians","C":"It is always measured in nominal rather than in real inflation-adjusted figures","D":"It cannot be compared between two different countries under any circumstances"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-NI-06","scaffold":"GDP per capita is a mean, so it says nothing about distribution — a country with high average income and extreme inequality may have widespread poverty. Build a full list for evaluation: it ignores the informal economy and unpaid work; it ignores negative externalities and resource depletion; it takes no account of leisure time, health, education or political freedom; and it ignores the composition of output, so military spending counts the same as healthcare. Alternatives address these gaps — the Human Development Index combines income with life expectancy and education, and the Gini coefficient captures distribution alongside the average.","reforge":{"stem":"Which three dimensions does the Human Development Index combine?","options":{"A":"Income, inflation and the rate of unemployment","B":"Income, inequality and environmental sustainability","C":"Income, life expectancy and educational attainment","D":"Output, investment and the balance of payments"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"NI-14","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"An increase in the marginal propensity to import will:","options":{"A":"Increase the multiplier, because more spending circulates within the economy","B":"Reduce the multiplier, because a larger share of income leaks abroad","C":"Leave the multiplier unchanged, as imports are not treated as a withdrawal","D":"Reduce national income directly without affecting the multiplier at all"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-NI-01","scaffold":"The multiplier is 1 ÷ MPW, where MPW = MPS + MPT + MPM. A higher marginal propensity to import raises total withdrawals, so the multiplier falls. This has a real policy consequence: small, very open economies have high import propensities and therefore small multipliers, so fiscal stimulus leaks abroad and boosts trading partners rather than the domestic economy. Large, relatively closed economies such as the United States retain more of the stimulus and enjoy larger multipliers. It also explains why coordinated international stimulus, as in 2009, is more effective than one country acting alone.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is fiscal stimulus less effective in a small, very open economy?","options":{"A":"Government spending is not counted in aggregate demand there","B":"Such economies always have very high rates of taxation","C":"The marginal propensity to save is always zero in small economies","D":"A large share of the stimulus leaks abroad through imports"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"NI-15","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Equilibrium in the circular flow of income occurs when:","options":{"A":"The value of total exports exactly equals the total amount of taxation","B":"Saving by households exactly equals total government spending","C":"Total injections into the economy equal total withdrawals from it","D":"National income reaches its maximum possible sustainable level"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-NI-01","scaffold":"Equilibrium requires J = W, that is I + G + X = S + T + M. The individual pairs need not match — saving need not equal investment on its own — only the totals. If injections exceed withdrawals, spending exceeds the amount leaking out, so income rises and continues rising until higher income generates enough extra withdrawals to restore balance. The reverse happens when withdrawals dominate. Note that equilibrium says nothing about desirability: the economy can settle at an equilibrium well below full employment, which is precisely Keynes's central claim and the justification for demand management.","reforge":{"stem":"What does it mean that equilibrium can occur below full employment?","options":{"A":"The economy can settle with persistent unemployment","B":"The economy always returns automatically to full capacity","C":"Injections can never equal withdrawals in a real economy","D":"Full employment output is impossible for any economy"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"NI-16","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Which would be counted within the UK's GDP?","options":{"A":"A state pension paid to a retired UK resident by the government","B":"The sale of a second-hand car by one private individual to another","C":"The purchase of shares in a company listed on the London market","D":"A haircut provided by a UK salon and paid for by a UK customer"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-NI-08","scaffold":"GDP counts the value of newly produced final goods and services in the current period. A haircut qualifies: it is a service produced now. The exclusions each have a distinct reason. Pensions and benefits are transfer payments — money is moved between households with no output produced. Second-hand goods were counted in GDP when originally produced, so counting the resale would be double counting, though any dealer's commission is a service that does count. Financial asset purchases are transfers of ownership, not production, though the broker's fee counts. Intermediate goods are excluded to avoid double counting.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is a state pension payment excluded from GDP?","options":{"A":"Pensioners do not spend the money that they receive","B":"It is a transfer payment with no output produced in exchange","C":"Pensions are paid from borrowing rather than from taxation","D":"Pension payments are counted in gross national income instead"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"NI-17","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"A negative output gap is best described as a situation where:","options":{"A":"Actual real output is below the economy's productive potential output","B":"Actual real output greatly exceeds the economy's productive potential output","C":"The rate of inflation is above the central bank's official target","D":"The government is running a deficit on its annual budget position"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-NI-01","scaffold":"A negative output gap means actual GDP is below potential GDP: there is spare capacity, unemployment sits above the natural rate, and downward pressure acts on inflation. The appropriate response is expansionary demand-side policy to close the gap. A positive gap is the opposite — actual above potential, an overheating economy with inflationary pressure, calling for contractionary policy. The persistent trap is that 'positive' sounds desirable when it actually signals overheating that cannot be sustained. Remember too that potential output is an estimate rather than an observed figure, so the size of the gap is always uncertain.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the appropriate policy response to a large negative output gap?","options":{"A":"Supply-side policy to reduce productive capacity","B":"Contractionary policy to reduce inflationary pressure","C":"Expansionary policy to raise aggregate demand","D":"No policy response, as the gap always closes itself"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"NI-18","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Why can national income statistics be difficult to compare between countries?","options":{"A":"Because all countries use entirely different definitions of a final good","B":"Because exchange rates, informal activity and data quality all differ","C":"Because national income is only ever measured once in every decade","D":"Because GDP is measured in physical units rather than in monetary values"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-NI-06","scaffold":"International comparison faces several genuine obstacles. Converting at market exchange rates distorts relative living standards, so PPP conversion is needed. The size of the informal economy varies enormously, so measured GDP understates activity more in some countries than others. Statistical capacity differs, making data less reliable in poorer countries. Population differences require per-capita figures. And the composition of output differs — a cold country spends heavily on heating, which raises GDP without raising welfare relative to a warm one. Acknowledging these limitations is exactly what converts a descriptive answer into an evaluative one.","reforge":{"stem":"Which adjustment best improves international GDP comparisons?","options":{"A":"Converting all figures using market exchange rates","B":"Using nominal rather than real GDP figures throughout the comparison","C":"Measuring total GDP rather than GDP per head","D":"Converting figures using purchasing power parity rates"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"NI-19","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"If the marginal propensity to save rises while all else stays constant, national income will:","options":{"A":"Rise, because higher saving automatically funds greater investment spending","B":"Remain unchanged, since saving is neither an injection nor a withdrawal","C":"Fall, because withdrawals increase and the size of the multiplier falls","D":"Rise, because banks will immediately reduce their interest rates in response"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-NI-01","scaffold":"A higher MPS means a larger share of each pound of income leaks out of the circular flow, so withdrawals rise and the multiplier, 1 ÷ MPW, falls. Equilibrium income therefore settles lower. This is the paradox of thrift: individually rational saving is collectively contractionary, because one household's spending is another's income. Option A reflects the classical view that saving finances investment through the loanable funds market, which has real force in the long run — but in a demand-deficient recession, extra saving depresses income rather than funding productive investment, which is why Keynes emphasised the short-run effect.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the paradox of thrift most damaging during a recession?","options":{"A":"Reduced spending deepens the fall in incomes and output","B":"Saving becomes impossible when incomes are falling","C":"Banks refuse to accept any new savings deposits","D":"Higher saving always causes interest rates to rise sharply"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"NI-20","spec":"2.2.3","stem":"Which measure would best indicate whether average living standards have improved over time?","options":{"A":"Nominal GDP, since it reflects the current market value of all of the output","B":"Total real GDP measured across the whole of the national economy","C":"The total size of the labour force employed within the economy","D":"Real GDP per capita, ideally alongside distribution and wellbeing data"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-NI-06","scaffold":"Three adjustments are needed to move from raw output to living standards. Use real rather than nominal figures, to remove inflation. Use per capita rather than total, to remove the effect of population change — total GDP can rise while individuals become worse off if population grows faster. And supplement with distribution and wellbeing measures, since an average conceals inequality and says nothing about health, education, leisure or environmental quality. The complete answer to 'have living standards risen?' is therefore real GDP per capita as the starting point, qualified by the Gini coefficient, HDI and wellbeing data.","reforge":{"stem":"Total real GDP grows 3% while population grows 3.5%. What has happened to average living standards?","options":{"A":"They have risen by approximately 3% over the period","B":"They have fallen slightly, as per-capita output has declined","C":"They have risen by approximately 6.5% in total","D":"They cannot be assessed without first knowing the rate of inflation"},"correct":"B"}}]},"2.3.1":{"label":"Fiscal Policy","color":"#b45309","questions":[{"id":"FIS-01","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"Which of the following best defines fiscal policy?","options":{"A":"Government spending and taxation used to influence aggregate demand.","B":"Supply-side policies designed to improve the productive capacity of the economy.","C":"The central bank creating money to purchase government bonds (quantitative easing).","D":"The Bank of England adjusting the base interest rate to influence borrowing."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIS-DEFINITION","scaffold":"Fiscal policy = government spending (G) and taxation (T) decisions, set by the Chancellor in the Budget. It directly affects AD (G is a component; T affects C and I). Don't confuse with monetary policy (interest rates, set by the BoE) or supply-side policies (structural reforms).","reforge":{"stem":"The Chancellor announces a £15bn increase in NHS spending funded by higher income tax. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Monetary policy.","B":"Supply-side policy.","C":"Fiscal policy — it involves changes to both government spending and taxation.","D":"This is a valid interpretation, since raising and spending revenue are the same fiscal policy tool."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"FIS-02","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"A student cannot explain the difference between automatic stabilisers and discretionary fiscal policy. Which answer correctly distinguishes them?","options":{"A":"They are the same thing — both require government decisions.","B":"Automatic stabilisers respond without a government decision; discretionary policy requires one.","C":"This is a valid interpretation, since raising and spending revenue are the same fiscal policy tool.","D":"Discretionary policy is automatic; stabilisers require decisions."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIS-STABILISERS","scaffold":"Automatic stabilisers kick in without any new policy decision. In a recession: tax receipts fall automatically (less income to tax), welfare spending rises automatically (more claimants) → this cushions the fall in AD. Discretionary = a deliberate new action: 'the Chancellor announced...' The 2022 examiner report flagged this as a question many candidates found challenging.","reforge":{"stem":"During a recession, unemployment benefit payments rise without any new legislation. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"The fiscal deficit (G − T) and current account deficit (M − X) measure different things.","B":"Supply-side policy.","C":"Monetary policy.","D":"An automatic stabiliser: welfare spending rises automatically as unemployment rises."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"FIS-03","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"A student writes about methods of 'raising tax revenue' but focuses entirely on what the government could SPEND the revenue on. What has gone wrong?","options":{"A":"The student needed to name more taxes, without discussing spending at all.","B":"Nothing — spending priorities are useful context for a tax revenue question.","C":"Off-task — the question asked about raising revenue, not spending it.","D":"This is a valid interpretation, since raising and spending revenue are the same fiscal policy tool."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIS-TAX-REVENUE","scaffold":"The 2022 examiner report explicitly flagged this: many candidates discussed what government could spend revenue on, rather than evaluating the methods of raising it. Answer the question set. Methods = which taxes, at what rates, on whom, with what economic effects (incentives, equity, Laffer curve).","reforge":{"stem":"'Evaluate methods of increasing tax revenue.' Which response is on-task?","options":{"A":"Raising top income-tax rates may increase revenue but could weaken work incentives; broader VAT could be regressive.","B":"The government could spend more on hospitals and schools.","C":"Tax revenue is not important for the economy.","D":"Water is always a luxury, purchased only when households have enough spare income left over after buying branded drinks"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"FIS-04","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"What is the difference between the fiscal (budget) deficit and the national debt?","options":{"A":"The fiscal deficit (G − T) and current account deficit (M − X) measure different things.","B":"No error — the fiscal deficit and current account deficit are the same concept.","C":"The current account only measures trade in goods, not services.","D":"The fiscal deficit cannot be expressed as a percentage of GDP."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIS-LIMITS","scaffold":"Deficit = a flow (this year's gap between spending and revenue). Debt = a stock (total accumulated over decades). Running a deficit of £100bn this year adds £100bn to the national debt. You can run a deficit while the debt-to-GDP ratio falls if GDP grows faster than borrowing. The 2019 examiner report flagged students confusing fiscal deficit with current account deficit.","reforge":{"stem":"A government runs a surplus of £10bn. What happens to the national debt?","options":{"A":"It increases by £10bn.","B":"It decreases by £10bn — the surplus is used to pay down accumulated debt.","C":"It works too quickly — the economy overheats before the policy can be reversed.","D":"It doubles."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"FIS-05","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"Evaluate whether expansionary fiscal policy is the most effective way to reduce unemployment. (12 marks — 8 KAA + 4 EV)","type":"extended_answer","model_answer_outline":{"KAA_strand_1":"Expansionary fiscal policy (higher G or lower T) → AD shifts right → firms produce more → derived demand for labour rises → unemployment falls. Multiplier amplifies the effect. Apply: UK furlough scheme during COVID or 2008 fiscal stimulus. Draw AD/AS diagram showing rightward AD shift.","KAA_strand_2":"However, fiscal policy may cause demand-pull inflation if the economy is near full capacity. Also: time lags (recognition, implementation, impact), crowding out (higher G funded by borrowing → higher interest rates → lower private I), and it only addresses cyclical unemployment — structural unemployment requires supply-side solutions.","EV_strand":"Depends on: (1) type of unemployment — fiscal policy tackles cyclical but not structural (need retraining/mobility); (2) position in the cycle — effective when there's spare capacity, inflationary near full employment; (3) alternatives — monetary policy (faster, no political lag), supply-side (addresses root causes but slow). Judgement: fiscal policy is effective for demand-deficient unemployment but must be complemented by supply-side reform for structural unemployment; no single policy is 'most effective' in all contexts."},"tag":"MC-FIS-LIMITS","scaffold":"Top-level answers need: AD/AS diagram, multiplier reference, real-world application, AND evaluation that distinguishes cyclical from structural unemployment and weighs fiscal against monetary and supply-side alternatives."},{"id":"FIS-06","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"The Laffer curve suggests that:","options":{"A":"Higher tax rates always generate more total revenue.","B":"Beyond a certain tax rate, further increases reduce total revenue as incentives to work diminish.","C":"This is a valid interpretation, since raising and spending revenue are the same fiscal policy tool.","D":"Tax revenue is independent of the rate at which income is taxed."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIS-TAX-REVENUE","scaffold":"The Laffer curve is an inverted U: at 0% tax, revenue is zero. At 100%, revenue is also zero (no one works). Somewhere in between is the revenue-maximising rate. Beyond that point, higher rates are counterproductive. The debate is about WHERE the peak is — not whether the relationship exists.","reforge":{"stem":"A country has a top income tax rate of 75% and is experiencing significant tax avoidance and brain drain. Using the Laffer curve, what might happen if it reduces the rate to 50%?","options":{"A":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make.","B":"Revenue is unchanged — rates don't matter.","C":"The Laffer curve only applies to VAT.","D":"Revenue could actually rise if the lower rate incentivises more work and reduces avoidance — the country may have been beyond the peak of the Laffer curve."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"FIS-07","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"Which of the following is a problem with using fiscal policy to manage aggregate demand?","options":{"A":"It works too quickly — the economy overheats before the policy can be reversed.","B":"Fiscal policy has no effect on AD.","C":"Time lags: recognition lag (identifying the problem)","D":"Only the Bank of England can change government spending."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIS-LIMITS","scaffold":"Three lags: (1) Recognition — takes time to confirm a recession is happening. (2) Implementation — budgets are annual; emergency measures need political approval. (3) Impact — spending takes time to feed through (contracts, hiring). By the time the stimulus hits, the economy may have already recovered → pro-cyclical rather than counter-cyclical.","reforge":{"stem":"Compared to fiscal policy, monetary policy has one key advantage in terms of timing. What is it?","options":{"A":"The MPC meets regularly and can change interest rates quickly without needing parliamentary approval, reducing the implementation lag.","B":"Monetary policy has no time lags at all.","C":"Because the lender of last resort function is never actually used in practice, since central banks have never once needed to lend emergency funds to a struggling bank throughout their entire history.","D":"Fiscal policy is always faster."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"FIS-08","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"Crowding out occurs when:","options":{"A":"The private sector spends more than the government.","B":"The government spends less than it taxes.","C":"Exports crowd out imports.","D":"Government spending replaces private spending"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIS-CROWDING-OUT","scaffold":"Government borrows to fund higher G → increased demand for loanable funds → interest rates rise → private firms face higher borrowing costs → private I falls. The government spending 'crowds out' private investment. This is a key evaluation point against expansionary fiscal policy. Note: crowding out is weaker when there's spare capacity (interest rates don't rise as much).","reforge":{"stem":"When is crowding out LEAST likely to occur?","options":{"A":"When the economy is at full capacity.","B":"During a deep recession with very low interest rates and spare capacity — government borrowing doesn't push rates up significantly because demand for private borrowing is already weak.","C":"When the government runs a surplus.","D":"Crowding out always occurs regardless of conditions. Even during a deep recession with substantial spare capacity, this option asserts interest rates would rise regardless — which contradicts the loanable funds model whenever the economy is not at full employment."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"ECON-POLICY-01","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"Automatic stabilisers are best described as:","options":{"A":"tax and benefit changes that respond to economic activity without a new decision","B":"a fixed exchange rate chosen by the central bank","C":"The central bank creating money to purchase government bonds (quantitative easing).","D":"a private investment fund used by firms"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIS-STABILISERS","scaffold":"Automatic stabilisers soften fluctuations as tax receipts and benefit payments change with income and employment.","reforge":{"stem":"A recession reduces household incomes and employment. Which automatic response helps support demand?","options":{"A":"Exports become zero by definition","B":"The central bank raises rates without a meeting","C":"Income-tax receipts fall while benefit payments rise","D":"They are the same thing — both require government decisions."},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.1"},{"id":"ECON-POLICY-02","spec":"2.3.1","stem":"Crowding out may occur when expansionary fiscal policy:","options":{"A":"reduces government spending during a recession","B":"raises public borrowing and pushes up interest rates faced by private borrowers","C":"lowers the demand for loanable funds","D":"Supply-side policies designed to improve the productive capacity of the economy."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIS-CROWDING-OUT","scaffold":"Higher government borrowing can compete for funds and reduce private investment, especially near full capacity.","reforge":{"stem":"A government funds a large stimulus by borrowing while the economy is close to full capacity. What risk may arise?","options":{"A":"The budget automatically moves into surplus","B":"The money supply must fall to zero","C":"The Bank of England adjusting the base interest rate to influence borrowing.","D":"Private investment may fall if borrowing pushes interest rates higher"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.1"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-ECON231-01","stem":"During a recession, automatic stabilisers usually cause:","options":{"A":"interest rates to rise automatically","B":"tax receipts to rise and benefits to fall","C":"tax receipts to fall and benefit payments to rise","D":"Automatic stabilisers only work in booms; discretionary only in recessions."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIS-STABILISERS","scaffold":"The budget moves toward deficit as incomes and employment weaken, supporting aggregate demand.","reforge":{"stem":"During a downturn, which pair of changes usually cushions household incomes without a fresh fiscal announcement?","options":{"A":"Lower tax receipts and higher benefit payments","B":"Higher tax receipts and lower welfare spending","C":"A compulsory rise in the policy rate","D":"A ban on government borrowing"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.1"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-ECON231-02","stem":"A government increases infrastructure spending during a downturn. In the short run this is most directly intended to:","options":{"A":"reduce the money supply","B":"Supply-side policies designed to improve the productive capacity of the economy.","C":"lower potential output","D":"raise aggregate demand through higher government expenditure"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIS-DEFINITION","scaffold":"Government spending is a component of AD, though the longer-term supply effect may also matter.","reforge":{"stem":"A public infrastructure project is brought forward during a recession. What is its immediate macroeconomic role?","options":{"A":"The central bank creating money to purchase government bonds (quantitative easing).","B":"It adds to aggregate demand through government expenditure","C":"It lowers the economy's potential output","D":"It guarantees an appreciation of the currency"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.1"}]},"2.3.2":{"label":"Monetary Policy","color":"#0d9488","questions":[{"id":"MON-01","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"Monetary policy in the UK is set by:","options":{"A":"The Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) of the Bank of England","B":"The central bank sets its own inflation target independently of government.","C":"The Prime Minister.","D":"The House of Commons."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MON-INDEPENDENCE","scaffold":"The BoE has been operationally independent since 1997. The government sets the inflation target (2% CPI); the MPC decides HOW to hit it (interest rates, QE). This independence is meant to remove political short-termism from monetary decisions.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was the Bank of England given operational independence in 1997?","options":{"A":"Because the EU required it.","B":"Because independence means the central bank no longer needs to consider inflation at all, focusing solely on financial stability and banking supervision instead of its original inflation-targeting remit.","C":"To stop politicians using rate cuts for election gains and strengthen inflation-target credibility.","D":"To allow the BoE to set tax rates."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"MON-02","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"The MPC cuts the base rate from 5% to 4%. Trace the transmission mechanism to AD. (4 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Lower base rate → commercial banks lower lending rates → cost of borrowing falls for households and firms (K). Households: cheaper mortgages increase disposable income, lower saving reward → consumption (C) rises (An). Firms: more investment projects become profitable (expected return > cost of borrowing) → investment (I) rises (An). Also: lower rates may weaken the exchange rate (less attractive for hot money) → exports cheaper → net exports improve (X−M) → AD shifts right (An).","tag":"MC-MON-TRANSMISSION","scaffold":"Transmission mechanism: base rate → commercial rates → borrowing/saving decisions → C and I → AD. Also: rate cut → exchange rate may depreciate → net exports improve → AD. Also: asset prices may rise (lower discount rate) → wealth effect → C."},{"id":"MON-03","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"A student writes: 'Raising interest rates will solve inflation without any trade-offs.' Why is this an oversimplification?","options":{"A":"It replaces the need for the central bank to ever hold interest rate meetings","B":"Interest rates have no effect on inflation.","C":"Higher rates reduce AD (lower C and I), which reduces demand-pull inflation","D":"Only fiscal policy can control inflation."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MON-LIMITS","scaffold":"The 2019 examiner report flagged this: students discussed 'conflicts that can occur as the central bank attempts to keep inflation on target.' Higher rates → lower inflation BUT also: lower growth, higher unemployment (Phillips curve trade-off), higher mortgage payments reducing living standards, exchange rate appreciation hurting exporters.","reforge":{"stem":"The MPC raises rates to combat 10% inflation. Which conflict is most likely?","options":{"A":"Unemployment rises as firms cut costs in response to lower demand — the classic inflation-unemployment trade-off.","B":"The exchange rate depreciates.","C":"Inflation rises further.","D":"Because interest rates have no effect on inflation or growth during stagflation, making monetary policy entirely powerless until the stagflation period ends on its own without intervention."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"MON-04","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"Quantitative easing (QE) involves:","options":{"A":"The government printing physical banknotes to fund public spending.","B":"Responsiveness of price to income, showing how far a firm's price must move whenever household income changes","C":"The government reducing public spending to lower the money supply.","D":"The central bank creating new money electronically to buy assets from financial institutions."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MON-QE","scaffold":"QE: BoE creates money → buys gilts from banks/pension funds → sellers deposit cash in banks → banks have more reserves → lend more → money supply rises → long-term rates fall → asset prices rise (wealth effect) → C and I stimulated → AD shifts right. Used when base rate is near zero (zero lower bound) and conventional rate cuts are exhausted.","reforge":{"stem":"Why was QE introduced after 2009 rather than further base rate cuts?","options":{"A":"The government banned rate cuts.","B":"The base rate was near zero, so normal rate cuts were exhausted and QE was needed.","C":"Responsiveness of demand to income, showing how far households alter their spending when their income changes","D":"QE is cheaper than rate cuts."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"MON-05","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"A limitation of monetary policy is that it may be ineffective when:","options":{"A":"Interest rates are near zero (liquidity trap)","B":"Inflation is high.","C":"It's correct — higher rates always solve inflation painlessly.","D":"The economy is growing."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MON-LIMITS","scaffold":"Liquidity trap: rates at/near zero → can't cut further. QE injects liquidity but banks may hoard it (risk aversion) rather than lend. Firms may not invest even at 0% if expected returns are negative (low confidence). Japan's 'lost decades' illustrate this. Monetary policy is 'pushing on a string.'","reforge":{"stem":"During the 2008 crisis, despite very low interest rates, bank lending remained subdued. Which concept explains this?","options":{"A":"Because raising interest rates to control inflation carries no effect on borrowing, spending or investment","B":"Interest rates were too high.","C":"A liquidity trap: risk-averse banks and cautious borrowers mean low rates fail to boost spending.","D":"Fiscal policy was too tight."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"MON-06","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"Higher interest rates tend to cause the exchange rate to:","options":{"A":"Depreciate — investors move money out.","B":"Appreciate — higher rates attract hot money inflows, increasing demand for the currency.","C":"Farmers can create land instantly whenever a higher price makes it profitable to plant a larger harvest","D":"Depreciate in the short run but appreciate in the long run."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-ER-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Higher UK rates → higher return on UK assets → hot money flows in (foreign investors buy sterling to invest) → demand for £ rises → £ appreciates. This strengthens the currency, making imports cheaper (reducing inflation) but exports more expensive (worsening net exports).","reforge":{"stem":"The ECB raises eurozone interest rates while UK rates stay unchanged. What is the likely effect on the £/€ exchange rate?","options":{"A":"Both currencies appreciate.","B":"A harvest already planted, where farmers can nonetheless expand this season's output the moment price rises","C":"No effect on the exchange rate.","D":"Sterling depreciates because higher eurozone rates attract hot-money flows away from sterling."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"MON-07","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"A student says: 'Monetary policy can solve cost-push inflation.' Evaluate this claim. (4 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Monetary policy (raising rates) reduces AD, which can lower the price level — but cost-push inflation is caused by SRAS shifting left (higher costs), not excess demand (K). Raising rates would reduce output and increase unemployment without addressing the cost shock (An). This creates a dilemma: the BoE must choose between tolerating above-target inflation or causing a recession — this is why central banks often 'look through' temporary supply shocks rather than responding aggressively (Ev).","tag":"MC-MON-LIMITS","scaffold":"Cost-push = supply-side problem. Monetary policy is a demand-side tool. Using demand restriction to fight a supply shock risks stagflation (rising prices AND falling output). The BoE's 2022 dilemma with energy-driven inflation illustrates this tension."},{"id":"MON-08","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"Forward guidance is a monetary policy tool where the central bank:","options":{"A":"The central bank raising its base rate to tighten monetary conditions.","B":"Forces banks to lend at specific rates.","C":"Pegs the exchange rate.","D":"Communicates its future policy intentions to influence expectations"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MON-QE","scaffold":"Forward guidance: the BoE signals where it expects rates to go (e.g. 'rates will remain low until unemployment falls below 7%'). This manages expectations — if firms believe rates will stay low, they're more likely to invest now. It works through the expectations channel rather than direct rate changes.","reforge":{"stem":"The BoE announces: 'We do not expect to raise rates for at least 12 months.' What is this intended to achieve?","options":{"A":"Force banks to lower rates immediately.","B":"Reduce uncertainty and encourage spending/investment now — businesses and households can plan knowing borrowing costs will remain stable.","C":"Shifts left, because a government subsidy is treated by firms as if it were an extra cost that reduces profit margins on every unit sold, so output falls at each price.","D":"Replace QE entirely."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"ECON-MONETARY-01","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"Quantitative easing is intended mainly to:","options":{"A":"lower longer-term borrowing costs and support spending by buying financial assets","B":"increase direct taxes on households","C":"How supply responds to wages, measuring the change in output firms choose whenever wage costs rise or fall","D":"fix the exchange rate at a permanent level"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MON-QE","scaffold":"QE creates money to purchase assets, aiming to improve liquidity, confidence and aggregate demand when rates are low.","reforge":{"stem":"When conventional interest rates are already very low, what is the purpose of an asset-purchase programme?","options":{"A":"The government printing physical banknotes to fund public spending.","B":"To increase direct taxation on households","C":"To lower longer-term yields and encourage lending or spending","D":"To reduce the number of bank deposits"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.2"},{"id":"ECON-MONETARY-02","spec":"2.3.2","stem":"A higher policy interest rate is most likely to reduce aggregate demand through:","options":{"A":"lower saving and cheaper mortgages","B":"more expensive borrowing and a stronger incentive to save","C":"a guaranteed rise in exports","D":"To allow the government to set interest rates more frequently."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MON-TRANSMISSION","scaffold":"Higher rates tend to reduce consumption and investment, though the effect depends on debt, confidence and exchange rates.","reforge":{"stem":"A rate rise makes mortgages and business loans more expensive. What is the most likely short-run effect?","options":{"A":"Exports become cheaper because the currency appreciates","B":"The government reducing public spending to lower the money supply.","C":"The economy's productive capacity doubles","D":"Consumption and investment weaken, reducing aggregate demand"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.2"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-ECON232-01","stem":"If the central bank raises the base rate, highly indebted households are most likely to experience:","options":{"A":"an immediate fall in import prices only","B":"To allow the government to set interest rates more frequently.","C":"higher interest payments and lower discretionary consumption","D":"higher disposable income from cheaper credit"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MON-TRANSMISSION","scaffold":"Higher borrowing costs can reduce household consumption and therefore AD.","reforge":{"stem":"Which households are most exposed to the consumption effect of a rise in the base rate?","options":{"A":"Highly indebted households facing higher interest payments","B":"Households with no borrowing and no savings","C":"Stay the same — interest rates don't affect exchange rates.","D":"Households that receive an automatic tax refund"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.2"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-ECON232-02","stem":"Quantitative easing is intended to stimulate demand mainly by:","options":{"A":"raising income tax to reduce inflation","B":"reducing the supply of bank deposits","C":"A luxury holiday, since spending on any luxury good automatically falls whenever household income increases","D":"buying financial assets to increase liquidity and lower longer-term borrowing costs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MON-QE","scaffold":"Asset purchases can lower yields and encourage lending and spending when rates are constrained.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a central bank buy bonds when its policy rate is close to zero?","options":{"A":"Demand is price elastic — a 1% rise in price causes a 10% fall in demand.","B":"Asset purchases can lower longer-term borrowing costs and support demand","C":"They permanently fix the exchange rate","D":"They remove all commercial-bank deposits"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.2"}]},"2.3.3":{"label":"Supply-Side Policies","color":"#e11d48","questions":[{"id":"SSP-01","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"Supply-side policies aim to:","options":{"A":"Increase the productive capacity of the economy by shifting LRAS to the right.","B":"Reduce the money supply to control inflation.","C":"Deregulation — removing barriers to entry to increase market competition and supply.","D":"Increase imports to improve consumer choice."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SSP-01","scaffold":"Supply-side policies target the economy's potential output — shifting LRAS right. They improve the quantity or quality of factors of production (labour, capital, enterprise, land/resources) or remove barriers to efficient resource allocation. They are about the capacity to produce, not the level of demand.","reforge":{"stem":"On an AD/AS diagram, successful supply-side policies would show:","options":{"A":"Infrastructure spending can only have one economic effect — fiscal or supply-side, not both.","B":"SRAS shifting left as costs rise.","C":"LRAS shifts right, allowing higher output at a lower or stable price level.","D":"AD shifting left to reduce inflation."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"SSP-02","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"Which of the following is a market-based (interventionist vs free-market) supply-side policy?","options":{"A":"Infrastructure spending can only have one economic effect — fiscal or supply-side, not both.","B":"Deregulation — removing barriers to entry to increase market competition and supply.","C":"Building new public infrastructure to improve productive capacity.","D":"Increased public spending on state education and vocational training."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SSP-02","scaffold":"Free-market/market-based: deregulation, privatisation, trade union reform, reducing income tax/corporation tax, flexible labour markets. Interventionist: government spending on education/training, infrastructure investment, R&D subsidies, regional policy. Both shift LRAS right but through different mechanisms.","reforge":{"stem":"A government invests £5bn in apprenticeship programmes for young people. This is:","options":{"A":"A free-market supply-side policy.","B":"How supply responds to a firm's revenue, measuring the change in output triggered by a rise in sales income","C":"Monetary policy.","D":"An interventionist supply-side policy — direct government spending to improve human capital."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"SSP-03","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"A student lists 'cutting interest rates' as a supply-side policy. Why is this incorrect?","options":{"A":"lower rates boost the supply side.","B":"Interest rates have no economic effect.","C":"Cutting interest rates is monetary policy aimed at boosting AD (demand-side)","D":"Infrastructure spending only affects imports, not domestic output or productivity."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SSP-DEMAND-VS-SUPPLY","scaffold":"Supply-side policies specifically target productive capacity (LRAS). Interest rate cuts target AD through the monetary transmission mechanism. Yes, lower rates may encourage I (which builds capacity over time), but the primary classification is monetary/demand-side. Examiners want clean categorisation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these is correctly classified as a supply-side policy?","options":{"A":"Reducing corporation tax to incentivise business investment and attract FDI — targeting the productive capacity of the economy.","B":"Quantitative easing.","C":"Economics diagrams do not require labelled axes in exam answers, since examiners mark only the position and slope of the curves drawn.","D":"Cutting VAT to boost consumer spending."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"SSP-04","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"Evaluate the effectiveness of supply-side policies in reducing unemployment. (12 marks — 8 KAA + 4 EV)","type":"extended_answer","model_answer_outline":{"KAA_strand_1":"Supply-side policies (e.g. retraining, education reform, deregulation) can reduce structural unemployment by addressing skills mismatches and occupational/geographical immobility. They can also reduce the natural rate of unemployment by making labour markets more flexible. Example: UK apprenticeship levy aimed at closing skills gaps.","KAA_strand_2":"However, supply-side policies take a long time to work (education reform may take 5-10 years), are expensive (infrastructure, training programmes), and may increase inequality in the short term (e.g. deregulation/trade union reform can suppress wages). They also do not address cyclical unemployment caused by deficient demand.","EV_strand":"Depends on: (1) type of unemployment — supply-side is effective for structural/natural rate but irrelevant for demand-deficient unemployment (need fiscal/monetary); (2) time horizon — long-run benefits vs short-run costs; (3) type of supply-side policy — interventionist (education) may be more equitable but slower; market-based (deregulation) may be faster but increase inequality. Judgement: supply-side policies are necessary for sustainably low unemployment but insufficient alone — demand management is needed in recessions, and the choice between interventionist and market-based approaches involves trade-offs between efficiency and equity."},"tag":"MC-SSP-LIMITS","scaffold":"Top-level: distinguish structural from cyclical, give real examples, weigh interventionist vs market-based, and reach a judgement about when supply-side works and when it doesn't."},{"id":"SSP-05","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"Trade union reform (reducing union power) is argued to improve labour market flexibility. Which is a valid counter-argument?","options":{"A":"Weaker unions may lead to lower wages and worse working conditions","B":"Government-funded retraining programmes targeting unemployed workers.","C":"Trade unions have no effect on the labour market.","D":"Union reform only affects the public sector."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SSP-LIMITS","scaffold":"Weaker unions → wages determined more by market forces → potentially lower wages for low-skilled workers → higher inequality. Also: reduced bargaining power may worsen working conditions, reduce job security. Counter: lower wages may reduce costs and increase employment. This is a classic efficiency vs equity trade-off.","reforge":{"stem":"Privatisation of a state-owned monopoly is expected to increase efficiency. Which is a valid concern?","options":{"A":"Infrastructure spending can only have one economic effect — fiscal or supply-side, not both.","B":"Private firms are always less efficient.","C":"The firm may become a private monopoly, raising prices unless regulation is effective.","D":"Only public firms can make profits."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"SSP-06","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"A key advantage of supply-side policies over demand-side policies is:","options":{"A":"They work immediately.","B":"They can increase output without causing inflation","C":"They are free to implement.","D":"Shift AD to the right by increasing government spending."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SSP-01","scaffold":"Demand-side (fiscal/monetary) shifts AD right → higher output BUT also higher price level (inflationary). Supply-side shifts LRAS right → higher output at same or lower price level = non-inflationary growth. This is the key comparative advantage. The trade-off: supply-side takes much longer to work.","reforge":{"stem":"An economy is at full capacity with high inflation. Which policy approach can increase output WITHOUT worsening inflation?","options":{"A":"Expansionary fiscal policy.","B":"Cutting interest rates.","C":"Infrastructure spending can only have one economic effect — fiscal or supply-side, not both.","D":"Supply-side policies expand LRAS, allowing more output without demand-pull inflation."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"SSP-07","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a supply-side policy?","options":{"A":"Deregulation — removing barriers to entry to increase market competition and supply.","B":"Government-funded retraining programmes targeting unemployed workers.","C":"Increased public spending on state education and vocational training.","D":"Building new public infrastructure to improve productive capacity."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SSP-02","scaffold":"Increasing JSA is a fiscal transfer (automatic stabiliser) that supports incomes — it doesn't improve productive capacity. In fact, higher benefits could arguably REDUCE labour supply if they weaken the incentive to find work. Infrastructure, deregulation, and tax incentives all aim to expand capacity or improve efficiency.","reforge":{"stem":"A government reduces the top rate of income tax from 50% to 40%. Supply-side economists argue this will:","options":{"A":"Stronger incentives can raise labour supply and investment, shifting LRAS right.","B":"Shift AD left because people pay less tax.","C":"Infrastructure spending only affects imports, not domestic output or productivity.","D":"Reduce tax revenue with no other effects."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"SSP-08","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"Infrastructure investment is sometimes classified as BOTH a demand-side AND supply-side policy. Why?","options":{"A":"Guaranteed profits for every firm, since competition forces rivals to match whatever price and output level is most profitable","B":"Infrastructure spending is always offset by the taxation needed to fund it.","C":"Infrastructure spending only affects imports, not domestic output or productivity.","D":"Infrastructure spending is both fiscal (raises G → AD) and supply-side (raises LRAS) simultaneously."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SSP-DEMAND-VS-SUPPLY","scaffold":"Infrastructure is dual-impact: Short run = demand injection (construction workers hired, materials bought → G increases → AD right). Long run = capacity expansion (better transport reduces costs, improves connectivity, boosts productivity → LRAS right). The 2022 examiner report used the Bow Street station example to illustrate this dual effect.","reforge":{"stem":"A government builds a new high-speed rail line. In year 1 of construction, the primary macroeconomic effect is:","options":{"A":"LRAS shifts right immediately.","B":"AD shifts right because construction spending is government expenditure and creates jobs and income.","C":"SRAS shifts left due to higher costs.","D":"Prices must always rise, because rival firms respond to competition by coordinating to raise their prices together"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"ECON-SUPPLY-01","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"A government-funded apprenticeship scheme is a supply-side policy because it:","options":{"A":"improves workers' skills and may raise the economy's productive capacity","B":"reduces the policy interest rate","C":"Infrastructure spending is always offset by the taxation needed to fund it.","D":"sets a maximum price for all goods"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-SSP-INTERVENTIONIST","scaffold":"Training can improve human capital, labour productivity and the long-run aggregate supply of the economy.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can a publicly funded training programme increase long-run output?","options":{"A":"It guarantees a fall in imports","B":"It directly fixes the policy interest rate","C":"It can raise human capital and labour productivity","D":"raises imports without affecting productive capacity"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.3"},{"id":"ECON-SUPPLY-02","spec":"2.3.3","stem":"A possible limitation of supply-side reforms is that they:","options":{"A":"always reduce the budget deficit immediately","B":"may take time to affect productivity and can require substantial public spending","C":"cannot affect employment or investment","D":"Deregulation — removing barriers to entry to increase market competition and supply."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-SSP-LIMITS","scaffold":"Structural reforms may be slow, expensive and uncertain, with benefits depending on implementation and spare capacity.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a supply-side reform fail to improve output quickly?","options":{"A":"Infrastructure spending is always offset by the taxation needed to fund it.","B":"Supply-side policy always reduces the labour force","C":"Structural reform only affects the exchange rate","D":"Implementation can be costly and its productivity effects may take years"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.3"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-ECON233-01","stem":"A government-funded vocational training programme may raise long-run aggregate supply because it:","options":{"A":"lowers the working-age population","B":"reduces the economy's capital stock","C":"increases workers' skills and potential productivity","D":"Building new public infrastructure to improve productive capacity."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-SSP-INTERVENTIONIST","scaffold":"Human-capital investment can improve labour productivity and productive capacity.","reforge":{"stem":"A vocational training scheme can shift long-run aggregate supply because it:","options":{"A":"improves skills and may raise worker productivity","B":"reduces the capital stock","C":"shrinks the working-age population","D":"raises imports without affecting productive capacity"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.3"},{"id":"A1-PHASE7-ECON233-02","stem":"A possible limitation of supply-side reforms is that they: (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"always reduce productivity immediately","B":"Deregulation — removing barriers to entry to increase market competition and supply.","C":"cannot affect labour markets","D":"may take years to affect capacity and can require substantial public spending"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-SSP-LIMITS","scaffold":"Education, infrastructure and labour-market reforms often have delayed and uncertain returns.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a realistic drawback of a major education reform?","options":{"A":"It must reduce productivity immediately","B":"Benefits may be delayed and the programme may require substantial public funding","C":"It cannot affect labour-market outcomes","D":"Infrastructure spending only affects imports, not domestic output or productivity."},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.3.3"}]},"2.4.1":{"label":"Inequality","color":"#9333ea","questions":[{"id":"INQ-01","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"A student draws a Lorenz curve but labels the axes 'income' (y) and 'people' (x). Why does this lose marks?","options":{"A":"The labels are already correct — no change needed.","B":"Price (£ or P) on the vertical axis and quantity (Q) per time period on the horizontal.","C":"The axes should be reversed: quantity on vertical, price on horizontal.","D":"Economics diagrams do not require labelled axes in exam answers."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-TECH-04","scaffold":"The 2022 examiner report explicitly flagged Lorenz curve axis labelling as a common error. Both axes must show cumulative percentages. The 45° line of equality shows what perfect equality looks like (bottom 20% earn 20% of income, etc). The further the curve bows from this line, the greater the inequality.","reforge":{"stem":"On a Lorenz curve, the 45° diagonal line represents:","options":{"A":"Average income.","B":"Economics diagrams do not require labelled axes in exam answers, since examiners mark only the position and slope of the curves drawn.","C":"Perfect equality — each percentage of the population earns exactly that percentage of total income.","D":"The poverty line."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"INQ-02","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"The UK's Gini coefficient is 0.34. What does this mean?","options":{"A":"A developing economy with very low levels of industrialisation and low inequality","B":"The Gini coefficient ranges from 0 (perfect equality) to 1 (perfect inequality).","C":"The UK ranks 34th in global league tables of income inequality.","D":"34% of UK national income is captured by the top 1% of earners."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-INQ-MEASURES","scaffold":"Gini coefficient = Area between Lorenz curve and 45° line ÷ total area under the 45° line. 0 = perfect equality (Lorenz curve IS the 45° line). 1 = perfect inequality (one person has everything). UK at 0.34 means moderate inequality — for comparison, Nordics are ~0.25, South Africa ~0.63.","reforge":{"stem":"Country A has a Gini of 0.28; Country B has 0.52. Which has greater income inequality?","options":{"A":"Inequality is unrelated to a country's stage of economic development","B":"Country A — higher Gini means more equality.","C":"They are equally unequal.","D":"Country B: a higher Gini coefficient indicates greater inequality."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"INQ-03","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"A student explains causes of income inequality but offers no evaluation. In an 8-mark question, how many marks are they likely to lose?","options":{"A":"None — evaluation is optional on 8-mark questions.","B":"All 8 marks.","C":"Up to 2 marks","D":"1 mark at most."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-INQ-CAUSES","scaffold":"The 2022 examiner report stressed this repeatedly: on 8-mark questions, marks are split equally (2K + 2Ap + 2An + 2Ev). Many candidates scored 6/8 with strong KAA but zero evaluation. Evaluation means questioning significance, considering counter-arguments, or weighing causes against each other.","reforge":{"stem":"On a 12-mark levels question (8 KAA + 4 EV), a student writes excellent analysis but no evaluation. What is their likely maximum mark?","options":{"A":"8 — they can access full KAA marks but score zero on the 4 evaluation marks.","B":"A rapidly industrialising economy showing rising inequality in its early stages","C":"12 — analysis is enough.","D":"10 — they lose only 2 marks."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"INQ-04","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"Explain one cause of income inequality within the UK. (2 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Regional differences in educational attainment mean that workers in some areas have lower skills and qualifications (knowledge). This limits their access to high-paying professional jobs, trapping them in lower-wage employment and widening the income gap between regions like London and the North East (analysis).","tag":"MC-INQ-CAUSES","scaffold":"Causes include: education/skills disparities, regional economic imbalances, discrimination, globalisation (skilled workers gain, unskilled lose), technological change (automation replacing routine jobs), tax and benefit policy, inheritance. K for identifying; An for explaining the mechanism."},{"id":"INQ-05","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"What is the difference between income and wealth?","options":{"A":"Income is a flow","B":"Income is only wages; wealth is only property.","C":"Wealth is always larger than income.","D":"They are the same thing."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-INQ-05","scaffold":"The 2019 examiner report flagged students confusing these. Income = flow (£ per year — salary, benefits, investment returns). Wealth = stock (£ at a point — house value, pension pot, savings, shares minus debts). You can have high income but low wealth (spend everything), or low income but high wealth (retiree with a paid-off house).","reforge":{"stem":"A retired homeowner has no salary but owns a £500,000 house and £200,000 in savings. They have:","options":{"A":"High income and high wealth.","B":"No wealth because they have no income.","C":"Low income (no earnings flow) but high wealth (£700,000 in assets).","D":"Income tax automatically also taxes accumulated assets and inheritance"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"INQ-06","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"A question asks about rising WEALTH inequality, but a student discusses INCOME inequality throughout. What mark impact does this have?","options":{"A":"None — they are the same concept.","B":"Significant","C":"Minimal — examiners don't distinguish them.","D":"The student gains extra marks for breadth."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-INQ-05","scaffold":"The 2019 examiner report flagged this directly: candidates discussed income inequality when asked about wealth inequality. Wealth inequality causes: inheritance, house price appreciation (owners vs renters), pension differences, share ownership concentrated among the wealthy. Answer the question SET.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor specifically drives WEALTH inequality rather than income inequality?","options":{"A":"Differences in hourly wages.","B":"A developing economy with very low levels of industrialisation and low inequality","C":"Different working hours.","D":"Rising house prices can widen the wealth gap between homeowners and non-owners."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"INQ-07","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"Progressive taxation means:","options":{"A":"Tax rates decrease as income rises.","B":"Everyone pays the same percentage of their income in tax.","C":"The marginal rate of tax increases as income rises","D":"Only the wealthy pay tax."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-INQ-REDISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Progressive: marginal rate rises with income (UK: 20% basic → 40% higher → 45% additional). Proportional: same rate for all. Regressive: takes a larger proportion from lower incomes (e.g. VAT — same rate but represents a larger share of a poorer person's income). Progressive taxation is the main fiscal tool for reducing income inequality.","reforge":{"stem":"VAT is often described as regressive because:","options":{"A":"A fall in a firm's average cost that happens automatically whenever a new competitor enters the market","B":"The same tax rate takes a larger share of income from lower earners, who spend more of their income.","C":"Only poor people pay VAT.","D":"It is progressive in disguise."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"INQ-08","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"Evaluate whether government redistribution (taxes and benefits) is the most effective way to reduce inequality. (12 marks — 8 KAA + 4 EV)","type":"extended_answer","model_answer_outline":{"KAA_strand_1":"Progressive taxation takes more from higher earners; transfer payments (benefits, tax credits, state pension) boost incomes at the bottom. Together they compress the income distribution. Apply: UK Gini before taxes/benefits is ~0.52, after redistribution ~0.34 — redistribution reduces inequality significantly.","KAA_strand_2":"However, high marginal tax rates may create disincentive effects (Laffer curve), encourage avoidance/evasion, and reduce enterprise. Means-tested benefits can create poverty traps (withdrawal of benefits as income rises → effective marginal tax rates over 60%).","EV_strand":"Depends on: (1) pre-distribution vs redistribution — investing in education and skills addresses root causes (human capital) but takes decades; (2) minimum/living wage — raises floor incomes but may cause unemployment if set too high; (3) type of inequality — redistribution addresses income but not wealth (need wealth taxes, inheritance reform). Judgement: redistribution is effective short-term but insufficient alone — sustainable equality requires investment in education and opportunity alongside fiscal transfers."},"tag":"MC-INQ-REDISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Top-level: use before/after Gini data, distinguish income from wealth inequality, weigh redistribution against pre-distribution (education, minimum wage), and consider unintended consequences (disincentives, poverty traps)."},{"id":"INQ-09","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"What is the Kuznets curve theory of inequality and growth?","options":{"A":"Inequality rises then falls as an economy moves from agriculture to industry and later to services","B":"Inequality is unrelated to a country's stage of economic development","C":"Inequality only ever rises as economies grow from agriculture to industry","D":"A loss suffered by the consumer only, arising every time they consume a good that also affects other people"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-INQ-MEASURES","scaffold":"Kuznets argued inequality follows an inverted-U shape: as workers shift from low-productivity agriculture into higher-paid industrial jobs, inequality first rises because only some workers move; as the whole economy industrialises and matures into services, wages converge and inequality falls. The theory is contested — some advanced economies (UK, US) have seen inequality rise again since the 1980s, which the simple Kuznets curve does not explain, so use it as a starting point for evaluation rather than a fixed law.","reforge":{"stem":"Which observation would most challenge the simple Kuznets curve theory?","options":{"A":"A rapidly industrialising economy showing rising inequality in its early stages","B":"It creates only private costs, since the benefits of studying are received solely by the student who pays for it","C":"A mature, high-income economy where inequality has been rising since the 1980s rather than continuing to fall","D":"An economy where agricultural and industrial wages are identical"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"INQ-10","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"Which of the following best describes wealth inequality compared to income inequality in the UK?","options":{"A":"Demand determines productive capacity, so the frontier itself shifts in and out purely in response to changes in consumer spending","B":"Wealth is typically far more unequally distributed than income, since assets can be accumulated and inherited over generations","C":"Wealth and income inequality are always identical in size","D":"Wealth inequality only matters for those above retirement age"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-INQ-05","scaffold":"Income is a flow (what you earn each year); wealth is a stock (what you own: property, pensions, savings, shares). Because wealth can be built up and inherited over decades, it tends to be far more concentrated than income — the top 10% of UK households by wealth hold around half of all wealth, a much larger share than the top 10% hold of income. This matters for policy: taxes on income do little to address wealth concentration, which is why economists debate wealth taxes, inheritance tax reform and capital gains tax as separate tools.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might taxing income alone fail to reduce wealth inequality significantly?","options":{"A":"Firms and consumers have equal knowledge, since every buyer and seller can observe exactly the same information before trading","B":"Wealth inequality does not exist in developed economies","C":"Wealth and income are always equal in size for every household","D":"Wealth is built up over generations through asset ownership and inheritance, which income tax does not directly touch"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"ECON-INEQUALITY-01","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"A progressive income tax is designed to:","options":{"A":"take a higher proportion of income from higher-income taxpayers","B":"charge every taxpayer exactly the same amount","C":"reduce the tax paid as income rises","D":"The axes should be reversed: quantity on vertical, price on horizontal."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-INQ-REDISTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Progressive taxation can reduce post-tax income inequality, although its effects depend on rates, thresholds and avoidance.","reforge":{"stem":"Which tax design is most likely to reduce post-tax income inequality?","options":{"A":"A progressive income tax taking a larger percentage from higher earners","B":"Inequality only ever rises as economies grow from agriculture to industry","C":"A tax that falls as income rises","D":"A tax applied only to people below the poverty line"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.4.1"},{"id":"ECON-INEQUALITY-02","spec":"2.4.1","stem":"A Lorenz curve further from the line of equality indicates:","options":{"A":"that all households receive the same income","B":"a greater degree of income inequality","C":"a fall in the population size","D":"that the economy has no unemployment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-INQ-MEASURES","scaffold":"The greater the gap from the equality line, the more unequal the income distribution.","reforge":{"stem":"On a Lorenz curve diagram, what does a larger gap from the equality line indicate?","options":{"A":"A more unequal distribution of income","B":"Identical income for every household","C":"A smaller population but unchanged distribution","D":"The absence of unemployment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.4.1"}]},"2.4.2":{"label":"Poverty","color":"#64748b","questions":[{"id":"POV-01","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"What is the difference between absolute and relative poverty?","options":{"A":"Absolute = can't afford basics; relative = below 60% of median income.","B":"They are the same concept measured differently.","C":"Relative poverty always moving in the same direction as absolute poverty","D":"Relative poverty is always more severe than absolute."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-POV-01","scaffold":"Absolute: fixed standard — can you survive? (World Bank: $2.15/day). Relative: moving standard — how far below the typical income are you? (UK: 60% of median). A country can eliminate absolute poverty while relative poverty rises (if median incomes grow but bottom incomes grow slower). They measure different things.","reforge":{"stem":"GDP per capita doubles over 20 years but the bottom 10% see only a 20% income rise while the median doubles. What happens to each type of poverty?","options":{"A":"Both fall equally.","B":"Both rise because growth causes poverty.","C":"Absolute poverty likely falls, but relative poverty may rise as the bottom 10% lag behind the median.","D":"Users can be excluded easily, since access to the good can always be restricted to those who have paid for it"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"POV-02","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"A student says: 'Economic growth will automatically eliminate poverty.' Evaluate this. (4 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Growth increases national income and can reduce absolute poverty by raising incomes across the distribution (K). However, if growth is unevenly distributed — e.g. captured by the wealthy or concentrated in certain regions — relative poverty may persist or worsen despite rising GDP (An). Furthermore, 'trickle-down' effects are contested: supply-side growth may take decades to benefit the poorest, and without targeted redistribution (benefits, minimum wage) growth alone does not guarantee poverty reduction (Ev).","tag":"MC-POV-ABSOLUTE-RELATIVE","scaffold":"Growth is necessary but not sufficient. It helps with absolute poverty (more output) but not automatically with relative poverty (distribution matters). Evaluation: type of growth (inclusive vs extractive), redistribution policy, and whether benefits reach the poorest."},{"id":"POV-03","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"The poverty trap (or unemployment trap) occurs when:","options":{"A":"Everyone who enters poverty remains there permanently.","B":"Firms must supply it at a profit, since no good can ever be provided unless a private firm can charge for using it","C":"The withdrawal of means-tested benefits as income rises creates very high effective margin","D":"Only universal benefit schemes create poverty traps."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-POV-TRAP","scaffold":"Example: a person on benefits earns £100 extra → loses £65 in withdrawn benefits + pays £20 tax/NI → keeps only £15. Effective marginal rate = 85%. Rational response: why bother? This traps people in benefit dependency. Solutions include: Universal Credit taper (gradual withdrawal), raising personal allowance, in-work benefits.","reforge":{"stem":"A government replaces means-tested benefits with a Universal Basic Income (flat payment to all, not withdrawn as income rises). How does this affect the poverty trap?","options":{"A":"It removes benefit withdrawal as income rises, improving work incentives.","B":"Demand is price elastic — quantity fell proportionally more than price rose.","C":"It has no effect on the poverty trap.","D":"It creates a new trap because no one would work."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"POV-04","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"Which government policy would most directly reduce absolute poverty?","options":{"A":"Cutting the top rate of income tax to stimulate growth.","B":"Minimum wage increases always cause poverty to rise instead of fall","C":"Reducing corporation tax to attract investment.","D":"Raising the National Living Wage and strengthening housing benefit"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-POV-ABSOLUTE-RELATIVE","scaffold":"Absolute poverty = can't afford basics. Direct interventions: raise floor incomes (minimum/living wage), provide essentials directly (social housing, free school meals, NHS), strengthen safety net (UC, housing benefit). Supply-side policies may help long-run but don't address immediate deprivation.","reforge":{"stem":"A charity argues: 'The best anti-poverty policy is a job.' Which economic concept supports this, and what is a limitation?","options":{"A":"No concept supports it — jobs don't reduce poverty.","B":"Jobs can reduce benefit dependency, but low-paid or insecure work can still mean in-work poverty.","C":"All consumers pay voluntarily, since free riding is prevented once a good is available to everyone at no charge","D":"Only public sector jobs reduce poverty."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POV-05","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"The UK measures relative poverty as income below 60% of median household income. A limitation of this measure is:","options":{"A":"It's relative to the median, which can itself fall.","B":"It only measures wealth, not income.","C":"60% is an arbitrary figure with no policy relevance.","D":"It is too generous — the threshold is too high."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-POV-01","scaffold":"Relative poverty is tied to the median, so it moves with the distribution. In a recession: median income drops → 60% threshold drops → some people 'escape' relative poverty despite being no better off. This is why we need both absolute and relative measures. Relative poverty is really an inequality measure wearing poverty's label.","reforge":{"stem":"During COVID-19, median incomes fell sharply. What likely happened to the UK's relative poverty rate?","options":{"A":"It rose sharply as everyone got poorer.","B":"Relative poverty always moving in the same direction as absolute poverty","C":"It may have fallen because the 60% median-income threshold also fell.","D":"It doubled immediately."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"POV-06","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"Explain one cause of poverty in a developed country like the UK. (2 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Structural unemployment caused by deindustrialisation has left former industrial regions with fewer well-paid jobs (knowledge). Workers in these areas who lack transferable skills face long-term worklessness or are confined to low-wage service-sector employment, pushing households below the poverty line (analysis).","tag":"MC-POV-ABSOLUTE-RELATIVE","scaffold":"Causes: unemployment (structural, cyclical), low wages, insecure work (gig economy), regional deprivation, low educational attainment, family breakdown, disability/ill health, housing costs. K for cause; An for mechanism linking it to poverty."},{"id":"POV-07","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"What is meant by the 'cycle of poverty'?","options":{"A":"Poverty that only lasts for a single generation before disappearing","B":"Speculators always base their decisions purely on a firm's underlying long-term value, ignoring short-term price momentum, herd behaviour or the expectations of other traders — which is not how speculative trading actually tends to work in practice.","C":"A situation where poverty is passed from one generation to the next through limited access to education, healthcare and opportunity","D":"Poverty that affects only elderly people nearing retirement"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-POV-TRAP","scaffold":"The cycle of poverty describes how children born into poor households often face worse education, health and job prospects, making them more likely to remain poor as adults and pass similar disadvantages to their own children. Breaking the cycle usually requires intervention at multiple points — early years education, healthcare access, and policies that reduce the direct cost of staying in education — rather than a single one-off transfer of income.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy is most directly aimed at breaking the intergenerational cycle of poverty?","options":{"A":"Investment in early years education and childcare for children from low-income households","B":"Perfect information, because both the buyer and the seller can observe the car's full condition before agreeing a price","C":"A temporary reduction in VAT on luxury goods","D":"Raising the retirement age for all workers"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"POV-08","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"Which of the following is most likely to be classified as in-work poverty?","options":{"A":"To increase harmful consumption, by making the taxed good cheaper relative to substitutes once revenue is collected","B":"A retired person living entirely on a state pension","C":"A student living on a maintenance loan","D":"A full-time worker on a low wage whose household income still falls below the relative poverty line"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-POV-ABSOLUTE-RELATIVE","scaffold":"In-work poverty occurs when someone is employed, often full-time, but their wages are still too low relative to household size and costs to lift the household above the poverty line — common in low-paid, insecure or part-time work. It challenges the assumption that having a job is enough to escape poverty, and is one reason policies like the National Living Wage and in-work benefits exist alongside unemployment support.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might raising the minimum wage not fully eliminate in-work poverty?","options":{"A":"Banks only ever lending money for periods shorter than one year, matching every loan exactly to the length of the deposits that funded it, leaving no gap between borrowing and lending timeframes at all.","B":"Households may still have high costs, such as housing or childcare, or too few hours worked for a higher hourly wage to lift total income above the poverty line","C":"Minimum wage laws do not apply to full-time workers","D":"In-work poverty only affects the unemployed"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"POV-09","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"What does 'benefit withdrawal' mean in the context of the poverty trap?","options":{"A":"Means-tested benefits being reduced as earned income rises, which can leave a person little better off from working extra hours","B":"Scarcity is eliminated by trade, because specialising according to comparative advantage removes the need to make further choices","C":"Benefits being paid only once per year regardless of income","D":"Employers withdrawing job offers from benefit claimants"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-POV-TRAP","scaffold":"Many means-tested benefits are gradually withdrawn as a claimant's earnings rise, so for every extra pound earned, a large share is lost through reduced benefits and income tax combined — sometimes over 60-70p in the pound. This creates a high effective marginal tax rate that weakens the financial incentive to work more hours or seek a pay rise, which is the core mechanism behind the poverty (or unemployment) trap.","reforge":{"stem":"A worker's Universal Credit is reduced by 55p for every extra £1 earned, and they also pay 20% income tax on that pound. What is their approximate effective marginal tax rate?","options":{"A":"20%","B":"55%","C":"75%","D":"100%"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"POV-10","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"Why might relative poverty statistics fall during a recession even though living standards are worsening?","options":{"A":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make.","B":"If median household income falls sharply, the relative poverty line falls too, so fewer households appear below it even though everyone is worse off","C":"The government suspends poverty measurement during a recession","D":"Prices fall so quickly that poverty automatically disappears"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-POV-01","scaffold":"Relative poverty is measured against the median income at the time, not a fixed standard. If a recession drags median income down for everyone, the poverty line itself falls, which can make relative poverty statistics improve on paper even while absolute living standards are falling for most households — a well-known limitation examiners expect you to flag when evaluating this measure.","reforge":{"stem":"Which situation illustrates a key weakness of relative poverty as a measure of living standards?","options":{"A":"Relative poverty rising while absolute poverty also rises","B":"Because the lender of last resort function is never actually used in practice, since central banks have never once needed to lend emergency funds to a struggling bank throughout their entire history.","C":"Relative poverty being measured identically in every country","D":"Median income and the poverty line both falling in a recession, making relative poverty appear to fall even as living standards worsen"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"ECON-POVERTY-01","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"Relative poverty is measured by comparing a person's resources with:","options":{"A":"a threshold related to typical income or living standards in that society","B":"the cost of one specific imported good","C":"the highest income in the world","D":"Demand for margarine falls — consumers have less money after buying butter."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-POV-ABSOLUTE-RELATIVE","scaffold":"Relative poverty reflects the resources needed to participate in the living standards of the society concerned.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is relative poverty linked to the living standards of a particular society?","options":{"A":"Its threshold is compared with typical income or resources in that society","B":"It is based only on the price of one imported good","C":"It always means having the lowest income in the world","D":"Remove all energy demand, by pricing every unit of carbon so high that households stop using energy altogether"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.4.2"},{"id":"ECON-POVERTY-02","spec":"2.4.2","stem":"The poverty trap can discourage a person from taking work when:","options":{"A":"It is paid only by producers abroad, since a UK indirect tax cannot legally be passed on to a domestic consumer","B":"lost benefits and higher taxes outweigh the extra earnings from employment","C":"all benefits are paid regardless of income","D":"employment has no effect on disposable income"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-POV-TRAP","scaffold":"A high effective marginal tax rate can make the financial gain from employment small or negative.","reforge":{"stem":"A worker loses benefits and pays more tax after taking a low-paid job. What problem might this create?","options":{"A":"A poverty trap, because the gain in disposable income is very small","B":"A guaranteed increase in labour productivity","C":"A fall in the effective marginal tax rate","D":"A situation where poverty rises and falls with the business cycle only"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.4.2"}]},"2.5.1":{"label":"Financial Markets","color":"#0369a1","questions":[{"id":"FMK-01","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"Which of the following is a role of financial markets?","options":{"A":"Facilitating saving","B":"Setting the inflation target.","C":"Printing physical currency.","D":"Collecting taxes on behalf of the government."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FMK-01","scaffold":"Roles of financial markets: (1) Facilitate saving (deposit accounts, bonds). (2) Lend to individuals and firms (mortgages, business loans). (3) Facilitate exchange of goods/services (payment systems). (4) Provide forward markets for currencies and commodities (manage risk). (5) Provide a market for equities (shares — raising capital for firms).","reforge":{"stem":"A wheat farmer uses a forward contract to sell next season's harvest at a fixed price today. Which role of financial markets does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Facilitating saving.","B":"Speculators always base their decisions purely on a firm's underlying long-term value, ignoring short-term price momentum, herd behaviour or the expectations of other traders — which is not how speculative trading actually tends to work in practice.","C":"Providing forward markets in commodities — allowing the farmer to manage the risk of price fluctuations.","D":"Currency exchange."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"FMK-02","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"A student explains forward markets in currencies but struggles to explain HOW they work. Which is the correct explanation?","options":{"A":"Forward markets allow you to buy currency at today's spot rate at any future date.","B":"A forward contract locks in an exchange rate for a future transaction","C":"Forward markets predict future exchange rates with certainty.","D":"Forward markets are the same as the stock market."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FMK-02","scaffold":"The 2019 examiner report flagged lack of clarity on forward markets. Key: a forward contract is a binding agreement to exchange currency at a pre-agreed rate on a future date. This protects firms from adverse exchange rate movements between agreeing a deal and payment. Example: a UK importer agrees to buy $1m of goods, delivery in 3 months. They buy dollars forward at $1.25/£ — even if sterling falls to $1.10, they're protected.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK exporter expects to receive €500,000 in 6 months. Why might they use a forward contract?","options":{"A":"They are legally required to.","B":"To speculate on the euro rising.","C":"Speculators always base their decisions purely on a firm's underlying long-term value, ignoring short-term price momentum, herd behaviour or the expectations of other traders — which is not how speculative trading actually tends to work in practice.","D":"To lock in today’s exchange rate and protect the sterling value of future euro receipts."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"FMK-03","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"Market failure in financial markets can occur because of:","options":{"A":"Perfect information among all participants.","B":"Too much regulation.","C":"Asymmetric information","D":"Low interest rates."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FMK-ASYMMETRIC","scaffold":"Asymmetric info: borrowers know their risk; lenders don't. Adverse selection: riskier borrowers are more likely to seek loans. Moral hazard: once insured/bailed out, firms take excessive risks (banks before 2008 — 'too big to fail'). Also: speculation creating asset bubbles, externalities from bank failures (systemic risk).","reforge":{"stem":"After the 2008 bailout, some argued banks would take even more risks in future because they expected to be rescued again. This is an example of:","options":{"A":"Moral hazard — a bailout expectation weakens banks’ incentive to manage risk.","B":"Forward markets allow you to buy currency at today's spot rate at any future date.","C":"Adverse selection.","D":"Efficient markets."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"FMK-04","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"Why might a stock market crash have wider macroeconomic consequences?","options":{"A":"Financial markets in different countries are always fully insulated from one another","B":"Stock markets are separate from the real economy.","C":"Share prices only matter for inflation.","D":"Falling share prices reduce household wealth (negative wealth effect → lower C)"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FMK-CRASH","scaffold":"Transmission: share prices fall → (1) wealth effect: shareholders feel poorer → C falls. (2) Firms: harder/more expensive to raise capital via equity → I falls. (3) Confidence: negative sentiment spreads → precautionary saving rises. (4) Banks: if holding shares, their balance sheets weaken → lending tightens. All feed into lower AD.","reforge":{"stem":"During a stock market boom, household spending rises even among people who don't own shares. Which concept explains this?","options":{"A":"The multiplier.","B":"The wealth effect and confidence channel can encourage spending as markets rise.","C":"Speculators always base their decisions purely on a firm's underlying long-term value, ignoring short-term price momentum, herd behaviour or the expectations of other traders — which is not how speculative trading actually tends to work in practice.","D":"Fiscal policy."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"FMK-05","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"Explain one way financial markets contribute to economic growth. (2 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Financial markets channel funds from savers to borrowers, enabling firms to access capital for investment (knowledge). For example, a company can issue shares on the stock market to raise funds for new factories or R&D, increasing productive capacity and shifting LRAS to the right over time (analysis).","tag":"MC-FMK-01","scaffold":"Financial markets → growth link: (1) Channel savings into productive investment (allocative efficiency). (2) Reduce risk through diversification and insurance → firms more willing to invest. (3) Provide liquidity → assets can be bought/sold easily → encourages participation. (4) Price discovery → signals where capital should flow."},{"id":"FMK-06","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"What is 'asymmetric information' in the context of financial markets?","options":{"A":"Speculators always base their decisions purely on a firm's underlying long-term value, ignoring short-term price momentum, herd behaviour or the expectations of other traders — which is not how speculative trading actually tends to work in practice.","B":"A situation where one party to a transaction, such as a borrower, has more or better information than the other, such as a lender","C":"Information that is only available to government regulators","D":"Information that changes at exactly the same rate for buyers and sellers"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FMK-ASYMMETRIC","scaffold":"Asymmetric information is a key source of market failure in finance: a borrower usually knows more about their own ability to repay a loan than the lender does, and a firm's managers know more about its true prospects than outside investors. This can lead to adverse selection and moral hazard, both of which can cause financial markets to misallocate funds or, in extreme cases, seize up.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might asymmetric information cause a bank to raise interest rates on all loans rather than screen out risky borrowers individually?","options":{"A":"Because all borrowers are equally risky and rates should be identical","B":"Because higher rates always eliminate default risk entirely","C":"Because interest rates have no effect on inflation or growth during stagflation, making monetary policy entirely powerless until the stagflation period ends on its own without intervention.","D":"Because it is often too costly for the bank to fully verify each individual borrower's true risk, so it prices the average risk into a higher rate for everyone"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"FMK-07","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"What is 'moral hazard' in financial markets?","options":{"A":"A legal requirement for banks to act morally in all transactions","B":"Speculators always base their decisions purely on a firm's underlying long-term value, ignoring short-term price momentum, herd behaviour or the expectations of other traders — which is not how speculative trading actually tends to work in practice.","C":"A situation where a party takes on greater risk because they do not bear the full consequences of that risk, such as a bank expecting a government bailout","D":"A situation where financial markets have no risk at all"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FMK-ASYMMETRIC","scaffold":"Moral hazard arises when being insured or protected against a bad outcome changes behaviour to make that outcome more likely. If banks believe they are 'too big to fail' and will be bailed out by the government in a crisis, they have an incentive to take on excessive risk, since potential losses are partly borne by taxpayers rather than the bank itself. This was a major criticism of the 2008 bank bailouts and a driver of post-crisis regulation.","reforge":{"stem":"How does the expectation of a government bailout create moral hazard for a large bank?","options":{"A":"It encourages the bank to take on more risk than it otherwise would, since losses may ultimately be covered by the state","B":"It has no effect on the bank's decision-making","C":"Demand for cars rises, because drivers switch to buying more fuel-efficient models to offset the higher cost of running them.","D":"It removes all risk from the financial system entirely"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"FMK-08","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"Why did the collapse of the US sub-prime mortgage market in 2007-08 have global consequences?","options":{"A":"Shifts left, because a government subsidy is treated by firms as if it were an extra cost that reduces profit margins on every unit sold, so output falls at each price.","B":"The US government immediately absorbed all losses domestically","C":"Only US citizens were affected by the resulting recession","D":"Mortgage-backed securities containing sub-prime debt had been sold to banks and investors worldwide, spreading the losses internationally"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FMK-CRASH","scaffold":"Sub-prime mortgages were bundled into complex financial products and sold on to banks and investors around the world. When large numbers of borrowers defaulted, the value of these securities collapsed, and because they were held globally, losses spread far beyond the US housing market, freezing credit markets internationally and triggering the 2008 global financial crisis.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the 2008 financial crisis illustrate about interconnected financial markets?","options":{"A":"Shifts left, because a government subsidy is treated by firms as if it were an extra cost that reduces profit margins on every unit sold, so output falls at each price.","B":"Risk in one part of the global financial system can spread rapidly to other countries through interconnected institutions and financial products","C":"Only the country where a crisis originates experiences any economic impact","D":"Global financial markets reduce systemic risk to zero"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"FMK-09","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"What is the main role of the Financial Conduct Authority (FCA) in the UK?","options":{"A":"Regulating the conduct of financial firms and protecting consumers from unfair treatment or mis-selling","B":"Printing the UK's physical currency","C":"Only rich households buy fuel, since low-income households are assumed to spend nothing at all on household energy","D":"Deciding UK government fiscal policy"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FMK-01","scaffold":"The FCA regulates how financial firms behave towards their customers — for example, ensuring products are sold fairly, firms are transparent about risks and charges, and consumers are protected from mis-selling, as happened with Payment Protection Insurance (PPI). This is distinct from the Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA), which focuses on whether financial firms are financially sound and unlikely to collapse.","reforge":{"stem":"How does the FCA's role differ from that of the Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA)?","options":{"A":"The FCA and PRA have identical responsibilities","B":"Demand determines productive capacity, so the frontier itself shifts in and out purely in response to changes in consumer spending","C":"The FCA focuses on firm conduct and consumer protection, while the PRA focuses on the financial soundness and stability of firms","D":"The FCA only regulates government spending"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"FMK-10","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"Why might speculation in financial markets sometimes lead to asset price bubbles?","options":{"A":"Because firms always produce more when prices rise. The economic explanation must account for incentives, trade-offs, market conditions, distribution and the time period; the relevant relationship is reversed, so the proposed answer cannot explain the outcome.","B":"If investors buy an asset expecting its price to keep rising rather than based on its underlying value, rising demand can push prices well above what is justified by fundamentals, until the bubble eventually bursts","C":"Government regulation makes speculative bubbles impossible","D":"Speculation eliminates volatility from asset prices entirely"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FMK-CRASH","scaffold":"Speculative behaviour means buying an asset in the hope of selling it later at a higher price, rather than for its underlying value or income. If enough investors do this simultaneously, demand-driven price rises can become self-reinforcing, pushing prices far above any reasonable estimate of fundamental value — a bubble. When sentiment shifts, prices can collapse rapidly, causing wider financial instability, as seen in the dot-com bubble and the 2008 housing bubble.","reforge":{"stem":"What typically happens when a speculative asset price bubble bursts?","options":{"A":"Prices continue rising smoothly without any correction","B":"Government regulation automatically reverses all losses","C":"A subsidy for every consumer, paid directly by government to lower the price that shoppers face in the market","D":"Prices fall sharply and rapidly as investor confidence collapses, often causing wider financial instability"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"ECON-FINANCE-01","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"An equity market allows firms mainly to:","options":{"A":"raise finance by issuing shares to investors","B":"set the official interest rate for the economy","C":"provide unemployment benefits","D":"control the quantity of imported goods"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FMK-01","scaffold":"Equity markets help firms raise capital, while investors share in returns and risk.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main purpose of an equity market for a company?","options":{"A":"To raise finance by issuing ownership shares to investors","B":"The FCA sets interest rates while the PRA regulates fiscal policy","C":"To distribute unemployment benefits","D":"To control the volume of imports"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.5.1"},{"id":"ECON-FINANCE-02","spec":"2.5.1","stem":"A forward market can help an importer manage:","options":{"A":"the risk that all domestic wages will fall","B":"the risk that a future exchange rate will change","C":"the number of workers available in a region","D":"Forward markets predict future exchange rates with certainty."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FMK-02","scaffold":"A forward contract can lock in an exchange rate for a future transaction.","reforge":{"stem":"An importer agrees today to exchange currencies at a set rate in six months. What risk is being managed?","options":{"A":"The risk of an adverse future exchange-rate movement","B":"The risk that domestic wages will fall","C":"Forward markets allow you to buy currency at today's spot rate at any future date.","D":"The government's annual spending total"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.5.1"}]},"2.5.2":{"label":"Role of Banks","color":"#7c3aed","questions":[{"id":"BNK-01","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"The primary function of commercial banks is:","options":{"A":"Financial intermediation","B":"Setting interest rates for the economy.","C":"Printing money.","D":"Regulating other financial institutions."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BNK-FUNCTIONS","scaffold":"Commercial banks are intermediaries: they pool deposits (liabilities) and lend them out (assets), earning the spread between deposit and lending rates. They also: provide payment systems, offer financial products (insurance, investments), and — crucially — create money through the credit creation multiplier.","reforge":{"stem":"A bank pays 2% interest on savings accounts and charges 6% on loans. The 4% difference is called:","options":{"A":"Inflation.","B":"The base rate.","C":"The interest rate spread (or margin) — this is how the bank earns profit from its core intermediation role.","D":"Banks only ever lending money for periods shorter than one year, matching every loan exactly to the length of the deposits that funded it, leaving no gap between borrowing and lending timeframes at all."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"BNK-02","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"How do commercial banks 'create' money?","options":{"A":"They print banknotes in their vaults.","B":"Through the credit creation multiplier: when a bank receives a deposit","C":"Because liquidity risk only affects central banks, not commercial banks","D":"By raising interest rates."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-BNK-MONEY-CREATION","scaffold":"Example: £1,000 deposited, 10% reserve ratio. Bank lends £900 → deposited elsewhere → that bank lends £810 → and so on. Total money created = £1,000 × (1/0.1) = £10,000. Banks don't print money — they create it through lending. This is why bank regulation matters: excessive lending inflates the money supply.","reforge":{"stem":"If the reserve ratio increases from 10% to 20%, what happens to the credit creation multiplier?","options":{"A":"Because shadow banks are legally required to hold more capital than commercial banks","B":"It doubles.","C":"It is unchanged.","D":"It halves because banks hold more reserves, leaving less available to lend."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"BNK-03","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"Evaluate whether the government was right to bail out the banks in 2008. (4 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"Banks are 'systemically important' — their failure would have frozen lending, collapsing the payment system and triggering widespread business failures and unemployment (K+An). However, the bailout cost taxpayers billions, created moral hazard (banks expect future rescues, incentivising risk-taking), and diverted public funds from other priorities like health and education — the opportunity cost was significant (Ev).","tag":"MC-BNK-MORAL-HAZARD","scaffold":"The 2019 examiner report noted top answers discussed: systemic risk (bank failure spreads through the economy), the run on Northern Rock, moral hazard from the expectation of bailouts, and the opportunity cost of public funds. Both sides needed, with context."},{"id":"BNK-04","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"Which of the following is a regulatory measure introduced after the 2008 financial crisis?","options":{"A":"Abolishing all banking regulation.","B":"The number of customers a bank has relative to its competitors","C":"Merging all banks into one state bank.","D":"Higher capital requirements, so banks can absorb losses."},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-BNK-MORAL-HAZARD","scaffold":"Post-2008 reforms: (1) Higher capital/liquidity requirements (Basel III) — more reserves, less leverage. (2) Ring-fencing — separating retail banking from investment banking. (3) Stress testing — regulators check if banks can survive hypothetical shocks. (4) Living wills — plans for orderly failure without taxpayer bailouts. All aim to prevent 'too big to fail.'","reforge":{"stem":"Ring-fencing requires banks to:","options":{"A":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make.","B":"Separate retail banking from riskier investment banking to protect customers’ deposits.","C":"Merge with competitors.","D":"Stop lending entirely."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"BNK-05","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"Why are banks sometimes described as 'too big to fail'?","options":{"A":"Because their failure would cause systemic risk","B":"Because the government owns them.","C":"Because they hold all the country's gold reserves.","D":"Because large banks are always profitable."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BNK-MORAL-HAZARD","scaffold":"Systemic risk: banks lend to each other (interbank market). If one large bank fails → other banks lose money → they can't lend → credit freezes → businesses can't borrow → layoffs, closures, recession. This interconnectedness makes government reluctant to let them fail — but bailouts create moral hazard.","reforge":{"stem":"A policy of 'too big to fail' creates moral hazard because:","options":{"A":"Banks become too cautious.","B":"Banks only ever lending money for periods shorter than one year, matching every loan exactly to the length of the deposits that funded it, leaving no gap between borrowing and lending timeframes at all.","C":"Banks take excessive risks knowing they'll be rescued if things go wrong — the implicit government guarantee removes the downside, incentivising reckless behaviour.","D":"It increases competition."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"BNK-06","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"What is 'maturity transformation' in commercial banking?","options":{"A":"Banks converting foreign currency into domestic currency","B":"Banks borrowing short-term, such as via instant-access deposits, and lending long-term, such as via mortgages, bridging the mismatch between savers' and borrowers' time preferences","C":"Banks changing their legal structure over time","D":"Banks only ever lending money for periods shorter than one year, matching every loan exactly to the length of the deposits that funded it, leaving no gap between borrowing and lending timeframes at all."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-BNK-FUNCTIONS","scaffold":"Depositors generally want easy, short-notice access to their savings, while borrowers, especially for mortgages, want to repay over many years. Banks bridge this gap through maturity transformation, using a pool of short-term deposits to fund longer-term loans, relying on the fact that not all depositors will withdraw at once. This function is valuable but creates liquidity risk: if many depositors want their money back simultaneously, the bank may struggle to meet withdrawals even if it is fundamentally solvent.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does maturity transformation expose banks to liquidity risk?","options":{"A":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make.","B":"Because all bank loans and deposits have identical maturities","C":"Because banks never lend for periods longer than deposits are held","D":"Because banks fund long-term loans using short-term deposits, and a sudden wave of withdrawals may exceed the bank's readily available cash"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"BNK-07","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"What is a bank's 'capital ratio' intended to measure?","options":{"A":"The total value of a bank's physical branches","B":"The interest rate a bank charges on loans","C":"The proportion of a bank's assets funded by its own capital rather than borrowing, acting as a buffer against losses","D":"Banks only ever lending money for periods shorter than one year, matching every loan exactly to the length of the deposits that funded it, leaving no gap between borrowing and lending timeframes at all."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-BNK-MORAL-HAZARD","scaffold":"A bank's capital ratio compares its capital, funds provided by shareholders that absorb losses first, to its total risk-weighted assets. A higher capital ratio means the bank has a bigger buffer to absorb losses before depositors or the wider financial system are put at risk. Post-2008 regulation (Basel III) significantly raised minimum capital requirements for banks, precisely because many banks entered the crisis with capital buffers too thin to absorb the losses they faced.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did regulators raise minimum capital requirements for banks after the 2008 financial crisis?","options":{"A":"To ensure banks hold a larger buffer of their own capital to absorb losses, reducing the risk of failure and the need for taxpayer-funded bailouts","B":"To lower interest rates across the economy","C":"To increase the number of banks in the market","D":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"BNK-08","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"Which of the following best describes 'shadow banking'?","options":{"A":"Illegal banking activity conducted outside any legal system","B":"A bank's overseas branches only","C":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make.","D":"Financial institutions and activities that perform bank-like functions, such as lending, but operate outside traditional banking regulation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-BNK-STABILITY","scaffold":"Shadow banking refers to entities such as hedge funds, money market funds and some investment vehicles that carry out credit intermediation similar to banks but are not subject to the same regulatory oversight or capital requirements as licensed commercial banks. Because it sits outside much of the regulatory framework designed for banks, shadow banking can build up hidden risks in the financial system, which was a significant factor in the 2008 crisis.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might shadow banking pose a risk to overall financial stability?","options":{"A":"Because it is fully regulated in an identical way to commercial banks","B":"Because it can build up significant credit and liquidity risk outside the regulatory framework designed to monitor and limit risk-taking in traditional banks","C":"Banks only ever lending money for periods shorter than one year, matching every loan exactly to the length of the deposits that funded it, leaving no gap between borrowing and lending timeframes at all.","D":"Because shadow banking activity has no connection to the wider financial system"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"BNK-09","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"What is the primary purpose of deposit insurance schemes, such as the UK's Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS)?","options":{"A":"To protect depositors' savings up to a set limit if their bank fails, reducing the incentive for panicked bank runs","B":"Banks only ever lending money for periods shorter than one year, matching every loan exactly to the length of the deposits that funded it, leaving no gap between borrowing and lending timeframes at all.","C":"To fund government spending directly","D":"To set the interest rate banks pay on deposits"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BNK-STABILITY","scaffold":"Deposit insurance guarantees that depositors will get their money back, up to a limit, even if their bank collapses. This reduces the incentive for a bank run, since depositors know their savings are protected, they are less likely to rush to withdraw funds at the first sign of trouble, which in turn makes the banking system more stable overall.","reforge":{"stem":"How does deposit insurance help prevent a self-fulfilling bank run?","options":{"A":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make.","B":"It guarantees that share prices of banks never fall","C":"By assuring depositors their savings are protected, it removes much of the incentive to withdraw funds in a panic, reducing the chance that fear alone causes a bank to fail","D":"It requires depositors to keep their savings in cash at home"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"BNK-10","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"Why might excessive bank lending during an economic boom increase the risk of a future financial crisis?","options":{"A":"Because bank lending always falls during a boom","B":"Because rapid credit growth can fund increasingly risky borrowing and inflate asset price bubbles, which can unwind sharply once the economy slows","C":"Because lending during a boom has no effect on future risk","D":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-BNK-MORAL-HAZARD","scaffold":"During a boom, rising confidence and asset prices can encourage banks to lend more freely, sometimes to increasingly risky borrowers, fuelling further asset price rises in a self-reinforcing cycle. When the economy eventually slows or confidence turns, borrowers may struggle to repay, asset prices can fall sharply, and banks can face large losses simultaneously — a pattern central to both the 2008 crisis and many historical financial crises.","reforge":{"stem":"What tool might a central bank use to slow excessive credit growth during a boom before it becomes a systemic risk?","options":{"A":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make.","B":"Removing all capital requirements on banks","C":"Abolishing the central bank's regulatory powers entirely","D":"Macroprudential tools, such as raising banks' capital requirements or tightening mortgage lending limits, to restrain excessive credit growth"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"ECON-BANKS-01","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"Commercial banks create money mainly when they:","options":{"A":"make new loans that are credited to borrowers' accounts","B":"print banknotes for the central bank","C":"Banks converting foreign currency into domestic currency","D":"turn every deposit into physical cash"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BNK-MONEY-CREATION","scaffold":"Bank lending creates deposits, subject to capital, liquidity, regulation and borrowers' demand for credit.","reforge":{"stem":"How does a commercial bank usually create a deposit when it approves a new loan?","options":{"A":"It credits the borrower's account with a new deposit","B":"They cannot create money — only the central bank can.","C":"It collects a new tax from the borrower","D":"It converts every existing deposit into cash"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.5.2"},{"id":"ECON-BANKS-02","spec":"2.5.2","stem":"A lender of last resort provides emergency liquidity to:","options":{"A":"any firm seeking a guaranteed profit","B":"a bank or financial institution facing a temporary shortage of funds","C":"Because liquidity risk only affects central banks, not commercial banks","D":"exporters needing a lower world price"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-BNK-FUNCTIONS","scaffold":"Central-bank lending can limit contagion when a solvent institution cannot obtain short-term funding.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a central bank act as lender of last resort?","options":{"A":"To provide emergency liquidity to a solvent institution facing a temporary funding shortage","B":"It removes all risk from banking so that bank failures become impossible, since depositors and shareholders alike are guaranteed against every possible loss the bank could ever make.","C":"To remove income tax from households","D":"To set a lower world price for exporters"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.5.2"}]},"2.5.3":{"label":"Central Banks","color":"#475569","questions":[{"id":"CB-01","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the Bank of England?","options":{"A":"Setting government spending and tax rates.","B":"Setting the base rate via the MPC.","C":"Acting as lender of last resort to commercial banks.","D":"Issuing banknotes and maintaining financial stability."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-BNK-FUNCTIONS","scaffold":"BoE functions: (1) Monetary policy — setting the base rate (MPC). (2) Lender of last resort — providing emergency liquidity to banks. (3) Issuing banknotes. (4) Financial stability — macro-prudential regulation (FPC). (5) Banker to the government. Fiscal policy (G and T) is set by the Treasury/Chancellor, not the BoE.","reforge":{"stem":"During a banking crisis, the BoE provides emergency loans to a struggling bank to prevent its collapse. This is the BoE acting as:","options":{"A":"Fiscal authority.","B":"Tax collector.","C":"Lender of last resort — providing liquidity when no other lender will, to prevent a systemic crisis.","D":"Microprudential regulation covers the whole economy while macroprudential regulation covers only one firm"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CB-02","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"Central bank independence means:","options":{"A":"The central bank sets its own inflation target independently of government.","B":"The central bank has operational independence over HOW to meet the target","C":"The central bank operates as a private commercial entity.","D":"The government plays no role whatsoever in monetary policy."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MON-INDEPENDENCE","scaffold":"Key distinction: the GOVERNMENT sets the target (2% CPI). The BOE decides how to hit it (base rate, QE). This prevents politicians cutting rates before elections. The BoE is accountable (Governor reports to Parliament, letters when target missed) but operationally free. Independence ≠ no oversight.","reforge":{"stem":"A politician says: 'The Bank of England should cut rates to help my constituency before the election.' Why does central bank independence prevent this?","options":{"A":"The BoE can only raise rates, never cut them.","B":"The BoE always ignores politicians.","C":"Because independence means the central bank no longer needs to consider inflation at all, focusing solely on financial stability and banking supervision instead of its original inflation-targeting remit.","D":"The MPC sets rates using economic data and the inflation target, not political pressure."},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"CB-03","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"The Financial Policy Committee (FPC) is responsible for:","options":{"A":"Setting interest rates.","B":"Collecting taxes.","C":"Macro-prudential regulation","D":"Running the stock exchange."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-CB-FIN-STABILITY","scaffold":"MPC = monetary policy (interest rates, QE). FPC = financial stability (macro-prudential — looking at the system as a whole). PRA = micro-prudential (supervising individual firms). The FPC can impose counter-cyclical capital buffers, restrict loan-to-value ratios on mortgages, etc. Created after 2008 to fill the regulatory gap.","reforge":{"stem":"The FPC notices that mortgage lending is growing rapidly with very high loan-to-income ratios. What action might it take?","options":{"A":"Impose limits on loan-to-income ratios to prevent a housing bubble and protect financial stability.","B":"Print more money.","C":"Microprudential regulation covers the whole economy while macroprudential regulation covers only one firm","D":"Reduce government spending."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"CB-04","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"Why might controlling inflation conflict with other macroeconomic objectives? (4 marks)","type":"short_answer","model_answer":"To control inflation, the BoE raises interest rates, which reduces AD and dampens price pressures (K). However, this also slows economic growth and may increase unemployment — the Phillips curve trade-off (An). Furthermore, higher rates strengthen the exchange rate, making exports less competitive and potentially worsening the current account deficit (An). The central bank faces a dilemma: prioritising inflation control comes at the cost of growth, jobs, and trade competitiveness — particularly when inflation is cost-push rather than demand-pull, since rate rises address demand but not supply-side causes (Ev).","tag":"MC-MON-LIMITS","scaffold":"The 2019 examiner report stressed: top answers started from the inflation control objective and then showed how pursuing it creates conflicts with growth, employment, and the current account. Don't just list conflicts — show the causal chain from monetary tightening to each trade-off."},{"id":"CB-05","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"Which of the following best describes the role of the central bank in maintaining financial stability?","options":{"A":"Monitoring systemic risks, regulating bank capital requirements","B":"It refuses to lend to any financial institution under any circumstances","C":"Guaranteeing all investments against loss.","D":"Ensuring all banks make a profit."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-CB-FIN-STABILITY","scaffold":"Financial stability ≠ guaranteeing profits. It means: (1) preventing systemic collapse (lender of last resort). (2) Macro-prudential oversight (FPC monitors risks like asset bubbles, excessive leverage). (3) Stress tests (can banks survive a severe recession scenario?). (4) Resolution planning (living wills — how to wind down a failing bank without a bailout). Goal: a resilient system, not zero risk.","reforge":{"stem":"A stress test reveals that a bank would become insolvent if house prices fell 30%. What is the likely regulatory response?","options":{"A":"Nothing — house prices won't fall that much.","B":"Because the lender of last resort function is never actually used in practice, since central banks have never once needed to lend emergency funds to a struggling bank throughout their entire history.","C":"The regulator may require more capital, less property exposure or restructuring.","D":"The bank is immediately closed."},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CB-06","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"What does it mean for the Bank of England to act as 'lender of last resort'?","options":{"A":"Because interest rates have no effect on inflation or growth during stagflation, making monetary policy entirely powerless until the stagflation period ends on its own without intervention.","B":"It provides emergency liquidity to solvent banks facing short-term funding problems, when no other institution is willing or able to lend to them","C":"It sets the interest rate for all UK mortgages directly","D":"It only lends money to the UK government"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-CB-FIN-STABILITY","scaffold":"As lender of last resort, the central bank can lend to a bank that is fundamentally sound but temporarily unable to access funding elsewhere, preventing a short-term liquidity problem from turning into a full collapse that could threaten the wider financial system. This function is meant for genuinely solvent institutions in a liquidity crunch, not to bail out banks that are actually insolvent — though in practice, as in 2008, this distinction can become difficult to judge in a fast-moving crisis.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the lender of last resort function normally reserved for solvent banks facing temporary liquidity problems, rather than insolvent ones?","options":{"A":"Because solvency and liquidity mean exactly the same thing","B":"Because the lender of last resort function is never actually used in practice, since central banks have never once needed to lend emergency funds to a struggling bank throughout their entire history.","C":"Because insolvent banks are always immediately closed by law","D":"Because lending to a fundamentally insolvent bank would simply delay an inevitable failure while risking public money, whereas a solvent bank can repay once its liquidity problem passes"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"CB-07","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"What is 'forward guidance' as a tool of monetary policy?","options":{"A":"A central bank predicting exchange rates for exporters","B":"A method of printing new banknotes in advance of demand","C":"A central bank communicating its likely future intentions for interest rates, to influence current spending, saving and investment decisions","D":"Because interest rates have no effect on inflation or growth during stagflation, making monetary policy entirely powerless until the stagflation period ends on its own without intervention."},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MON-LIMITS","scaffold":"Forward guidance involves the central bank signalling how it expects interest rates or other policy to move in future, aiming to shape households' and firms' expectations today. If a central bank signals rates will stay low for an extended period, this can encourage firms to invest and households to spend now, since future borrowing costs are expected to remain low, influencing the economy even before any actual rate change occurs.","reforge":{"stem":"How can forward guidance influence the economy even before the central bank actually changes interest rates?","options":{"A":"By shaping expectations of future borrowing costs, it can influence spending, saving and investment decisions being made today","B":"It has no effect on behaviour until rates actually change","C":"It automatically changes the exchange rate immediately","D":"Because the lender of last resort function is never actually used in practice, since central banks have never once needed to lend emergency funds to a struggling bank throughout their entire history."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"CB-08","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"What is the main aim of macroprudential regulation, carried out by bodies like the Bank of England's Financial Policy Committee (FPC)?","options":{"A":"To set interest rates for the whole economy","B":"Because the lender of last resort function is never actually used in practice, since central banks have never once needed to lend emergency funds to a struggling bank throughout their entire history.","C":"To manage the government's day-to-day spending decisions","D":"To identify and reduce risks to the stability of the financial system as a whole, rather than focusing only on individual firms"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-CB-FIN-STABILITY","scaffold":"Macroprudential regulation looks at systemic risk, dangers to the financial system as a whole, such as excessive overall credit growth or interconnected institutions all being exposed to the same risk, rather than just whether one individual bank is sound (which is 'microprudential' regulation, the PRA's role). The FPC can use tools like restricting mortgage lending ratios across the whole banking sector to cool an overheating housing market before it threatens financial stability.","reforge":{"stem":"How does macroprudential regulation differ from microprudential regulation?","options":{"A":"They are two names for exactly the same regulatory approach","B":"Macroprudential regulation focuses on risks to the whole financial system, while microprudential regulation focuses on the safety and soundness of individual firms","C":"Macroprudential regulation only applies to central banks themselves","D":"Because independence means the central bank no longer needs to consider inflation at all, focusing solely on financial stability and banking supervision instead of its original inflation-targeting remit."},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"CB-09","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"Why is central bank independence often seen as helping to control inflation credibly?","options":{"A":"Because an independent central bank is less likely to be pressured into keeping interest rates artificially low for short-term political gain ahead of an election, which could otherwise fuel inflation","B":"Because independence guarantees inflation will always be exactly zero","C":"Because an independent central bank is required by law to always cut interest rates","D":"Because independence means the central bank no longer needs to consider inflation at all, focusing solely on financial stability and banking supervision instead of its original inflation-targeting remit."},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MON-INDEPENDENCE","scaffold":"If elected politicians directly controlled interest rates, they might be tempted to keep rates low before an election to boost short-term growth, even if this risked higher inflation later — a problem known as the political business cycle. By giving day-to-day interest rate decisions to an independent central bank, such as the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee, independent since 1997, governments aim to make low, stable inflation a more credible long-term commitment.","reforge":{"stem":"What problem does central bank independence aim to solve regarding interest rate decisions before elections?","options":{"A":"The problem of banks lending too little during a recession","B":"Because interest rates have no effect on inflation or growth during stagflation, making monetary policy entirely powerless until the stagflation period ends on its own without intervention.","C":"The risk that politicians could keep interest rates artificially low before an election for short-term political gain, at the cost of higher future inflation","D":"The need to abolish inflation targeting entirely"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"CB-10","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"Why might the Bank of England face a conflict between its inflation target and other macroeconomic objectives?","options":{"A":"Because inflation and unemployment are always completely unrelated to one another","B":"Because raising interest rates to reduce inflation can also slow economic growth and raise unemployment in the short run, creating a trade-off between objectives","C":"Because the inflation target is the only objective the Bank of England has ever been given","D":"Because independence means the central bank no longer needs to consider inflation at all, focusing solely on financial stability and banking supervision instead of its original inflation-targeting remit."},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MON-LIMITS","scaffold":"Tackling inflation with higher interest rates makes borrowing more expensive, discouraging spending and investment, which helps to reduce inflationary pressure but can also slow growth and raise unemployment in the short run. This is why the Monetary Policy Committee must weigh the inflation target against the wider health of the economy when setting interest rates, particularly during periods of stagflation when inflation and weak growth occur together.","reforge":{"stem":"During a period of 'stagflation' (high inflation alongside weak growth), why is setting interest rates especially difficult for the Bank of England?","options":{"A":"Because stagflation never actually occurs in a real economy","B":"Because interest rates have no effect on inflation or growth during stagflation, making monetary policy entirely powerless until the stagflation period ends on its own without intervention.","C":"Because stagflation only affects the exchange rate and nothing else","D":"Because raising rates to fight inflation risks worsening the weak growth and unemployment problem, while cutting rates to support growth risks fuelling inflation further"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"ECON-CENTRAL-01","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"The Financial Policy Committee is primarily concerned with:","options":{"A":"reducing risks to the stability of the financial system as a whole","B":"setting the income of every household","C":"choosing the government's annual tax rates","D":"Macroprudential regulation only applies to central banks themselves"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-CB-FIN-STABILITY","scaffold":"Macroprudential policy looks across the financial system and can address risks such as excessive lending.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main focus of macroprudential regulation?","options":{"A":"Reducing risks that could destabilise the financial system as a whole","B":"It refuses to lend to any financial institution under any circumstances","C":"Setting every household's annual income","D":"Writing the government's tax budget"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.5.3"},{"id":"ECON-CENTRAL-02","spec":"2.5.3","stem":"Central-bank independence is intended to:","options":{"A":"ensure the central bank can ignore its legal objectives","B":"allow monetary decisions to be made without short-term political pressure","C":"Because an independent central bank is required by law to always cut interest rates","D":"make interest rates identical in every country"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MON-INDEPENDENCE","scaffold":"Operational independence can support credibility while the government retains responsibility for the target framework.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main economic reason for giving a central bank operational independence?","options":{"A":"To reduce short-term political pressure on monetary decisions","B":"To let it ignore its statutory objectives","C":"Because independence guarantees inflation will always be exactly zero","D":"To make interest rates identical across all countries"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-a-econ-2.5.3"}]},"3.1.1":{"label":"Business Growth & Objectives","color":"#1e3a5f","questions":[{"id":"TH3-GO-01","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Why might a business choose to grow rather than remain small?","options":{"A":"It can lower unit costs and strengthen its market position","B":"To guarantee that every single decision the firm makes becomes far less complex","C":"To remove all financial risk from future operations","D":"To avoid needing any additional finance or staff"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-GROWTH-MOTIVE","scaffold":"Growth may reduce average costs through economies of scale, increase bargaining power and make the firm more resilient. It can also create diseconomies, require finance and make coordination harder, so the benefits are not automatic.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible advantage of external growth for a business?","options":{"A":"It always removes the need for integration planning","B":"It guarantees that the firm's average costs will fall almost immediately","C":"It can increase market share quickly through a merger or takeover","D":"It prevents the new owners from changing strategy"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-GO-02","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A firm has falling average costs as its output increases. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Its marginal revenue is below its average revenue","B":"It is benefiting from economies of scale","C":"It is experiencing diseconomies of scale","D":"Its total revenue must be falling at every output"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-FALLING-AC","scaffold":"Economies of scale occur when long-run average cost falls as output rises. They can arise from purchasing, technical, managerial, financial or risk-bearing advantages. A firm may eventually face diseconomies if it becomes too large.","reforge":{"stem":"Which change would most clearly create a purchasing economy of scale?","options":{"A":"A company reduces output while keeping its suppliers unchanged","B":"A business gives each manager responsibility for more departments","C":"A factory replaces specialist machinery with manual labour","D":"A large firm buys inputs in bulk at a lower unit price"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-GO-03","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is the main purpose of a business objective?","options":{"A":"To replace all financial information with a single target","B":"To ensure that every stakeholder receives the same return","C":"To set a goal for decisions and performance","D":"To prevent managers from changing plans when conditions change"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-OBJECTIVES","scaffold":"Objectives give a business direction and allow managers to assess performance. Profit maximisation, revenue growth, survival and social or environmental aims may conflict, so objectives can change with circumstances.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a new business prioritise survival over profit maximisation?","options":{"A":"It may need to build customers and cash flow before seeking higher returns","B":"Survival objectives require the firm to keep making a loss forever afterwards","C":"Profit is irrelevant to all firms during their first year","D":"A survival objective removes the need to monitor cash flow"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-GO-04","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Which situation is most likely to create diseconomies of scale?","options":{"A":"A factory spreads fixed cost across a larger output","B":"A retailer negotiates cheaper delivery for a bulk order","C":"A firm trains staff to use a more productive process","D":"Communication slows in a large hierarchy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-DISECONOMIES","scaffold":"Diseconomies arise when long-run average cost increases as a firm expands. Poor communication, weak coordination and lower staff motivation can raise unit costs in a very large organisation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one likely result of diseconomies of scale?","options":{"A":"Average cost falls because all fixed costs disappear","B":"Average cost rises as the organisation becomes harder to coordinate","C":"Productivity rises without any change in resources","D":"The firm will automatically gain a much larger share of every single market"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-GO-05","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is the difference between organic and external growth?","options":{"A":"It expands within the firm; external growth uses another business","B":"Organic growth uses a merger; external growth uses retained profit","C":"Organic growth always increases risk; external growth always reduces it","D":"Organic growth concerns costs; external growth concerns revenue only"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-ORGANIC","scaffold":"Organic growth expands a firm through its own investment, products or outlets. External growth occurs through merger, takeover or another agreement with an existing business. Each route has different speed, finance and integration risks.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of organic growth?","options":{"A":"A supermarket acquires a competing chain","B":"A bank merges with a building society","C":"A manufacturer opens a new factory using retained profit","D":"A firm simply purchases the existing brand name of a rival company"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-GO-06","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Why might shareholders prefer a profit objective to a revenue objective?","options":{"A":"Revenue includes costs, so it always gives a better measure of efficiency","B":"It leaves surplus for dividends and investment","C":"Profit objectives guarantee that sales volumes will be maximised","D":"Revenue objectives prevent firms from competing on price"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-PRINCIPAL-AGENT","scaffold":"Revenue is the money received from sales, while profit is revenue minus costs. Shareholders may value profit because it provides returns and funds investment, although a firm may sacrifice short-run profit to grow revenue or market share.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm increases revenue but profit falls. Which is the most likely explanation?","options":{"A":"Its average revenue must have doubled","B":"Its total fixed costs disappeared altogether as output steadily increased","C":"Its sales volume must have fallen to zero","D":"Its costs increased by more than its additional sales income"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-07","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Why might a business pursue growth even when short-run profit falls?","options":{"A":"Growth can always remove every business risk","B":"Larger firms never face diseconomies of scale","C":"Expansion may raise market power and lower unit costs","D":"A growing firm is guaranteed to charge the highest market price"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-GROWTH-MOTIVE","scaffold":"A firm may accept lower short-run profit to build market share, spread fixed costs, strengthen bargaining power and create future economies of scale. Growth also brings risks: finance costs, integration problems and diseconomies of scale. The correct judgement depends on whether the expected long-run gains outweigh the immediate cost and risk.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one possible long-run advantage of business growth?","options":{"A":"It can spread fixed costs across a larger output","B":"It guarantees that demand will remain unchanged","C":"It removes the need for managers to coordinate staff","D":"It prevents competitors from entering every market"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-08","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A firm has a high market share but weak customer loyalty. What is the main risk?","options":{"A":"Its current sales cannot be measured accurately","B":"Market share makes advertising legally impossible","C":"Its fixed costs will automatically fall to zero","D":"Rivals may win customers if conditions change"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-MARKET-SHARE","scaffold":"Market share measures the firm's proportion of market sales at a point in time; it does not prove that customers are loyal. If switching costs are low, rivals can win customers through lower prices, better quality or stronger branding. A firm should therefore track retention and repeat purchases as well as market share.","reforge":{"stem":"Which measure would best complement market-share data?","options":{"A":"The number of suppliers used by every rival","B":"Repeat-purchase rates among customers","C":"The total land area occupied by the firm","D":"The age of the firm's oldest machine"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-09","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a stakeholder objective?","options":{"A":"A goal reflecting an affected group's interests","B":"To replace all financial information with a single target","C":"A legal ban on business growth","D":"A measure of output per worker"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-OBJECTIVES","scaffold":"Stakeholders include owners, workers, customers, suppliers, government and communities. Their objectives may conflict, so firms often face trade-offs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which group is an internal stakeholder?","options":{"A":"A competing firm","B":"Local residents only","C":"Employees","D":"A government overseas"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-10","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Why might managers pursue sales revenue growth?","options":{"A":"Revenue includes costs, so it always gives a better measure of efficiency","B":"It may increase market share and managerial rewards","C":"It removes all business risk","D":"Sales growth makes costs irrelevant"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-PRINCIPAL-AGENT","scaffold":"Managers may value growth, status or bonuses linked to sales. However, revenue growth can reduce profit if costs rise faster than sales.","reforge":{"stem":"A risk of prioritising revenue over profit is:","options":{"A":"Shareholders receive higher profit automatically","B":"Costs must become zero","C":"Demand is guaranteed forever","D":"Sales may rise while margins fall"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-11","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is satisficing?","options":{"A":"Choosing the lowest wage by law","B":"Survival objectives require the firm to keep making a loss forever afterwards","C":"Accepting a satisfactory outcome rather than maximising one goal","D":"Guaranteeing the largest market share"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-OBJECTIVES","scaffold":"Managers may satisfice because information is imperfect, objectives conflict and maximisation is costly to calculate. The firm aims for an acceptable result.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a manager satisfice?","options":{"A":"Information and time for optimisation are limited","B":"To ensure that every stakeholder receives the same return","C":"Profit is never measured","D":"The firm has no stakeholders"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-12","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a takeover?","options":{"A":"A government raises corporation tax","B":"To guarantee that every single decision the firm makes becomes far less complex","C":"Two firms share a supplier only","D":"One business acquires control of another"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-EXTERNAL-GROWTH","scaffold":"A takeover is external growth in which one firm gains control of another. It can be rapid but may create integration and culture problems.","reforge":{"stem":"A merger differs from organic growth because it:","options":{"A":"Uses only retained profit","B":"Combines with an existing business","C":"Never changes market share","D":"A firm opens a new branch internally"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-13","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is horizontal integration?","options":{"A":"Combining with a firm at the same production stage","B":"To remove all financial risk from future operations","C":"Buying a retailer at a later stage","D":"Expanding without another firm"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-EXTERNAL-GROWTH","scaffold":"Horizontal integration joins competitors at the same stage and may increase market share. Vertical integration joins firms at different stages of the supply chain.","reforge":{"stem":"A manufacturer buys another manufacturer. This is:","options":{"A":"Forward vertical integration","B":"Backward vertical integration","C":"Horizontal integration","D":"Conglomerate diversification"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-14","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is diversification?","options":{"A":"Deliberately reducing its own product variety under standard assumptions in a typical market","B":"Expanding into a new product or market area","C":"Buying only an identical competitor","D":"Lowering every firm's fixed cost"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-CONGLOMERATE","scaffold":"Diversification spreads risk across products or markets but may take the firm away from its expertise and create management complexity.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might diversification reduce risk?","options":{"A":"It prevents any diseconomies","B":"It guarantees that the firm's average costs will fall almost immediately","C":"It removes the need for finance","D":"Weak performance in one market may be offset elsewhere"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-15","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a financial economy of scale?","options":{"A":"To avoid needing any additional finance or staff","B":"Large firms never need loans","C":"Large firms can borrow on more favourable terms","D":"Demand is fixed by banks"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-EOS-SOURCES","scaffold":"Large firms may be seen as less risky and have better access to capital markets, reducing borrowing costs relative to smaller firms.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may a large company raise finance more cheaply?","options":{"A":"Lenders may view it as more established and diversified","B":"It has no costs to repay","C":"A company reduces output while keeping its suppliers unchanged","D":"Small firms cannot borrow at all"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-16","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Why can external growth be faster than organic growth?","options":{"A":"Organic growth uses a merger; external growth uses retained profit","B":"It never needs finance","C":"It avoids all integration work","D":"It acquires existing capacity and customers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-ORGANIC","scaffold":"A merger or takeover can give immediate access to staff, facilities, brands and customers. The speed comes with valuation and integration risks.","reforge":{"stem":"A drawback of rapid external growth is:","options":{"A":"The firm gains no assets","B":"Different systems and cultures may be difficult to combine","C":"Market share must fall","D":"Organic growth always increases risk; external growth always reduces it"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-17","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a principal-agent problem?","options":{"A":"Managers may pursue their interests rather than owners' aims","B":"Profit objectives guarantee that sales volumes will be maximised","C":"Workers own every business","D":"Prices are fixed by regulators"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-PRINCIPAL-AGENT","scaffold":"Owners delegate decisions to managers, whose incentives and information may differ. Governance, monitoring and performance pay can reduce the conflict.","reforge":{"stem":"Which measure could reduce a principal-agent problem?","options":{"A":"Giving managers no objectives","B":"Removing all financial reporting","C":"Linking managers' rewards to long-term performance","D":"Revenue objectives prevent firms from competing on price"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-18","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a sunk cost?","options":{"A":"A cost that varies with output","B":"A cost that cannot be recovered once incurred","C":"A future cost avoided by entry","D":"It always removes the need for integration planning"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-CONTESTABILITY","scaffold":"Sunk costs should not affect a rational decision about future actions, although high sunk costs can deter new firms from entering a market.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is most likely to be a sunk cost?","options":{"A":"A future wage bill","B":"It prevents the new owners from changing strategy","C":"A refundable deposit","D":"A non-refundable advertising campaign"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-19","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Why can sunk costs create a barrier to entry?","options":{"A":"Entry becomes costless","B":"Incumbents have no fixed costs","C":"Entrants risk losing large costs if the venture fails","D":"A business gives each manager responsibility for more departments"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-CONTESTABILITY","scaffold":"High irreversible costs increase the risk of entering and make markets less contestable, especially when demand is uncertain.","reforge":{"stem":"Which industry feature would make entry more risky?","options":{"A":"Large non-recoverable setup costs","B":"A refundable licence fee","C":"Many buyers with clear information","D":"Low capital requirements"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-20","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a contestable market?","options":{"A":"A market where prices never change","B":"A factory replaces specialist machinery with manual labour","C":"A market with one legal supplier","D":"A market where entry and exit are relatively easy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-CONTESTABILITY","scaffold":"Contestability depends on low barriers and low sunk costs. Even a concentrated market may behave competitively if the threat of entry is credible.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can the threat of entry discipline an incumbent?","options":{"A":"It guarantees monopoly profits","B":"It may keep prices near competitive levels","C":"It prevents innovation","D":"Its marginal revenue is below its average revenue"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-21","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a price leader?","options":{"A":"A firm whose price changes influence rivals","B":"Its total revenue must be falling at every output","C":"The lowest-cost customer","D":"A firm that never changes price"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-COMPETITION-STRATEGY","scaffold":"In oligopoly, a dominant firm may set or signal a price that rivals follow. This can reduce visible competition and may attract regulatory attention.","reforge":{"stem":"Price leadership is most associated with:","options":{"A":"A single household","B":"Perfect competition","C":"Interdependent oligopolistic firms","D":"A regulator setting every market price"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-22","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a non-price competition strategy?","options":{"A":"To prevent managers from changing plans when conditions change","B":"Competing through quality, branding or service","C":"Leaving the market","D":"Setting a legal tax"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-COMPETITION-STRATEGY","scaffold":"Firms may avoid price cuts and compete through design, reliability, advertising, loyalty schemes or customer service, particularly in oligopoly.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of non-price competition?","options":{"A":"Profit is irrelevant to all firms during their first year","B":"A lower list price only","C":"A government tariff","D":"A loyalty scheme offering service benefits"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-23","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Why can economies of scope reduce average cost?","options":{"A":"Output is reduced to one good","B":"A survival objective removes the need to monitor cash flow","C":"The same resources support several products","D":"Demand becomes perfectly elastic"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-EOS-SOURCES","scaffold":"Economies of scope arise when producing a range of products together costs less than producing each separately, for example through shared distribution or branding.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm uses one delivery network for three product lines. This may create:","options":{"A":"Economies of scope","B":"A shortage of capital","C":"A fall in productive capacity","D":"A pure monopoly by definition"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-24","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a social enterprise?","options":{"A":"A business owned only by a bank","B":"A firm with no customers","C":"The firm will automatically gain a much larger share of every single market","D":"A business trading to achieve social aims as well as income"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-NONPROFIT-FORMS","scaffold":"Social enterprises use commercial activity to pursue objectives such as employment, housing or environmental improvement. They still need viable revenue and cost control.","reforge":{"stem":"Why must a social enterprise monitor profit or surplus?","options":{"A":"A factory spreads fixed cost across a larger output","B":"Financial viability supports its social mission","C":"It cannot employ workers","D":"Social aims remove opportunity cost"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-BO-25","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"What is a cooperative?","options":{"A":"A business owned and controlled by its members","B":"A retailer negotiates cheaper delivery for a bulk order","C":"A government department only","D":"A business with no voting rights"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-NONPROFIT-FORMS","scaffold":"Cooperatives can be owned by workers, consumers or producers. Member control may improve participation, though decision-making can be slower.","reforge":{"stem":"A worker cooperative is primarily owned by:","options":{"A":"Only its customers","B":"A distant regulator","C":"Its employees","D":"A single rival firm"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3C-03","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Which description of profit maximisation is accurate?","options":{"A":"Choosing the largest workforce","B":"Choosing output where marginal revenue equals marginal cost","C":"It increases output whenever marginal cost exceeds marginal revenue","D":"Minimising market share"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-PROFITMAX","scaffold":"Profit is maximised where marginal revenue equals marginal cost. Below that output the next unit adds more to revenue than to cost, so making it raises profit; beyond it the next unit costs more than it earns. State MR = MC and then apply it — average cost and price tell you the size of the profit, not the output that maximises it.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm keeps expanding output as long as producing one more unit adds more to revenue than it costs to make, and stops exactly where that stops being true. What objective is it pursuing?","options":{"A":"Profit maximisation, producing where marginal revenue equals marginal cost","B":"Choosing the largest possible workforce","C":"Maximising revenue regardless of cost","D":"Deliberately minimising its market share under standard assumptions in a typical market"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3C-04","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising profit maximisation. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Setting the selling price exactly equal to the average fixed cost","B":"Choosing the largest workforce","C":"Choosing output where marginal revenue equals marginal cost","D":"Minimising market share"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-PROFITMAX","scaffold":"Profit is maximised where marginal revenue equals marginal cost. Below that output the next unit adds more to revenue than to cost, so making it raises profit; beyond it the next unit costs more than it earns. State MR = MC and then apply it — average cost and price tell you the size of the profit, not the output that maximises it.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm keeps expanding output as long as producing one more unit adds more to revenue than it costs to make, and stops exactly where that stops being true. What objective is it pursuing?","options":{"A":"Choosing the largest possible workforce","B":"Profit maximisation, producing where marginal revenue equals marginal cost","C":"Maximising revenue regardless of cost","D":"Deliberately minimising its market share under standard assumptions in a typical market"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3C-05","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising revenue maximisation. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Avoiding all expansion","B":"It is experiencing diseconomies of scale","C":"Setting price equal to cost","D":"Seeking the greatest total sales income"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-REVMAX","scaffold":"Revenue is maximised where marginal revenue is zero, the point at which total revenue stops rising. That lies at a higher output and lower price than profit maximisation, which is why firms chasing market share or a product launch pursue it. Comparing it with MR = MC is how the divergence between managerial and owner objectives is examined.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm sets its price and output purely to bring in the largest possible amount of total sales income, even if this is not the most profitable point. What objective is this?","options":{"A":"Deliberately avoiding all expansion","B":"Organic growth concerns costs; external growth concerns revenue only","C":"Revenue maximisation, seeking the greatest total sales income","D":"Setting price exactly equal to cost"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3C-06","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising revenue maximisation. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Seeking the greatest total sales income","B":"A firm trains staff to use a more productive process","C":"Setting price equal to cost","D":"Avoiding all expansion"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-REVMAX","scaffold":"Revenue is maximised where marginal revenue is zero, the point at which total revenue stops rising. That lies at a higher output and lower price than profit maximisation, which is why firms chasing market share or a product launch pursue it. Comparing it with MR = MC is how the divergence between managerial and owner objectives is examined.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm sets its price and output purely to bring in the largest possible amount of total sales income, even if this is not the most profitable point. What objective is this?","options":{"A":"Deliberately avoiding all expansion","B":"A firm simply purchases the existing brand name of a rival company","C":"Setting price exactly equal to cost","D":"Revenue maximisation, seeking the greatest total sales income"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3C-07","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Which description of sales maximisation is accurate?","options":{"A":"Average cost falls because all fixed costs disappear","B":"Seeking the greatest quantity sold","C":"Maximising tax revenue","D":"Reducing customer numbers"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-SALESMAX","scaffold":"Sales maximisation means selling the greatest quantity subject to a constraint, usually earning at least normal profit. It sits at a higher output and lower price again than revenue maximisation. It is a volume objective rather than a revenue or profit one, and separating the three is exactly what this question tests.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm prices aggressively to sell as many units as possible, prioritising volume over the income each unit brings in. What objective is this?","options":{"A":"Sales maximisation, seeking the greatest quantity sold","B":"Seeking the lowest possible sales volume","C":"Maximising the tax revenue it pays","D":"Its total fixed costs disappeared altogether as output steadily increased"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3C-08","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising sales maximisation. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Maximising tax revenue","B":"Productivity rises without any change in resources","C":"Seeking the greatest quantity sold","D":"Reducing customer numbers"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-SALESMAX","scaffold":"Sales maximisation means selling the greatest quantity subject to a constraint, usually earning at least normal profit. It sits at a higher output and lower price again than revenue maximisation. It is a volume objective rather than a revenue or profit one, and separating the three is exactly what this question tests.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm prices aggressively to sell as many units as possible, prioritising volume over the income each unit brings in. What objective is this?","options":{"A":"Seeking the lowest possible sales volume","B":"Sales maximisation, seeking the greatest quantity sold","C":"Maximising the tax revenue it pays","D":"A growing firm is guaranteed to charge the highest market price"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3C-09","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising survival as an objective. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Selling every asset","B":"It removes the need for managers to coordinate staff","C":"Eliminating all borrowing","D":"Prioritising continued operation under pressure"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-SURVIVAL","scaffold":"Survival becomes the objective when a firm meets a shock or a downturn: the aim is to keep operating and cover variable costs rather than to maximise anything. It explains why firms trade at a loss in the short run instead of shutting down, and why they may cut price hard to protect cash flow.","reforge":{"stem":"During a severe downturn, a struggling firm cuts prices below normal levels simply to keep trading and avoid closure, setting aside profit for now. What objective is it pursuing?","options":{"A":"Selling off every asset it owns","B":"Eliminating all of its borrowing","C":"Survival, prioritising continued operation under pressure","D":"Deliberately minimising its market share under standard assumptions in a typical market"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3C-10","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising survival as an objective. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Prioritising continued operation under pressure","B":"It prevents competitors from entering every market","C":"Eliminating all borrowing","D":"Selling every asset"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-SURVIVAL","scaffold":"Survival becomes the objective when a firm meets a shock or a downturn: the aim is to keep operating and cover variable costs rather than to maximise anything. It explains why firms trade at a loss in the short run instead of shutting down, and why they may cut price hard to protect cash flow.","reforge":{"stem":"During a severe downturn, a struggling firm cuts prices below normal levels simply to keep trading and avoid closure, setting aside profit for now. What objective is it pursuing?","options":{"A":"Selling off every asset it owns","B":"Eliminating all of its borrowing","C":"Buying a retailer that sells the finished cars under standard assumptions in a typical market","D":"Survival, prioritising continued operation under pressure"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3C-11","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Which description of organic growth is accurate?","options":{"A":"A takeover of a rival","B":"Expansion through the firm's own investment","C":"Organic growth uses a merger; external growth uses retained profit","D":"A government acquisition"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-ORGANIC","scaffold":"Organic, or internal, growth comes from the firm reinvesting its own resources in new capacity, products or markets. It is slower but easier to control and finance. External growth through merger or takeover is faster but carries integration and culture risk — and that contrast is the evaluation the question is looking for.","reforge":{"stem":"Which application is most consistent with organic growth?","options":{"A":"It never needs finance","B":"A takeover of a rival","C":"Organic growth always increases risk; external growth always reduces it","D":"Expansion through the firm's own investment"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3C-12","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising organic growth. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Organic growth concerns costs; external growth concerns revenue only","B":"A takeover of a rival","C":"Expansion through the firm's own investment","D":"A government acquisition"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-ORGANIC","scaffold":"Organic, or internal, growth comes from the firm reinvesting its own resources in new capacity, products or markets. It is slower but easier to control and finance. External growth through merger or takeover is faster but carries integration and culture risk — and that contrast is the evaluation the question is looking for.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm grows larger over several years purely by reinvesting its own profits into new stores, equipment and staff, without buying any other business. What type of growth is this?","options":{"A":"Organic growth, expansion through the firm's own investment","B":"A takeover of a rival firm","C":"A merger with one of its suppliers","D":"A firm simply purchases the existing brand name of a rival company"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3C-13","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising backward vertical integration. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Larger firms never face diseconomies of scale","B":"Buying a retailer","C":"Buying a direct rival","D":"Buying a business at an earlier supply stage"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-VERT-BACK","scaffold":"Backward vertical integration is a merger with a business at an earlier stage of the supply chain, that is a supplier of inputs, usually to secure control of supply, cost or quality. Forward integration moves the other way, towards the customer, and horizontal integration is a merger at the same stage of the chain.","reforge":{"stem":"A car manufacturer buys the company that supplies it with steel, taking control of an earlier stage of its own supply chain. What type of integration is this?","options":{"A":"Buying a retailer that sells the finished cars under standard assumptions in a typical market","B":"Backward vertical integration, buying a business at an earlier supply stage","C":"Buying a direct rival car manufacturer","D":"Opening an entirely unrelated product line"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3C-14","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising backward vertical integration. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Buying a business at an earlier supply stage","B":"Buying a retailer","C":"Buying a direct rival","D":"It guarantees that demand will remain unchanged"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-VERT-BACK","scaffold":"Backward vertical integration is a merger with a business at an earlier stage of the supply chain, that is a supplier of inputs, usually to secure control of supply, cost or quality. Forward integration moves the other way, towards the customer, and horizontal integration is a merger at the same stage of the chain.","reforge":{"stem":"A car manufacturer buys the company that supplies it with steel, taking control of an earlier stage of its own supply chain. What type of integration is this?","options":{"A":"Buying a retailer that sells the finished cars under standard assumptions in a typical market","B":"Buying a direct rival car manufacturer","C":"Backward vertical integration, buying a business at an earlier supply stage","D":"Opening an entirely unrelated product line"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3C-15","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Which description of forward vertical integration is accurate?","options":{"A":"Buying a raw-material supplier","B":"Buying a business at a later supply stage","C":"Growth can always remove every business risk","D":"Closing distribution outlets"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-VERT-FORWARD","scaffold":"Forward vertical integration is a merger with a business at a later stage of the supply chain — a distributor or retailer — normally to secure access to the market and capture the retail margin. Backward integration moves towards the supplier instead. Ask whether the target is closer to the customer and the direction settles itself.","reforge":{"stem":"A coffee-bean grower buys a chain of coffee shops that sells drinks made from its beans directly to customers. What type of integration is this?","options":{"A":"Buying a raw-material supplier","B":"To guarantee that every single decision the firm makes becomes far less complex","C":"Closing down its distribution outlets","D":"Forward vertical integration, buying a business at a later supply stage"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3C-16","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising forward vertical integration. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Its current sales cannot be measured accurately","B":"Buying a raw-material supplier","C":"Buying a business at a later supply stage","D":"Closing distribution outlets"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-VERT-FORWARD","scaffold":"Forward vertical integration is a merger with a business at a later stage of the supply chain — a distributor or retailer — normally to secure access to the market and capture the retail margin. Backward integration moves towards the supplier instead. Ask whether the target is closer to the customer and the direction settles itself.","reforge":{"stem":"A coffee-bean grower buys a chain of coffee shops that sells drinks made from its beans directly to customers. What type of integration is this?","options":{"A":"Forward vertical integration, buying a business at a later supply stage","B":"Buying a raw-material supplier","C":"It guarantees that the firm's average costs will fall almost immediately","D":"Closing down its distribution outlets"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3C-17","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising conglomerate integration. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Market share makes advertising legally impossible","B":"Buying a direct rival","C":"Buying a supplier only","D":"Combining with a business in an unrelated market"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-CONGLOMERATE","scaffold":"Conglomerate integration is a merger between firms in unrelated markets, and its main motive is diversification: spreading risk so a downturn in one market does not sink the group. It offers no obvious synergies, which is why the evaluation usually asks whether managers can run businesses they do not understand.","reforge":{"stem":"A company that makes breakfast cereal buys a firm that manufactures garden furniture, two businesses with no connection in their supply chains. What type of integration is this?","options":{"A":"Buying a direct rival in the cereal market","B":"Conglomerate integration, combining with a business in an unrelated market","C":"Buying only a raw-material supplier","D":"Deliberately reducing its own product variety under standard assumptions in a typical market"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3C-18","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising conglomerate integration. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Combining with a business in an unrelated market","B":"Buying a direct rival","C":"Buying a supplier only","D":"Because banks automatically lower interest rates for larger firms"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-CONGLOMERATE","scaffold":"Conglomerate integration is a merger between firms in unrelated markets, and its main motive is diversification: spreading risk so a downturn in one market does not sink the group. It offers no obvious synergies, which is why the evaluation usually asks whether managers can run businesses they do not understand.","reforge":{"stem":"A company that makes breakfast cereal buys a firm that manufactures garden furniture, two businesses with no connection in their supply chains. What type of integration is this?","options":{"A":"Buying a direct rival in the cereal market","B":"Buying only a raw-material supplier","C":"Conglomerate integration, combining with a business in an unrelated market","D":"Deliberately reducing its own product variety under standard assumptions in a typical market"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3C-19","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Which description of managerial economies of scale is accurate?","options":{"A":"Bulk buying lowers input price","B":"Specialist managers improve organisational efficiency","C":"Banks lower interest rates","D":"Managerial economies, as managers earn higher salaries"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-EOS-MANAGERIAL","scaffold":"Managerial economies of scale arise when a larger firm can employ specialists — a dedicated finance director, a marketing team — whose fixed salary is spread across far more units, lowering average cost. Technical economies come from plant and machinery, risk-bearing economies from diversification. Naming the right type is where the mark is.","reforge":{"stem":"As a firm grows larger, it can afford to hire specialist finance, marketing and operations managers instead of relying on one generalist. Why does this reduce average costs?","options":{"A":"Because bulk buying lowers the price of inputs","B":"Buying a retailer that sells the finished cars under standard assumptions in a typical market","C":"Because advertising no longer reaches any consumers","D":"Managerial economies of scale, as specialist managers improve organisational efficiency"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3C-20","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising managerial economies of scale. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Banks lower interest rates","B":"Bulk buying lowers input price","C":"Specialist managers improve organisational efficiency","D":"Risk-bearing economies, as risk is spread across shareholders"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-EOS-MANAGERIAL","scaffold":"Managerial economies of scale arise when a larger firm can employ specialists — a dedicated finance director, a marketing team — whose fixed salary is spread across far more units, lowering average cost. Technical economies come from plant and machinery, risk-bearing economies from diversification. Naming the right type is where the mark is.","reforge":{"stem":"As a firm grows larger, it can afford to hire specialist finance, marketing and operations managers instead of relying on one generalist. Why does this reduce average costs?","options":{"A":"Managerial economies of scale, as specialist managers improve organisational efficiency","B":"Because bulk buying lowers the price of inputs","C":"Deliberately reducing its own product variety under standard assumptions in a typical market","D":"Because advertising no longer reaches any consumers"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3C-21","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising technical economies of scale. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Tax rates fall automatically","B":"Managers earn higher salaries","C":"A supermarket acquires a competing chain","D":"Large-scale machinery lowers unit cost"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-EOS-TECHNICAL","scaffold":"Technical economies of scale come from the production process itself: larger, more specialised or indivisible machinery that only becomes viable at high output, lowering cost per unit. Managerial economies come from specialist staff, purchasing economies from bulk buying. The exam wants the correct category, not simply that bigger is cheaper.","reforge":{"stem":"A large factory installs an automated production line that produces far more units per pound spent than the manual process a smaller rival uses. What kind of economy of scale does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Managerial economies, as managers earn higher salaries","B":"Technical economies of scale, where large-scale machinery lowers unit cost","C":"Survival objectives require the firm to keep making a loss forever afterwards","D":"A fall in tax rates that happens automatically"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3C-22","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising technical economies of scale. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Large-scale machinery lowers unit cost","B":"Managers earn higher salaries","C":"Its sales volume must have fallen to zero","D":"Tax rates fall automatically"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-EOS-TECHNICAL","scaffold":"Technical economies of scale come from the production process itself: larger, more specialised or indivisible machinery that only becomes viable at high output, lowering cost per unit. Managerial economies come from specialist staff, purchasing economies from bulk buying. The exam wants the correct category, not simply that bigger is cheaper.","reforge":{"stem":"A large factory installs an automated production line that produces far more units per pound spent than the manual process a smaller rival uses. What kind of economy of scale does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Managerial economies, as managers earn higher salaries","B":"The firm will automatically gain a much larger share of every single market","C":"Technical economies of scale, where large-scale machinery lowers unit cost","D":"A fall in tax rates that happens automatically"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3C-23","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Which description of risk-bearing economies of scale is accurate?","options":{"A":"A firm removes every risk","B":"A large firm spreads risk across products or markets","C":"Customers switching brand after every single purchase","D":"Consumers face one product"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-EOS-RISK","scaffold":"Risk-bearing economies of scale come from diversification: a firm operating across several products or markets can absorb a downturn in one of them, so the cost of bearing risk per unit falls. Note that it reduces exposure rather than removing risk, and it is distinct from technical and managerial economies.","reforge":{"stem":"A large multinational sells hundreds of different products across many countries, so a failure in one market barely affects its overall profits. What kind of economy of scale does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"A firm that has removed every risk entirely","B":"A small firm that avoids external finance","C":"Deliberately minimising its market share under standard assumptions in a typical market","D":"Risk-bearing economies of scale, spreading risk across products or markets"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3C-24","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising risk-bearing economies of scale. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A company reduces output while keeping its suppliers unchanged","B":"A firm removes every risk","C":"A large firm spreads risk across products or markets","D":"Consumers face one product"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-FIRM-EOS-RISK","scaffold":"Risk-bearing economies of scale come from diversification: a firm operating across several products or markets can absorb a downturn in one of them, so the cost of bearing risk per unit falls. Note that it reduces exposure rather than removing risk, and it is distinct from technical and managerial economies.","reforge":{"stem":"A large multinational sells hundreds of different products across many countries, so a failure in one market barely affects its overall profits. What kind of economy of scale does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Risk-bearing economies of scale, spreading risk across products or markets","B":"A firm that has removed every risk entirely","C":"A small firm that avoids external finance","D":"To guarantee that every single decision the firm makes becomes far less complex"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3C-25","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising brand loyalty. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"A fall in product quality","B":"A business gives each manager responsibility for more departments","C":"A legal barrier only","D":"Customers repeatedly prefer a familiar firm or product"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-FIRM-BRAND","scaffold":"Brand loyalty is the tendency of customers to keep buying a familiar brand rather than switch on price. It makes demand less price-elastic and acts as a barrier to entry, since a new firm must overcome the incumbent's reputation as well as match its price. That is why it appears in questions about market power.","reforge":{"stem":"Customers keep buying the same well-known brand of trainers even when cheaper, similar alternatives are available on the shelf next to them. What does this behaviour show?","options":{"A":"A fall in the product's quality","B":"Brand loyalty, customers repeatedly preferring a familiar firm or product","C":"Survival objectives require the firm to keep making a loss forever afterwards","D":"A legal barrier preventing customers from switching"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3C-26","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"A student is revising brand loyalty. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Customers repeatedly prefer a familiar firm or product","B":"A factory replaces specialist machinery with manual labour","C":"A legal barrier only","D":"A fall in product quality"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-FIRM-BRAND","scaffold":"Brand loyalty is the tendency of customers to keep buying a familiar brand rather than switch on price. It makes demand less price-elastic and acts as a barrier to entry, since a new firm must overcome the incumbent's reputation as well as match its price. That is why it appears in questions about market power.","reforge":{"stem":"Customers keep buying the same well-known brand of trainers even when cheaper, similar alternatives are available on the shelf next to them. What does this behaviour show?","options":{"A":"A fall in the product's quality","B":"The firm will automatically gain a much larger share of every single market","C":"Brand loyalty, customers repeatedly preferring a familiar firm or product","D":"A legal barrier preventing customers from switching"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3C-27","spec":"3.1.1","stem":"Which description of market share is accurate?","options":{"A":"The firm's total profit","B":"A firm's sales as a proportion of market sales","C":"Its fixed costs will automatically fall to zero","D":"The average market price"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-MARKET-SHARE","scaffold":"Market share is a firm's sales expressed as a proportion of total sales in the market, so it measures relative position rather than size. A firm can grow its sales and still lose share if the market grows faster, which is why share rather than revenue is used to judge competitive success and market power.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's sales make up 30% of all sales in its industry during a given year. What is this percentage a measure of?","options":{"A":"The firm's total profit for the year","B":"Revenue includes costs, so it always gives a better measure of efficiency","C":"The average price charged across the market","D":"Market share, a firm's sales as a proportion of total market sales"},"correct":"D"}}]},"3.2.1":{"label":"Revenue, Costs & Profit","color":"#1e3a5f","questions":[{"id":"TH3-RP-01","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A firm's total revenue is £90,000 and total cost is £72,000. What is its profit?","options":{"A":"£18,000","B":"£62,000","C":"£90,000","D":"£162,000"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-PROFIT-CALC","scaffold":"Profit equals total revenue minus total cost: £90,000 − £72,000 = £18,000. Total revenue is the money received from sales; total cost includes fixed and variable costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A business earns £145,000 revenue and has £120,000 total cost. Its profit is:","options":{"A":"£145,000","B":"£120,000","C":"£25,000","D":"£265,000"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-02","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Which cost does not change when a firm's output changes in the short run?","options":{"A":"A raw material cost","B":"A fixed cost such as rent","C":"A delivery cost per unit","D":"A sales commission paid per item"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-FIXED","scaffold":"Fixed costs do not vary directly with output in the short run, although they may change over a longer period. Variable costs rise as output increases, while total cost equals fixed cost plus variable cost.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is most likely to be a variable cost for a bakery?","options":{"A":"The annual rent of its premises","B":"The insurance premium for the year","C":"The manager's fixed salary","D":"The flour used in each loaf"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-03","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A firm's average cost falls from £8 to £6 when output rises. What has happened?","options":{"A":"Its marginal revenue has decreased","B":"Its total cost has become zero","C":"Its unit cost has decreased","D":"Its fixed costs have necessarily increased"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-FALLING-AC","scaffold":"Average cost is total cost divided by output. A fall from £8 to £6 means each unit costs less on average, which may result from spreading fixed costs or improving productivity.","reforge":{"stem":"If total cost is £48,000 at an output of 8,000 units, average cost is:","options":{"A":"£6 per unit","B":"£8 per unit","C":"£40,000 per unit","D":"£56,000 per unit"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-04","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is the break-even point of a business?","options":{"A":"The output where profit is guaranteed to be maximum","B":"The output where fixed costs are at their highest","C":"The price that makes variable cost equal to zero","D":"The output where total revenue equals total cost"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"At break-even, total revenue equals total cost, so the firm makes neither a profit nor a loss. Break-even analysis can show a safety margin, but it relies on assumptions such as constant price and unit variable cost.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm's break-even output rises. Which could explain this?","options":{"A":"Its selling price has risen with costs unchanged","B":"Its fixed costs have increased","C":"Its variable cost per unit has fallen","D":"Its contribution per unit has increased"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-05","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Why might a firm use contribution costing for a short-run decision?","options":{"A":"It shows how each unit helps cover fixed costs","B":"It proves that all fixed costs will disappear after one month","C":"It calculates the exact market demand without any assumptions","D":"It shows that a product with a low price cannot make a contribution"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-CONTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Contribution per unit equals price minus variable cost per unit. Total contribution helps cover fixed costs; after fixed costs are covered, further contribution becomes profit. It is useful for spare-capacity decisions but should not replace long-run cost analysis.","reforge":{"stem":"A product sells for £12 and has a variable cost of £7. Its contribution per unit is:","options":{"A":"£12","B":"£7","C":"£5","D":"£19"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-06","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Which condition is needed for a firm to maximise profit?","options":{"A":"It produces where average cost is always at its minimum","B":"It produces where marginal revenue equals marginal cost","C":"It sets price equal to total cost for every unit","D":"It increases output whenever marginal cost exceeds marginal revenue"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-FIRM-PROFITMAX","scaffold":"Profit is maximised at the output where marginal revenue equals marginal cost, provided the marginal cost curve cuts marginal revenue from below. Producing beyond this point adds more to cost than to revenue.","reforge":{"stem":"If marginal cost is below marginal revenue at the current output, what should a profit-seeking firm consider?","options":{"A":"Reducing output immediately","B":"Setting the selling price exactly equal to the average fixed cost","C":"Closing because profit must be negative","D":"Increasing output until marginal cost rises to marginal revenue"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-07","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A firm's average revenue is £18 and average cost is £15 at its current output. What is its profit per unit?","options":{"A":"£18","B":"£15","C":"£3","D":"£33"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-PROFIT-CALC","scaffold":"Profit per unit is average revenue minus average cost: £18 − £15 = £3. Total profit would require multiplying this figure by the number of units sold. Average figures are useful for comparing unit performance, but they do not reveal the firm's total profit without an output figure.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm has average revenue of £24 and average cost of £19. Its profit per unit is:","options":{"A":"£5","B":"£19","C":"£24","D":"£43"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-08","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Why can marginal cost eventually rise as output increases?","options":{"A":"Fixed costs rise with every additional unit","B":"Total revenue must fall at higher output","C":"Average revenue becomes equal to the market price","D":"Diminishing returns can raise marginal cost"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-MARGINAL","scaffold":"In the short run, at least one factor is fixed. Adding more variable inputs to a fixed factor eventually creates diminishing marginal returns, so each extra unit requires disproportionately more resources and marginal cost rises. This is why the marginal cost curve is often U-shaped. Fixed costs do not themselves change with each extra unit in the short run.","reforge":{"stem":"What does diminishing marginal returns imply for marginal cost?","options":{"A":"Each extra unit becomes cheaper without limit","B":"More input is needed per extra unit","C":"Fixed costs increase whenever variable input is added","D":"Total revenue becomes independent of the output level"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-09","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is total revenue?","options":{"A":"Price multiplied by quantity sold","B":"Total cost divided by output","C":"Profit minus fixed cost","D":"The insurance premium for the year"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-REVENUE","scaffold":"Total revenue is the money received from sales: TR = P × Q. It is distinct from profit, which subtracts total cost.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm sells 400 units at £6 each. Total revenue is:","options":{"A":"£406","B":"£394","C":"£2,400","D":"£2,406"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-10","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is average revenue?","options":{"A":"Total cost per firm","B":"Revenue per unit of output","C":"Profit from the last unit","D":"Total revenue before any sales"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-REVENUE","scaffold":"Average revenue equals total revenue divided by output. In many markets it is equal to the price received per unit.","reforge":{"stem":"If total revenue is £900 from 300 units, average revenue is:","options":{"A":"£1,200","B":"£300","C":"£600","D":"£3"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-11","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is marginal revenue?","options":{"A":"Its fixed costs have necessarily increased","B":"Revenue per worker on average","C":"Revenue from selling one more unit","D":"The highest possible price"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-REVENUE","scaffold":"Marginal revenue is the change in total revenue from an additional unit. It helps determine the profit-maximising output with marginal cost.","reforge":{"stem":"If total revenue rises from £1,000 to £1,018 after one extra sale, marginal revenue is:","options":{"A":"£18","B":"£982","C":"£1,000","D":"£1,018"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-12","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is total fixed cost?","options":{"A":"The cost of the final unit","B":"The output where profit is guaranteed to be maximum","C":"Revenue from fixed assets","D":"A cost unchanged by output in the short run"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-FIXED","scaffold":"Rent and some salaries may remain fixed as output changes in the short run. Fixed cost exists even when output is zero.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is most likely a fixed cost for a theatre?","options":{"A":"Catering ingredients per show","B":"Annual building rent","C":"Ticket processing per booking","D":"Royalties per performance"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-13","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is total variable cost?","options":{"A":"The combined cost that changes with output","B":"Rent paid regardless of output","C":"Revenue from variable prices","D":"The output where fixed costs are at their highest"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-VARIABLE","scaffold":"Variable costs such as materials, packaging and piece-rate labour rise as output increases. Total cost equals fixed cost plus variable cost.","reforge":{"stem":"If output doubles, which cost is most likely to rise?","options":{"A":"A fixed licence fee","B":"Annual rent","C":"Materials used per unit","D":"The original share capital"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-14","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is average fixed cost?","options":{"A":"Variable cost divided by price","B":"Fixed cost divided by output","C":"Profit divided by sales","D":"The cost of one extra unit"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-FIXED","scaffold":"AFC falls as output rises because the same fixed cost is spread across more units. This is a source of internal economies of scale.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does average fixed cost usually fall as output expands?","options":{"A":"Price must fall","B":"The price that makes variable cost equal to zero","C":"Variable cost becomes zero","D":"The fixed cost is spread over more units"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-15","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is average variable cost?","options":{"A":"Revenue divided by output","B":"The difference between price and profit","C":"Variable cost divided by output","D":"The cost of all machinery"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-VARIABLE","scaffold":"Average variable cost shows the variable cost per unit. It often falls initially through productivity gains before rising with diminishing returns.","reforge":{"stem":"A variable cost of £2,000 is incurred for 500 units. AVC is:","options":{"A":"£4","B":"£250","C":"£1,500","D":"£2,500"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-16","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Why does marginal cost often cut average cost at its minimum?","options":{"A":"Revenue determines cost curves","B":"Fixed cost becomes zero there","C":"Fixed costs increase whenever variable input is added","D":"A marginal value pulls the average towards itself"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-MARGINAL","scaffold":"If marginal cost is below average cost, it pulls the average down; if above, it pulls it up. The turning point occurs where MC equals the minimum AC.","reforge":{"stem":"When marginal cost is below average cost, average cost is likely to:","options":{"A":"Rise","B":"Fall","C":"Remain fixed by definition","D":"Equal marginal revenue"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-17","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is a firm's contribution per unit?","options":{"A":"Price minus variable cost per unit","B":"It proves that all fixed costs will disappear after one month","C":"Total cost minus revenue","D":"Revenue divided by output"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-CONTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Contribution per unit helps cover fixed costs and then profit: contribution = price − AVC. It is useful for short-run decisions with spare capacity.","reforge":{"stem":"A product sells for £20 and has variable cost £13. Contribution per unit is:","options":{"A":"£20","B":"£13","C":"£7","D":"£33"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-18","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is the margin of safety?","options":{"A":"Total revenue above total cost at zero output","B":"Actual output above the break-even output","C":"Fixed cost below variable cost","D":"The maximum possible market size"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Margin of safety shows how far output can fall before the firm reaches break-even. A larger margin gives more protection against weaker demand.","reforge":{"stem":"If actual output is 8,000 and break-even is 5,000, margin of safety is:","options":{"A":"13,000 units","B":"5,000 units","C":"8,000 units","D":"3,000 units"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-19","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What assumption is common in simple break-even analysis?","options":{"A":"Output cannot change","B":"Costs never exist","C":"Selling price per unit remains constant","D":"Its selling price has risen with costs unchanged"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Break-even charts often assume constant price, constant variable cost per unit and a clear separation of fixed and variable costs. Real markets may violate these assumptions.","reforge":{"stem":"Why may a break-even forecast be unreliable?","options":{"A":"Price or unit cost may change as output changes","B":"Revenue is always measurable","C":"The output where profit is guaranteed to be maximum","D":"Firms never have capacity limits"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-20","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is an explicit cost?","options":{"A":"A benefit from free labour","B":"A forgone owner opportunity","C":"It calculates the exact market demand without any assumptions","D":"A direct money payment made by the firm"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-NORMAL-PROFIT","scaffold":"Explicit costs include wages, rent, energy and materials paid for in money. Economic profit also recognises implicit opportunity costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an explicit cost for a restaurant?","options":{"A":"The owner's forgone salary elsewhere","B":"A monthly rent payment","C":"The owner's leisure time","D":"The value of a family-owned building"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-21","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is an implicit cost?","options":{"A":"The opportunity cost of resources owned by the firm","B":"It shows that a product with a low price cannot make a contribution","C":"A tax shown on an invoice","D":"Revenue from a sale"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-NORMAL-PROFIT","scaffold":"Implicit costs reflect what owner-provided resources could earn elsewhere, such as the owner's time or a building the firm already owns.","reforge":{"stem":"An owner works without taking a salary. The implicit cost is:","options":{"A":"The firm's total revenue","B":"Zero in every calculation","C":"The income the owner could earn elsewhere","D":"It produces where average cost is always at its minimum"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-22","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is economic profit?","options":{"A":"Revenue minus variable cost only","B":"Revenue minus explicit and implicit costs","C":"Sales revenue before costs","D":"It sets price equal to total cost for every unit"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-NORMAL-PROFIT","scaffold":"Economic profit includes opportunity costs, so it can be lower than accounting profit. A zero economic profit can still mean resources earn their normal return.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm has accounting profit but zero economic profit. This means:","options":{"A":"Fixed costs rise with every additional unit","B":"It makes no sales","C":"It has no fixed costs","D":"It covers explicit and opportunity costs"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-23","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is normal profit?","options":{"A":"Revenue when output is zero","B":"Total revenue becomes independent of the output level","C":"The minimum return needed to keep resources employed","D":"A guaranteed dividend for owners"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-NORMAL-PROFIT","scaffold":"Normal profit is an implicit cost: the return required to keep the entrepreneur and other resources in their current use. It is included in total economic cost.","reforge":{"stem":"When economic profit is zero, a firm is earning:","options":{"A":"Normal profit","B":"No return at all","C":"A monopoly rent automatically","D":"Only accounting loss"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-24","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is productive efficiency?","options":{"A":"Maximising total revenue regardless of cost","B":"Charging the highest price","C":"Producing where demand is zero","D":"Producing at the minimum average cost"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MICRO-ALLOCATIVE","scaffold":"Productive efficiency occurs at the lowest point of average cost, meaning output is produced using the least resources per unit.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm at minimum average cost is likely to be:","options":{"A":"Allocatively inefficient by definition","B":"Productively efficient","C":"Operating at zero output","D":"Making no economic decision"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3-RP-25","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"What is allocative efficiency?","options":{"A":"Resources produce what consumers value at MC = price","B":"Maximum productive capacity, reached whenever an economy operates exactly on its production possibility frontier","C":"All firms earn monopoly profit","D":"The firm uses no scarce resources"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MICRO-ALLOCATIVE","scaffold":"Allocative efficiency occurs when price equals marginal cost, so the value consumers place on the last unit equals the opportunity cost of resources used.","reforge":{"stem":"In a competitive market, allocative efficiency is associated with:","options":{"A":"Marginal cost equal to zero","B":"Price above average revenue","C":"Price equal to marginal cost","D":"Output below the market equilibrium"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3COST-03","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Which description of total cost is accurate?","options":{"A":"Revenue minus profit","B":"Fixed cost plus variable cost","C":"The cost of producing one extra unit","D":"The cost of one extra unit"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-TOTAL","scaffold":"Total cost is fixed cost plus variable cost at a given level of output. Fixed costs do not change with output in the short run; variable costs rise as more is produced. Every other measure in this topic — average cost, marginal cost — is derived from this one, so identifying the two components correctly comes first.","reforge":{"stem":"Which application is most consistent with total cost?","options":{"A":"£62,000","B":"Revenue minus profit","C":"A sales commission paid per item","D":"Fixed cost plus variable cost"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3COST-04","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising total cost. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"The annual rent of its premises","B":"Revenue minus profit","C":"Fixed cost plus variable cost","D":"The cost of one extra unit"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-TOTAL","scaffold":"Total cost is fixed cost plus variable cost at a given level of output. Fixed costs do not change with output in the short run; variable costs rise as more is produced. Every other measure in this topic — average cost, marginal cost — is derived from this one, so identifying the two components correctly comes first.","reforge":{"stem":"A bakery adds up everything it spends producing 500 loaves in a week, both its rent and its flour and labour costs. What is this overall figure called?","options":{"A":"Total cost, the sum of fixed cost and variable cost","B":"Price multiplied by output","C":"Revenue minus profit","D":"It proves that all fixed costs will disappear after one month"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3COST-05","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising marginal cost. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Average revenue becomes equal to the market price","B":"Total cost per worker","C":"Fixed cost divided by sales","D":"The change in total cost from one extra unit"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-MARGINAL","scaffold":"Marginal cost is the addition to total cost from producing one more unit: the change in total cost divided by the change in output. It is a rate of change, not a level, which is why it is set against marginal revenue to find the profit-maximising output. Average cost, by contrast, is the cost per unit produced so far.","reforge":{"stem":"Which application is most consistent with marginal cost?","options":{"A":"Each extra unit becomes cheaper without limit","B":"The change in total cost from one extra unit","C":"Fixed cost divided by sales","D":"Revenue from the final unit"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3COST-06","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising marginal cost. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"The change in total cost from one extra unit","B":"Total cost per worker","C":"Revenue becoming constant regardless of output","D":"Revenue from the final unit"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-MARGINAL","scaffold":"Marginal cost is the addition to total cost from producing one more unit: the change in total cost divided by the change in output. It is a rate of change, not a level, which is why it is set against marginal revenue to find the profit-maximising output. Average cost, by contrast, is the cost per unit produced so far.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory works out exactly how much extra it costs to produce one additional unit beyond what it currently makes. What is this additional cost called?","options":{"A":"Total cost per worker","B":"Marginal cost, the change in total cost from one extra unit","C":"Fixed cost divided by sales","D":"It calculates the exact market demand without any assumptions"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3COST-07","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Which description of average total cost is accurate?","options":{"A":"Variable cost minus fixed cost","B":"Total cost divided by output","C":"Revenue per customer","D":"The price of capital"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-AVERAGE","scaffold":"Average total cost is total cost divided by output — the cost per unit. It falls while marginal cost lies below it and rises once marginal cost is above it, which is why the MC curve cuts ATC at its minimum point. Cost per unit and the cost of the next unit are different things.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm divides its total cost of production by the number of units it made, to find the cost per item. What is this figure called?","options":{"A":"The output where fixed costs are at their highest","B":"Revenue per customer","C":"Average total cost, total cost divided by output","D":"The price of capital"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3COST-08","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising average total cost. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Revenue per customer","B":"Variable cost minus fixed cost","C":"Total cost divided by output","D":"The price of capital"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-AVERAGE","scaffold":"Average total cost is total cost divided by output — the cost per unit. It falls while marginal cost lies below it and rises once marginal cost is above it, which is why the MC curve cuts ATC at its minimum point. Cost per unit and the cost of the next unit are different things.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm divides its total cost of production by the number of units it made, to find the cost per item. What is this figure called?","options":{"A":"It shows that a product with a low price cannot make a contribution","B":"Revenue per customer","C":"The price of capital","D":"Average total cost, total cost divided by output"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3COST-09","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising falling average cost. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Its marginal revenue is below its average revenue","B":"A fall in total output only","C":"Higher cost at every quantity","D":"Lower cost per unit as output expands"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-FALLING-AC","scaffold":"Average cost falls as output expands because fixed costs are spread over more units and economies of scale take effect; in the long run this is the downward-sloping section of the LRAC curve. It cannot fall for ever — past the minimum efficient scale, diseconomies push average cost back up.","reforge":{"stem":"As a factory increases its output, the cost per unit it produces gets progressively lower. What does this pattern describe?","options":{"A":"Falling average cost, where cost per unit falls as output expands","B":"A fall in total output only","C":"It increases output whenever marginal cost exceeds marginal revenue","D":"A fall in revenue, by definition"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3COST-10","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising falling average cost. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Lower cost per unit as output expands","B":"A fall in total output only","C":"Higher cost at every quantity","D":"It is experiencing diseconomies of scale"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-FALLING-AC","scaffold":"Average cost falls as output expands because fixed costs are spread over more units and economies of scale take effect; in the long run this is the downward-sloping section of the LRAC curve. It cannot fall for ever — past the minimum efficient scale, diseconomies push average cost back up.","reforge":{"stem":"As a factory increases its output, the cost per unit it produces gets progressively lower. What does this pattern describe?","options":{"A":"A fall in total output only","B":"Falling average cost, where cost per unit falls as output expands","C":"It shows that a product with a low price cannot make a contribution","D":"A fall in revenue, by definition"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3COST-11","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Which description of diseconomies of scale is accurate?","options":{"A":"A factory spreads fixed cost across a larger output","B":"Rising long-run average cost as scale grows","C":"A rise in consumer income","D":"A fall in market share"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-DISECONOMIES","scaffold":"Diseconomies of scale are rising long-run average costs as a firm grows beyond its minimum efficient scale, caused by coordination problems, communication failures and weaker motivation in a large organisation. The point is that cost per unit rises: total cost rises with output in every case, so that alone proves nothing.","reforge":{"stem":"As a company grows even larger, communication breaks down and management becomes less efficient, causing its long-run average cost per unit to start rising again. What is this called?","options":{"A":"The firm will automatically gain a much larger share of every single market","B":"A rise in consumer income","C":"Diseconomies of scale, rising long-run average cost as scale grows","D":"A fall in market share"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3COST-12","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising diseconomies of scale. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A rise in consumer income","B":"A retailer negotiates cheaper delivery for a bulk order","C":"Rising long-run average cost as scale grows","D":"A fall in market share"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-DISECONOMIES","scaffold":"Diseconomies of scale are rising long-run average costs as a firm grows beyond its minimum efficient scale, caused by coordination problems, communication failures and weaker motivation in a large organisation. The point is that cost per unit rises: total cost rises with output in every case, so that alone proves nothing.","reforge":{"stem":"As a company grows even larger, communication breaks down and management becomes less efficient, causing its long-run average cost per unit to start rising again. What is this called?","options":{"A":"The firm will automatically gain a much larger share of every single market","B":"A rise in consumer income","C":"A fall in market share","D":"Diseconomies of scale, rising long-run average cost as scale grows"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3COST-13","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising fixed cost. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"A cost that always falls","B":"A cost per extra unit","C":"Total revenue above total cost at zero output","D":"A cost unchanged by output in the short run"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-FIXED","scaffold":"A fixed cost does not vary with output in the short run — rent, insurance, loan interest — and is owed even at zero output. That is precisely why fixed costs are irrelevant to the short-run shutdown decision: only whether revenue covers variable cost matters. In the long run, every cost is variable.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm still has to pay its factory rent every month, whether it produces 100 units or none at all, at least in the short run. What type of cost is this rent?","options":{"A":"A fixed cost, unchanged by output in the short run","B":"A cost per extra unit produced","C":"Revenue earned from fixed capital","D":"It produces where average cost is always at its minimum"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3COST-14","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising fixed cost. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A cost unchanged by output in the short run","B":"A cost per extra unit","C":"The price that makes variable cost equal to zero","D":"A cost that always falls"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-FIXED","scaffold":"A fixed cost does not vary with output in the short run — rent, insurance, loan interest — and is owed even at zero output. That is precisely why fixed costs are irrelevant to the short-run shutdown decision: only whether revenue covers variable cost matters. In the long run, every cost is variable.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm still has to pay its factory rent every month, whether it produces 100 units or none at all, at least in the short run. What type of cost is this rent?","options":{"A":"A cost per extra unit produced","B":"A fixed cost, unchanged by output in the short run","C":"Revenue earned from fixed capital","D":"Setting the selling price exactly equal to the average fixed cost"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3COST-15","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Which description of variable cost is accurate?","options":{"A":"The difference between price and profit","B":"A cost that changes with output","C":"The firm's share capital","D":"A sunk advertising payment"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-VARIABLE","scaffold":"A variable cost changes directly with output: raw materials, piece-rate wages, power for the machines. With fixed cost it makes up total cost, and it alone sets the short-run shutdown point — if revenue cannot cover variable cost, producing makes the loss worse than stopping.","reforge":{"stem":"A bakery's spending on flour rises when it bakes more loaves and falls when it bakes fewer. What type of cost is this flour expense?","options":{"A":"Annual rent paid regardless of use","B":"The firm's share capital","C":"A variable cost, changing directly with output","D":"Its selling price has risen with costs unchanged"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3COST-16","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising variable cost. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"The firm's share capital","B":"The difference between price and profit","C":"A cost that changes with output","D":"A sunk advertising payment"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-VARIABLE","scaffold":"A variable cost changes directly with output: raw materials, piece-rate wages, power for the machines. With fixed cost it makes up total cost, and it alone sets the short-run shutdown point — if revenue cannot cover variable cost, producing makes the loss worse than stopping.","reforge":{"stem":"A bakery's spending on flour rises when it bakes more loaves and falls when it bakes fewer. What type of cost is this flour expense?","options":{"A":"Annual rent paid regardless of use","B":"The firm's share capital","C":"It sets price equal to total cost for every unit","D":"A variable cost, changing directly with output"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3COST-17","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising break-even revenue. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Revenue minus variable cost","B":"Revenue above all costs","C":"Revenue at zero output","D":"Revenue equal to total cost"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Break-even is where total revenue equals total cost, so the firm makes neither profit nor loss beyond normal profit. In per-unit terms it is where price equals average total cost. Below break-even a firm makes a loss but may still produce in the short run, as long as price covers average variable cost.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm calculates the exact level of sales revenue at which it neither makes a profit nor a loss, covering its costs precisely. What is this revenue level called?","options":{"A":"Break-even revenue, where revenue equals total cost","B":"Revenue minus variable cost","C":"Revenue earned above all costs","D":"Fixed costs increase whenever variable input is added"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3COST-18","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising break-even revenue. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Revenue equal to total cost","B":"Revenue above all costs","C":"Revenue at zero output","D":"Revenue minus variable cost"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-BREAKEVEN","scaffold":"Break-even is where total revenue equals total cost, so the firm makes neither profit nor loss beyond normal profit. In per-unit terms it is where price equals average total cost. Below break-even a firm makes a loss but may still produce in the short run, as long as price covers average variable cost.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm calculates the exact level of sales revenue at which it neither makes a profit nor a loss, covering its costs precisely. What is this revenue level called?","options":{"A":"Revenue minus variable cost","B":"Break-even revenue, where revenue equals total cost","C":"Revenue earned above all costs","D":"Total revenue becomes independent of the output level"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3COST-19","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Which description of shutdown point is accurate?","options":{"A":"The point of maximum revenue","B":"The output or price where variable costs are not covered","C":"The minimum average cost","D":"It proves that all fixed costs will disappear after one month"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-SHUTDOWN","scaffold":"The short-run shutdown point is where price falls below average variable cost: below it, producing loses more than closing, because fixed costs are paid either way. Between AVC and ATC the firm makes a loss but should keep producing, since the revenue is contributing something towards those fixed costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A struggling firm works out the price below which it would earn less than its variable costs, meaning it would lose less by closing than by continuing to trade. What is this critical point called?","options":{"A":"The point of maximum revenue","B":"It calculates the exact market demand without any assumptions","C":"The shutdown point, where variable costs are not covered","D":"A point of perfect competition"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3COST-20","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising shutdown point. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"The minimum average cost","B":"The point of maximum revenue","C":"The output or price where variable costs are not covered","D":"It increases output whenever marginal cost exceeds marginal revenue"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-SHUTDOWN","scaffold":"The short-run shutdown point is where price falls below average variable cost: below it, producing loses more than closing, because fixed costs are paid either way. Between AVC and ATC the firm makes a loss but should keep producing, since the revenue is contributing something towards those fixed costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A struggling firm works out the price below which it would earn less than its variable costs, meaning it would lose less by closing than by continuing to trade. What is this critical point called?","options":{"A":"The point of maximum revenue","B":"Setting the selling price exactly equal to the average fixed cost","C":"A point of perfect competition","D":"The shutdown point, where variable costs are not covered"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3COST-21","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising contribution. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"The value of imports","B":"It proves that all fixed costs will disappear after one month","C":"Fixed cost per unit","D":"Revenue left after variable cost"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-CONTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Contribution is revenue minus variable cost — what each sale contributes towards covering fixed costs and then towards profit. It underpins break-even analysis: fixed costs divided by contribution per unit gives the break-even quantity. It is not profit, because fixed costs have not yet been taken off.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm sells a product for £20, and it costs £12 in materials and direct labour to make one unit. What is the £8 difference called, before fixed costs are even considered?","options":{"A":"Contribution, the revenue left after variable cost","B":"The value of imports","C":"It calculates the exact market demand without any assumptions","D":"Fixed cost per unit"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3COST-22","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising contribution. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Revenue left after variable cost","B":"It shows that a product with a low price cannot make a contribution","C":"Fixed cost per unit","D":"The value of imports"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-CONTRIBUTION","scaffold":"Contribution is revenue minus variable cost — what each sale contributes towards covering fixed costs and then towards profit. It underpins break-even analysis: fixed costs divided by contribution per unit gives the break-even quantity. It is not profit, because fixed costs have not yet been taken off.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm sells a product for £20, and it costs £12 in materials and direct labour to make one unit. What is the £8 difference called, before fixed costs are even considered?","options":{"A":"The value of imports","B":"Contribution, the revenue left after variable cost","C":"It proves that all fixed costs will disappear after one month","D":"Fixed cost per unit"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3COST-23","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Which description of profit margin is accurate?","options":{"A":"Maximising total revenue regardless of cost","B":"Profit as a proportion of revenue or sales","C":"Revenue per worker only","D":"The firm's tax bill"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-MARGIN","scaffold":"Profit margin expresses profit as a proportion of revenue, so it measures how much of each pound of sales the firm keeps. It is a ratio, not an amount: total profit can rise while the margin falls, which is exactly what a volume-led strategy does. Compare margins between firms, absolute profit within one.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm earns £50,000 profit on £500,000 of sales revenue in a year. What is this 10% figure a measure of?","options":{"A":"It increases output whenever marginal cost exceeds marginal revenue","B":"Revenue earned per worker only","C":"Profit margin, profit as a proportion of revenue or sales","D":"The firm's total tax bill"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH3COST-24","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising profit margin. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Revenue per worker only","B":"Fixed costs rise with every additional unit","C":"Profit as a proportion of revenue or sales","D":"The firm's tax bill"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-COST-MARGIN","scaffold":"Profit margin expresses profit as a proportion of revenue, so it measures how much of each pound of sales the firm keeps. It is a ratio, not an amount: total profit can rise while the margin falls, which is exactly what a volume-led strategy does. Compare margins between firms, absolute profit within one.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm earns £50,000 profit on £500,000 of sales revenue in a year. What is this 10% figure a measure of?","options":{"A":"Setting the selling price exactly equal to the average fixed cost","B":"Revenue earned per worker only","C":"The firm's total tax bill","D":"Profit margin, profit as a proportion of revenue or sales"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH3COST-25","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising labour productivity. Which explanation is correct?","options":{"A":"Its marginal revenue has decreased","B":"Workers divided by wages","C":"Total profit per firm","D":"Output per worker or per hour"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-COST-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Labour productivity is output per worker, or per hour worked. Rising productivity lowers the labour cost per unit even when wages are unchanged, which is why it is central to competitiveness. It is not the same as total output, which can rise simply because more workers were hired.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory measures how many units each of its workers makes on average during a single shift. What is this measure called?","options":{"A":"Labour productivity, output per worker or per hour","B":"The market price of labour","C":"The output where profit is guaranteed to be maximum","D":"Total profit per firm"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH3COST-26","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"A student is revising labour productivity. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Output per worker or per hour","B":"Workers divided by wages","C":"Total profit per firm","D":"Its total cost has become zero"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-COST-PRODUCTIVITY","scaffold":"Labour productivity is output per worker, or per hour worked. Rising productivity lowers the labour cost per unit even when wages are unchanged, which is why it is central to competitiveness. It is not the same as total output, which can rise simply because more workers were hired.","reforge":{"stem":"A factory measures how many units each of its workers makes on average during a single shift. What is this measure called?","options":{"A":"The market price of labour","B":"Labour productivity, output per worker or per hour","C":"It produces where average cost is always at its minimum","D":"Total profit per firm"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH3COST-27","spec":"3.2.1","stem":"Which description of diminishing marginal returns is accurate?","options":{"A":"Average revenue becomes equal to the market price","B":"Extra output from an input eventually falls","C":"Fixed cost rises per unit","D":"Revenue becomes constant"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-COST-MARGINAL","scaffold":"The law of diminishing marginal returns says that as more of a variable factor is added to at least one fixed factor, the extra output from each additional unit eventually falls. It is a short-run idea, because it depends on something being fixed. Diseconomies of scale are the long-run counterpart, where every factor varies and average cost rises.","reforge":{"stem":"A farm keeps adding extra workers to the same fixed plot of land, and each additional worker adds progressively less extra output than the one before. What does this describe?","options":{"A":"Total output immediately falling","B":"Fixed cost rising per unit produced","C":"Diminishing marginal returns, where extra output from an input eventually falls","D":"The policy needs no public funds, since the higher price paid to farmers is covered entirely by consumers at the till"},"correct":"C"}}]},"4.1.1":{"label":"Globalisation & Trade","color":"#1e3a5f","questions":[{"id":"TH4-GT-01","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is globalisation?","options":{"A":"Economies become more connected through trade and investment","B":"A policy requiring every country to produce all goods domestically","C":"The removal of all national differences in income and living standards","D":"A fall in international communication caused by higher transport costs"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-INTEGRATION","scaffold":"Globalisation is the increasing interdependence of economies. Trade, multinational production, capital flows, migration and digital communication connect countries, although the benefits and costs are distributed unevenly.","reforge":{"stem":"Which development has most directly increased global economic integration?","options":{"A":"Higher tariffs on imported components","B":"A complete ban on all foreign ownership of any domestic firms at all","C":"Faster digital communication and cheaper international transport","D":"A reduction in cross-border investment flows"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-02","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why might a multinational corporation locate production in a developing economy?","options":{"A":"To avoid selling its products to local consumers","B":"To access lower costs or a growing market","C":"To ensure that transport and labour costs are always higher","D":"To remove the need for any quality-control systems"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-MNC","scaffold":"Multinationals may seek lower labour or land costs, access to raw materials, proximity to consumers and favourable policies. Host economies may gain jobs and investment, but concerns include low wages, tax avoidance and environmental damage.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a possible benefit to a host economy of inward investment?","options":{"A":"It always eliminates domestic competition","B":"It prevents the economy from developing local firms","C":"It guarantees that all profits remain in the host economy","D":"It can create jobs and transfer skills or technology"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-03","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a tariff?","options":{"A":"A subsidy paid to overseas consumers","B":"A limit on the quantity of exports","C":"A tax on imports","D":"A voluntary agreement to remove customs checks"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-TARIFF","scaffold":"A tariff is a tax placed on imported goods. It raises the domestic price of imports, protects some domestic producers and raises government revenue, but consumers may pay more and trading partners may retaliate.","reforge":{"stem":"What is one likely effect of an import tariff on domestic consumers?","options":{"A":"They may pay a higher price for the imported product","B":"They face a lower price and more choice in every case","C":"They receive the tariff revenue directly as a payment","D":"They are protected from all future inflation"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-04","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why might trade create gains from specialisation?","options":{"A":"Specialisation guarantees that no worker changes occupation","B":"Countries no longer need to consider resource scarcity","C":"Trade makes every country equally productive in all goods","D":"Countries specialise where opportunity cost is lower"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-GAINS","scaffold":"Comparative advantage means a country has a lower opportunity cost in producing a good. Specialisation followed by trade can increase total output, although adjustment costs and unequal distribution may create losers.","reforge":{"stem":"A country with comparative advantage in wheat should generally:","options":{"A":"Produce every good without importing any products","B":"Produce wheat at a lower opportunity cost than its trading partner","C":"Have the highest absolute level of productivity in every single industry","D":"Stop trading once domestic wheat demand is satisfied"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-05","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Which is a possible cost of protectionism?","options":{"A":"Retaliation can reduce exports and trade","B":"Domestic firms face more competition from overseas producers","C":"Consumers always receive lower prices for imported products","D":"Government revenue from tariffs must fall to zero"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-PROTECTION","scaffold":"Protectionism can preserve jobs in protected industries, but it may raise prices, reduce choice, weaken competitive pressure and provoke retaliation. The final effect depends on the policy and the response of other countries.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might a trading partner retaliate after a tariff is imposed?","options":{"A":"It automatically receives all of the original tariff revenue raised","B":"It must remove all restrictions on its imports","C":"It may impose its own tariff to protect domestic producers","D":"It becomes unable to produce any export goods"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-06","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is meant by foreign direct investment?","options":{"A":"A government payment made only to domestic households","B":"A firm invests in productive assets overseas","C":"A short-term purchase of imported consumer goods","D":"A tax charged when a tourist exchanges currency"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-FDI","scaffold":"Foreign direct investment involves control or a lasting interest in productive activity in another country, such as building a factory or acquiring a business. It differs from short-term portfolio investment in shares or bonds.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is an example of foreign direct investment?","options":{"A":"A pension fund briefly buys overseas government bonds","B":"A tourist exchanges pounds for euros at an airport","C":"A household buys an imported laptop","D":"A UK firm builds and operates a factory in Poland"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-07","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How can foreign direct investment affect a host country's balance of payments?","options":{"A":"It always worsens the current account immediately","B":"It removes all imports from the host economy","C":"It can attract capital now but send profits abroad later","D":"It changes the exchange rate without affecting any other account"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-FDI","scaffold":"An inward investment is recorded as a financial-account inflow. It may also raise exports, substitute for imports and increase productive capacity, but profits later remitted to the overseas owner appear as a primary-income outflow in the current account. The overall effect therefore depends on the investment's scale, time horizon and links to the domestic economy.","reforge":{"stem":"What is a possible later cost of inward foreign direct investment?","options":{"A":"Profits may be sent abroad to the overseas owner","B":"The host country must stop exporting all goods","C":"Domestic productivity is guaranteed to fall","D":"The financial account records no investment flow"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-08","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why might a country impose an import quota rather than a tariff?","options":{"A":"A quota always lowers domestic prices below world prices","B":"A quota prevents trading partners from responding","C":"A quota guarantees higher tax revenue for government","D":"A quota sets a physical limit on imported units"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-NONTARIFF","scaffold":"A quota restricts the quantity of a good entering the country, whereas a tariff raises its price through a tax. A quota can give domestic producers more certainty about the volume of foreign competition, but it may create quota rents and does not automatically raise government revenue. Trading partners may still retaliate.","reforge":{"stem":"What distinguishes an import quota from an import tariff?","options":{"A":"A quota raises tax receipts; a tariff limits physical quantities","B":"A quota fixes quantity; a tariff taxes imports","C":"A quota applies only to services; a tariff applies only to goods","D":"A quota removes protection; a tariff guarantees free trade"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-09","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is trade liberalisation?","options":{"A":"Reducing barriers to international trade","B":"Banning all imports","C":"Raising every tariff","D":"A policy requiring every country to produce all goods domestically"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-INTEGRATION","scaffold":"Trade liberalisation removes or lowers tariffs, quotas and other restrictions. It can expand trade and choice but may expose domestic industries to adjustment costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A bilateral free-trade agreement usually aims to:","options":{"A":"Raise import quotas","B":"End all exports","C":"Reduce barriers between two countries","D":"The removal of all national differences in income and living standards"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-10","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is protectionism?","options":{"A":"Domestic firms face more competition from overseas producers","B":"Policies that restrict foreign competition","C":"A fall in all domestic taxes","D":"A guarantee of equal global wages"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-PROTECTION","scaffold":"Protectionism uses tariffs, quotas, subsidies or regulations to support domestic producers against imports. It can protect jobs but may raise prices and provoke retaliation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a protectionist measure?","options":{"A":"Consumers always receive lower prices for imported products","B":"Removing customs checks","C":"Reducing an import tax","D":"A quota limiting imported quantities"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-11","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is an import quota?","options":{"A":"A subsidy to foreign firms","B":"A tax on domestic exports","C":"A limit on the quantity imported","D":"A ban on all domestic production"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-NONTARIFF","scaffold":"A quota fixes a maximum volume of imports. It can protect local firms but may raise domestic prices and create quota rents.","reforge":{"stem":"A quota differs from a tariff because it directly limits:","options":{"A":"Physical import quantity","B":"The tax paid to government","C":"Domestic consumer income","D":"The exchange rate"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-12","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is an export subsidy?","options":{"A":"A ban on overseas sales","B":"A tax on foreign buyers","C":"A fall in international communication caused by higher transport costs","D":"Government support lowering exporters' costs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-DUMPING","scaffold":"Export subsidies can make domestic goods more competitive abroad, but they cost public funds and may distort trade or trigger disputes.","reforge":{"stem":"An export subsidy is intended to make domestic exports:","options":{"A":"More expensive to foreign buyers","B":"More price competitive overseas","C":"Unavailable to foreign buyers","D":"Independent of production costs"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-13","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why can tariffs reduce consumer welfare?","options":{"A":"They can raise prices and reduce choice","B":"They always lower domestic prices","C":"A voluntary agreement to remove customs checks","D":"They remove every supply constraint"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-TARIFF","scaffold":"A tariff raises the domestic price of imports, protecting some producers but creating a consumer loss through higher prices and less choice.","reforge":{"stem":"A tariff may benefit domestic producers because it:","options":{"A":"Guarantees export demand","B":"Eliminates their costs","C":"Reduces competition from imports","D":"A subsidy paid to overseas consumers"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-14","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is trade diversion?","options":{"A":"All trade disappears","B":"Imports switch from a low-cost outsider to a higher-cost member","C":"Exports become domestic consumption","D":"A complete ban on all foreign ownership of any domestic firms at all"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-CREATION-DIVERSION","scaffold":"A customs union can divert imports if its common external tariff makes a cheaper non-member supplier less attractive than a higher-cost member.","reforge":{"stem":"Trade diversion can reduce efficiency because:","options":{"A":"To ensure that transport and labour costs are always higher","B":"Specialisation always rises","C":"All tariffs are removed globally","D":"Purchases move away from the lowest-cost producer"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-15","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is trade creation?","options":{"A":"Have the highest absolute level of productivity in every single industry","B":"A ban on imports from members","C":"Lower trade barriers shift purchases to a lower-cost member","D":"A fall in specialisation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-CREATION-DIVERSION","scaffold":"Trade creation occurs when integration allows consumers to buy from a more efficient partner rather than a higher-cost domestic producer.","reforge":{"stem":"Trade creation is likely to increase:","options":{"A":"Specialisation and consumer choice","B":"The cost of every import","C":"Exports become domestic consumption","D":"Domestic monopoly power"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-16","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a customs union?","options":{"A":"A domestic labour union representing only one country's workers","B":"A single global currency","C":"A ban on internal trade","D":"Free internal trade plus a common external tariff"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-BLOC","scaffold":"Members remove barriers between themselves and apply a common tariff to non-members. This is deeper integration than a simple free-trade area.","reforge":{"stem":"A customs union differs from a free-trade area because members share:","options":{"A":"Free trade with no external policy","B":"A common external tariff","C":"One national budget","D":"Identical tax rates"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-17","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a free-trade area?","options":{"A":"Members reduce internal trade barriers but set their own external policy","B":"Members must use one currency","C":"A change in tastes, which shifts the whole demand curve rather than moving along the existing one","D":"All members share one parliament"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-BLOC","scaffold":"A free-trade area removes many internal barriers while allowing each country to choose its own tariffs against non-members.","reforge":{"stem":"A country in a free-trade area can usually:","options":{"A":"Control every partner's fiscal policy","B":"Ban trade with all members","C":"Set its own tariff on non-members","D":"Use only one national currency"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-18","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the balance of trade?","options":{"A":"The government budget balance","B":"The value of exports minus imports of goods","C":"The value of services only","D":"A reduction in cross-border investment flows"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-CURRENT-ACCOUNT","scaffold":"The balance of trade usually refers to visible goods. The wider current account also includes services, income and transfers.","reforge":{"stem":"A deficit in the balance of trade means:","options":{"A":"Capital inflows are zero","B":"Services exports exceed services imports","C":"Government spending exceeds tax","D":"Goods imports exceed goods exports"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-19","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the current account?","options":{"A":"It automatically receives all of the original tariff revenue raised","B":"Only goods exports","C":"Trade, income and current transfers with the rest of the world","D":"The government's annual budget"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-CURRENT-ACCOUNT","scaffold":"The current account includes goods, services, primary income and secondary income. It is distinct from the financial account, which records asset flows.","reforge":{"stem":"Which item belongs in the current account?","options":{"A":"Dividend income received from overseas","B":"Services exports exceed services imports","C":"A central bank reserve transfer","D":"A domestic factory purchase"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-20","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a current account deficit?","options":{"A":"Foreign investment is impossible","B":"To avoid selling its products to local consumers","C":"The government has no tax revenue","D":"Current payments abroad exceed current receipts"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-CURRENT-ACCOUNT","scaffold":"A current account deficit means the country spends more on goods, services and income payments abroad than it receives. It must be financed by capital or financial inflows.","reforge":{"stem":"A current account deficit may be financed by:","options":{"A":"No financial transaction","B":"Inward investment from overseas","C":"A fall in all imports","D":"Exports exceed imports in every category"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-21","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a floating exchange rate?","options":{"A":"A rate mainly determined by foreign-exchange demand and supply","B":"A rate fixed permanently by law","C":"It changes the exchange rate without affecting any other account","D":"A rate chosen by exporters"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-ER-REGIME","scaffold":"Under a float, market forces and expectations determine the currency price, although central banks may sometimes intervene.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in demand for sterling under a float tends to:","options":{"A":"Leave it legally fixed","B":"Depreciate sterling","C":"Appreciate sterling","D":"End foreign exchange trading"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-22","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a fixed exchange rate?","options":{"A":"A rate that never needs reserves","B":"A rate maintained near a chosen value","C":"To remove the need for any quality-control systems","D":"A rate that changes every second"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-ER-REGIME","scaffold":"A government or central bank supports a target by intervention, interest rates or reserves. Maintaining it can constrain domestic policy.","reforge":{"stem":"To defend a fixed currency value, a central bank may:","options":{"A":"Remove every interest rate","B":"It always eliminates domestic competition","C":"Ban all exports","D":"Buy its currency using foreign reserves"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-23","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What causes a currency depreciation?","options":{"A":"A permanent trade surplus","B":"Depreciate in the short run but appreciate in the long run.","C":"A fall in its value against other currencies","D":"A legal rise in domestic wages"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-ER-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Depreciation makes a currency cheaper in foreign exchange markets. It may boost net exports but can raise the domestic price of imports.","reforge":{"stem":"After a depreciation, imported fuel is likely to become:","options":{"A":"More expensive in domestic currency","B":"Free to domestic firms","C":"Cheaper in every case","D":"Depreciate — investors move money out."},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-24","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why might an exchange-rate appreciation reduce inflation?","options":{"A":"Domestic supply disappears","B":"Exports become more expensive abroad only","C":"Wages are legally frozen","D":"Imported goods and inputs become cheaper"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-ER-EFFECTS","scaffold":"A stronger currency lowers the domestic price of imports and imported inputs, reducing imported inflation. It can also weaken export demand.","reforge":{"stem":"A stronger currency may harm exporters because:","options":{"A":"Their inputs always cost more","B":"Their goods become more expensive to foreign buyers","C":"Farmers can create land instantly whenever a higher price makes it profitable to plant a larger harvest","D":"Imports become illegal"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-25","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is foreign portfolio investment?","options":{"A":"Buying overseas financial assets without controlling production","B":"Building a foreign factory","C":"It changes the exchange rate without affecting any other account","D":"A tariff on foreign shares"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-FDI","scaffold":"Portfolio investment purchases shares or bonds for financial returns, unlike FDI, which involves lasting control or influence over productive activity.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is portfolio rather than direct investment?","options":{"A":"A government payment made only to domestic households","B":"Building a factory abroad","C":"Buying overseas government bonds","D":"Operating a foreign mine"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-26","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How can FDI raise a host country's productive capacity?","options":{"A":"It removes all domestic workers","B":"It can add capital, technology and management skills","C":"It guarantees no profit outflow","D":"A pension fund briefly buys overseas government bonds"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-FDI","scaffold":"Inward FDI can expand the capital stock, transfer technology and improve skills. Benefits depend on local linkages and whether profits are retained or remitted.","reforge":{"stem":"A possible spillover from a foreign factory is:","options":{"A":"A short-term purchase of imported consumer goods","B":"All domestic firms close","C":"Workers lose every skill","D":"Local suppliers learn new production methods"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-27","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is profit repatriation?","options":{"A":"Taxing domestic consumers","B":"It changes the exchange rate without affecting any other account","C":"Sending profits earned in a host country to the foreign owner","D":"Buying a local firm's shares"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-FDI","scaffold":"Profit repatriation can reduce the host economy's current-account income balance, although the original investment may have created jobs and output.","reforge":{"stem":"Why can profit repatriation limit the net benefit of FDI?","options":{"A":"Some income leaves the host economy","B":"Foreign firms cannot employ locals","C":"FDI never adds capacity","D":"The host country receives all profits"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-28","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a transnational corporation?","options":{"A":"It prevents the economy from developing local firms","B":"A firm selling only locally","C":"A national government agency","D":"A firm operating across multiple countries"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-MNC","scaffold":"TNCs coordinate production, finance and marketing across borders. They can bring investment and jobs but may exert significant bargaining power.","reforge":{"stem":"A possible concern about TNC power is:","options":{"A":"It cannot move production","B":"It may influence policy or negotiate tax concessions","C":"It has no access to finance","D":"It guarantees that all profits remain in the host economy"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-29","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why do firms use global supply chains?","options":{"A":"To access specialised inputs and lower costs","B":"To eliminate transport risks","C":"To avoid every foreign market","D":"A national budget covering all production costs"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-VALUECHAIN","scaffold":"Global chains can exploit comparative advantage and economies of scale. They also create dependence on ports, suppliers, energy and geopolitical conditions.","reforge":{"stem":"A disadvantage of a long global supply chain is:","options":{"A":"Transport becomes unnecessary","B":"Inputs become locally guaranteed","C":"A disruption in one location can delay production elsewhere","D":"A quota raises tax receipts; a tariff limits physical quantities"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-30","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is global economic interdependence?","options":{"A":"A policy requiring every country to produce all goods domestically","B":"Countries rely on one another for trade, finance or resources","C":"Countries have identical incomes","D":"Trade has no effect on domestic policy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-INTEGRATION","scaffold":"Interdependence can spread growth and knowledge but also transmit shocks through trade, capital flows, migration and supply chains.","reforge":{"stem":"A rise in overseas demand for UK services illustrates:","options":{"A":"No international connection","B":"Autarky","C":"A closed economy","D":"Economic interdependence"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-31","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a terms-of-trade improvement?","options":{"A":"Exports fall to zero","B":"They are unaffected, since terms of trade only track foreign reserves","C":"Export prices rise relative to import prices","D":"The exchange rate is legally fixed"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-TOT","scaffold":"The terms of trade compare export prices with import prices. An improvement may increase purchasing power, though export volumes and demand conditions still matter.","reforge":{"stem":"A deterioration in the terms of trade means:","options":{"A":"Import prices rise relative to export prices","B":"Exports become more valuable relative to imports","C":"All trade stops","D":"The current account must balance"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-32","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why can a commodity-exporting country face volatile export revenue?","options":{"A":"They face a lower price and more choice in every case","B":"Commodity demand is perfectly stable","C":"Exports cannot be affected by weather","D":"World commodity prices can fluctuate sharply"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-COMMODITY","scaffold":"Commodity prices respond to global demand, supply shocks, weather and speculation. Dependence on one commodity can therefore make income and government revenue unstable.","reforge":{"stem":"A way to reduce commodity dependence is:","options":{"A":"Banning all imports","B":"Diversifying production and exports","C":"Relying on one crop","D":"Prices are fixed by domestic consumers"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-33","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is an infant industry argument for protection?","options":{"A":"Temporary protection may help a new industry develop","B":"Established firms need no learning","C":"Protection always lowers prices","D":"They receive the tariff revenue directly as a payment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-INFANT","scaffold":"An infant industry may need time to achieve economies of scale and learn before facing global competition. Protection risks becoming permanent and inefficient.","reforge":{"stem":"Why should infant-industry protection usually be temporary?","options":{"A":"Trade barriers have no cost","B":"Learning ends immediately","C":"Permanent shelter can reduce incentives to become efficient","D":"A quota applies only to services; a tariff applies only to goods"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-34","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is dumping in international trade?","options":{"A":"Banning goods from domestic markets","B":"Selling exports abroad at an unusually low price","C":"Specialisation guarantees that no worker changes occupation","D":"Subsidising every household"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-DUMPING","scaffold":"Dumping may involve selling below domestic price or cost, potentially harming foreign competitors. Anti-dumping measures are intended to prevent unfair injury.","reforge":{"stem":"A possible effect of dumping is:","options":{"A":"Exports are legally impossible","B":"Import competition disappears","C":"They have worsened, since tax revenue has fallen relative to spending","D":"Domestic producers in the importing country may lose market share"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-35","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the infant-industry problem with protectionism?","options":{"A":"Protection guarantees innovation","B":"Firms are forced to export more","C":"Protected firms may become dependent on shelter","D":"Government revenue from tariffs must fall to zero"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-PROTECTION","scaffold":"If protection remains, firms may face weak competitive pressure and fail to improve productivity. Government must judge when and how to remove support.","reforge":{"stem":"Which condition makes infant-industry protection more credible?","options":{"A":"A clear plan for raising productivity and ending support","B":"No performance targets","C":"Permanent import bans","D":"Domestic firms face more competition from overseas producers"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-36","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why might governments use tariffs to raise revenue?","options":{"A":"Revenue is paid by no one","B":"They are protected from all future inflation","C":"Tariffs remove all imports","D":"A tariff creates a tax on imported goods"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-TARIFF","scaffold":"Tariffs can generate revenue, especially where domestic tax collection is weak. However, the revenue may fall if imports contract sharply.","reforge":{"stem":"Tariff revenue may disappoint if:","options":{"A":"A voluntary agreement to remove customs checks","B":"Imports fall substantially after the tariff","C":"The tariff is collected at the border","D":"Consumers buy fewer imports"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-37","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a non-tariff barrier?","options":{"A":"A trade restriction other than an import tax","B":"A tax on domestic profits","C":"A subsidy for all consumers","D":"A quota always lowers domestic prices below world prices"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-NONTARIFF","scaffold":"Quotas, licensing rules, product standards and customs procedures can restrict trade without being tariffs. Their effects may be less transparent.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is a non-tariff barrier?","options":{"A":"A domestic wage rise","B":"A lower import tax","C":"A complex product standard that imports must meet","D":"A quota guarantees higher tax revenue for government"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-38","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why can trade increase dynamic efficiency?","options":{"A":"Trade makes every country equally productive in all goods","B":"Competition and larger markets can encourage innovation","C":"Trade removes all investment","D":"Markets become permanently closed"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-GAINS","scaffold":"Exposure to rivals, access to larger markets and technology transfer can increase investment and innovation over time, although benefits are not automatic.","reforge":{"stem":"A larger export market may encourage firms to:","options":{"A":"Countries no longer need to consider resource scarcity","B":"Reduce all productivity","C":"Stop product development","D":"Invest in technology and new capacity"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-39","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a possible distributional cost of globalisation?","options":{"A":"Trade removes structural unemployment","B":"Every worker gains equally","C":"Some workers lose jobs in import-competing industries","D":"A quota removes protection; a tariff guarantees free trade"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Globalisation can raise total efficiency while creating losers in regions or industries exposed to imports, especially where workers cannot retrain or move easily.","reforge":{"stem":"A policy to support workers displaced by trade is:","options":{"A":"Retraining and relocation assistance","B":"Permanent unemployment","C":"All regions receive identical investment","D":"Reducing labour mobility"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-40","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why may trade raise inequality within a country?","options":{"A":"Stop trading once domestic wheat demand is satisfied","B":"All factor rewards become identical","C":"Imports remove every wage difference","D":"Gains may favour skilled workers and capital owners"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Trade can reward factors used intensively in expanding sectors while reducing demand for others. Education, tax and regional policy affect the distribution of gains.","reforge":{"stem":"Which policy could spread gains from globalisation?","options":{"A":"Produce every good without importing any products","B":"Investment in skills and affected regions","C":"Reducing worker mobility","D":"Closing export markets"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-41","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a balance-of-payments surplus?","options":{"A":"Receipts from international transactions exceed payments overall","B":"They are unaffected, since terms of trade only track government debt","C":"The government budget is balanced","D":"No capital enters the country"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-CURRENT-ACCOUNT","scaffold":"The balance of payments records transactions with the rest of the world and balances overall through accounting entries. A surplus can still hide a current-account deficit financed by capital inflows.","reforge":{"stem":"A country can have a current-account deficit while overall balance is maintained by:","options":{"A":"A fall in exports only","B":"The government budget balance","C":"A financial-account surplus","D":"A domestic tax cut"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-42","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why can exchange-rate volatility deter international trade?","options":{"A":"Currencies never affect contracts","B":"Firms face uncertainty about future prices and revenues","C":"Depreciate in the short run but appreciate in the long run.","D":"It removes the need for hedging"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-ER-EFFECTS","scaffold":"Unpredictable currency movements make future costs and receipts harder to forecast. Firms may delay trade, raise prices or use financial hedging.","reforge":{"stem":"A firm can manage currency risk by:","options":{"A":"A harvest already planted, where farmers can nonetheless expand this season's output the moment price rises","B":"Ignoring all foreign prices","C":"Banning overseas sales","D":"Using a forward contract to lock an exchange rate"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-43","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is a sovereign wealth fund?","options":{"A":"A tariff on imports","B":"A private household budget","C":"A state-owned fund investing national financial assets","D":"Trade in goods, since only physical items count as exports"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-DEV-FINANCE","scaffold":"Sovereign wealth funds may invest resource revenues or foreign-exchange reserves in domestic and overseas assets. Governance and political objectives matter.","reforge":{"stem":"A resource-exporting state may create a sovereign wealth fund to:","options":{"A":"Save volatile resource income for future use","B":"It must remove all restrictions on its imports","C":"Guarantee one commodity price","D":"Prevent public saving"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-44","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is aid conditionality?","options":{"A":"A ban on development finance","B":"Country A always produces the largest absolute output","C":"A tax on humanitarian goods","D":"Financial support linked to required policy changes"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-DEV-FINANCE","scaffold":"Conditionality may require fiscal reform, liberalisation or governance changes. It can improve accountability but may limit policy autonomy or impose social costs.","reforge":{"stem":"A concern about aid conditionality is that it may:","options":{"A":"Guarantee local ownership","B":"Reduce the recipient government's policy freedom","C":"Remove all adjustment costs","D":"A tourist exchanges pounds for euros at an airport"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-45","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is remittance income?","options":{"A":"Money sent home by workers living abroad","B":"Profit from a domestic tariff","C":"It becomes unable to produce any export goods","D":"A tax on foreign investment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-DEV-FINANCE","scaffold":"Remittances are transfers from migrants to households in their origin country. They can support consumption, education and foreign exchange but may create dependence.","reforge":{"stem":"A possible benefit of remittances is:","options":{"A":"A tax charged when a tourist exchanges currency","B":"Lower household resources","C":"Higher household income for recipients","D":"No effect on poverty"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-46","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why can foreign borrowing create vulnerability?","options":{"A":"Borrowing never requires repayment","B":"Debt repayments may rise when income or exchange rates change","C":"The removal of all national differences in income and living standards","D":"Debt always raises export revenue"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-DEV-FINANCE","scaffold":"External debt can finance investment but creates repayment obligations. Currency depreciation and higher global interest rates may make servicing debt more difficult.","reforge":{"stem":"A depreciation can worsen foreign-currency debt because:","options":{"A":"It always worsens the current account immediately","B":"Foreign debt disappears","C":"Interest becomes legally zero","D":"More domestic currency is needed for repayments"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-47","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is economic integration?","options":{"A":"One firm controls every economy","B":"A fall in international communication caused by higher transport costs","C":"Countries coordinate or remove barriers between their economies","D":"Trade becomes impossible"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-INTEGRATION","scaffold":"Integration ranges from a free-trade area to economic and monetary union. Deeper integration brings benefits but reduces some national policy independence.","reforge":{"stem":"A cost of deeper economic integration may be:","options":{"A":"Less freedom to set independent trade or monetary policy","B":"No increase in trade","C":"A complete ban on all foreign ownership of any domestic firms at all","D":"Guaranteed national isolation"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-48","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why can trade agreements include rules of origin?","options":{"A":"To remove customs documentation","B":"A domestic labour union representing only one country's workers","C":"To set every country's income tax","D":"To identify where goods qualify for preferential treatment"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-BLOC","scaffold":"Rules of origin prevent goods from entering through a low-tariff member simply to avoid tariffs that would apply elsewhere. They can also increase administrative costs.","reforge":{"stem":"Rules of origin are intended to prevent:","options":{"A":"All intra-regional trade","B":"Trade deflection through the lowest-tariff member","C":"Domestic investment","D":"The number of domestic firms operating in that economy"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-49","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is trade deflection?","options":{"A":"Imports enter through a member with the lowest external barrier","B":"Exports return to their producer","C":"Domestic firms stop importing","D":"Have the highest absolute level of productivity in every single industry"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-BLOC","scaffold":"Trade deflection can occur in a free-trade area because members set separate external tariffs. Rules of origin and customs checks can limit it.","reforge":{"stem":"A customs union reduces trade deflection by using:","options":{"A":"No border rules","B":"Separate tariffs in every member","C":"A common external tariff","D":"A ban on internal trade"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4-GT-50","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"Why might a government support strategic industries?","options":{"A":"The financial account records no investment flow","B":"They may have security or spillover importance","C":"Support has no opportunity cost","D":"Strategic goods cannot be imported"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-INFANT","scaffold":"Governments may support industries linked to defence, energy security, technology or learning spillovers. Support can also preserve inefficient firms if poorly targeted.","reforge":{"stem":"A risk of strategic-industry subsidies is:","options":{"A":"Consumers always pay less","B":"All innovation stops","C":"A quota prevents trading partners from responding","D":"Public funds may protect inefficient producers"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-03","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should comparative advantage be understood here?","options":{"A":"The highest wage rate","B":"The host country must stop exporting all goods","C":"A lower opportunity cost of producing a good","D":"A permanent trade surplus"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-COMPARATIVE","scaffold":"Comparative advantage is a lower opportunity cost of producing a good — what has to be given up to make it — not simply making more of it. It is the basis of the gains from trade: countries specialise where their opportunity cost is lowest, and both gain even if one is better at everything. Absolute advantage is the output comparison, and confusing the two is the classic Theme 4 error.","reforge":{"stem":"Country A can produce a car by giving up fewer units of wheat than Country B would need to give up. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Country A has a comparative advantage in cars, a lower opportunity cost","B":"Country A has the highest wage rate in the region","C":"Have the highest absolute level of productivity in every single industry","D":"Country A runs a permanent trade surplus"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-04","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising comparative advantage. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A permanent trade surplus","B":"Exports become more valuable relative to imports","C":"The highest wage rate","D":"A lower opportunity cost of producing a good"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-COMPARATIVE","scaffold":"Comparative advantage is a lower opportunity cost of producing a good — what has to be given up to make it — not simply making more of it. It is the basis of the gains from trade: countries specialise where their opportunity cost is lowest, and both gain even if one is better at everything. Absolute advantage is the output comparison, and confusing the two is the classic Theme 4 error.","reforge":{"stem":"Country A can produce a car by giving up fewer units of wheat than Country B would need to give up. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Country A has the highest wage rate in the region","B":"Country A has a comparative advantage in cars, a lower opportunity cost","C":"Have the highest absolute level of productivity in every single industry","D":"Country A runs a permanent trade surplus"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-05","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of absolute advantage?","options":{"A":"Greater output from the same resources","B":"Lower opportunity cost only","C":"A higher import tariff","D":"Producing without using scarce resources"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-ABSOLUTE","scaffold":"Absolute advantage means producing more of a good from the same resources than another country can. It is about output, whereas comparative advantage is about opportunity cost — and it is comparative advantage that determines the pattern of trade. A country can hold an absolute advantage in everything and still gain by specialising.","reforge":{"stem":"Using the same workers and machines, Country A produces more wheat per year than Country B. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Country A has a lower opportunity cost only","B":"Country A charges a higher import tariff","C":"Country A has an absolute advantage, producing more from the same resources","D":"Having the lower opportunity cost of production compared with every other producer in the market"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-06","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising absolute advantage. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Lower opportunity cost only","B":"Greater output from the same resources","C":"A higher import tariff","D":"Producing without using scarce resources"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-ABSOLUTE","scaffold":"Absolute advantage means producing more of a good from the same resources than another country can. It is about output, whereas comparative advantage is about opportunity cost — and it is comparative advantage that determines the pattern of trade. A country can hold an absolute advantage in everything and still gain by specialising.","reforge":{"stem":"Using the same workers and machines, Country A produces more wheat per year than Country B. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Country A has a lower opportunity cost only","B":"Country A charges a higher import tariff","C":"Its inflation rate, calculated from the change in the cost of producing cars and wheat over the period","D":"Country A has an absolute advantage, producing more from the same resources"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-07","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should terms of trade be understood here?","options":{"A":"Exports become more valuable relative to imports","B":"The ratio of tax to spending","C":"The ratio of export prices to import prices","D":"The balance of government debt"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-TOT","scaffold":"The terms of trade is the ratio of export prices to import prices, conventionally expressed as an index. An improvement means each unit of exports buys more imports, which sounds favourable but can worsen the current account when demand is price-elastic. Link a movement here to competitiveness and the balance of payments.","reforge":{"stem":"A country's export prices rise faster than its import prices. What has happened to its terms of trade?","options":{"A":"They have improved, since the ratio of export to import prices has risen","B":"A shift of the demand curve caused by a change in the size or age structure of the population over time","C":"They have worsened, since tax revenue has fallen relative to spending","D":"They are unaffected, since terms of trade only track government debt"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-08","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising terms of trade. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"They are unaffected, since terms of trade only track foreign reserves","B":"The ratio of tax to spending","C":"The volume of foreign reserves","D":"The ratio of export prices to import prices"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-TOT","scaffold":"The terms of trade is the ratio of export prices to import prices, conventionally expressed as an index. An improvement means each unit of exports buys more imports, which sounds favourable but can worsen the current account when demand is price-elastic. Link a movement here to competitiveness and the balance of payments.","reforge":{"stem":"What would provide evidence of terms of trade?","options":{"A":"Banning all imports","B":"The ratio of export prices to import prices","C":"They have worsened, since tax revenue has fallen relative to spending","D":"The balance of government debt"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-09","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of trade intensity?","options":{"A":"Trade flows relative to the size of an economy","B":"Country A has the highest wage rate in the region","C":"The price of one export","D":"A country's birth rate"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-INTENSITY","scaffold":"Trade intensity measures trade flows relative to the size of an economy, typically exports plus imports as a share of GDP, so it captures openness rather than absolute volume. A large economy can trade enormous amounts and still be relatively closed, which is why the ratio rather than the total is the meaningful comparison.","reforge":{"stem":"A small economy's exports and imports together equal 80% of its GDP. What does this measure?","options":{"A":"It automatically receives all of the original tariff revenue raised","B":"Trade intensity, trade flows relative to the size of the economy","C":"The price charged for a single export product","D":"The economy's birth rate"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-10","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising trade intensity. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Country A automatically has a stronger currency","B":"Trade flows relative to the size of an economy","C":"The price of one export","D":"A country's birth rate"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-INTENSITY","scaffold":"Trade intensity measures trade flows relative to the size of an economy, typically exports plus imports as a share of GDP, so it captures openness rather than absolute volume. A large economy can trade enormous amounts and still be relatively closed, which is why the ratio rather than the total is the meaningful comparison.","reforge":{"stem":"A small economy's exports and imports together equal 80% of its GDP. What does this measure?","options":{"A":"They are unaffected, since terms of trade only track government debt","B":"The price charged for a single export product","C":"Trade intensity, trade flows relative to the size of the economy","D":"The economy's birth rate"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-11","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should global value chain be understood here?","options":{"A":"A national budget","B":"Firms gain complete control of every border","C":"Production stages spread across countries","D":"A domestic price ceiling"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-VALUECHAIN","scaffold":"A global value chain is a production process split into stages carried out in different countries, each adding value where it is cheapest or most skilled. It explains why one product carries components from many economies, and why gross trade figures double-count the same value. Value added is the honest measure.","reforge":{"stem":"A smartphone has its chips designed in one country, assembled in a second, and sold worldwide from a third. What does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"The removal of all national differences in income and living standards","B":"A single-country production line","C":"A domestic price ceiling on electronics","D":"A global value chain, with production stages spread across countries"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-12","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising global value chain. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A domestic price ceiling","B":"Firms gain complete control of every border","C":"A national budget","D":"Production stages spread across countries"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-VALUECHAIN","scaffold":"A global value chain is a production process split into stages carried out in different countries, each adding value where it is cheapest or most skilled. It explains why one product carries components from many economies, and why gross trade figures double-count the same value. Value added is the honest measure.","reforge":{"stem":"A smartphone has its chips designed in one country, assembled in a second, and sold worldwide from a third. What does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"A global value chain, with production stages spread across countries","B":"A fall in international communication caused by higher transport costs","C":"A single-country production line","D":"A domestic price ceiling on electronics"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-13","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of outsourcing?","options":{"A":"Contracting an activity to an external provider","B":"Buying every supplier outright","C":"A transfer payment made within a single government","D":"Producing without labour"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-OUTSOURCING","scaffold":"Outsourcing means contracting an activity to another firm rather than performing it in-house, and it says nothing about geography — outsourced work can stay in the same country. Offshoring is the move abroad. The two are independent: a firm can offshore work that it still carries out itself.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK firm stops running its own call centre and instead pays an external company to handle customer calls. What does this describe?","options":{"A":"Buying out every one of its suppliers","B":"Outsourcing, contracting an activity out to an external provider","C":"Closing all of its overseas operations","D":"A policy requiring every country to produce all goods domestically"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-14","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising outsourcing. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Buying every supplier outright","B":"Contracting an activity to an external provider","C":"A predictable, pre-announced domestic tax change","D":"Producing without labour"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-OUTSOURCING","scaffold":"Outsourcing means contracting an activity to another firm rather than performing it in-house, and it says nothing about geography — outsourced work can stay in the same country. Offshoring is the move abroad. The two are independent: a firm can offshore work that it still carries out itself.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK firm stops running its own call centre and instead pays an external company to handle customer calls. What does this describe?","options":{"A":"Buying out every one of its suppliers","B":"Closing all of its overseas operations","C":"Outsourcing, contracting an activity out to an external provider","D":"A complete ban on all foreign ownership of any domestic firms at all"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-15","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should offshoring be understood here?","options":{"A":"Reducing import competition","B":"A fixed production schedule agreed years in advance","C":"Moving production or services to another country","D":"Raising domestic wages by law"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-OFFSHORING","scaffold":"Offshoring means relocating production or a service to another country, whether or not another firm ends up performing it. Outsourcing is about who does the work; offshoring is about where it happens. Keeping those two axes separate is exactly what this question tests.","reforge":{"stem":"A US company closes its domestic factory and opens a new one in another country to make the same product. What does this describe?","options":{"A":"A policy that reduces import competition","B":"A national central bank serving one country alone","C":"A law that raises domestic wages","D":"Offshoring, moving production to another country"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-16","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising offshoring. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Raising domestic wages by law","B":"Simple free trade with no policy coordination at all","C":"Reducing import competition","D":"Moving production or services to another country"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-OFFSHORING","scaffold":"Offshoring means relocating production or a service to another country, whether or not another firm ends up performing it. Outsourcing is about who does the work; offshoring is about where it happens. Keeping those two axes separate is exactly what this question tests.","reforge":{"stem":"A US company closes its domestic factory and opens a new one in another country to make the same product. What does this describe?","options":{"A":"Offshoring, moving production to another country","B":"A policy that reduces import competition","C":"The movement of physical goods across borders only","D":"A law that raises domestic wages"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-17","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of trade in services?","options":{"A":"Cross-border exchange of intangible activities","B":"Trade in goods, since only physical items count as exports","C":"A transfer payment within government","D":"A domestic retail sale"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-SERVICES","scaffold":"Trade in services is the cross-border exchange of intangibles — finance, tourism, education, consultancy — recorded in the current account alongside trade in goods, and often called invisible trade. It matters particularly for the UK, whose surplus in services offsets a persistent deficit in goods.","reforge":{"stem":"Which application is most consistent with trade in services?","options":{"A":"Banning all imports","B":"A transfer payment made within a single government","C":"Cross-border exchange of intangible activities","D":"A domestic retail sale"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-18","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising trade in services. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A fixed price agreed for a single imported good","B":"Cross-border exchange of intangible activities","C":"A transfer payment within government","D":"A domestic retail sale"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-SERVICES","scaffold":"Trade in services is the cross-border exchange of intangibles — finance, tourism, education, consultancy — recorded in the current account alongside trade in goods, and often called invisible trade. It matters particularly for the UK, whose surplus in services offsets a persistent deficit in goods.","reforge":{"stem":"A UK accountancy firm is paid by an overseas client for advice delivered entirely online. What does this represent?","options":{"A":"It automatically receives all of the original tariff revenue raised","B":"Trade in services, a cross-border exchange of intangible activity","C":"A transfer payment made within a single government","D":"An ordinary domestic retail sale"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-19","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should invisible exports be understood here?","options":{"A":"Domestic tax receipts","B":"Higher tariffs on imported components","C":"Services sold to overseas customers","D":"Foreign loans only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-INVISIBLES","scaffold":"Invisible exports are services sold to overseas customers — insurance, tourism, transport — earning foreign currency exactly as goods exports do and recorded in the current account. Visible trade means goods. The UK's invisible surplus is a standard evaluation point in balance of payments questions.","reforge":{"stem":"A country earns foreign income by selling insurance and banking services to overseas customers, rather than physical goods. What is this income called?","options":{"A":"To ensure that transport and labour costs are always higher","B":"Goods imported by domestic firms","C":"Invisible exports, services sold to overseas customers","D":"Foreign loans taken out by the government"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-20","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising invisible exports. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Foreign loans only","B":"It removes all imports from the host economy","C":"Domestic tax receipts","D":"Services sold to overseas customers"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-INVISIBLES","scaffold":"Invisible exports are services sold to overseas customers — insurance, tourism, transport — earning foreign currency exactly as goods exports do and recorded in the current account. Visible trade means goods. The UK's invisible surplus is a standard evaluation point in balance of payments questions.","reforge":{"stem":"A country earns foreign income by selling insurance and banking services to overseas customers, rather than physical goods. What is this income called?","options":{"A":"It guarantees that all profits remain in the host economy","B":"Goods imported by domestic firms","C":"Foreign loans taken out by the government","D":"Invisible exports, services sold to overseas customers"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-21","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of import penetration?","options":{"A":"Imports as a share of domestic market demand","B":"The price charged for a single export product","C":"The exchange rate level","D":"Foreign aid per person"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-PENETRATION","scaffold":"Import penetration measures imports as a share of domestic demand, showing how much of what a country consumes is supplied from abroad. Rising penetration points to weakening domestic competitiveness. It is a share of the home market, not a share of GDP or of total trade.","reforge":{"stem":"Imports make up 40% of all cars sold in a country's domestic market. What does this percentage measure?","options":{"A":"Import penetration, imports as a share of domestic market demand","B":"They are unaffected, since terms of trade only track foreign reserves","C":"The level of the exchange rate","D":"Foreign aid received per person"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-22","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising import penetration. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Producing its service without using any labour","B":"Imports as a share of domestic market demand","C":"The exchange rate level","D":"Foreign aid per person"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-PENETRATION","scaffold":"Import penetration measures imports as a share of domestic demand, showing how much of what a country consumes is supplied from abroad. Rising penetration points to weakening domestic competitiveness. It is a share of the home market, not a share of GDP or of total trade.","reforge":{"stem":"Imports make up 40% of all cars sold in a country's domestic market. What does this percentage measure?","options":{"A":"They have worsened, since tax revenue has fallen relative to spending","B":"Import penetration, imports as a share of domestic market demand","C":"The level of the exchange rate","D":"Foreign aid received per person"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-23","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should export diversification be understood here?","options":{"A":"Banning service exports","B":"Domestic productivity is guaranteed to fall","C":"Selling a wider range of products abroad","D":"Reducing market access"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-DIVERSIFICATION","scaffold":"Export diversification means selling a wider range of products, or selling into a wider range of markets, so that a shock to any single export or customer does less damage. It is the standard policy answer to commodity dependence, and it concerns the spread of exports rather than their volume.","reforge":{"stem":"A country that once relied on exporting only oil begins exporting textiles, electronics and tourism services as well. What does this show?","options":{"A":"A ban on exporting any services","B":"They are unaffected, since terms of trade only track government debt","C":"Export diversification, selling a wider range of products abroad","D":"A reduction in market access abroad"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-24","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising export diversification. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Reducing market access","B":"Exports become more expensive abroad only","C":"Banning service exports","D":"Selling a wider range of products abroad"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-DIVERSIFICATION","scaffold":"Export diversification means selling a wider range of products, or selling into a wider range of markets, so that a shock to any single export or customer does less damage. It is the standard policy answer to commodity dependence, and it concerns the spread of exports rather than their volume.","reforge":{"stem":"A country that once relied on exporting only oil begins exporting textiles, electronics and tourism services as well. What does this show?","options":{"A":"A ban on exporting any services","B":"A policy requiring every country to produce all goods domestically","C":"A reduction in market access abroad","D":"Export diversification, selling a wider range of products abroad"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-25","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of commodity dependence?","options":{"A":"Heavy reliance on a small number of raw-material exports","B":"A wide export base","C":"Specialisation guarantees that no worker changes occupation","D":"A fixed currency value"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-COMMODITY","scaffold":"Commodity dependence means relying on a small number of primary-product exports for the bulk of export earnings. It exposes an economy to volatile world prices and, over the long run, to the Prebisch-Singer argument that primary prices fall relative to manufactures. Diversification is the standard remedy.","reforge":{"stem":"A country earns most of its foreign currency from selling a single raw material, such as copper. What does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Commodity dependence, heavy reliance on a small number of raw-material exports","B":"A wide and diversified export base","C":"A movement caused only by price, which shifts the whole curve to a new position on the diagram","D":"A permanently fixed currency value"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-26","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising commodity dependence. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A wide export base","B":"Heavy reliance on a small number of raw-material exports","C":"Trade makes every country equally productive in all goods","D":"A fixed currency value"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-COMMODITY","scaffold":"Commodity dependence means relying on a small number of primary-product exports for the bulk of export earnings. It exposes an economy to volatile world prices and, over the long run, to the Prebisch-Singer argument that primary prices fall relative to manufactures. Diversification is the standard remedy.","reforge":{"stem":"A country earns most of its foreign currency from selling a single raw material, such as copper. What does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"A wide and diversified export base","B":"Commodity dependence, heavy reliance on a small number of raw-material exports","C":"Revenue above total cost, which is earned by a firm rather than enjoyed directly by a consumer","D":"A permanently fixed currency value"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-27","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should trade shock be understood here?","options":{"A":"A rise in local school enrolment","B":"A rule stating inflation always equals wage growth","C":"A sudden change in external demand or trade costs","D":"A fixed production schedule"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-SHOCK","scaffold":"A trade shock is a sudden change originating outside the economy in demand for its exports or in the cost of trading — a key market collapsing, freight costs spiking, a new tariff. Because it arrives from outside, it hits the current account and output before domestic policy has any chance to respond.","reforge":{"stem":"A sudden collapse in global demand for a country's main export causes its trade revenue to fall sharply overnight. What is this an example of?","options":{"A":"A rise in local school enrolment","B":"A predictable, pre-announced domestic tax change","C":"A trade shock, a sudden change in external demand or trade costs","D":"The removal of all national differences in income and living standards"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-28","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising trade shock. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A fixed production schedule","B":"A claim that all exports are perfectly price inelastic","C":"A rise in local school enrolment","D":"A sudden change in external demand or trade costs"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-SHOCK","scaffold":"A trade shock is a sudden change originating outside the economy in demand for its exports or in the cost of trading — a key market collapsing, freight costs spiking, a new tariff. Because it arrives from outside, it hits the current account and output before domestic policy has any chance to respond.","reforge":{"stem":"A sudden collapse in global demand for a country's main export causes its trade revenue to fall sharply overnight. What is this an example of?","options":{"A":"A rise in local school enrolment","B":"A predictable, pre-announced domestic tax change","C":"A fall in international communication caused by higher transport costs","D":"A trade shock, a sudden change in external demand or trade costs"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-29","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of tariff escalation?","options":{"A":"Higher tariffs applied to more processed goods","B":"A national fiscal rule adopted by one government","C":"A subsidy to consumers","D":"A ban on raw materials"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-TARIFF-ESCALATION","scaffold":"Tariff escalation is the practice of setting higher tariffs on more processed goods than on raw materials. It lets an economy import cheap inputs while protecting its own manufacturers, and it discourages developing countries from moving up the value chain. That development consequence is the point of the concept.","reforge":{"stem":"A country charges a low tariff on raw cotton but a much higher tariff on finished clothing made from it. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Tariff escalation, higher tariffs applied to more processed goods","B":"Falling tariffs applied to finished goods","C":"A subsidy paid directly to consumers","D":"A complete ban on all foreign ownership of any domestic firms at all"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-30","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising tariff escalation. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A fall in import prices following an appreciation","B":"Higher tariffs applied to more processed goods","C":"A subsidy to consumers","D":"A ban on raw materials"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-TARIFF-ESCALATION","scaffold":"Tariff escalation is the practice of setting higher tariffs on more processed goods than on raw materials. It lets an economy import cheap inputs while protecting its own manufacturers, and it discourages developing countries from moving up the value chain. That development consequence is the point of the concept.","reforge":{"stem":"A country charges a low tariff on raw cotton but a much higher tariff on finished clothing made from it. What does this show?","options":{"A":"Falling tariffs applied to finished goods","B":"Tariff escalation, higher tariffs applied to more processed goods","C":"A subsidy paid directly to consumers","D":"It automatically receives all of the original tariff revenue raised"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-31","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should trade bloc be understood here?","options":{"A":"Consumers always receive lower prices for imported products","B":"A single private firm","C":"A group of countries reducing barriers between members","D":"A national bank only"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-BLOC","scaffold":"A trade bloc is a group of countries that reduce trade barriers between members, and the forms escalate: a free trade area removes internal tariffs, a customs union adds a common external tariff, a single market adds free movement of factors. Naming the right level of integration is usually where the mark is.","reforge":{"stem":"Several neighbouring countries agree to remove tariffs on trade between themselves while keeping their own tariffs on outsiders. What have they formed?","options":{"A":"A single private firm operating across borders","B":"They are unaffected, since terms of trade only track foreign reserves","C":"A trade bloc, a group of countries reducing barriers between members","D":"A national central bank serving one country alone"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-32","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising trade bloc. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A national bank only","B":"A single private firm","C":"A quota always lowers domestic prices below world prices","D":"A group of countries reducing barriers between members"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-BLOC","scaffold":"A trade bloc is a group of countries that reduce trade barriers between members, and the forms escalate: a free trade area removes internal tariffs, a customs union adds a common external tariff, a single market adds free movement of factors. Naming the right level of integration is usually where the mark is.","reforge":{"stem":"Several neighbouring countries agree to remove tariffs on trade between themselves while keeping their own tariffs on outsiders. What have they formed?","options":{"A":"A single private firm operating across borders","B":"They have worsened, since tax revenue has fallen relative to spending","C":"A national central bank serving one country alone","D":"A trade bloc, a group of countries reducing barriers between members"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-33","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of economic union?","options":{"A":"Integration including coordinated economic policies","B":"They face a lower price and more choice in every case","C":"A fixed price for one import","D":"A ban on migration"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-ECON-UNION","scaffold":"An economic union goes beyond a single market by coordinating economic policy across members — competition, regional and often fiscal rules — on top of free movement of goods, services, capital and labour. A monetary union, which adds a shared currency, is a further step again.","reforge":{"stem":"Member states not only trade freely but also coordinate tax, spending and regulatory policy with each other. What level of integration is this?","options":{"A":"An economic union, integration including coordinated economic policy","B":"The removal of all national differences in income and living standards","C":"A fixed price agreed for a single imported good","D":"An outright ban on migration between members"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-34","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising economic union. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"They receive the tariff revenue directly as a payment","B":"Integration including coordinated economic policies","C":"A fixed price for one import","D":"A ban on migration"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-ECON-UNION","scaffold":"An economic union goes beyond a single market by coordinating economic policy across members — competition, regional and often fiscal rules — on top of free movement of goods, services, capital and labour. A monetary union, which adds a shared currency, is a further step again.","reforge":{"stem":"Member states not only trade freely but also coordinate tax, spending and regulatory policy with each other. What level of integration is this?","options":{"A":"A fall in international communication caused by higher transport costs","B":"An economic union, integration including coordinated economic policy","C":"A fixed price agreed for a single imported good","D":"An outright ban on migration between members"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-35","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should monetary union be understood here?","options":{"A":"A private currency exchange","B":"A strategy of exporting domestic services abroad","C":"Countries sharing a currency or monetary policy","D":"A national fiscal rule"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-MON-UNION","scaffold":"A monetary union means members share a currency, or a single monetary policy, so an individual country gives up both the interest rate and the exchange rate as adjustment tools. That loss is the core evaluation: an asymmetric shock can no longer be met with a national devaluation.","reforge":{"stem":"A group of countries gives up their separate national currencies in favour of one shared currency and monetary policy. What is this called?","options":{"A":"A private currency exchange run by one bank","B":"Countries that share only a common tariff","C":"A monetary union, countries sharing a currency or monetary policy","D":"They are unaffected, since terms of trade only track government debt"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-36","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising monetary union. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A national fiscal rule","B":"A strategy of removing local production capacity","C":"A private currency exchange","D":"Countries sharing a currency or monetary policy"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-MON-UNION","scaffold":"A monetary union means members share a currency, or a single monetary policy, so an individual country gives up both the interest rate and the exchange rate as adjustment tools. That loss is the core evaluation: an asymmetric shock can no longer be met with a national devaluation.","reforge":{"stem":"A group of countries gives up their separate national currencies in favour of one shared currency and monetary policy. What is this called?","options":{"A":"A private currency exchange run by one bank","B":"Countries that share only a common tariff","C":"A policy requiring every country to produce all goods domestically","D":"A monetary union, countries sharing a currency or monetary policy"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-37","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of capital mobility?","options":{"A":"The ease with which financial funds cross borders","B":"To remove the need for any quality-control systems","C":"A limit on imports","D":"A fixed wage contract"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-CAPITAL-MOBILITY","scaffold":"Capital mobility is the ease with which financial funds move across borders. High mobility lets investment flow to the highest return, but it equally permits sudden reversals — hot money leaving at speed — which is why it recurs in exchange rate and financial crisis questions.","reforge":{"stem":"Investors can move billions of pounds between countries within seconds using modern financial markets. What does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"High capital mobility, the ease with which financial funds cross borders","B":"Consumer benefit above price, which is enjoyed by the buyer rather than the seller of a good","C":"A legal limit placed on imports","D":"A wage fixed permanently by contract"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-38","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising capital mobility. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"It prevents the economy from developing local firms","B":"The ease with which financial funds cross borders","C":"A limit on imports","D":"A fixed wage contract"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-CAPITAL-MOBILITY","scaffold":"Capital mobility is the ease with which financial funds move across borders. High mobility lets investment flow to the highest return, but it equally permits sudden reversals — hot money leaving at speed — which is why it recurs in exchange rate and financial crisis questions.","reforge":{"stem":"Investors can move billions of pounds between countries within seconds using modern financial markets. What does this illustrate?","options":{"A":"Consumer surplus, the extra benefit enjoyed by the buyer over and above the price they actually paid for the apples","B":"High capital mobility, the ease with which financial funds cross borders","C":"A legal limit placed on imports","D":"A wage fixed permanently by contract"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-39","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should financial account be understood here?","options":{"A":"Household tax payments","B":"Trade in goods only","C":"Recorded flows of investment and financial assets","D":"Countries no longer need to consider resource scarcity"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-FIN-ACCOUNT","scaffold":"The financial account records cross-border flows of investment and financial assets: direct investment, portfolio investment and reserve movements. The current account records trade in goods and services plus income. The accounts must balance overall, which is why a current account deficit implies a financial account surplus.","reforge":{"stem":"A balance of payments record shows foreign investors buying UK shares and property. Which part of the accounts does this appear in?","options":{"A":"A rise in supply caused by higher prices, since landlords respond to the rent ceiling by building more rental homes","B":"Trade in goods only","C":"The financial account, recording flows of investment and financial assets","D":"Domestic government spending"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-40","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising financial account. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Stop trading once domestic wheat demand is satisfied","B":"Trade in goods only","C":"Household tax payments","D":"Recorded flows of investment and financial assets"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-FIN-ACCOUNT","scaffold":"The financial account records cross-border flows of investment and financial assets: direct investment, portfolio investment and reserve movements. The current account records trade in goods and services plus income. The accounts must balance overall, which is why a current account deficit implies a financial account surplus.","reforge":{"stem":"A balance of payments record shows foreign investors buying UK shares and property. Which part of the accounts does this appear in?","options":{"A":"A rise in quantity demanded caused by the law, since any price ceiling automatically pulls more buyers into the market","B":"Trade in goods only","C":"Domestic government spending","D":"The financial account, recording flows of investment and financial assets"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-41","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of foreign-exchange reserves?","options":{"A":"Foreign assets held to support currency policy","B":"Domestic business profits","C":"To avoid selling its products to local consumers","D":"A private pension fund"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-RESERVES","scaffold":"Foreign exchange reserves are the foreign currency and assets held by a central bank, used to intervene in currency markets and to meet external obligations. Selling reserves supports the domestic currency; buying them holds it down. They are the instrument behind any fixed or managed exchange rate.","reforge":{"stem":"A central bank holds large stocks of US dollars and gold specifically so it can intervene to support its own currency. What are these holdings called?","options":{"A":"Foreign-exchange reserves, foreign assets held to support currency policy","B":"A maximum below equilibrium, imposed by government to stop a price rising any further above the market price","C":"A country's annual exports only","D":"A private pension fund's assets"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-42","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising foreign-exchange reserves. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Domestic business profits","B":"Foreign assets held to support currency policy","C":"Produce every good without importing any products","D":"A private pension fund"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-RESERVES","scaffold":"Foreign exchange reserves are the foreign currency and assets held by a central bank, used to intervene in currency markets and to meet external obligations. Selling reserves supports the domestic currency; buying them holds it down. They are the instrument behind any fixed or managed exchange rate.","reforge":{"stem":"A central bank holds large stocks of US dollars and gold specifically so it can intervene to support its own currency. What are these holdings called?","options":{"A":"A fall in the wage paid by firms, since a minimum wage is treated as removing the legal requirement for firms to pay it","B":"Foreign-exchange reserves, foreign assets held to support currency policy","C":"A country's annual exports only","D":"A private pension fund's assets"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-43","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should currency convertibility be understood here?","options":{"A":"A fixed import quota","B":"A ban on foreign exchange","C":"The ability to exchange a currency for others","D":"A single private firm operating across borders"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-CONVERTIBILITY","scaffold":"Currency convertibility is the ability to exchange a currency freely for others. Full convertibility supports trade and investment but exposes the currency to speculative pressure, which is why some economies restrict it. It concerns the freedom to exchange, not the rate at which the exchange happens.","reforge":{"stem":"A traveller can freely exchange a country's currency for US dollars or euros at any bank. What property of the currency does this show?","options":{"A":"A fixed quota placed on imports","B":"An outright ban on foreign exchange","C":"Currency convertibility, the ability to exchange a currency for others","D":"Have the highest absolute level of productivity in every single industry"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-44","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising currency convertibility. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Government revenue from tariffs must fall to zero","B":"A ban on foreign exchange","C":"A fixed import quota","D":"The ability to exchange a currency for others"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-CONVERTIBILITY","scaffold":"Currency convertibility is the ability to exchange a currency freely for others. Full convertibility supports trade and investment but exposes the currency to speculative pressure, which is why some economies restrict it. It concerns the freedom to exchange, not the rate at which the exchange happens.","reforge":{"stem":"A traveller can freely exchange a country's currency for US dollars or euros at any bank. What property of the currency does this show?","options":{"A":"A fixed quota placed on imports","B":"An outright ban on foreign exchange","C":"A change in tastes, which shifts the whole demand curve rather than moving along the existing one","D":"Currency convertibility, the ability to exchange a currency for others"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-45","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of real exchange rate?","options":{"A":"An exchange rate adjusted for relative price levels","B":"The nominal rate alone","C":"The domestic tax rate","D":"A government payment made only to domestic households"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-REAL-ER","scaffold":"The real exchange rate adjusts the nominal rate for relative price levels, showing what a currency actually buys abroad and therefore how competitive exports really are. A nominal depreciation cancelled out by higher domestic inflation leaves the real rate — and competitiveness — unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"Two countries have the same nominal exchange rate, but one has much higher inflation, making its goods relatively more expensive abroad. Which measure captures this difference?","options":{"A":"The real exchange rate, adjusted for relative price levels","B":"A quota raises tax receipts; a tariff limits physical quantities","C":"The domestic tax rate","D":"A country's export volume"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-46","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising real exchange rate. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"The nominal rate alone","B":"An exchange rate adjusted for relative price levels","C":"The domestic tax rate","D":"A pension fund briefly buys overseas government bonds"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-REAL-ER","scaffold":"The real exchange rate adjusts the nominal rate for relative price levels, showing what a currency actually buys abroad and therefore how competitive exports really are. A nominal depreciation cancelled out by higher domestic inflation leaves the real rate — and competitiveness — unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"Two countries have the same nominal exchange rate, but one has much higher inflation, making its goods relatively more expensive abroad. Which measure captures this difference?","options":{"A":"A quota applies only to services; a tariff applies only to goods","B":"The real exchange rate, adjusted for relative price levels","C":"The domestic tax rate","D":"A country's export volume"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-47","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should J-curve effect be understood here?","options":{"A":"The current account always worsens permanently after depreciation.","B":"A permanent trade surplus","C":"A current account worsens before improving after depreciation","D":"A vertical supply curve"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-JCURVE","scaffold":"The J-curve describes a current account that worsens immediately after a depreciation before improving. In the short run contracts are fixed and demand is inelastic, so the higher import bill dominates; over time volumes adjust and the balance improves. It is the timing of those two effects that gives the shape.","reforge":{"stem":"After a currency depreciates, a country's trade balance initially gets worse before improving several months later. What is this pattern called?","options":{"A":"No error — the fiscal deficit and current account deficit are the same concept.","B":"A permanent trade surplus that never changes","C":"The J-curve effect, where the current account worsens before improving","D":"A perfectly vertical supply curve"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-48","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising J-curve effect. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"A vertical supply curve","B":"A ban on all domestic production","C":"The current account always worsens permanently after depreciation.","D":"A current account worsens before improving after depreciation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-JCURVE","scaffold":"The J-curve describes a current account that worsens immediately after a depreciation before improving. In the short run contracts are fixed and demand is inelastic, so the higher import bill dominates; over time volumes adjust and the balance improves. It is the timing of those two effects that gives the shape.","reforge":{"stem":"After a currency depreciates, a country's trade balance initially gets worse before improving several months later. What is this pattern called?","options":{"A":"No error — the fiscal deficit and current account deficit are the same concept.","B":"A permanent trade surplus that never changes","C":"A perfectly vertical supply curve","D":"The J-curve effect, where the current account worsens before improving"},"correct":"D"}},{"id":"TH4C-49","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"What is the most accurate description of Marshall-Lerner condition?","options":{"A":"Export and import demand elasticities together exceed one","B":"Inflation equals wage growth","C":"A quota removes protection; a tariff guarantees free trade","D":"Imports are banned"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-GLOB-MARSHALL-LERNER","scaffold":"The Marshall-Lerner condition states that a depreciation improves the current account only if the combined price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceed one. Where demand is inelastic, the higher import bill outweighs the extra export volume. It is the condition behind the J-curve's eventual upturn.","reforge":{"stem":"A depreciation will only improve the trade balance if the combined price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceed one. What is this condition called?","options":{"A":"The Marshall-Lerner condition","B":"A rule stating inflation always equals wage growth","C":"A claim that all exports are perfectly price inelastic","D":"An outright ban on all imports"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"TH4C-50","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising Marshall-Lerner condition. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Inflation equals wage growth","B":"Export and import demand elasticities together exceed one","C":"To ensure that transport and labour costs are always higher","D":"Imports are banned"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-GLOB-MARSHALL-LERNER","scaffold":"The Marshall-Lerner condition states that a depreciation improves the current account only if the combined price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceed one. Where demand is inelastic, the higher import bill outweighs the extra export volume. It is the condition behind the J-curve's eventual upturn.","reforge":{"stem":"A depreciation will only improve the trade balance if the combined price elasticities of demand for exports and imports exceed one. What is this condition called?","options":{"A":"A rule stating inflation always equals wage growth","B":"The Marshall-Lerner condition","C":"A claim that all exports are perfectly price inelastic","D":"An outright ban on all imports"},"correct":"B"}},{"id":"TH4C-51","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"How should import substitution be understood here?","options":{"A":"Exporting domestic services","B":"Country A has a lower opportunity cost only","C":"Replacing imports with domestic production","D":"Removing local capacity"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-GLOB-IMPORT-SUB","scaffold":"Import substitution is a development strategy of replacing imports with domestic production behind protective barriers, in order to build local industry. The standard critique is that protected firms remain inefficient and the domestic market limits scale, which is why export-led growth is the usual comparison.","reforge":{"stem":"A government encourages domestic factories to manufacture goods that were previously bought from abroad. What strategy is this?","options":{"A":"A quota raises tax receipts; a tariff limits physical quantities","B":"A strategy of increasing reliance on imports","C":"Import substitution, replacing imports with domestic production","D":"A strategy of removing local production capacity"},"correct":"C"}},{"id":"TH4C-52","spec":"4.1.1","stem":"A student is revising import substitution. Which conclusion is correct?","options":{"A":"Removing local capacity","B":"A rule limiting sales to local consumers only","C":"Exporting domestic services","D":"Replacing imports with domestic production"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-GLOB-IMPORT-SUB","scaffold":"Import substitution is a development strategy of replacing imports with domestic production behind protective barriers, in order to build local industry. The standard critique is that protected firms remain inefficient and the domestic market limits scale, which is why export-led growth is the usual comparison.","reforge":{"stem":"A government encourages domestic factories to manufacture goods that were previously bought from abroad. What strategy is this?","options":{"A":"A quota applies only to services; a tariff applies only to goods","B":"A strategy of increasing reliance on imports","C":"A strategy of removing local production capacity","D":"Import substitution, replacing imports with domestic production"},"correct":"D"}}]}});
